{"issue": 53, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYOrG2g", "before": "The rules below are what the code mostly follows. Where the code does not\nfollow them, the difference is work rather than taste. Nothing states them\ntoday.\n\n## The rules for a native resource\n\n1. A `CoreValue`, a `CoreEvalState` and a `CoreLockedFlake` are confined to\n   one evaluator thread. Every call reaches them through the\n   `NixThreadExecutor` of that evaluator.\n2. A `CoreStore` is thread-safe, and the store pool may share it.\n3. Every native resource has exactly one owner, and the owner is the object\n   that created it.\n4. Dropping the Python handle releases the native resource, on both engines,\n   with no action by the caller. An explicit `release()` is the deterministic\n   form of the same thing, and the close of the evaluator is the backstop.\n   See nixidae/nixidae#40.\n5. A finalizer never calls Nix. A finalizer queues, and the next operation on\n   the owning thread drains the queue.\n\n## The rules for layering\n\n```\nbindings -> _core -> {settings, stores, models, exceptions, protocols, logging} -> {inproc, rpc}\n```\n\nDependencies point from the right to the left only. `_core` never imports an\nengine. An engine never imports the other engine.\n\nOne exception is permanent, and it must stay documented at the import site:\n`rpc/client/_pool.py` imports `rpc/worker/_worker.worker_service_factory`,\nbecause the forkserver pickles it by module path.\n\n## Scope\n\nPut the rules for a native resource in the module docstring of\n`_core/_objects.py`. Put both sets in\n`docs/nanopynix/architecture-principles.md`.\n\nSection 4 of `CODE_IMPROVEMENT_PLAN.md`.\n"}
{"issue": 53, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdVdVFQ", "before": "## Half of this issue is already done\n\nChecked on 2026-08-07 against the tree.\n\n### `docs/nanopynix/architecture-principles.md` -- complete\n\nThe file carries both sets of rules that this issue asks for. The layering\nrule is there, in the shape this issue gives it:\n\n```\nbindings  \u2192  _core  \u2192  {settings, stores, models, exceptions, protocols, logging}  \u2192  {inproc, rpc}\n```\n\nwith `Dependencies point right to left only`, the two named rules\n(`_core` never imports an engine`, `An engine never imports the other\nengine`), and the permanent `worker_service_factory` exception.\n\nThe five rules for a native resource are under `## Native resource ownership`.\n\n### The module docstring of `_core/_objects.py` -- three rules short\n\n`nanopynix/src/nanopynix/_core/_objects.py` has an 18-line docstring. It\ncarries rule 1 and rule 2:\n\n> The public ``inproc`` API may share a ``CoreStore`` across Store-pool\n> threads, but each ``CoreEvalState`` and its values remain confined to one\n> evaluator thread.\n\nThe rest of the docstring explains why the class is named `Core` and not\n`Local`, which is worth keeping and is not one of these rules.\n\n**Rules 3, 4 and 5 are not there:**\n\n3. Every native resource has exactly one owner, and the owner is the object\n   that created it. (The docstring says the objects \"own L1 bindings\", which\n   is not the same statement.)\n4. Dropping the Python handle releases the native resource, on both engines,\n   with no action by the caller.\n5. A finalizer never calls Nix. A finalizer queues, and the next operation on\n   the owning thread drains the queue.\n\n## What is left\n\nAdd rules 3, 4 and 5 to the docstring of `_core/_objects.py`. Nothing else in\nthis issue needs work.\n\nnixidae/nixidae#40, which rule 4 refers to, is closed, so the behaviour that rule 4 states\nexists and is only undocumented at that site.\n"}
{"issue": 55, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYOrSSQ", "before": "This issue tracks the roadmap of the code review that\n`CODE_IMPROVEMENT_PLAN.md` reports. Each item below is one issue, with its own\nevidence, scope, acceptance criteria and required tests.\n\n**Status lives on each issue, and nowhere else.** GitHub renders every\nreference below as open or closed, so this list never needs a checkbox and\nnever goes stale. It carried 20 checkboxes until 2026-08-02, and 11 of them\nwere wrong -- closed issues still showing unchecked, under a heading that\nclaimed this was the source of truth. A status you have to maintain by hand is\na status that disagrees with reality.\n\n`CODE_IMPROVEMENT_PLAN.md` maps a finding to an issue number and holds no\nstatus of its own, for the same reason.\n\n## Phase 0 -- correctness and safety\n\n- nixidae/nixidae#35 Settings: route each scope to the Nix door that accepts it (critical, L)\n- nixidae/nixidae#36 Settings: merge session-level eval, fetch and flake defaults (high, M)\n- nixidae/nixidae#37 RPC: the worker does not enforce the construction-time settings guard (high, S)\n- nixidae/nixidae#38 inproc: Session.close() swallows cancellation (medium, S)\n- nixidae/nixidae#39 inproc: NIX_USER_CONF_FILES is set and never restored (low, S)\n\n## Phase 1 -- lifecycle hardening\n\n- nixidae/nixidae#40 inproc: a Nix value is never released when its handle is collected (high, M)\n- nixidae/nixidae#41 Tests: worker-death coverage never kills a worker (medium, M)\n- nixidae/nixidae#42 Logging: a stalled log consumer stalls Nix (medium, M)\n\n## Phase 2 -- API and type safety\n\n- nixidae/nixidae#43 RPC: give the worker a typed state and a typed handle registry (medium, M)\n- nixidae/nixidae#44 API: the public surface disagrees with itself (medium, M)\n- nixidae/nixidae#45 core: retire CoreEvalState.__getattr__ (low, M)\n\n## Phase 3 -- test architecture\n\n- nixidae/nixidae#46 Tests: a settings routing matrix (high, M)\n- nixidae/nixidae#47 Tests: property-based round trips for stores.py (medium, M)\n- nixidae/nixidae#48 Tests: serialization round trips for the wire types (medium, S)\n- nixidae/nixidae#49 Tests: hostile boundary conditions (medium, M)\n- nixidae/nixidae#50 Tests: the drift check under 2.31 and 2.35 in CI (medium, S)\n\n## Phase 4 -- tooling and documentation\n\n- nixidae/nixidae#51 CI: no lint, type or format gate runs anywhere (high, M)\n- nixidae/nixidae#52 Docs: documentation code is never executed (medium, M)\n- nixidae/nixidae#53 Docs: write down the ownership rules and the layering rules (low, S)\n- nixidae/nixidae#54 Resolve the stdio worker: give it a client, or delete it (low, S)\n\n## Baseline at the time of the review\n\n| Command | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `pytest tests --nix-test-backends local,daemon` | 2534 passed, 0 failed, 3 skipped, 691.5 s |\n| `pyright` | 0 errors |\n| `ruff check` | All checks passed |\n| `ruff check --config ruff-strict.toml` | All checks passed |\n| `ruff format --check` | 259 files already formatted |\n\n**No item above comes from a failing test.** Every confirmed defect is a\nbehaviour that the suite does not exercise. That is the main conclusion about\nthe test strategy: the checks that exist all pass, and the gaps are where the\nchecks are absent.\n\n## Order\n\nPhase 0 and Phase 1 are the defects. Do not start Phase 2 before they land:\nnixidae/nixidae#43 and nixidae/nixidae#45 refactor the layer that nixidae/nixidae#37 and nixidae/nixidae#40 correct.\n\n## Reasoning that is not an issue\n\nFour parts of the review are not work items. They live in `docs/`:\n\n- `docs/nanopynix/architecture-principles.md` -- layer boundaries, ownership\n  of a native resource, the error taxonomy, the sync and async policy, and the\n  compatibility policy.\n- `docs/nanopynix/quality-gates.md` -- the exact commands, and which gate runs\n  when.\n- `docs/nanopynix/decisions.md` -- the ideas that this review rejected or\n  deferred, and the open questions with a recommended default for each.\n\n"}
{"issue": 67, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYOslhQ", "before": "## TL;DR\n\nnixidae/nixidae#66 gave a cancelled operation a bounded grace, and then abandonment. rpc could\ndo better: it owns a worker process and can replace it. nixidae/nixidae#66 deliberately did\nnot expose that, because both engines then behave the same and no caller has to\nask which engine it holds.\n\nThis issue is the opt-in that changes the answer, plus the property a caller\nreads to know which guarantee it has.\n\n## Why it was left out of nixidae/nixidae#66\n\nOne worker hosts many evaluators, keyed by `eval_handle` in `_worker_eval.py`.\nTo replace the worker destroys every sibling evaluator. That is a policy that\nneeds a caller who asks for it, and it is never a safe default.\n\n## What this issue asks for\n\n1. **An opt-in escalation policy on the rpc session.** When an operation does\n   not answer its interrupt inside the grace, replace the worker instead of\n   poisoning the one evaluator. State clearly what it costs: every other\n   evaluator on that worker dies with it.\n2. **A capability property that a caller reads.** Something a caller can ask\n   before it decides how long to wait, and whether it may retry. inproc answers\n   \"the thread is abandoned\"; rpc with escalation answers \"the process is\n   replaced\".\n\nThe property earns its place here, and it did not earn a place in nixidae/nixidae#66. In nixidae/nixidae#66\nthe two engines behave the same, so there was nothing for a caller to branch\non.\n\n## What nixidae/nixidae#66 already established, and this must not undo\n\n* Both engines raise `EvaluatorAbandonedError`, and the state is permanent.\n* To close an abandoned evaluator always succeeds, on both engines.\n* The two engines reach that state at different moments, and process isolation\n  forces this. The parity contract in\n  `docs/nanopynix/architecture-principles.md` records it.\n\n## Why it matters more than the grace value suggests\n\nAn abandoned thread is not a free resource. It continues to allocate, and Boehm\nstops the world when it signals each registered thread and waits approximately\n0.45s in total for all of them. A machine that swaps under the extra heap\nmisses that budget, and Boehm aborts with \"Signals delivery fails constantly\".\nA long-lived host application that abandons several threads therefore degrades.\nEscalation is the only lever that gives the memory back, and only rpc has it.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n* The policy is opt-in, and the default behaviour of nixidae/nixidae#66 does not change.\n* A test proves that escalation replaces the worker, and that it reports the\n  loss of the sibling evaluators.\n* A test proves that the capability property tells the two engines apart.\n* The parity ledger records the new divergence as forced by process isolation.\n\nRefs nixidae/nixidae#66\n"}
{"issue": 73, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdVf6rg", "before": "## A second instance, with a root cause -- and it is not the one this issue records\n\n`1265fc97` fixes a race with the same symptom as this issue, in a different\ndriver. **This issue stays open**: it records `tests/pynix/test_lsp_e2e.py`\nover a real client, and the fix below touches only the in-process driver.\n\n### The mechanism\n\n`pynix/src/pynix/_lsp/_handlers.py:294` starts `_warm_module_args` as a\nbackground task and returns without awaiting it. That is deliberate, and the\ndocstring above it gives the reason: a real editor cannot wait for the module\narguments of a file to resolve.\n\n`tests/support/lsp_drivers.py`'s `InProcessDriver` calls `_sync_document`\ndirectly, so `open` returned while the warm-up still ran. A hover on a module\nargument then answered `None`. `WireDriver` was never affected, because a\nJSON-RPC round trip usually outlasts the warm-up.\n\nThat explains a detail this issue could not: **only the `[in_process-...]`\nparametrisation failed.**\n\n### The evidence\n\nRun [31618055409](https://github.com/Lillecarl/nanopynix/actions/runs/31618055409),\non `develop`. Two jobs failed on the same easykubenix hovers, in two different\nways, while **14 jobs that ran the same tests passed**:\n\n| job | failure |\n| --- | --- |\n| `test-nogc-git` | 2 tests, 120s deadline -- the signature of this issue |\n| `test-local-nix_2_31` | 3 tests, `AssertionError: expected a hover result, got None` |\n\nA re-run of both, at the same commit, separated them:\n\n| job | re-run |\n| --- | --- |\n| `test-nogc-git` | **success** -- non-determinism, as this issue demonstrates it |\n| `test-local-nix_2_31` | **failure again** -- reproducible, so not a flake |\n\n`test-local-nix_2_31` loses this race reliably because it is the slowest job of\nthe matrix: 12m22s against 7m39s for `test-local-git` (see nixidae/nixidae#161).\n\n### After the fix\n\nTwo dispatched runs of `test-local-nix_2_31` on `ci-develop`, both green:\n[31625283471](https://github.com/Lillecarl/nanopynix/actions/runs/31625283471)\nand [31626487823](https://github.com/Lillecarl/nanopynix/actions/runs/31626487823).\nTwo failures before, two successes after.\n\n### What is not fixed\n\nThe `None` mode is gone from the in-process driver. **The timeout mode over a\nreal client is untouched**, and that is what this issue is about: a stall in the\nshared session that no amount of waiting in a test driver explains. The\n`hang_report` work from the comment above still stands as the way the next\noccurrence gets read.\n\nThe regression test is `tests/harness/test_lsp_driver_settle.py`. It lives\nthere because no other test reports when the wait goes away -- the scenarios\npass on a development machine, where the warm-up wins the race every time. I\ncould not reproduce the CI failure locally on Nix 2.31 either with or without\nthe fix.\n"}
{"issue": 73, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdVf7Hg", "before": "The macOS job reproduces this **every run**, which turns the non-determinism of\nthis issue into something that can be measured. I filed nixidae/nixidae#188 for it before\nfinding this one; that is a duplicate and I have closed it. Everything it\nlearned is below.\n\n## It is the same defect\n\nRun [31955684530](https://github.com/Lillecarl/nanopynix/actions/runs/31955684530),\n`test-darwin-nix_2_34`: the same five tests, the same 120-second deadline, the\nsame two-minute spacing, and the same teardown:\n\n```\npytest_lsp/protocol.py:53 in _handle_notification\n    future.set_result(params)\nconcurrent.futures._base.InvalidStateError: CANCELLED: <Future ... state=cancelled>\n```\n\nThat `set_result` on a cancelled future is the notification **arriving after\nthe deadline cancelled the waiter**. So this is slowness, not a deadlock. The\nanswer does come.\n\n## What it is not\n\nThe same server, spawned by hand over real stdio and driven with the same three\nmessages, answers the same `didOpen` for the same file in **1.0 s** on macOS\n26.5.1 arm64. Each of these was tried, and none changed that number:\n\n- the exact `client_capabilities(\"visual-studio-code\")` the fixture sends,\n  rather than an empty capability set;\n- `initialize` with and without `rootUri`;\n- the `NIX_CONFIG` that `_configure_worker_local_stores` exports;\n- draining the stderr of the server, and leaving it undrained.\n\nIt is not one test poisoning another either: it fails the same way when it is\nthe only test of the run.\n\nSo the difference is the process pytest runs in, and not the protocol, the\ncapabilities, the environment or the order. That matches the shape here: a\nshared session-scoped fixture, and a server that is slow to answer rather than\nstuck.\n\n## What was done meanwhile\n\nThe five behavioural tests moved to `pynix/tests/test_lsp_protocol.py`, on the\n`lsp_wire` fixture: the same `pytest_lsp.LanguageClient`, the same\ncapabilities, the same JSON-RPC framing and serialization, both halves in one\nprocess, and a live reference to the server.\n\n**The same five assertions run in 3.4 s there, against 10 minutes of deadlines\nover stdio.** Coverage went up rather than down: two of the five already ran at\nthe wire tier through `test_lsp_scenarios.py`, and the other three ran\nin-process only.\n\n`test_lsp_e2e.py` keeps the two tests that need a real process, and its fixture\nnow reports the stderr of the server on a failure -- it collected nothing\nbefore, which is why the list above took six experiments by hand.\n\n## What is left for this issue\n\nA document round trip against a spawned `pynix lsp` is now tested nowhere, and\nthat gap belongs here. The round trip is the defect, so a test of it today\nasserts the defect rather than the behaviour.\n\n- Find why the pytest process makes the same request 120 times slower.\n- Give `test_lsp_e2e.py` its round-trip test back, ungated.\n"}
{"issue": 76, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYOtmZQ", "before": "## What\n\nNix builds its evaluator without the Boehm collector when `-Dgc=disabled` sets\n`NIX_USE_BOEHMGC` to 0. The evaluator then allocates and never releases.\nSupport that build for an RPC worker process, and recycle the process instead\nof collecting inside it.\n\n## Why\n\n**A worker process can afford to leak.** The RPC engine already gives each\nworker its own process, and the operating system reclaims the memory of a\nprocess that exits. A recycle policy replaces the collector. The in-process\nengine cannot make that trade, so this is a worker capability and not a\nproject-wide switch.\n\n**AddressSanitizer needs it.** libexpr refuses ASAN together with the\ncollector, in `src/libexpr/meson.build`:\n\n```meson\nbdw_gc_required = get_option('gc').disable_if(\n  'address' in get_option('b_sanitize'),\n  error_message : 'Building with Boehm GC and ASAN is not supported',\n)\n```\n\nnixidae/nixidae#64 first built an ASAN variant that met this test only because the flag\narrived through `NIX_CFLAGS_COMPILE`, which meson does not read. That build\nreported a tag read of a `Value` in `value.hh` on three Nix versions, which is\nwhat a conservative collector produces when it frees an object that is still\nlive. The report was not usable as evidence. nixidae/nixidae#64 now carries UBSan alone, and\na worker without the collector is the supported route to ASAN.\n\n## What the evaluator does without the collector\n\nFrom `eval-gc.hh` and `eval-inline.hh`:\n\n| | `NIX_USE_BOEHMGC=1` | `=0` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `allocBytes(n)` | `GC_MALLOC(n)` | `calloc(n, 1)` |\n| `allocValue()` | `GC_malloc_many` batch, thread-local free list | `allocBytes(sizeof(Value))` |\n| `allocEnv(1)` | the same batch | `allocBytes(...)` |\n| `traceable_allocator`, `gc_allocator` | the Boehm allocators | `std::allocator` |\n| `GC_MALLOC_ATOMIC` | Boehm | `std::malloc` |\n| `gc`, `gc_cleanup` base classes | the Boehm classes | empty structs |\n| `initGC()` | starts and tunes Boehm | does nothing |\n\n`eval.cc` and `eval.hh` contain no `free`, no `delete[]` and no `~EvalMemory`.\nNothing releases a `Value` or an `Env` in either mode.\n\n## Work\n\n1. **Guard the Boehm API in `nanopynix-bindings`.** `nix_expr.cpp` calls\n   `GC_get_stack_base`, `GC_register_my_thread`, `GC_gcollect` and the\n   counters with no `#if NIX_USE_BOEHMGC`. The headers are absent in a non-GC\n   build, so the extension does not compile today. Four functions carry all of\n   it: `enter_evaluator_thread` and `exit_evaluator_thread` become no-ops,\n   because there is no collector to register a thread with. `_gc_collect` and\n   `_gc_stats` must report that no collector is present.\n\n2. **Add a non-GC variant to the package scope.**\n   `nix-expr.override { enableGC = false; }` already evaluates.\n\n3. **Choose how the worker gets that build.** Two seams exist, and they are\n   not equal:\n\n   - The **stdio worker** (`nanopynix-worker`, `_worker:_stdio_main`) execs a\n     fresh process, so it can come from a non-GC environment, and the choice\n     can differ for each worker.\n   - The **forkserver** preloads `nanopynix.rpc.worker._worker`\n     (`_pool.py:256`), so each child inherits the extension that the\n     forkserver loaded. The forkserver is itself a fresh exec and copies the\n     environment once when it starts, so the choice is possible there, but it\n     is one choice for every worker of the process.\n\n4. **Give the worker a recycle policy.** A nixpkgs evaluation allocates\n   gigabytes, and no memory returns until the process exits. The trigger is\n   part of this work: a count of operations, a resident-size watermark, or\n   both.\n\n5. **Add the ASAN variant on top.** `nix/sanitizer.nix` takes a `name`, and\n   the file says at both relevant places why `address` is absent today. A\n   non-GC worker removes that reason.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- `nanopynix-bindings` compiles against a libexpr with `NIX_USE_BOEHMGC=0`.\n- An RPC worker runs the full RPC test suite against a non-GC build.\n- A worker recycles on a stated trigger, and a caller does not observe the\n  recycle.\n- The in-process engine keeps the collector. A build with no collector is a\n  worker capability, and `tests/nanopynix/test_engine_parity.py` records the\n  difference or shows that no difference reaches the API.\n- An ASAN job builds and runs against the non-GC worker, with no override of\n  the libexpr test quoted above.\n\n## Tests\n\n- A test that proves `_gc_stats` and `_gc_collect` report the absence of a\n  collector rather than raising an unrelated error.\n- A test that drives a worker past its recycle trigger and proves that the\n  session continues, and that the process identity changed.\n- A memory test that proves the resident size of a worker returns after a\n  recycle. This is the test that says the design works.\n- The existing RPC suite, against the non-GC worker, in CI.\n\n## This blocks a decision already recorded\n\nQ4 of `docs/nanopynix/decisions.md` asks whether the stdio worker has a\nfuture, and its recommended default is to delete it: *\"It has an entry point,\nno client, a stale docstring and no test.\"* This issue is the client that it\nlacks, and the stdio worker is the cleaner of the two seams in item 3. Decide\nQ4 and this issue together, and do not action Q4 first.\n"}
{"issue": 76, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdVgTwA", "before": "Item 4 (the worker recycle policy) is now nixidae/nixidae#79, and this issue keeps items 1, 2, 3 and 5.\n\nThe reason for the split is item 3, and it changes the answer this issue gives to it.\n\n**No Python code can pick between a build with the collector and one without.** `multiprocessing_worker_with_backchannel` passes `worker_service_factory` as the `Process` target (`grpclib-transports/src/grpclib_transports/multiprocessing.py:255`). The forkserver child unpickles that target, and unpickling a module-level function imports its module \u2014 so `nanopynix.rpc.worker._worker`, and the extension under it, is loaded before any code of ours runs in the child. Preload only moves that import earlier. And `multiprocessing` keeps one forkserver for each process, started from the parent's `sys.path`. So the choice is made once, by the venv.\n\nTwo facts behind that:\n\n- `EvalState` carries a `baseEnvP` member only under `NIX_USE_BOEHMGC` (`eval.hh:790`), so the two builds are ABI-incompatible and one process must load exactly one of them, whichever mechanism selects it.\n- The compile break is small. `eval-gc.hh` aliases `traceable_allocator` and `gc_allocator` to `std::allocator` when the macro is 0, so `nix::allocRootValue`, `PyValue` and every other binding compile unchanged. Only `#include <gc/gc.h>` and the four functions this issue names break.\n\nSo the non-GC build is a scope of `default.nix`, beside `-tsan` and `-ubsan`, and not a runtime choice. Measured: `-Dgc=disabled` rebuilds `nix-expr`, `nix-flake` and the bindings, and nothing else \u2014 three derivations.\n\n**This also answers the last section of this issue.** Q4 of `docs/nanopynix/decisions.md` asks whether the stdio worker has a future, and this issue said it would be the client that it lacks. It is not: the forkserver needed no change, and the stdio worker gained no client here. `decisions.md` now records that, and Q4 stands with its original recommendation.\n\n**Acceptance criterion 4 needs no code, and here is why.** It asks `tests/nanopynix/test_engine_parity.py` to record the difference between the engines or to show that none reaches the API. With one build for each closure, both engines lose the collector together, so there is no difference to record."}
{"issue": 76, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdVgUgQ", "before": "The ASAN job finds a real memory defect. The proof is on nixidae/nixidae#64, and it is a `stack-use-after-return` that names the variable and the source line of the restored defect of nixidae/nixidae#63. nixidae/nixidae#89 records the one thing that still makes a red job ambiguous."}
{"issue": 76, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdVgU6A", "before": "Acceptance criterion 2 is met. Run [31016955422](https://github.com/Lillecarl/nanopynix/actions/runs/31016955422) ran the full suite against the non-GC build on all four versions -- `test-nogc-nix_2_31`, `test-nogc-nix_2_34`, `test-nogc-nix_2_35` and `test-nogc-git` -- and all four passed.\n\nWhat unblocked it was the base environment overflow, now closed as nixidae/nixidae#81. Criterion 1 is met with it, because the suite cannot run against a build that does not compile.\n\nCriteria 3 and 5 stay open: the recycle policy is nixidae/nixidae#79, and the ASAN job is nixidae/nixidae#64 with nixidae/nixidae#90 and nixidae/nixidae#100 behind it.\n\nOne correction to the last section of this issue. It cites Q4 of `docs/nanopynix/decisions.md`, whose recommended default is to delete the stdio worker. That default is withdrawn -- see nixidae/nixidae#54 for the evidence. `grpclib_transports.ssh.connect_ssh_stdio` is its client, and it is the only SSH path that reaches a host running normal OpenSSH."}
{"issue": 76, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdVgVXw", "before": "## Acceptance criterion 5 is now met\n\nThe comment above says: *\"Criteria 3 and 5 stay open: the recycle policy is\nnixidae/nixidae#79, and the ASAN job is nixidae/nixidae#64 with nixidae/nixidae#90 and nixidae/nixidae#100 behind it.\"*\n\nnixidae/nixidae#64 is closed, and nixidae/nixidae#89, nixidae/nixidae#90 and nixidae/nixidae#100 are closed with it. Criterion 5 asks for\n*\"an ASAN job that builds and runs against the non-GC worker, with no override\nof the libexpr test\"*. Run\n[31202037009](https://github.com/Lillecarl/nanopynix/actions/runs/31202037009)\non `develop` passes `test-asan-git`, `test-asan-nix_2_31`, `test-asan-nix_2_34`\nand `test-asan-nix_2_35`, and `test-nogc-*` on the same four versions.\n\n## What is left\n\n| criterion | state |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1. the bindings compile against `NIX_USE_BOEHMGC=0` | met |\n| 2. a worker runs the full RPC suite against a non-GC build | met |\n| 3. a worker recycles on a stated trigger | **#50** |\n| 4. the in-process engine keeps the collector | met, and it needs no code |\n| 5. an ASAN job runs against the non-GC worker | met |\n\nOnly criterion 3 is left, and nixidae/nixidae#79 owns it with its own acceptance criteria and\nits own tests. This issue therefore holds no work of its own.\n\nRefs nixidae/nixidae#64, nixidae/nixidae#79\n"}
{"issue": 79, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYOt6SQ", "before": "## What\n\nGive an RPC worker a recycle policy: a stated trigger that ends the worker\nprocess and starts another, without the caller losing its session.\n\nSplit out of nixidae/nixidae#76, which built the non-GC worker this policy is for. nixidae/nixidae#76\ncarries items 1, 2, 3 and 5 of its own list, and this issue carries item 4.\n\n## Why\n\nA build with `-Dgc=disabled` allocates and never releases. `nix-expr`'s own\npackage option says why that is tolerable: *\"we just leak memory, but this is\nnot as bad as it sounds so long as evaluation just takes places within\nshort-lived processes.\"* A worker of the RPC engine is such a process, and the\noperating system reclaims it on exit. **A recycle policy is what makes the\nprocess short-lived.** Without it, a build with no collector is only usable\nfor a test run.\n\nA nixpkgs evaluation allocates gigabytes. The trigger is part of this work: a\ncount of operations, a resident-size watermark, or both.\n\n## The part that needs a design, and not only a trigger\n\n**The worker hands out integer handles, and every one of them dies with the\nprocess.** `rpc/worker/_handle_registry.py` holds four kinds -- `STORE`,\n`EVAL`, `VALUE` and `LOCKED_FLAKE` -- and the client refers to each by an\n`int64`. A recycle throws all of them away.\n\nSo \"a caller does not observe the recycle\" is only true at a quiescent point,\nand nothing on the client side tracks handle liveness today. Answer these\nbefore any code:\n\n- **When is a recycle legal?** With no live handle is the simple answer, and\n  it may never come true in a long session. A rule that reopens the store and\n  the evaluator is the other, and it has to say what happens to a `Value` that\n  the caller still holds.\n- **Who decides?** The worker knows its own resident size; the client owns the\n  process and the reconnect. `WorkerClient.open` and `close` already hold the\n  spawn and the teardown (`rpc/client/_pool.py`), so a recycle is a `close`\n  and an `open` with the session state replayed.\n- **What does a caller see when the rule cannot be met?** A worker that never\n  reaches a quiescent point still grows. Refusing to recycle silently is the\n  failure this policy exists to prevent.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- A worker recycles on a stated trigger, and the trigger is a setting.\n- A caller that holds no handle does not observe the recycle.\n- A caller that does hold one gets a stated, tested answer. Whether that is a\n  replay, a refusal or an exception is the decision above.\n- The in-process engine is unchanged. The process is the unit of reclamation,\n  and only the RPC engine has a spare one.\n\n## Tests\n\n- A test that drives a worker past its recycle trigger and proves that the\n  session continues, and that the process identity changed.\n- A test that proves the resident size of a worker returns after a recycle.\n  **This is the test that says the design works**, and without it the feature\n  is a process restart with no measurement behind it.\n- A test for each answer the decision above gives to a live handle.\n\n## Where it runs\n\n`nanopynix-tests-nix_2_34-nogc` is the job that exercises this, and nixidae/nixidae#76 added\nit to the scheduled workflow. A recycle test against a build that still has\nthe collector proves the mechanism and not the reason.\n"}
{"issue": 80, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYOuBiw", "before": "## The problem\n\n`nanopynix/tests.nix` builds the packaged test runner with\n`writeShellApplication`. The `text` attribute is now about 200 lines of Nix\nstring that generates a 136-line bash script. That script is the gate: CI runs\nit in every `test-*`, `test-tsan-*`, `test-ubsan-*`, `test-asan-*` and\n`test-nogc-*` job.\n\nBash is the wrong language for what the script does now:\n\n- It orchestrates coverage. It selects `coverage run` against plain `python`,\n  and it runs `combine`, `report` and `xml` after pytest exits, with its own\n  rule for how a coverage failure joins the exit status of the test run.\n- It does post-mortem debugging. On a status above 128 it finds a core file by\n  glob, drives `gdb -batch` with eight `-ex` arguments, counts the lines of the\n  result, and prints a truncation notice.\n- It composes sanitizer environments, one branch for each of `thread`,\n  `undefined` and `address`.\n- It repeats its own exit logic. The coverage-and-status block exists twice,\n  once in the `NANOPYNIX_CORE_DEBUG` path and once beside it.\n\nEvery part of that is easier to write, to read and to test in Python. The\nrunner already has a Python interpreter with the whole test environment in it,\nso a Python entry point costs no start-up that the current script does not\nalready pay.\n\n## Two things the script does that Python cannot\n\nKeep these in the shell, and let them be all that is in the shell:\n\n1. **The environment of the process.** `LD_PRELOAD` must be set before the\n   process starts, because the sanitizer runtime must load before the first\n   allocation. `PYTHONMALLOC` must be set before the interpreter starts.\n2. **The wait for a core file.** The parent that reads the exit status of a\n   process cannot be that process.\n\nA Python entry point that the shell `exec`s answers both: the shell sets the\nenvironment and execs, and Python then runs pytest as a child, so it is the\nparent that sees the signal and reads the core file.\n\n## What this issue asks for\n\n- Move the coverage orchestration, the gdb post-mortem, and the exit-status\n  rule into a Python module in `nanopynix/`, or a new `tests/support/` module,\n  as the layering decides.\n- Reduce the generated shell to the environment it must set, and one `exec`.\n- Give the moved logic tests in `tests/meta/`, which is where the self-checks\n  of this repository live. The exit-status rule and the truncation of the\n  backtrace are both testable, and neither is tested today.\n- Prefer a pytest hook wherever the work belongs inside the run rather than\n  around it. `tests/conftest.py` already refuses `--capture=fd` under a\n  sanitizer for exactly this reason: the rule is a Python rule, and it reads\n  as one.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- CI runs the same jobs, with the same exit statuses, on a green tree and on a\n  tree with a deliberate failure.\n- The core-dump path still prints a backtrace. Reproduce it the way nixidae/nixidae#64 does,\n  with a deliberate crash, rather than trusting the port.\n- `tests/meta/` covers the exit-status rule.\n- The remaining shell is short enough to read in one screen.\n\nRefs nixidae/nixidae#55\n"}
{"issue": 85, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdVhowg", "before": "The `/fork` run that filed this issue filed nixidae/nixidae#86 as well, which covers the same\nsubject. This issue keeps the subject, because the AGPL question decides\nwhether any of the work happens.\n\nTwo measurements from nixidae/nixidae#86 are worth keeping here:\n\n- `pkgs.zuban.version` is `0.9.0` in the pinned nixpkgs, so the gate needs no\n  packaging work: `nix eval --file . 'pkgs.zuban.version'`.\n- `reportPrivateUsage` is 192 of the 315 `# type: ignore[report...]`\n  suppressions. One rule is most of the migration cost, so measure that rule\n  first.\n- `pyproject.toml` sets `typeCheckingMode = \"strict\"` and 24 `extraPaths` for\n  the six packages of this repository. zuban must find the same first-party\n  code."}
{"issue": 110, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYOxiWg", "before": "## What\n\n`pynix flake` can read a lock file, and it cannot write one. `nix flake lock`\ncreates the missing entries. `nix flake update` moves an input to its newest\nrevision.\n\nTogether these two commands are the whole write half of the flake lifecycle.\n`pynix flake` today offers `show`, `metadata` and `info`, which only read.\n\n## What exists already\n\nAlmost all of it. `nix_flake.cpp` binds `lock_flake` and `write_lock_file`,\nand `AsyncLockedFlake` exposes both. `pynix flake metadata` already passes\n`write_lock_file=False`, so the opposite is one argument.\n\n`find_input` and `metadata_json` arrived with nixidae/nixidae#108, so the command can report\nwhich node changed, and it can report the node the way Nix reports it.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- `pynix flake lock` writes the entries that are missing, and it changes no\n  existing entry.\n- `pynix flake update` updates every input.\n- `pynix flake update <input>...` updates only the inputs that the caller\n  names.\n- Each command prints the input that moved, with the old revision and the new\n  revision.\n- A flake that needs no change writes no file, and it says so.\n\n## Tests\n\nUse the three linked local git flakes that `tests/support/git.py` builds for\nnixidae/nixidae#108. That fixture gives a transitive node and a `follows` edge, and it needs\nno network.\n\n- `lock` on a flake with no lock file writes every node.\n- `lock` on a complete lock file changes no byte.\n- `update <input>` moves one node, and it leaves the other node alone.\n- The lock file that `pynix` writes equals the lock file that `nix` writes,\n  for the same fixture. This is the acceptance test, and it follows the\n  oracle in `tests/pynix/test_flake_metadata.py`.\n\n## Note\n\nIssue nixidae/nixidae#30 asks for `--update-fod` in flake mode. That request needs this\ncommand first.\n"}
{"issue": 113, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYOx5sg", "before": "## What\n\n`nix run` builds an app and runs it. `nix env shell` starts a shell in which\nthe named packages are available. These are two of the most used commands of\nthe Nix CLI, and `pynix` has neither.\n\n`pynix develop` already builds an environment and executes a program in it, so\nthe hard parts are done.\n\n## What exists already\n\n- `nix_expr.cpp` binds `realise_argv` and `realise_string`.\n- `pynix develop` (`pynix/src/pynix/develop.py`) builds a derivation, prepares\n  an environment, and executes a program. It also resolves the interactive\n  shell from the `nixpkgs` input of the target flake, which nixidae/nixidae#108 added.\n- `pynix build` resolves an installable to a store path already.\n\n`nix run` needs the `apps.<system>` output, and it falls back to\n`packages.<system>.<name>` with the `meta.mainProgram` of that package.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- `pynix run <installable> [-- args...]` builds the app and executes it.\n- The command follows Nix's own order: `apps.<system>` first, then the default\n  package with `meta.mainProgram`, then the package name.\n- The command replaces the process, so the exit status of the program is the\n  exit status of `pynix`.\n- `pynix env shell <installable>...` puts the `bin` directory of each package\n  on `PATH`, and it starts the shell.\n- `--command` runs one command instead of the shell.\n- A `--bin <name>` flag selects which binary to execute from the `bin`\n  directory of the output, instead of the resolved default. `nix run` offers\n  no such override. An absent name fails with the list of names that exist.\n\n## Tests\n\n- `run` on a flake with an explicit `apps.<system>.default`.\n- `run` on a flake with no `apps`, which must use `meta.mainProgram`.\n- The exit status of the program reaches the caller. Use a program that exits\n  with a known non-zero status.\n- `env shell --command` finds a binary that is not on the outer `PATH`.\n- `run --bin` executes the named binary from a package with several binaries,\n  and an unknown name reports the names that exist.\n\nThe end-to-end tests build a derivation, so send them to CI and keep the\nresolution logic in a unit test, as `tests/pynix/test_develop.py` does.\n\n"}
{"issue": 113, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdVlhBA", "before": "Usage data supports the priority. Agent transcripts hold 30,148 `nix` invocations, and `run` ranks 4th (2,812 uses) with `shell` 5th (1,657). The full table lives in nixidae/nixidae#824.\n\nOne requirement goes beyond `nix run`: `pynix run` must accept a flag that names which binary to execute from the built output. `nix run` resolves `apps.<system>`, then `meta.mainProgram`, and offers no override. A package with several binaries forces the user to take the default. The flag selects one binary from the `bin` directory of the output, and it fails with the list of names when the name is absent."}
{"issue": 130, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYOz8ng", "before": "Today an rpc worker serves one client, and the client owns the process. Give\nnanopynix a server mode instead: one long-lived Nix process that many clients\nconnect to over a Unix-domain socket.\n\n## Why\n\n**A forked child needs no Nix at all.** The connection information is a path,\nand a path crosses a `fork()` with no state. A child's client would hold no\nNix, no Boehm and no `multiprocessing` forkserver. nixidae/nixidae#129 makes a forked child\nspawn a worker of its own, which works, and this is the better answer for a\nconsumer that forks thousands of times.\n\n**One warm process across a whole run.** `ansible-core` forks once per\n(host, task) pair, so today every child re-opens the store and starts with a\ncold eval cache. A shared server keeps one store connection and one warm\ncache for the whole play. For a copy-closure workload over a fleet that is a\nfunctional difference, not a micro-optimisation.\n\nThe same shape serves any repeated-invocation consumer, `pynix` included.\n\n## What already exists\n\n`grpclib-transports` carries this transport today:\n\n- `server.py:81` -- `Endpoint.listen_unix`\n- `client.py:16` -- `connect_unix`\n- `protocol.py:285` -- `_unix_socket_identity`, which reads the peer identity\n  of an `AF_UNIX` socket\n\nSo the transport, the server endpoint and the material for an authorisation\ndecision are all in the repository.\n\n## What needs a design, and not only an endpoint\n\n**`WorkerState` (`rpc/worker/_state.py:56`) is one object per process, and it\nmixes two things multi-tenancy has to separate.**\n\n- **Settings are process-global.** `Init` runs `NixCore.initialize`, which\n  writes Nix's global registry. A second client with different settings meets\n  the construction-time guard of nixidae/nixidae#37. Decide: settings become *server*-scoped\n  and fixed when the server starts, and a `Session` whose settings disagree\n  is refused rather than silently given the first client's.\n- **One `HandleRegistry` for the process.** Handles are `int64` across\n  `STORE`, `EVAL`, `VALUE` and `LOCKED_FLAKE`. Today a client's handles are\n  freed by its worker dying. A shared server needs per-connection ownership\n  and release-on-disconnect, or one crashed task leaks an evaluator for the\n  rest of the run.\n- **Concurrent evaluators in one process is nixidae/nixidae#99's configuration.**\n  `docs/collector-and-threads.md` proves the collector is in that causal\n  path. Store work is the safer half, because libstore never touches the\n  Boehm heap -- that is one of nixidae/nixidae#99's recorded exclusions. Say which\n  operations a server accepts concurrently, and why.\n- **Who may connect.** A socket that grants arbitrary builds and store writes\n  needs a stated model, even if the answer is a `0700` directory.\n\n## This conflicts with nixidae/nixidae#79\n\n**A process that many clients share cannot be recycled.** nixidae/nixidae#79 gives a worker\na recycle policy so that a build with no collector is usable, and the policy\ndepends on the process belonging to one session. Server mode and nixidae/nixidae#79 are\nmutually exclusive as each is designed today. Decide them together, and do\nnot action either alone.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- A server listens on a Unix-domain socket, and two clients hold sessions\n  against it at the same time.\n- A client that disconnects, cleanly or by dying, releases every handle it\n  owned, and the other client is unaffected.\n- A client whose settings disagree with the server's gets a stated answer.\n- The forked-child case works: fork, connect to the inherited path, and the\n  child's session answers.\n- The relationship with nixidae/nixidae#79 is recorded, and one of the two is chosen.\n\nRefs nixidae/nixidae#37, nixidae/nixidae#79, nixidae/nixidae#99, nixidae/nixidae#129.\n"}
{"issue": 131, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYO0Eaw", "before": "**Blocked by nixidae/nixidae#54.** Nothing here can be built until a worker starts by exec.\n\n## What nixidae/nixidae#94 left\n\nnixidae/nixidae#94 landed `Session(env=...)` on both engines. It serves every name Nix reads\nafter `initLibStore`: `NIX_SSHOPTS`, the proxy variables, `TMPDIR`.\n\nIt cannot serve a name Nix reads **while `libnixstore` loads**, and it refuses\neach one rather than accept a value that would do nothing.\n`nanopynix._env.READ_WHILE_LIBSTORE_LOADS` is the list, eleven names read in\n`src/libstore/globals.cc` of Nix 2.31, 2.34 and 2.35:\n\n`NIX_STORE_DIR`, `NIX_STORE`, `NIX_STATE_DIR`, `NIX_LOG_DIR`, `NIX_DATA_DIR`,\n`NIX_CONF_DIR`, `NIX_DAEMON_SOCKET_PATH`, `NIX_SSL_CERT_FILE`,\n`SSL_CERT_FILE`, `NIX_REMOTE_SYSTEMS`, `NIX_IGNORE_SYMLINK_STORE`.\n\nThe reason is the same on both engines: the library is already loaded when a\nsession exists. The rpc worker imports the bindings in the forkserver, which\npreloads `nanopynix.rpc.worker._worker`, and an inproc caller imported them to\nconstruct the session at all.\n\n**An exec sets the environment before the interpreter starts.** That is the one\nshape that changes the answer, and it is nixidae/nixidae#54.\n\n## What it would unlock\n\n**The eleven names.** Eight have a first-class route today\n(`stores.Local(store_dir=...)`, `NixSettings(ssl_cert_file=...)` and so on), so\nthe gain there is a second spelling rather than a new capability. Three have no\nroute at all \u2014 `NIX_DATA_DIR`, `NIX_CONF_DIR` and `NIX_IGNORE_SYMLINK_STORE` \u2014\nand today the only place to set one is the environment of the whole process.\n\n**`Session(nix_conf=...)` on Nix 2.31, which is broken now.** The worker sets\n`NIX_USER_CONF_FILES` from the message, and 2.31 read that variable while\n`libnixstore` loaded, so the assignment is a silent no-op there.\n`tests/support/nix_markers.py::NIX_CONF_FILE_IGNORED` carries the measurement\nand skips four tests in `tests/nanopynix/test_config_flow.py`:\n\n- `test_the_substituters_of_the_host_reach_the_session`\n- `test_a_named_substituter_beats_the_host`\n- `test_the_features_of_the_host_survive_beside_ours`\n- `test_an_ambient_variable_overrides_nothing_of_the_host`\n\nA stdio worker would let all four run on 2.31, and `NIX_CONF_FILE_IGNORED`\nwould be deleted rather than narrowed.\n\n## The decision this issue has to make\n\nA stdio worker makes these names work on **one** start method. That\ncontradicts the rule nixidae/nixidae#94 established, which is that the refusal set is the\nunion over the supported versions of Nix and gives one program one answer.\nThree shapes are possible, and each costs something different:\n\n**a. Refuse unless `worker_start=\"stdio\"`.** Nothing is silent, and the message\ncan name the setting that would accept the value. But the refusal set stops\nbeing one answer for one program: the same `Session(env=...)` succeeds or\nraises depending on a setting somewhere else.\n\n**b. Escalate to `stdio` when `env` names one of them.** The name works\neverywhere and the refusal set disappears. But a session silently changes start\nmethod, and therefore silently changes cost. `worker_start` already documents\n7.7 ms per worker for `forkserver` against 72.1 ms for `spawn`; nobody has\nmeasured the exec.\n\n**c. Refuse always, and document `stdio` as the escape.** The smallest change\nand the one that keeps nixidae/nixidae#94's rule intact. It also makes the stdio worker the\nonly way to say something the API otherwise refuses, which is the shape that\nmade this an issue in the first place.\n\n**Measure the exec before picking.** The choice between (a) and (b) turns on\nwhether an escalation is cheap enough to be invisible, and that number does not\nexist yet.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- The exec cost of a stdio worker is measured, beside the existing 7.7 ms and\n  72.1 ms figures in `NanopynixSettings.worker_start`.\n- One of (a), (b) or (c) is implemented, and the reason is in the docstring of\n  `nanopynix._env` rather than in this issue.\n- `tests/nanopynix/test_session_env.py` asserts the chosen behaviour on every\n  start method, not only on the default one.\n- If the answer is (a) or (b): `NIX_CONF_FILE_IGNORED` is deleted, and the four\n  tests above run on Nix 2.31.\n\n## Related\n\nnixidae/nixidae#54 blocks this. nixidae/nixidae#94 is what created it. nixidae/nixidae#76 item 3 wants the same exec for a\ndifferent reason.\n"}
{"issue": 135, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdVopdw", "before": "nixidae/nixidae#136 depends on this one: the language server moves out of `pynix`, and `_nix_syntax.py` and `_completion.py` go to the shared package this issue creates rather than to the server. Do this one first."}
{"issue": 135, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdVoqUA", "before": "The premise here has changed: **`ekn/` has left this repository.** `flake.nix`\nrecords the move \u2014 \"The `ekn` CLI used to live in this tree and was moved to\neasykubenix, which owns it outright\" \u2014 and there is no `ekn/` directory now.\n\nSo the two copies this issue describes are no longer two copies *here*. What\nremains is a copy in easykubenix and the original in `pynix._settings`, which\nis a different problem: it is drift across repositories rather than within one,\nand the gate that would notice cannot live in either.\n\nThis also unblocked nixidae/nixidae#136. That issue required \"the shared package of nixidae/nixidae#135\" as a\nhome for `_nix_syntax.py` and `_completion.py`; both turn out to be leaf\nmodules that read nothing from `pynix/__init__.py`, so `pynix-lsp` can import\nthem directly and needs no third package.\n\nWorth re-reading with that in mind \u2014 the issue may want narrowing to the\ncross-repository question, or closing if easykubenix is content with its copy."}
{"issue": 140, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdVpXyQ", "before": "Status after the move from zig to gcc (2026-08-12). The body above still\ndescribes zig as the route, and one acceptance criterion is now wrong.\n\n**The route is gcc.** `nix/cxx-stdenv.nix` is the ordinary gcc stdenv of\nnixpkgs plus three additions: a private C++ runtime built from `libstdc++.a`\n(`nix/cxx-runtime.nix`), a post-link glibc rewrite (`nix/lower-glibc.py`), and\n`-mtls-dialect=gnu` on x86-64. The five zig files are gone.\n\n**One acceptance criterion needs a correction.** The body asks for a test that\n\"the extension holds no `__cxx11` symbol\". That test was right while the\nruntime was libc++, which spells its own types `std::__1::`. The runtime is\n`libstdc++.a` now, so `__cxx11` symbols are what the private runtime exports\nfor itself: 3330 of them are in `libnanopynixcxx.so.1` alone. The invariant\nthat still holds is gate 2 of `nix/wheel-gates.py` -- no object of the wheel\nmay name a C++ standard library of the host in `DT_NEEDED`.\n`scripts/wheel-inspect.sh` now measures that instead.\n\n**Measured on both architectures:**\n\n| | objects | floor | host C++ | payload |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| x86-64 | 51 | `GLIBC_2.34` | 0 | 49 MiB |\n| aarch64 | 50 | `GLIBC_2.34` | 0 | 54 MiB |\n\nBoth carry the tag `manylinux_2_34`. `scripts/wheel-smoke.sh` loads the x86-64\nwheel on Rocky Linux 9, whose glibc is 2.34 exactly: it imports, opens a store,\nevaluates, forces a collection, registers every store backend including `s3`,\ntranslates a Nix error to `ThrownError` with the message intact, and casts a\n`LocalStore` for `find_roots`.\n\nThe aarch64 wheel is built on `aarch64-build-box.nix-community.org` and read\nwith `scripts/wheel-inspect.sh`. It is not smoke tested, because that needs an\ninterpreter of its own architecture.\n\n**What is left before publishing:**\n\n1. nixidae/nixidae#155 -- no CI job builds the wheel, so none of this is gated.\n2. The upload itself, and the account and the trusted publisher on PyPI.\n\nThe body says \"Not in this issue: macOS and aarch64 wheels\". The aarch64 wheel\nis built and measured now. macOS is still out."}
{"issue": 146, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdVqEzw", "before": "## The payload for `postDeploy`: which units changed\n\nTwo deploys on nixlab2 today needed the same manual step afterwards, and\nboth would be closed by a post-deploy hook that knows what changed.\n\n**The one that matters.** After a cache migration, the root Application\nreached the new revision while the `nixkube` child sat at the previous\none, reporting `Synced/Healthy`, with the old substituters still live in\nthe nix-node ConfigMap. Fixed by hand:\n\n    kubectl -n argocd annotate application nixkube \\\n      argocd.argoproj.io/refresh=normal --overwrite\n\n`Synced/Healthy at the wrong revision` looks identical to the right one\nin every list view, so nothing tells the operator to wait. It was\nharmless this time only because the old cache stayed readable. On a\ndeploy that moves a store path *and* the substituter list, the node looks\nfor a new path in a cache nothing pushes to -- see nixidae/nixidae#371.\n\n**The other.** A change to `argocd-cmd-params-cm`, which the application\ncontroller reads only at startup. Deploy reported success; nothing\napplied until the controller was restarted by hand.\n\nWhat actually changed in the first case was one unit:\n\n    nixkube/nixkube/ConfigMap/{builder,nix-node,pynixd}.yaml\n    nixkube/nixkube/DaemonSet/nix-node.yaml\n\nExactly the Application that lagged. A loop over that list closes the\nwindow.\n\n## Proposed shape\n\n`deployment.postDeploy` runs after the push, a non-zero exit fails the\ndeploy, and the environment carries `EKN_CHANGED_UNITS` (a JSON array),\n`EKN_DEPLOY_REVISION`, `EKN_SOURCE_REVISION`, `EKN_DEPLOY_BRANCH`,\n`EKN_SOURCE_BRANCH`.\n\n## The trap, which is the reason this is worth writing down\n\n**Do not derive the unit from the first path segment of the diff.** It is\nwrong precisely in the case that matters.\n\nOn nixlab2:\n\n    units.core.path      = \"./\"          -> Application `root`\n    units.argocd.path    = \"./argocd/\"\n    units.bootstrap.path = \"./bootstrap/\"  -> no Application, kubeapplied\n\nThe only file at the branch root is `kustomization.yaml`, which lists the\nApplication CRs -- and those all live under `argocd/`. So\n`argocd/Application/nixkube.yaml` sits in the **argocd unit's directory**\nwhile belonging to the **core unit's Application**. That is\n`mkArgoApplication` working as designed: an Application is routed through\n`rootUnit`, never through its own target.\n\nPath-segment derivation attributes it to `argocd` and refreshes the wrong\napp. The one that must sync a changed Application spec is `root`.\n\n**The correct version is simpler, not harder.** ekn does not need to\nre-derive anything: routing objects to units is how it builds the tree,\nso it already knows the unit for every object. `diff_manifests` receives\n`new_files` as `(path, content)` with the unit already dropped. Carry the\nunit alongside, or keep the path->unit map the writer already holds, and\nthe changed set is exact -- root-routed objects included, no string\nsurgery, no assumption about layout.\n\n## Two smaller points\n\n**Vocabulary: units, not Applications.** easykubenix should not know\nArgoCD exists; unit -> Application is the consumer script's job. Worth a\nsentence in the option docs naming the routing exception above, because\nthe naive loop fails intermittently and reads as flakiness rather than as\na mapping bug.\n\n**Ordering.** When the changed set includes the root unit, refresh root\nand wait for it before the children. A changed Application spec has to\nland before refreshing that child means anything.\n\n## Not a webhook\n\nA git webhook to argocd-server would fix the second case and neither of\nthe others, and nixlab2's argocd-server is not reachable from gitlab.com\n-- the cluster is IPv6-only. A complement at best.\n\nInvestigated and written up by the solid-kubernetes session, including\nthe routing trap, which it walked into and then measured rather than\nassumed. git-bug 61cca42 there."}
{"issue": 153, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYO2ZZw", "before": "## What\n\nMove the switch that turns evaluation counting on, and the two counters that\nstill belong to the process, into `EvalSettings` and `EvalState`. Then send\nthe change upstream.\n\n`nix-2.34-count-calls.patch` and `nix-2.35-count-calls.patch` carry the first\nhalf of this already. They make `count-calls` an `EvalSettings` option, and\nthey make the three call-count tables concurrent. The rest is here.\n\n## The state today\n\n`EvalState.statistics_json` reports fifteen counted fields. Thirteen of them\nbelong to the evaluator, because Nix keeps them in `EvalMemory::Statistics`\nand in `EvalState`. Two do not:\n\n- `Expr::nrExprs`, a static member (`src/libexpr/include/nix/expr/nixexpr.hh`)\n- `nrThunks`, a file static of `src/libexpr/eval.cc`\n\nA process with two evaluators reports the sum of both evaluators in each of\nthose two fields.\n\nThe switch has the same shape. `nix::Counter::enabled` is one static for the\nwhole process (`src/libexpr/include/nix/expr/counter.hh`), and every counter\ntests it on each increment. So an evaluator cannot count while another\nevaluator beside it does not count.\n\n**This is why `nanopynix.set_eval_counters_enabled` names the process.** The\nfunction is honest about what Nix does, and the docstrings say that the report\nis unreliable when one process holds more than one evaluator.\n\n## Why upstream keeps a static\n\n`counter.hh` gives the reason: the counters are off unless `NIX_SHOW_STATS` is\nset, \"to prevent contention on these counters when multi-threaded evaluation\nis enabled\". The flag is read on the hottest paths of the evaluator, and a\nstatic `bool` read is a load from a fixed address that costs nothing.\n\n**A `thread_local` is not the answer.** `libnixexpr` is a shared library that\nPython opens with `dlopen`, so the general-dynamic TLS model applies and each\nread becomes a `__tls_get_addr` call on that same hot path. Every caller pays\nit, and a caller that never asks for statistics pays it too.\n`tls_model(\"initial-exec\")` removes the call, and it can then fail at load\nwith \"cannot allocate memory in static TLS block\", which is a known failure of\na Python extension module.\n\n## What to do\n\n1. Give `EvalMemory` and `EvalState` each a `bool` that says whether to count,\n   and read it from the `count-calls` setting that the patch already adds. The\n   flag then sits in a cache line that the evaluator already touches, so the\n   hot path costs no more than the static does.\n2. Move `nrExprs` and `nrThunks` beside the other counters, so each evaluator\n   reports its own. `nrExprs` counts the AST, which the parser builds for one\n   evaluator, so a member is the correct home for it.\n3. Delete `Counter::enabled`, and keep `NIX_SHOW_STATS` as the value that\n   `EvalSettings` takes by default.\n4. Send the change to upstream Nix. The patches of this repository shrink to\n   nothing if upstream takes it.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- Two evaluators in one process, with different settings, report separate and\n  correct numbers in each of the fifteen fields.\n- One evaluator that counts, beside one that does not, does not change the\n  numbers of the second one.\n- `nanopynix.set_eval_counters_enabled` goes away, and `count_calls` on\n  `NixEvalSettings` is the only switch.\n- The docstrings that call the report unreliable for two evaluators go away\n  with it.\n- A measurement of the evaluator with counting off, before the change and\n  after it. The reason for the static is speed, so a change to it needs a\n  number.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Two evaluators in one process, one counting and one not. The counting one\n  reports what the expression did, and the other reports zero.\n- Two evaluators that both count, with different expressions. Neither reports\n  the work of the other, in any field.\n- The existing per-engine tests keep passing, and their numbers do not change.\n\nRefs nixidae/nixidae#55\n"}
{"issue": 159, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYO3C2w", "before": "## The decision\n\nWrite the protoc plugin that generates `nanopynix_proto`, and emit pydantic\n`BaseModel` for each message. Retire `betterproto2` and\n`betterproto2-compiler` with it.\n\n## The measurement that supports the form\n\n168 classes and 421 fields, which is the size of `nanopynix_proto`, declared\nfour ways. Best of seven, in a subprocess, on CPython 3.14. The third column\nsubtracts the 70.6 ms that `import pydantic` costs on its own, so it is the\ncost of the classes alone:\n\n| form | import | classes alone |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| plain stdlib dataclass | 88.6 ms | -- (no pydantic) |\n| **pydantic dataclass**, which betterproto2 emits today | 324.7 ms | **254.1 ms** |\n| **pydantic `BaseModel`** | 245.4 ms | **174.8 ms** |\n| `BaseModel` with `model_config = ConfigDict(defer_build=True)` | 199.0 ms | 128.3 ms |\n\n`BaseModel` costs 31 per cent less than the pydantic dataclass, and\n`defer_build` costs 50 per cent less. The real modules pay 247.7 ms of self\ntime today (#123), so the two forms save about 76 ms and about 124 ms.\n\n**This is the argument for the plugin, and not for a flag.** betterproto2 has\n`pydantic_dataclasses`, and it has no option for either row below it. A plugin\nthat this repository owns picks any of the four, and can change the choice\nafter a measurement.\n\n## The surface a plugin must cover\n\nFive files and 1217 lines, and the feature list is short:\n\n| feature | count |\n| --- | --- |\n| services | 4 |\n| rpc methods | 79 |\n| streaming methods | 3 |\n| `map<...>` fields | 24 |\n| `repeated` fields | 37 |\n| `optional` fields | 20 |\n| `oneof` blocks | 6 |\n| well-known types (`google/protobuf/*`) | **0** |\n\nNo `Any`, no `Struct`, no `Timestamp`, no `Duration`. Those are where a\nprotobuf implementation gets long, and this schema uses none of them.\n\n`--python_betterproto2_opt=google_protobuf_descriptors` is on today\n(`nanopynix-proto/generated.nix:62`), and no module outside the generated code\nreads the pool it fills. Settle whether anything needs it before the plugin\nreproduces it.\n\n## The risk, and how to answer it\n\nThe wire codec is where a hand-written protobuf implementation goes wrong:\nvarint, presence, the default that is not serialized, and the field order of a\nmap. A test that constructs a message and reads it back does not find these,\nbecause the same defect writes and reads.\n\n**Test differentially against betterproto2, while it is still here.** Generate\nboth, and for each message assert that the plugin and betterproto2 produce the\nsame bytes for the same value, and that each one parses the other's output.\nDelete the comparison when the plugin has run in CI for a while, and not\nbefore.\n\n`greeter-proto/` is the place to prove the plugin first. It is a fixture that\nno shipped code imports, it already runs through\n`--python_betterproto2_opt`, and its tests exercise four transports.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A plugin in this repository writes `nanopynix_proto` and `greeter-proto`.\n- Each message is a pydantic `BaseModel`.\n- A differential test against betterproto2 covers every message of both\n  schemas, in both directions.\n- The 79 rpc methods keep their `grpclib` shape, and the 3 streaming methods\n  keep theirs.\n- `betterproto2` and `betterproto2-compiler` leave `default.nix`,\n  `nix/betterproto2.nix` and `nix/betterproto2-compiler.nix`.\n- A measurement of `import nanopynix` before and after, recorded on nixidae/nixidae#158.\n\n## Not in this issue\n\nThe form of the classes is one of four causes in nixidae/nixidae#158. The other three stay\nthere, and this issue does not close it.\n"}
{"issue": 159, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdVrwGg", "before": "Measured while working nixidae/nixidae#158: removing `--python_betterproto2_opt=pydantic_dataclasses` from `nanopynix-proto/generated.nix` takes the import of the four generated modules from **591.3 ms to 326.3 ms**, and from 359 modules to 316. Best of seven, same interpreter and same dependencies, only the generated code differs.\n\nThat is the size of the prize a generator here is competing for. `betterproto2` itself is 121.3 ms of the floor, so a generator that does not import it starts further ahead again. The full measurement is on nixidae/nixidae#158."}
{"issue": 159, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdVrwmw", "before": "## The evidence from nixidae/nixidae#162 argues for this issue, and not against it\n\nI filed nixidae/nixidae#162 to ask whether the validation is needed at all, because a \"no\"\nwould give the plain-dataclass row of your table with no plugin to write. I\nran the experiment, and the answer is \"yes\". The detail is in\nhttps://github.com/Lillecarl/nanopynix/issues/127#issuecomment-5306952019.\n\nThe short form: `NANOPYNIX_BEARTYPING` is set only by `tests/`, so beartype\nchecks nothing in a released process. pyright covers a typed caller only.\nThe option is the one check that a production process performs on a message\nfield, and `rpc/client/store.py:352` turns beartype off on purpose and names\npydantic as its replacement.\n\nSo the plain-dataclass row is not free. It costs the only runtime validation\nthat ships, and it converts a `ValidationError` that names the field into a\n`TypeError` raised inside `betterproto2.dump_varint`.\n\nThat leaves your `defer_build` row as the best of the four: it keeps the\nvalidation and recovers 128 ms of the 254 ms.\n\nOne measurement is still missing before anyone writes the plugin: a clean full\nsuite run against a protocol without the option. The run I made died of a full\ndisk after test 1051 of 2602.\n"}
{"issue": 159, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdVrw-A", "before": "## Withdrawing my previous comment: it rested on a measurement of a stale tree\n\nI said nixidae/nixidae#162 had answered \"yes, the validation is needed\", and that this left\nthe `defer_build` row as the best of your four forms. Both halves are wrong,\nand the reason is that I measured a branch that had not merged `develop`.\n\nnixidae/nixidae#162 closed on 2026-08-13. It took the plain-dataclass row, and it kept the\nvalidation by writing the check where the value arrives:\n\n```python\nif action not in GcAction:\n    raise ValueError(f\"unsupported garbage-collection action: {action!r}\")\n```\n\nSo the row your table costs at 88.6 ms is the one in the tree, and the 254 ms\nis already recovered. The validation that I claimed it destroyed is one\nexplicit line in the one method that read it.\n\n**That does not decide this issue, and I am not arguing that it does.** Your\ncase for a repo-owned plugin was never only about import time: it is about\n`betterproto2` offering no option for a `BaseModel`, and about what a bound\nmessage should be. What has changed is the baseline it is measured against.\nThe comparison is now 88.6 ms against 245.4 ms for `BaseModel`, or 199.0 ms\nwith `defer_build`, and a plugin to maintain. On import time alone the tree is\nalready at the cheapest of the four.\n\nThe question worth putting to this issue is therefore whether anything wants\nthe pydantic API on a message, rather than whether it is worth its cost. If\nnothing does, the plugin buys nothing that the explicit check does not already\ngive.\n"}
{"issue": 160, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYO3Jmw", "before": "**This issue explores an idea, and it does not adopt one.** The evidence below\npoints both ways. Close it as \"no\" if the exploration says no; that outcome is\na result and not a failure.\n\n## The idea\n\n`on_commit` runs every Nix version at once. Run one version first instead, and\nstart the next set only when that one is green.\n\n## What runs today\n\n`on_commit` holds 27 jobs. 23 of them are the same suite across four Nix\nversions: `git`, `nix_2_31`, `nix_2_34` and `nix_2_35`, in five variants\n(`local`, `daemon`, `asan`, `ubsan`, `nogc`, and `tsan` on three versions).\n\nThe caps sum to 4720 minutes. That is a worst case and not a measurement: the\njobs run together, and the median run takes about 30 minutes of wall clock.\nThe last 20 runs range from 15 minutes to 1h31m.\n\n## The evidence for the idea\n\nThe five red runs of the last 20, and the jobs that failed in each:\n\n| run | failed | shape |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 31574135845 | 20 of 25 | every version, every variant |\n| 31399965157 | 16, and `static-checks` | every version, every variant |\n| 31392771547 | `commit-subjects`, `test-local-nix_2_34` | one version |\n| 31351216986 | `test-tsan-nix_2_34` | one version |\n| 31202037009 | `commit-subjects` | no test job |\n\n**Three of the five were broad.** One job would have reported the same defect,\nand 20 jobs reported it 20 times. That is the case for a pilot stage.\n\n**`nix_2_34` failed in every red run that had a test failure.** A first stage\nof `nix_2_34` plus the cheap gates would have caught all five.\n\n**Treat that last sentence with suspicion.** I picked `nix_2_34` after reading\nwhich version failed, on a sample of five. The exploration has to test the\nclaim on a longer history, and has to say what happens when the defect is in\n`nix_2_31` alone.\n\n## The evidence against the idea\n\n**This repository is public, so a standard runner costs nothing.** The 4720\nminutes are free. Staging does not save money, and the thing it spends is the\none thing that is scarce: a green run today takes about 30 minutes, and three\nstages make the same green run take about 90.\n\nMost runs are green. A design that makes the common case three times slower to\nmake the rare case cheaper needs an argument that is not about money.\n\n**`fail-fast = false` is deliberate.** `on_schedule.nix:58` and its neighbours\nset it so that one red version does not hide the others. Staging is the same\ntrade in a larger form, and the reason it was refused inside a matrix applies\nhere too.\n\n## The cheaper thing to try first\n\n`on_commit` has **no `concurrency` block**. Three pushes in ten minutes run\nthree full matrices, and the first two are already obsolete. This costs the\nsame runners that the idea above is trying to save, and it costs nothing in\nwall clock to fix:\n\n```yaml\nconcurrency:\n  group: on-commit-${{ github.ref }}\n  cancel-in-progress: true\n```\n\n**Measure this before restructuring anything.** If superseded runs are most of\nthe waste, then the staged design solves a problem that this block already\nsolved.\n\n## What would decide it\n\n1. Count, over 100 runs rather than 5, how many red runs were broad and how\n   many were one version. Record which version failed alone.\n2. Measure how many runs each week are superseded by a later push, which is\n   the saving the concurrency block gives on its own.\n3. Measure the median wall clock of a green run, and state what a slower green\n   run is worth against a cheaper red one. This is a judgement, so write it\n   down rather than compute it.\n4. Only then, if the answer is still yes, design the stages. `needs:` already\n   expresses the chain, so the change is in `ci/workflows/on_commit.nix` and\n   needs no new mechanism.\n\n## Related\n\n- nixidae/nixidae#156, which makes any edit to `ci/workflows/lib.nix` produce a large diff.\n  Fix that one first if this issue leads to real work.\n"}
{"issue": 165, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYO3vOA", "before": "## What happens\n\nThis repository runs three separate pytest suites, and only one of them can\nreach the shared test machinery.\n\n| suite | rootdir | configuration | test modules |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| the repository suite | `.` | `pytest.ini` | 112 |\n| `grpclib-transports` | `grpclib-transports/` | `grpclib-transports/pytest.ini` | 9 |\n| `pytest-agent` | `pytest-agent/` | `pytest-agent/pyproject.toml` | 14 |\n\n`tests/support/` holds 3830 lines over 22 modules. A module there imports as\n`tests.support.<name>`, which resolves from the repository rootdir alone, so\nneither subproject suite can use any of it.\n\n## Two problems, and they are the same problem\n\n### 1. Setup that every suite needs is available to one suite\n\nSome of `tests/support/` is nanopynix's own -- `nix_environment.py`,\n`nix_runtime.py`, `soak.py`, the six `lsp_*.py` modules. Those belong to the\npackage they serve. Others answer a question that any Python suite in this\nrepository has:\n\n| module | lines | what it answers |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `hang_report.py` | 159 | what the process was doing when a test hit its deadline |\n| `subprocess_output.py` | 104 | run a subprocess without deadlocking on its two pipes |\n| `git.py` | 99 | build a deterministic Git repository |\n| `beartype_hook.py` | 64 | install the beartype import hook before the package loads |\n| `notes.py` | 26 | `pytest_agent.note`, and a stand-in when the plugin is absent |\n\n`tests/conftest.py` adds one more: every async test runs under\n`anyio.fail_after`, which is what makes `hang_report.py` fire at all.\n\n**`notes.py` is the sharpest example, because its docstring records the CI\nfailure that produced it.** A module-level `from pytest_agent import note`\nfails at *collection* under the packaged runner, which leaves pytest-agent out\non purpose. CI stayed red for a day. `tests/meta/test_agent_note_imports.py`\nnow keeps every module under `tests/` importing the shim instead -- and reaches\nno module of either subproject, which can hit the same failure with no guard.\n\nThe duplication has already started. `grpclib-transports/tests/conftest.py:13`\nwrites its own sandbox check:\n\n```python\ndef _in_nix_build_sandbox() -> bool:\n    return \"NIX_BUILD_TOP\" in os.environ or os.environ.get(\"IN_NIX_SHELL\") == \"pure\"\n```\n\n**Two scanners are already repository-wide and are not examples of this.**\n`suppressions.py` and `ansi_regexes.py` take a root and walk it, and\n`tests/meta/` passes `REPO_ROOT`, so both already read every subproject. They\nare the shape the rest should take.\n\n### 2. A package's tests live somewhere else\n\n| package | where its tests are | modules |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `nanopynix` | `tests/nanopynix/` | 67 |\n| `nanopynix-bindings` | `tests/nanopynix/bindings/` | 14 |\n| `pynix` | `tests/pynix/` | 29 |\n| `nanopynix-helpers` | `tests/nanopynix_helpers/` | 2 |\n| `grpclib-transports` | `grpclib-transports/tests/` | 9 |\n| `pytest-agent` | `pytest-agent/tests/` | 14 |\n\nThe first four are the odd ones. `pytest.ini` prepends\n`nanopynix/src pynix/src nanopynix-helpers/src` to `sys.path`, so one suite\nreaches into three packages' sources at once.\n\n**This is what stops a package leaving.** nixidae/nixidae#135 says `ekn` cannot leave the\nrepository until `pynix` and `ekn` stop duplicating their CLI foundation, and\nnixidae/nixidae#136 moves the language server to its own project. Both move code whose 29\ntest modules are at the repository root and do not travel with it.\n\n## The constraint that shapes the answer\n\n**The answer is not one configuration for every suite, and\n`grpclib-transports/pytest.ini` says why.** Its own comment gives the reason it\nis separate: the repository configuration installs beartype over `nanopynix`,\nadds nine nanopynix-specific markers, and prepends five source trees, \"none of\nwhich this library needs, and all of which would make a transport failure\nharder to read\".\n\nThat reason is correct and it must survive. What is wrong is that opting out of\nnanopynix's setup currently means opting out of the generic setup as well,\nbecause the two are one thing.\n\nSo the work is a split, not a merge: a layer that any suite here opts into, and\na nanopynix layer built on top of it. A subproject that wants the deadline, the\nhang report and the note shim should get them without also getting\n`nix_environment.py`.\n\n## Open questions, and they belong in the work\n\n- **How does a subproject suite import the shared layer?** A package that each\n  suite depends on under a `test` extra is one answer, a pytest plugin entry\n  point is another, and `pythonpath` in each `pytest.ini` is a third. The\n  packaged runner (`nanopynix/tests.nix`) and `nix/checks.nix` both have to\n  keep working, and the first deliberately runs from a read-only store copy.\n- **Does `tests/meta/` move?** Its subject is the repository as a whole, so it\n  belongs to no package. `tests/gates/` and `tests/harness/` are the same case.\n- **What happens to `tests/AGENTS.md`?** It maps `tests/` and it is the file a\n  contributor reads before adding a test. A split makes most of it wrong.\n- **Is this worth doing before nixidae/nixidae#135 and nixidae/nixidae#136, or as part of them?** Those two\n  are the callers that need it. Doing it first makes them smaller; doing it\n  with them keeps the change attached to a consumer that proves it works.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A subproject suite reaches the generic setup without reaching nanopynix's.\n  Demonstrated by `grpclib-transports` using the note shim and the per-test\n  deadline, and still not loading `nix_environment.py` or the beartype hook\n  over `nanopynix`.\n- `_in_nix_build_sandbox` exists once.\n- Each package's tests sit inside that package, and running that package's\n  suite alone works.\n- A self-check in `tests/meta/` keeps the split, in the shape `AGENTS.md`\n  asks for: a convention a machine can check goes there rather than into\n  another paragraph of prose. The rule to check is that a module of the\n  nanopynix layer does not import from the generic layer's consumers, and that\n  every suite imports `note` from the shim.\n- `nix/checks.nix` still runs the two subproject suites, and the packaged\n  runner still runs from a read-only store copy.\n- `tests/AGENTS.md` describes the layout that exists after the change.\n\nRelated: nixidae/nixidae#135 and nixidae/nixidae#136 are blocked by the second half of this. nixidae/nixidae#128 and nixidae/nixidae#73\nare the failures that produced `hang_report.py`."}
{"issue": 166, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYO32yQ", "before": "## What this does\n\n`Lillecarl/pynixd` moves into this repository, which becomes the nixidae\nmonorepo. The measurement on nixidae/nixidae#165 is the reason the direction is inward: over\nthe last 400 commits, 52% touch more than one of the candidate projects, and\n25% still do after nixidae/nixidae#165 moves each package's tests into that package. Splitting\nthese projects apart costs a multi-repository change every fourth commit.\n\n## What pynixd is\n\nA Nix daemon protocol proxy and distributed build cache, in pure Python over\nAsyncSSH. It sits between a Nix client and remote builders, and it caches\nqueries, deduplicates builds and schedules across backends.\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| repository | `Lillecarl/pynixd`, default branch `develop` |\n| last push | 2026-07-18 |\n| size | 3.5 MB, 333 files |\n| Python | about 1.1 MB over 151 files in `pynixd/` |\n| tests | 89 files: 35 functional, 21 unit, 9 `_conftest`, 8 Nix fixtures, 3 benchmark |\n| also carries | `nix/nixos/default.nix` (a NixOS module), `shell.nix`, `justfile`, `docs/` |\n\n## Why it belongs here rather than beside here\n\n**It implements in Python what nanopynix binds from C++.** `pynixd/wire.py`,\n`nar.py`, `drv_parser.py` and `store/daemon.py` are a second answer to\nquestions `nanopynix-bindings` already answers through libnixstore.\n\nThat is the interesting part, not a problem to delete. Two things fall out of\none repository that do not fall out of two:\n\n- **`nanopynix.store_impl` lets Python implement a `Store`.** pynixd is a\n  Python implementation of a store. Whether it can *be* one is a question\n  worth answering, and it is cheap to answer when both trees are in one\n  checkout.\n- **This suite already runs a daemon backend.** `--nix-test-backends\n  local,daemon` speaks the real protocol to a real `nix-daemon`. A pure-Python\n  implementation of the other end is a differential test of both, and neither\n  project can write that test alone.\n\nThere is a recorded case where the two disagree and it matters:\n`project_daemon_protocol_error_loss` -- `HashMismatch` is downgraded on the\nwire, and the FOD hashes survive only in prose, which is why\n`nanopynix-helpers/fod.py` scrapes the log. pynixd owns the other side of that\nwire.\n\n## What has to be decided before the move\n\n**Licence, and this one blocks.** pynixd carries no licence file and no\n`license` field. This repository is `LGPL-2.1-or-later` with a `LICENSE` that\n`nanopynix/pyproject.toml` names in `license-files`. Merging unlicensed code\ninto a licensed tree needs an explicit decision, taken before the commit and\nnot after.\n\n**The agent-workflow trees.** `todo/` is 47 files, and `research/`, `ai/`,\n`.opencode`, `opencode.json`, `progress.md`, `recurring.md` and `REASONIX.md`\nare the same kind of content. This repository keeps that state in issues --\n`CODE_IMPROVEMENT_PLAN.md` holds no status \"because the issue holds the\nstatus\". Decide what carries over, what becomes issues, and what is dropped.\n\n**The dependency set.** pynixd adds ten dependencies that nothing here has:\n`asyncssh`, `aiohttp`, `aiosqlite`, `environs`, `pyinstrument`, `structlog`,\n`passlib`, `cachetools`, `prometheus_client`, `uvloop`. Each has to exist in\nnixpkgs or get a package, and `structlog` overlaps `nanopynix.logging`.\n\n**`ruff-strict.toml` bans the raw asyncio primitives, and pynixd is asyncio\ncode.** The `TID251` block routes `asyncio.Lock`, `asyncio.sleep` and the rest\nto their anyio equivalents. That ban is right for nanopynix and it is already\nlifted for one subproject: `grpclib-transports` is exempt because its subject\nmatter *is* `asyncio.Protocol` callbacks, and the file records that argument.\npynixd is AsyncSSH-native, so it needs the same judgement -- a per-directory\nexemption with the reason written down, or a port. Do not let it land with a\nblanket suppression.\n\nTwo things need no decision: `requires-python = \">=3.13\"` matches, and\n`line-length = 120` matches.\n\n## How it should land\n\n**#130 first.** It defines where a package's tests live and how a subproject\nreaches the shared test setup without reaching nanopynix's. pynixd arrives\nwith 89 test files, its own `pytest.ini` and its own `_conftest` package. If\nit lands before nixidae/nixidae#165 it is a fourth pytest configuration to migrate later; if\nit lands after, it is the first project built to the new shape and the proof\nthat the shape works for a project that was written elsewhere.\n\n**The history comes with it.** A squashed import loses the reasoning behind\n1.1 MB of Python. Use `git subtree add` or a graft, so `git log` still answers\nwhy a line is the way it is.\n\n**CI is a separate step from the move.** The move is one commit. Adding pynixd\nto the test matrix is another, and it should not silently join the existing 22\njobs: that matrix is three Nix versions times the sanitizer variants, and\npynixd needs neither the C++ bindings nor most of it. `nix/checks.nix` already\nholds the pattern for a subproject suite that runs as its own derivation.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `pynixd/` is in the tree, with its history, under a licence this repository\n  states.\n- Its suite runs in CI, as a gate that names itself when it fails. `nix run` or\n  `nix build --file . checks.pynixd` runs it the way CI runs it.\n- Its dependencies resolve from nixpkgs, or carry a package with a reason.\n- The asyncio question is answered in `ruff-strict.toml` with the argument\n  written beside it, in the shape the `grpclib-transports` entry uses.\n- `tests/AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` say what pynixd is and where its tests go.\n- The agent-workflow trees are either converted to issues or removed, and not\n  carried across unread.\n- The NixOS module in `nix/nixos/` has a stated home, since nothing here ships\n  a NixOS module today.\n\n## Not in this issue\n\n- Reconciling `wire.py`, `nar.py` and `drv_parser.py` against the bindings.\n  That question is the reason to be in one repository, and it is worth its own\n  issue once both trees are here.\n- Making pynixd a `nanopynix.store_impl` store.\n- Moving the repository to the `nixidae` organisation, which is an\n  administrative step and not a code change.\n\nRelated: nixidae/nixidae#165 defines the test layout this depends on. nixidae/nixidae#135 and nixidae/nixidae#136 are the\nother two moves the monorepo has to absorb."}
{"issue": 166, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdVssUA", "before": "Status against the acceptance list. Six of seven are done; the seventh is now\nnixidae/nixidae#173.\n\n| item | state |\n| --- | --- |\n| in the tree, with its history, under a stated licence | done |\n| its suite runs in CI, as a gate that names itself | **partly** \u2014 see below |\n| dependencies resolve from nixpkgs | done |\n| the asyncio question answered in `ruff-strict.toml` | done |\n| `tests/AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` say what pynixd is | done |\n| agent-workflow trees converted or removed | done |\n| the NixOS module has a stated home | done |\n\n**Licence.** The whole repository is Apache-2.0 now, and\n`nanopynix-bindings` alone is LGPL-2.1-or-later, because that project links\nlibnixexpr and libnixstore. `pynix` states `Apache-2.0 AND LGPL-2.1-or-later`,\nbecause it vendors Nix's own `get-env.sh`. The `# Licensing` section of\n`AGENTS.md` gives the compatibility argument, and `tests/meta/test_licensing.py`\ngates every part of it that a machine can check \u2014 the `-or-later` included,\nwhich is the word that makes Apache-2.0 code legal to move into the bindings.\n\n**The gate.** `nix build --file . checks.pynixd` runs the unit suite, 221\ntests, in a sandbox. Two things were in the way and both are fixed: the venv is\nnot editable, so the gate runs from `pynixd/` to put the project on\n`sys.path`; and the live probes of `test_drv_parser.py` skip when `nix` is not\non PATH. The functional suite, 115 tests, needs a real daemon and SSH, so it\ncannot run in a sandbox at all. That is nixidae/nixidae#173.\n\n**The asyncio question got a port, not an exemption.** The exemption was the\nexpected answer, and it is wrong here: `pynixd/__main__.py` already starts the\nprogram with `anyio.run(backend=\"asyncio\", ...)`, so every anyio primitive\nalready worked in it. All 48 uses are ported \u2014 13 in the library, 35 in its\ntests. `pynixd/ruff.toml` bans the same names as `ruff-strict.toml`, so\n`checks.lint` holds the port, and `tests/meta/test_asyncio_ban.py` keeps the\ntwo lists equal.\n\n**The trees.** 33 of the 54 files were in a `completed/` directory,\n`progress.md` described work already finished, and `todo/migration-status.md`\ndescribed a migration that was reversed. The open work is nixidae/nixidae#168 to nixidae/nixidae#172, each\nissue carrying the whole file. The reference material on Nix behaviour moved to\n`pynixd/docs/notes/`.\n\nThree defects turned up on the way, none of which would fail a build:\n\n- `StorePath.path` returned `self`, so `endswith` recursed until RecursionError\n  \u2014 three tests of the daemon over HTTP found it.\n- `ProbeFeatures` did not set `is_extension`, so `DaemonProxy` re-raised\n  `OpNotImplementedError` instead of asking the other stores.\n- The NixOS module put pynixd in `environment.systemPackages` outside its\n  `mkIf cfg.enable`, so importing it installed pynixd on a system that had the\n  service switched off."}
