{"issue": 179, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYO5WUw", "before": "## The question\n\nToday each binding that returns a Nix struct builds an `nb::dict` by hand. A\nhelper reads each field, converts it, and puts it in the dictionary. The\nquestion is whether nanobind can bind the struct as a Python type instead, so\nthat `def_ro` exposes each field and the helper goes away.\n\nThe answer is yes for most fields. This issue holds the measurements, and it\nholds the spike that proves the shape on one real type.\n\n## What the bindings are made of now\n\nCounted over the nine `.cpp` files of `nanopynix-bindings/src`:\n\n| binding declarations | 147 |\n| --- | --- |\n| a bare `&nix::Member` pointer | 2 (1.4%) |\n| a hand-written local helper | 99 (67.3%) |\n| an inline lambda | 46 (31.3%) |\n\nWe declare 9 `nb::class_` types. We use `def_ro` **zero** times, `def_rw` zero\ntimes, and `def_prop_ro` once. `nb::bind_vector` and `nb::bind_map` ship with\nnanobind, and this repository uses neither.\n\nFourteen helpers build a dictionary, and they are 428 lines together:\n\n| helper | lines | what stops a plain `def_ro` |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `read_derivation` | 79 | the store renders each path |\n| `value_to_python_arg` | 75 | the GIL, and error translation |\n| `path_info_to_dict` | 40 | the store renders each path, and a hash format |\n| `build_info` | 37 | nothing |\n| `json_to_python` | 29 | nothing |\n| `collect_garbage` | 28 | the GIL |\n| `nix_error_info.hh:to_dict` | 28 | nothing |\n| `store_dirs_to_dict` | 26 | a format choice |\n| `find_roots` | 19 | the store renders each path |\n| `pos_to_dict` | 17 | error translation |\n| `attrs_to_nb_dict` | 15 | nothing |\n| `query_missing` | 14 | the GIL |\n| `derived_path_node_to_dict` | 12 | nothing |\n| `build_result_util.hh:to_dict` | 9 | nothing |\n\nSix of the fourteen need nothing that `def_ro` cannot give. They are about\n110 lines.\n\n## What nanobind does, measured\n\nA probe module mirrors `nix::ValidPathInfo` field for field. It uses\nstand-in types of the same C++ shape. nanobind is 2.13.0, and GCC is 15.3.0.\n\n| field shape | result of `def_ro` |\n| --- | --- |\n| `uint64_t`, `bool`, `time_t` | an `int` or a `bool` |\n| `StorePath` (a bound type) | the bound object, and `i.path is i.path` is `True` |\n| `std::optional<StorePath>` | the bound object, or `None` |\n| `std::set<std::string>` | a Python `set`, and `is` is `False` |\n| `Hash` | **no**. `to_string` needs a format and a flag |\n| `std::optional<ContentAddress>` | **no**. A free function renders it |\n\nTwo results are worth the attention:\n\n- **A bound type as a field is a reference, and not a copy.** nanobind keeps\n  the parent alive. `def_ro` gives `rv_policy::reference_internal`.\n- **An STL container is a copy on each read.** The set converts again for\n  each attribute access. `nb::bind_vector` and `nb::bind_map` exist for this,\n  and this issue must measure them.\n\nThe cost of one attribute read:\n\n```\ni.nar_size   (a scalar, def_ro)      86 ns     d[\"nar_size\"]     56 ns\ni.path       (a bound type)         203 ns\ni.references (a set, it copies)     276 ns     d[\"references\"]   55 ns\ni.nar_hash   (def_prop_ro)          145 ns\n```\n\nA dictionary read is cheaper. The dictionary converts all nine fields first,\nand it renders each reference through the store. A caller that reads two\nfields of a path with 50 references pays much less with a bound type.\n\n## What nanobind does not do\n\n**nanobind supports no custom holder.** It ships `shared_ptr` and\n`unique_ptr` alone. pybind11 has `PYBIND11_DECLARE_HOLDER_TYPE`, and nanobind\nhas no equivalent. `nix::ref<T>` is the holder that libstore returns, for\nexample from `Store::queryPathInfo`.\n\nThe failure arrives late. The module compiles, and the call raises at run\ntime:\n\n```\nTypeError: Unable to convert function return value to a Python type!\nThe signature was  make_ref() -> ref<ValidPathInfo>\n```\n\nA `nb::detail::type_caster` for `nix::ref<T>` removes this, and one caster\nserves every use. Write that caster first.\n\n**C++ gives no reflection, so no tool reads the methods of a class.** A\ngenerator that reads the headers with libclang can write the `.def` list, for\nexample `binder` or `litgen`. The list is not the work. The work is the\nmarshalling, the GIL, the error translation and the lifetime policy. Those\ncarry 15 `nb::call_guard`, 12 `nb::keep_alive` and 4 `nb::call_policy`\nannotations that no signature states.\n\n## The obstacle that belongs to us\n\n**A `ValidPathInfo` does not know its store.** The `path` field is a\n`StorePath`, which holds a hash and a name. `Store::printStorePath` makes the\n`/nix/store/...` string, and `path_info_to_dict` takes a `nix::Store &` to do\nit.\n\nA bound `ValidPathInfo` returns a `StorePath` object. Python then calls\n`store.print_store_path(p)`. This changes the Python API, and it reaches each\ncaller. The change is not a limit of nanobind. It is a decision about the API\nof this library.\n\nThe new shape is lazy and typed. The old shape renders each reference even\nwhen the caller reads none of them.\n\n## The spike\n\nPort one type end to end, and keep the old method beside it.\n\n1. Write the `nb::detail::type_caster` for `nix::ref<T>`. Nothing else works\n   until a `ref` crosses the boundary.\n2. Bind `nix::ValidPathInfo` as a type. Use `def_ro` for each field that\n   takes it, and `def_prop_ro` for `narHash` and `ca`.\n3. Add `Store.query_path_info_typed`, which returns the bound type.\n   `Store.query_path_info` stays, and it keeps returning a dictionary.\n4. Measure `nb::bind_vector` against the plain `std::set` caster, for the\n   `references` field.\n5. Measure both methods on a real store: the time to the first field, and\n   the time to read every field, for a path with many references.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- The `ref<T>` caster exists, and a test proves that a `ref` crosses.\n- `Store.query_path_info_typed` returns each field that the dictionary holds.\n- A test shows that both methods report the same values for the same path.\n- The measurement above is a comment on this issue.\n- The spike changes no caller. `pynix` and `nanopynix` still use the\n  dictionary.\n\nThen this issue decides. A success continues to the other thirteen helpers,\nand it states what the API change costs. A failure records the reason, and\nthis issue closes.\n\n## Related\n\n- nixidae/nixidae#158 records the import cost of nanopynix.\n- nixidae/nixidae#140 publishes the bindings on PyPI, so the ABI surface matters there.\n- nixidae/nixidae#99 is the lifetime defect, and the lifetime policy of a bound field\n  touches the same area.\n"}
{"issue": 179, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdVufbw", "before": "State after nixidae/nixidae#180, and the one constraint that the spike did not meet.\n\n## What is bound now\n\nFour types are \\`nb::class_\\` and carry the fields the dictionary carried:\n\\`ValidPathInfo\\`, \\`MissingPaths\\`, \\`Derivation\\` and \\`DerivationOutput\\`, plus\n\\`DerivationOutputs\\` for the \\`DerivedPathMap\\` tree. Each one has a \\`_typed\\`\nquery beside the dictionary one, and \\`nanopynix._core._objects.CoreStore\\` now\nreads the typed form. Both engines therefore get the bound type, because the\nrpc worker builds its message from the same call.\n\nSix helpers still return a dictionary alone: \\`StoreDirs\\`, \\`BuildResult\\` with\n\\`RealisedOutput\\`, \\`GCResults\\`, \\`GCRoot\\` and \\`EditLocation\\`.\n\n## The dictionary cannot simply be deleted\n\nThe spike said \"the dictionary stays, and no caller changes\". The first half\nturns out to be a requirement and not a courtesy.\n\n\\`query_path_info\\`, \\`query_missing\\` and \\`read_derivation\\` are three of the\nfifteen names in \\`NANOPYNIX_STORE_DISPATCH_METHODS\\`. That macro is the\n**inbound** protocol of a Python store, and \\`py_store_impl.cpp\\` reads each\nanswer as a dictionary and nothing else:\n\n```cpp\nauto result = py_store.attr(\"query_path_info\")(...);\nauto d = nb::cast<nb::dict>(result);\n```\n\nSo the dictionary shape is what a \\`nanopynix.StoreImpl\\` **returns to Nix**, and\nnot only what the bindings hand a caller. Removing the dictionary form of these\nthree methods changes what every Python store must produce, and that is a\nseparate decision from the one this issue took.\n\nThree answers are available, and this issue should pick one:\n\n1. Keep both. The dictionary is the inbound protocol and the bound type is the\n   outbound one, and each is named for its direction.\n2. Make the trampoline accept either. It reads the fields it needs, so it can\n   take a bound type as well as a dictionary. That costs one branch for each\n   field and gives a Python store the same vocabulary the caller has.\n3. Move the inbound protocol to the bound types too. This needs a constructor\n   for each bound type, which none of them has today, and it breaks every\n   existing \\`StoreImpl\\`.\n\n## \\`BuildResult\\` is not a drop-in either\n\nnixidae/nixidae#180 exposed \\`built_outputs\\` through the dictionary, and recorded why:\n\\`build_result_util.hh\\` serves \\`nanopynix.exceptions.build_error_from_result\\`\nand the rpc worker as well as the caller, so binding the struct moves three\nlayers at once. It also has no entry in\n\\`tests/meta/test_binding_wire_agreement.py\\`, because that ledger reads a\nruntime \\`nb::class_\\` with \\`dir()\\` and a \\`TypedDict\\` in a \\`.pat\\` file exists in\nthe stub alone. Binding it would give the ledger a fifth entry, which is one\nmeasurable argument for doing it."}
{"issue": 182, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYO5rOA", "before": "macOS defects are found by hand, one at a time, and no runner catches them or\nconfirms a correction.\n\n## What this asks for\n\nOne job, or a small set of jobs, on `macos-latest` in `ci/workflows/on_commit.nix`.\nNot the whole matrix. The subset has to be the part that answers a question\nLinux cannot answer, and nothing else.\n\n## Why\n\n**Two open issues are macOS-only, and both came from a person, not from CI.**\n\n- **#139**, the rpc worker ignores the `builders` setting, so IFD fails on\n  macOS. It was found while a user evaluated a Linux configuration from a Mac.\n- **#140**, `-fvisibility-ms-compat` is dead weight on Linux and load-bearing\n  on Darwin, because Darwin is clang with libc++abi and that library compares\n  `type_info` by address. **Nobody can confirm that claim today**, and nobody\n  can confirm a correction to it. The issue records a property of a build that\n  this repository never builds.\n\n**The suite has never run off Linux.** A search of every test directory for\n`sys.platform`, `platform.system()` and a skip on Darwin returns nothing. So\nthe first macOS run does not report a macOS defect. It reports that the suite\nassumes Linux, and that is the first piece of work.\n\n**The runner is free.** This repository is public, so GitHub charges no minutes\nfor `macos-latest`. The cost is queue time and maintenance, not money.\n\n`flake.nix:54` already uses `lib.systems.flakeExposed`, so every Darwin\nattribute exists and none is built.\n\n## What has to be decided first\n\n**Which jobs.** The value is in the bindings against a real Nix, so\n`test-local-nix_2_34` is the first candidate. `static-checks` gives close to\nnothing, because ruff and pyright do not read the host. The sanitizer jobs and\nthe wheel job are Linux by construction: `nix/wheel.nix` repairs to\n`manylinux_2_34`.\n\n**What to do with the tests that cannot pass.** These modules use Linux\nnamespaces, `unshare` or `store_exec`:\n\n```\nnanopynix/tests/test_namespace.py\nnanopynix/tests/test_store_exec.py\nnanopynix/tests/test_config_flow.py\nnanopynix/tests/test_store_dump_db.py\nnanopynix/tests/test_stores.py\nnanopynix/tests/inproc/test_inproc_multithreaded.py\nnanopynix/tests/rpc/client/test_stdio_worker.py\nnanopynix/tests/rpc/test_worker_argv.py\nnanopynix/tests/rpc/worker/test_worker_eval_unit.py\npynix/tests/test_cli_defaults.py\n```\n\nEach needs a marker that names what it excludes and why, in the shape that\n`nanopynix_testing.nix_markers` already uses for a Nix version. **Do not add a\nplatform branch to library code.** The rule that `CLAUDE.md` gives for an old\nNix applies here without change: gate the test, and give the gate its reason.\n\n**Whether it blocks a merge.** A job that fails for a week and that nobody\nreads is worse than no job. It can start as `continue-on-error`, or on\n`on_schedule.nix` rather than `on_commit.nix`, until it is green.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `ci/render.py` runs, and both the Nix source and the rendered YAML are\n  committed. `.github/workflows/*.yml` is generated.\n- The macOS job is green, on a named subset, with every excluded test carrying\n  a marker that says why.\n- nixidae/nixidae#175 gets a comment that says whether its claim holds, because that is the\n  first thing the job can answer.\n- `tests/meta/test_ci_step_policy.py` still passes: a `run:` body stays one\n  line and carries no `${{ }}`.\n\n## Not in this\n\n- The whole matrix on macOS.\n- A macOS wheel.\n- Correcting nixidae/nixidae#174. This issue gives that correction a way to be confirmed."}
{"issue": 182, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdVu2Kg", "before": "## The runner needs a case-sensitive `/nix`, and the case hack off\n\nA report from a person who runs nanopynix on macOS today, sharing one store\nbetween macOS and an `aarch64-linux` VM. Two things were necessary to install:\n\n1. `/nix` on a **case-sensitive** file system.\n2. The **case hack** turned off.\n\nThis is a prerequisite of the job, and not a detail. The setting is a\ncompile-time default, so it does not read the file system:\n\n```c++\n// src/libutil/archive.cc, 2.34\nSetting<bool> useCaseHack{\n    this,\n#ifdef __APPLE__\n    true,\n#else\n    false,\n#endif\n    \"use-case-hack\",\n    \"Whether to enable a macOS-specific hack for dealing with file name case collisions.\"};\n```\n\nEvery Darwin Nix therefore turns it on, on a case-sensitive volume as well.\n`useCaseHack` reaches `dumpPath` and `parseDump`, so it changes the bytes of a\nNAR and the hash of that NAR. A macOS job that leaves it on does not compare\nwith the Linux jobs, and it does not match the one setup that is known to\nwork.\n\n**So the job sets `use-case-hack = false` and puts `/nix` on a case-sensitive\nvolume.** `nanopynix.settings` already models the setting\n(`settings.py:562`), so nothing new is needed to express it. Nothing in this\nrepository mentions the case hack today, and that is the gap.\n\nSharing a store between macOS and `aarch64-linux` is also the strongest\nargument for the job: the two platforms have to agree on a store, and only a\nmacOS runner can show that they do.\n\n## Which jobs, and which tests\n\n**One job, `test-local-nix_2_34`, running the whole suite.** Narrowing the\ntests would hide the Linux assumptions that the first run exists to find.\n\nThe sharp reason is **#140**, and macOS is the only host in the matrix that\ncan answer it. Confirmed by evaluation:\n\n```\nnanopynixVersions.nix_2_34.nanopynix-bindings.stdenv.cc.name  -> clang-wrapper-21.1.8\n...stdenv.cc.libcxx.name                                      -> libcxx-21.1.6+apple-sdk-26.5\n```\n\nThat is the libc++abi which compares `type_info` by address, which is the\nwhole claim in `nanopynix-bindings/package.nix`. The tests that answer it:\n\n- *translation lost* -> `test_exception_translation.py`,\n  `test_error_boundaries.py`, `test_exceptions_classify.py`,\n  `rpc/test_status_details.py`.\n- *`dynamic_cast` returns null* -> `bindings/test_l1_store_bindings.py`, which\n  calls `find_roots` on a real `LocalStore`. **This is the highest-value test\n  on macOS**, because its failure is a wrong answer -- \"store does not support\n  garbage collection\" -- and not an error. No Linux job can catch a silent\n  wrong answer.\n\nThe bindings **evaluate** for `aarch64-darwin` with no change, so no\nLinux-only input blocks the expression.\n\n## The excluded set is smaller than this issue assumed\n\nThis issue lists ten modules. Most hold one assertion, and two need nothing:\n\n| mechanism | where | real scope |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `/proc/self/task/<tid>` | `test_expr.py`, `test_inproc.py` | one test each |\n| `/proc/<pid>/oom_score_adj` | `test_stdio_worker.py` | one test |\n| `/proc/self/fd` | `test_stores.py` | two tests |\n| `unshare`, overlayfs | `test_namespace.py` | **none**: it asks `probe_namespace_support`, which answers \"needs Linux, not darwin\" |\n| relocated store | `test_store_exec.py` | **none**: it skips a store that is not relocated |\n| `OverlayNamespace` as a value | `test_worker_argv.py`, `test_stores.py` | none: these render a URI and never unshare |\n\nSo five tests, and no whole module. A `nix_platform(name)` marker now carries\nthem, in the shape `nix_sanitizer` already uses. Control: with the marker\nturned to `darwin`, those four modules report 7 skipped against 2.\n\n## Still to do\n\n- The job itself, in `ci/workflows/on_commit.nix`, with `ci/render.py` run and\n  both files committed.\n- Non-blocking at first, per the reasoning in this issue.\n- A comment on nixidae/nixidae#175 with the answer, once the job runs.\n"}
{"issue": 185, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYO6CHw", "before": "## The state of it\n\n`on_commit.yml` renders **24 jobs**, and a push to `develop` starts every one\nof them. Measured on run 31938799703, the last full push run:\n\n| kind | git | 2.34 | 2.35 | total |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| asan | 26m | 26m | 26m | **78m** |\n| nogc | 13m | 14m | 12m | 39m |\n| ubsan | 13m | 12m | 13m | 38m |\n| daemon | 12m | 10m | 11m | 33m |\n| local | 11m | 10m | 10m | 31m |\n| tsan | 8m | 8m | 8m | 24m |\n| static-checks | | | | 2m |\n| docs-build, docs-deploy, commit-subjects | | | | under 1m each |\n\nThat is about **245 runner-minutes for each push**, and the wall clock is\nabout 26 minutes. ASAN alone is 78 of those minutes, and it sets the wall\nclock: every other job is finished while the three ASAN jobs still run.\n\n`test-darwin-nix_2_34` arrived after that run and adds a 25th job.\n\n## Why this asks for a change\n\n**The iteration loop is 26 minutes long, and most of it answers nothing.** A\nchange to one Python module gets three sanitizer builds, three no-collector\nbuilds and a full daemon matrix. A defect that only ASAN can find is rare, and\nthe cost of finding it one push later is small.\n\n**GitHub gives this compute away, and that is a reason to use less of it.** A\npublic repository pays no minutes. A repository that takes 245 minutes for\neach push is still a repository that a scheduler can decide to put behind\nother work.\n\n## What this asks for\n\n1. **A default set of jobs on a push.** Small enough that a person waits for\n   it, and wide enough that a red one means something.\n2. **Every other job stays rendered, and stays reachable.** Nothing is\n   deleted. A `workflow_dispatch` must still be able to name any job.\n3. **The nightly runs everything**, and it records the commit that it ran\n   against, so a failure that appears later can be bisected to a range of\n   commits rather than to \"some time in the last week\".\n\n## What already exists\n\n**The selection mechanism is already there, and it needs widening rather than\nwriting.** `ci/workflows/on_commit.nix` gives each test job this condition:\n\n```nix\nwithCond \"github.event_name != 'workflow_dispatch' || inputs.jobs == '' || contains(format(',{0},', inputs.jobs), ',${name},')\"\n```\n\nSo a dispatch already selects a subset, and an empty input already means \"all\nof them\". The change is what a **push** means, and the same expression is the\nplace to say it. `on_schedule.nix` carries the same expression under the name\n`dispatchable`.\n\n**`ci-develop` is already outside the push matrix.** `on_commit.nix` sets\n`push.branches-ignore = [ \"ci-develop\" ]`, so the branch that this repository\niterates on already starts nothing, and a dispatch with `-f jobs=...` is how a\nchange reaches CI today. The default-set question is about `develop` and about\na pull request, and not about that loop.\n\n**`gatingTestJobs` already names what a docs deploy waits for.** A job that\nleaves the default set must also leave that list, or `docs-deploy` waits for a\njob that never starts.\n\n## What has to be decided\n\n**Which jobs are in the default set.** Some candidates, and the argument for\neach:\n\n- `static-checks` and `commit-subjects` are 2 minutes together, and they stay\n  whatever else happens.\n- One `local` and one `daemon` job, on the floor version, cover the two\n  backends and cost about 20 minutes together.\n- `tsan` is the cheapest sanitizer at 8 minutes, and it is the one that found\n  a real defect most recently.\n- `asan` is the expensive one, and the strongest candidate to leave out.\n- The `git` version is the one that breaks from outside, so it is the best\n  candidate for the nightly and the worst for a push.\n\n**Whether a pull request differs from a push to `develop`.** A merge into\n`develop` is the last moment a wide run costs nothing extra in wall clock,\nbecause nobody waits for it.\n\n**Where the \"last full matrix pass\" marker lives.** Three shapes, and none is\nobviously right:\n\n- a git ref that the nightly moves, for example a `last-full-pass` branch or\n  tag. A reader compares it with `git log`, and `git bisect` takes it\n  directly.\n- a file on `develop` that the nightly commits. `update-lockfile-commit`\n  already writes to the branch, so the machinery is there. It adds a commit\n  for each night.\n- a repository variable or a GitHub deployment. It costs no commit, and it is\n  invisible to anyone reading the repository.\n\n**What the marker means, and this is the part that is harder than it looks.**\nThe nightly runs `nix flake update` **before** the test jobs, and the jobs\nread the updated lockfile from an artifact. So a green nightly says \"this\ncommit, with these newer inputs\". It does not say \"this commit, as it is\ncommitted\". A marker that records the commit alone cannot support a bisect,\nbecause a nightly that goes red may be reporting an input bump and not a\ncommit. The marker has to record the lockfile identity as well, or the\nnightly needs a run against the checked-in lock.\n\n## Not in this issue\n\n- Removing a job, or a Nix version. The matrix stays as it is.\n- The macOS job, which is nixidae/nixidae#182 and is `continue-on-error` today.\n- Making any job faster. That is worth doing and it is a different question.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A push to `develop` starts a named default set, and the set is small enough\n  to wait for.\n- Every job of the matrix still runs on a `workflow_dispatch` that names it,\n  and on the nightly.\n- `docs-deploy` waits only for jobs that a push starts.\n- The nightly records what it passed against, in a shape a person can read\n  and `git bisect` can take.\n- `ci/render.py` is re-run and both files are committed.\n"}
{"issue": 186, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYO6JQg", "before": "## What happens\n\nOn macOS, a process that forks and then opens an inproc session **deadlocks**.\nIt does not fail, and it does not time out. It stops.\n\n`nanopynix/tests/test_fork_safety.py` calls that pattern the one supported\ninproc pattern, and its docstring names `ansinix` as the consumer that relies\non it:\n\n```python\nimport nanopynix\nimport nanopynix.inproc          # loads _ext; does not call initLibStore\n\nif os.fork() == 0:\n    asyncio.run(use_a_session())  # initLibStore runs here, in the child\n```\n\nThe macOS CI job of nixidae/nixidae#182 found it. `faulthandler` dumped this stack, innermost\nfirst:\n\n```\nCurl_macos_init\nglobal_init\ncurl_global_init\nnix::initLibStore\n<a nanobind function in _ext.so>\n_PyEval_EvalFrameDefault ...\n```\n\nThe `_ext.so` frames print as `PyInit__ext+0xd428`, which is `faulthandler`\nresolving an address to the nearest exported symbol of a stripped library. The\ncall is at run time and not at import.\n\n## Why\n\n**curl calls SystemConfiguration once, at `curl_global_init`, on macOS only.**\n`lib/macos.c` of curl 8.21.0:\n\n```c\n#ifdef CURL_MACOS_CALL_COPYPROXIES\nCURLcode Curl_macos_init(void)\n{\n  /*\n   * The automagic conversion from IPv4 literals to IPv6 literals only\n   * works if the SCDynamicStoreCopyProxies system function gets called\n   * first. As curl currently does not support system-wide HTTP proxies, we\n   * therefore do not use any value this function might return.\n   */\n  CFDictionaryRef dict = SCDynamicStoreCopyProxies(NULL);\n  if(dict)\n    CFRelease(dict);\n  return CURLE_OK;\n}\n```\n\ncurl throws the result away. Apple needs the call before IPv4 literals become\nIPv6 literals on a DNS64/NAT64 network, so it is deliberate and it stays.\n\n**A fork child may not call it.** Apple permits only async-signal-safe calls\nbetween `fork` and `exec`. CoreFoundation and libdispatch are neither: they\nabort, or they hold a lock that the fork copied in a locked state. This one\nholds.\n\n`nix::initLibStore` calls `curl_global_init`, so any first Nix call in a fork\nchild reaches it.\n\n## What already covers half of this\n\n`nanopynix.settings.resolve_worker_start` answers `spawn` on Darwin, and its\ndocstring names the same call chain. So the **rpc** engine is already correct:\neach worker execs a fresh interpreter, and no fork child initialises Nix.\n\nThe **inproc** engine has no equivalent, because it starts no process. The\ncaller forks, and the library only learns about it afterwards.\n\n## What could be done\n\nFour shapes, and the issue does not choose between them:\n\n1. **Refuse, and say why.** `nanopynix._fork` already carries a process guard,\n   and inproc already refuses a fork that inherited a session. Extend it: on\n   Darwin, refuse to run `initLibStore` in a forked child, and name curl and\n   SystemConfiguration in the message. This turns a hang into a readable error\n   in one line. It removes the pattern on macOS rather than repairing it.\n\n2. **Initialise before the fork.** `curl_global_init` counts its callers\n   (`lib/easy.c:126`, `if(initialized++)`), and a fork child inherits the\n   counter. A parent that calls `nanopynix.init_libstore()` before it forks\n   leaves the child returning early, so `Curl_macos_init` never runs there.\n   **This is a mitigation and not a repair.** The child still holds\n   CoreFoundation state that it inherited across a fork, which Apple does not\n   support. It needs measuring before it is recommended.\n\n3. **Document the pattern as Linux-only**, and give macOS consumers `spawn` or\n   the rpc engine instead. Cheapest, and it costs `ansinix` a supported path.\n\n4. **Ask Nix for an `initLibStore` that does not initialise curl.** The store\n   layer needs curl for a substituter and for a fetcher, and a local store with\n   neither needs none of it. This is an upstream question, and it is the only\n   option that repairs the pattern rather than removing it.\n\n## Meanwhile\n\n`test_fork_first_then_open_still_works_for_inproc` carries a\n`nix_platform(\"linux\")` marker, so the macOS job of nixidae/nixidae#182 does not hang for 30\nminutes on it. The marker names this issue. Remove the marker with the fix.\n\n`test_auto_picks_the_start_method_from_the_fork` carries the same marker for a\ndifferent reason: it asserts that an unforked process resolves `auto` to\n`forkserver`, and Darwin answers `spawn` whatever the fork state is. That\nexpectation is the Linux answer, and it is correct on Linux.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A fork child that initialises Nix on macOS gives an answer, and the answer\n  arrives in less than a second.\n- The chosen shape is written down where a consumer reads it, and not only in\n  a commit message.\n- `test_fork_safety.py` loses the marker, or the test states the macOS\n  behaviour that replaced the pattern.\n\nRefs nixidae/nixidae#182\n"}
{"issue": 190, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYO6lNQ", "before": "## What happens\n\n`test_a_forkserver_child_is_instrumented_not_merely_flagged` killed the pytest\nprocess on the runner of the macOS job, at 72 percent of run 31955684530. The\nlog of that run stops mid-line, on the name of the test:\n\n```\nnanopynix/tests/test_beartype_instrumentation.py::test_a_forkserver_child_is_instrumented_not_merely_flagged\n<end of log>\n```\n\nThe cleanup of the job then reported what the docstring of that test already\npredicts: `Terminate orphan process: pid (17662) (python3.14)`.\n\n**It is not the hang that the docstring bounds.** That call already carries a\n`timeout=` on `.get()`, and a timeout would have named the test and left a\ntraceback. The process died instead.\n\n## What it cost\n\nThe whole report of the run:\n\n- no `= FAILURES =` section, so the seven real failures of that run have no\n  text;\n- no summary line;\n- no `junit.xml`, because pytest writes it at the end and never got there. The\n  upload step still reported success, and uploaded nothing;\n- every test after 72 percent unrun.\n\nSeven failures were visible only as `FAILED` lines. All seven pass on an\nM-series Mac in the same configuration, so the runner is what decides.\n\n## Why the test provokes it\n\nA forkserver child is a process that forked and never exec'd.\n`nanopynix.settings.resolve_worker_start` answers `spawn` on Darwin for that\nexact reason, and says so: libdispatch and CoreFoundation do not survive a fork\nwithout an exec, and `initLibStore` reaches both through libcurl.\n\nThe test builds that child on purpose, and preloads\n`nanopynix.rpc.worker._worker` into it. So it asserts a property of a path that\nno macOS caller of nanopynix ever takes.\n\nIt passes on an M-series Mac, three runs out of three, at 0.46 s.\n\n## What was done meanwhile\n\n`LINUX_FORK_THEN_INIT`, the marker that already names this call chain and issue\n nixidae/nixidae#186. The macOS job then reaches the end of the suite and reports what fails.\n\nGating it costs no coverage of the product: the forkserver path is Linux-only\nby the library's own decision.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- The reason the child dies on that host, and not on an M-series Mac, is\n  written down.\n- Either the test runs on macOS again, or the marker names the reason it\n  cannot rather than the symptom.\n\n## The wider question this leaves\n\nA crash of the pytest process loses the whole report, and the two artifacts\nthat nixidae/nixidae#182 added exist to stop that. They did not, because both are written at\nthe end of a run. `pytest-agent` writes its per-test detail as the run goes,\nand `nanopynix/tests.nix` leaves it out of the runner on purpose, so that\ndecision is the one to revisit if this happens again.\n\nRefs nixidae/nixidae#182\nRefs nixidae/nixidae#186\n"}
{"issue": 191, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYO6r0Q", "before": "## What happens\n\n`pynix develop` writes an rc script and runs it with the `bash` it found. On\nmacOS that is Apple's bash, which is **3.2.57**, and the script does not parse:\n\n```\n/var/folders/.../pynix-develop-eyr3pqv3/rc: line 1865: syntax error near unexpected token `;'\n/var/folders/.../pynix-develop-eyr3pqv3/rc: line 1865: `            ;&'\n```\n\n`;&` is the fallthrough form of a `case` arm, and bash added it in 4.0. Apple\nstill ships 3.2 because every later release is GPLv3.\n\nThree tests failed on this in run 31958491707, all with the same line number:\n\n- `test_develop_runs_a_command_in_the_environment[daemon]`\n- `test_develop_reports_the_exit_status_of_its_command[daemon]`\n- `test_print_dev_env_prints_bash_that_restores_the_environment[daemon]`\n\n## Where the `;&` comes from\n\nNot from pynix. It is in the environment that the derivation itself carries --\na bash function of stdenv, written for the bash that Nix builds with. The rc\nscript restores that environment faithfully, which is its job.\n\n## Why the fallback reaches it\n\n`develop.py` already prefers a bash it chooses. `_interactive_shell` resolves\n`nixpkgs#bashInteractive` and returns `from_nixpkgs=True`, and `_exec_bash`\nruns that binary. When the resolution finds nothing it calls\n`_fallback_shell()`:\n\n```python\ndef _fallback_shell() -> InteractiveShell:\n    \"\"\"The bash on PATH, which is what Nix falls back to at ``develop.cc:642``.\"\"\"\n    return InteractiveShell(path=shutil.which(\"bash\") or \"bash\", from_nixpkgs=False, exec_prefix=[])\n```\n\nMatching `develop.cc:642` is right in general. It is wrong on a host whose\n`bash` cannot read what the environment contains, and macOS is that host by\ndefault.\n\n## Why it passes on a developer machine\n\nThe dev shell puts bash 5.3 on PATH, so the fallback is harmless there:\n\n| | version | `;&` |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `/bin/bash` on macOS | 3.2.57 | no |\n| the dev shell | 5.3.15 | yes |\n\nSo this reproduces only where nothing else has already put a modern bash on\nPATH. A user who installed pynix and ran it in their own terminal is exactly\nthat case.\n\n## What to decide\n\n- **Refuse rather than fall back**, when the resolution fails and the ambient\n  bash is too old. A message naming the version beats a syntax error at line\n  1865 of a generated file.\n- **Or make the resolution not fail.** It looks up `nixpkgs#bashInteractive`\n  through the registry, which needs a registry and a network. Depending on the\n  bash of the closure that pynix itself was built from would need neither.\n\nThe second is the fix a user wants; the first is what stops the current\nfailure being unreadable. They are not exclusive.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `pynix develop -- <command>` works on a macOS host whose only bash is 3.2.\n- A bash that cannot read the environment is reported by name and version.\n- The three tests above run on the macOS job, ungated.\n\nRefs nixidae/nixidae#182\n"}
{"issue": 193, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYO66Sw", "before": "## The idea\n\nBind Lua next to the Python bindings, so a caller can drive a Nix evaluator\nfrom Lua as well.\n\n**This is an idea, and nothing depends on it.** It is written down so the\nquestion is asked once rather than remembered vaguely.\n\n## Why it is worth asking\n\nLua is the easiest language to embed in C++ that anyone actually uses. The\nwhole interpreter is a C library with a stack API, `sol2` gives it a modern\nC++ binding in a header, and the runtime is a few hundred kilobytes rather\nthan the tens of megabytes CPython brings.\n\nThat matters for the shapes where nanopynix is heavy today. Issue nixidae/nixidae#158 records\n820 ms to import nanopynix and 1.6 s for `pynix` to print its help, and most\nof it is Python. A Lua surface would not be a smaller Python; it would be a\ndifferent order of size.\n\n## What would have to be decided\n\n**What the surface is for.** Two answers, and they are not the same project:\n\n- **An embedding surface**, so a C++ or Lua program drives Nix through\n  nanopynix's own object model. That competes with the Python API and has to\n  keep pace with it.\n- **A scripting surface for Nix itself**, so an expression can call out to Lua\n  the way `register_primop` lets it call out to Python. That is a primop\n  question, and it shares everything with nixidae/nixidae#192: what the script can reach, and\n  what happens when it reaches for something that is not there.\n\n**What it costs to keep.** The bindings are one C++ layer today, and every\nNix version in the matrix builds it. A second language surface doubles what a\nchange to `py_eval.hh` has to be checked against, and `nanopynix-bindings`\nalready carries the type-visibility question of nixidae/nixidae#175.\n\n**Whether anything wants it.** `pynix` is Python, `pynixd` is Python, and\n`ekn` is Python. Nothing in this repository would use a Lua surface, so it\nwould start with no consumer to keep it honest -- which is exactly the\nposition `grpclib-transports` was in before it moved here.\n\n## What would make this a no\n\n- No consumer, and no prospect of one.\n- The embedding surface has to track the Python one, and nobody is going to.\n- The scripting question turns out to be nixidae/nixidae#192 with a different language, in\n  which case answer that one first.\n"}
{"issue": 195, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYO7JVw", "before": "## What\n\n`nanopynix/src/nanopynix/primops/` holds three families of primops:\n`ipaddress_primops()`, `jsonschema_primops()` and `yaml_primops()`. Move them\ninto a `nanopynix-primops` project, beside `pynix-lsp/`, and make the closure\nof `nanopynix` carry them by default without making them part of `nanopynix`\nitself.\n\nThey arrived one at a time, and each one added a dependency to the library\nthat evaluates Nix. That is the wrong place for them: nothing in the engine\nreads a YAML document or validates a JSON schema.\n\n## The evidence\n\n`nanopynix/pyproject.toml` depends on `jsonschema` and on `pyyaml`, and those\ntwo dependencies exist for these three files alone.\n\nMeasured on 2026-08-16, in the dev shell:\n\n- `import nanopynix` takes 0.229 s and loads 526 modules.\n- 18 of those modules are `yaml`. `nanopynix/__init__.py:131` re-exports\n  `from_yaml`, `to_yaml` and their peers, so every caller loads PyYAML.\n- 0 of those modules are `jsonschema`. `jsonschema_spec` defers the import of\n  `_jsonschema_impl`, so that cost is already paid only on use -- but the\n  distribution is still in the closure of every consumer.\n\n## The packaging question, and a proposal\n\n**\"Optional, enabled by default\" has no direct expression in Python\npackaging.** An extra is opt-in; there is no default-on extra, and a\ndependency that a consumer can decline does not exist in PEP 508. So the\nrequest needs a mechanism, and there are two that give the intended result:\n\n1. **`nanopynix-primops` depends on `nanopynix`, and the arrow points that\n   way.** This is the shape that issue nixidae/nixidae#136 used for `pynix-lsp`, and it\n   works: the base library carries no `yaml` and no `jsonschema`, and this\n   repository's own applications, dev shell and test venvs name\n   `nanopynix-primops` so the primops are there by default *for us*. A lean\n   consumer installs `nanopynix` alone and gets a smaller closure.\n\n2. **`nanopynix[primops]`, an extra of the library.** Simpler to state, and it\n   inverts the default: a consumer who says nothing gets no primops, and every\n   consumer here has to remember the extra. It also keeps the primop source\n   inside `nanopynix/`, which is the thing this issue asks to end.\n\nOption 1 is the recommendation. It gives the same result for every consumer in\nthis repository, and it is the only one of the two that actually moves the\nfiles out.\n\n**Registration is the part that needs a design.** Today\n`nanopynix/__init__.py` re-exports the yaml helpers and `__all__` names\n`yaml_primops`, so the split is not a pure file move: those four public names\nleave the top level of `nanopynix`, or `nanopynix` grows a module `__getattr__`\nthat imports them when the other project is installed. The second one keeps\nevery existing caller working and is the same soft-mount pattern as `pynix lsp`.\n\nAn entry-point registry is the other option worth measuring: a primop project\ndeclares its specs under an entry-point group, and the evaluator collects them\nwhen it starts. That makes \"installed\" mean \"enabled\", which is exactly the\ndefault the request asks for, and it opens the door to a primop project that\nthis repository does not own. It also costs an `importlib.metadata` scan at\nstart-up, which issue nixidae/nixidae#158 would then have to account for.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- `nanopynix-primops/` exists, with its own `pyproject.toml`, `LICENSE`,\n  `pytest.ini` and suite. The suite moves with the code.\n- `nanopynix/pyproject.toml` depends on neither `jsonschema` nor `pyyaml`.\n- `import nanopynix` loads no `yaml` module. A gate states this, in the shape\n  of `checks.pynix-isolated`, which issue nixidae/nixidae#136 added for the same question.\n- Every existing caller of `nanopynix.from_yaml`, `to_yaml`,\n  `from_yaml_stream`, `from_yaml11`, `from_yaml11_stream` and `yaml_primops`\n  either keeps working or is updated, and the decision is written down.\n- `tests/support/suite_roots.py`, `tests/support/consumer_imports.py`,\n  `nix/py-packages.nix`, `nix/dev-env.nix`, `nix/checks.nix`, `pytest.ini`,\n  `pyproject.toml` (coverage and pyright) and `ruff.toml` all name the new\n  project. Issue nixidae/nixidae#136 is the worked example of that list.\n\nRefs nixidae/nixidae#136, nixidae/nixidae#158"}
{"issue": 243, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPAG-A", "before": "## What happens\n\nTwo tests of `nanopynix/tests/rpc/client/test_workerpool.py` fail on the macOS\njob, at `[ 0%]`:\n\n- `test_an_aborted_worker_reports_its_signal`\n- `test_an_abort_nobody_was_waiting_for_fails_the_close`\n\nBoth stop at the same assertion, `test_workerpool.py:429`:\n\n```\nassert not proc.is_alive(), await _why_it_is_still_alive(proc)\nE   AssertionError: the signalled child was not collected. pid=5367 exit_status=None waited=30.0s ps:\nE      5367  5356 Z<   <defunct>\n```\n\nThe child took the signal and died. The state `Z<` says so. The parent never\ncollected the child, so `proc.is_alive()` stays true for the whole 30 s bound.\nThe defect is the reaping, and it is not the signal delivery.\n\n## How often\n\nThe job `test-darwin-nix_2_34` failed in 7 of the last 12 complete runs. These\nruns carry the same two tests and the same assertion, and each one is older\nthan the work that found this:\n\n| run | commit | test |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 32058200930 | 701369be | `test_an_abort_nobody_was_waiting_for_fails_the_close` |\n| 32067106917 | 21d38628 | `test_an_aborted_worker_reports_its_signal` |\n| 32108631675 | 0a477cb4 | `test_an_abort_nobody_was_waiting_for_fails_the_close` |\n| 32122105113 | d5eeef6b | both |\n\nOne test fails, or both fail, and the run decides which. A run in between\npasses. So the two tests share one cause, and that cause is a race.\n\n## Why it is not issue nixidae/nixidae#238\n\nIssue nixidae/nixidae#238 is `main:multiple-outputs-substitute-failure: the managed upstream\ndaemon does not start`. It names a different subject. No open issue names the\nreaping of a signalled worker child.\n\n## What to look at\n\nThe parent waits for the child through `anyio.open_process`. On macOS the\nharness starts a worker with `spawn`, because `resolve_worker_start` answers\n`spawn` on Darwin. A `spawn` child has a different parent chain than a `fork`\nchild, so the process that calls `waitpid` may not be the process that owns\nthe child.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- The reason the parent does not collect the child on this host is written\n  down.\n- The two tests pass on the macOS job in five runs out of five, or a marker\n  names the reason they cannot.\n"}
{"issue": 243, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdV20RQ", "before": "## The findings of nixidae/nixidae#251 belong here, and the state has changed since\n\nI filed nixidae/nixidae#251 for this failure without searching first. This issue is the\noriginal; nixidae/nixidae#251 closes and its work moves here.\n\n**The state in the report is not the state the runs show now.** This issue\nrecords `Z<` and `<defunct>`, which is a child that died and was never\ncollected \u2014 a reaping defect. Every run of 19 August reports `R<` instead,\nwhich is a live process:\n\n```\npid=4763 exit_status=None waited=30.0s parent_sigabrt=<Handlers.SIG_DFL: 0>\n  4763  4761 R<   .../python3.14 ... spawn_main ...\nsigkill_collected_it=yes crash_reporters=(none)\n```\n\nA defunct child and a running one need different corrections, so whether both\nstates really occur is now the first question of this issue.\n\n## What the runs of 19 August establish\n\n| ruled out | how |\n| --- | --- |\n| the signal is ignored | `parent_sigabrt` is `SIG_DFL`, and the worker installs handlers for `SIGUSR1` and `SIGINT` alone |\n| the signal is not delivered | `SIGKILL` collects the same child at once, so it was reachable |\n| the macOS crash reporter holds it | run `32277961214` unloaded `ReportCrash` and `spindump`, reported `(none)` running, and the test failed the same way |\n| `spawn` alone | the same shape on Linux with `spawn` forced kills the worker normally |\n\n**It is intermittent, and it is not two defects.** Five of six macOS runs\ncarry at least one failure, and which of the two tests fails changes between\nruns:\n\n| run | `..._reports_its_signal` | `..._nobody_was_waiting...` |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `32262353209` | fail | fail |\n| `32264745390` | fail | fail |\n| `32269175882` | pass | fail |\n| `32274388607` | pass | fail |\n| `32277961214` | fail | pass |\n| `32281706131` | pass | pass |\n\n## The open question, and it answers itself now\n\nA **blocked** signal and an **ignored** one look the same from outside, and\nthe diagnostic read only the disposition:\n\n| | `SIG_IGN` | blocked |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| the kernel | discards it | holds it pending |\n| the process | runs | **runs** |\n| `ps` | `R` | **`R`** |\n| survives `SIGKILL` | no | no |\n| inherited across `exec` | yes | **yes** |\n\nA mask is inherited across `exec`, so a `spawn` worker takes the mask of the\nthread that spawned it and a `forkserver` worker takes the forkserver's.\nmacOS spawns, Linux does not.\n\n`6310d467` makes `_why_it_is_still_alive` report\n`signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_BLOCK, [])`, so **the next failing run\nanswers this at no cost**. `pthread_sigmask` reads the calling thread alone,\nso a `False` narrows the question to the worker rather than closing it.\n\nThis blocks the removal of `continue-on-error` in nixidae/nixidae#249.\n"}
{"issue": 243, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdV21Bw", "before": "## It reproduces on a re-run of the same commit\n\nRun 32315191629 is commit c82dc704. Its macOS job failed\n`test_an_aborted_worker_reports_its_signal`. I re-ran that one job, with no\nchange to the commit, and the second attempt failed\n`test_an_abort_nobody_was_waiting_for_fails_the_close` instead -- the other\ntest that this issue names.\n\nSo the failure does not follow a commit. The same tree gives a different one of\nthe two tests on each attempt, which is what a race on the collection of the\nchild looks like.\n\n## The next commit failed it again\n\nRun 32319131923 is commit 36a68be1. Its macOS job failed\n`test_an_aborted_worker_reports_its_signal` again, so three macOS jobs in a row\nnow carry a failure of this issue.\n\n## It may cost a second test\n\nThat same job also failed\n`test_inproc_one_evaluator_extracts_and_builds_fifty_derivations`, which\nmeasures how many builds Nix dispatches in one wave. The diagnostic of this\nissue names `ReportCrash` and `spindump` as the programs that hold the\nuncollected child, and `spindump` samples every process on the machine. A\nsampler of that kind takes CPU from the 25 forks that the other test measures.\n\nIssue nixidae/nixidae#258 holds that failure, and it names this one as the hypothesis to test\nfirst.\n"}
{"issue": 249, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPAuCA", "before": "`test-darwin-nix_2_34` builds the bindings against Nix 2.34 and then runs the\nwhole suite against the daemon of the runner, which the installer picks. That\ndaemon is 2.35.2 today. The job name states a version that the job does not\nreally measure, and the two ends of every store operation come from different\nNix versions.\n\n## The same cause as nixidae/nixidae#247, at a place that cannot take the same correction\n\nIssue nixidae/nixidae#247 was one test that opened the daemon of the machine. The correction\nthere was to reach the host store through `local?read-only=true`, so no\nprotocol and no second version enter the test.\n\n**The macOS job cannot do that.** `mkDarwinTestJob` in `ci/workflows/lib.nix`\ngives the reason, and it is a real one:\n\n- Every store the suite makes is a chroot store, and\n  `derivation-builder.cc:1780` refuses to build in a diverted store off\n  Linux. Linux answers a diverted store with a mount namespace and a bind\n  mount, and macOS has neither. So `NANOPYNIX_TEST_SYSTEM_STORE=1` is what\n  makes a build a plain build.\n- The runner takes the multi-user installer, so `/nix/var/nix/db` belongs to\n  root and a direct `local://` store cannot take the big lock.\n\nBoth hold. The version skew is the price of the only configuration that runs\nthere.\n\n## What the job should measure instead\n\nThe differences that macOS reports are differences of the Nix below, and this\nrepository can correct none of them. So the job earns its minutes by\nanswering one question: **do the basics work on macOS?** It does not earn\nthem by running a matrix cell that the machine cannot really hold.\n\nTwo parts:\n\n1. **Name what \"the basics\" are, and run those alone.** Open a store, query a\n   path, evaluate an expression, build one derivation, copy a closure. A\n   marker or a path list decides the set, and the set is small enough to read.\n2. **Stop stating a version that the job does not pick.** Either the job\n   takes the Nix of the runner and says so in its name, or the setup step\n   installs the version that the job names. The second one costs an install\n   of a second Nix on every run, so measure it before choosing it.\n\n`continue-on-error = true` stays until the job passes. It becomes a gate at\nthe moment the flag comes off, and a small set of basics is what makes that\nmoment reachable.\n\n## What this does not ask for\n\nDo not add a version branch to library code for macOS. The rule of\n`CLAUDE.md` holds: gate the test, and name what the gate excludes.\n"}
{"issue": 249, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdV3oLg", "before": "## The baseline, and one failure that is not this issue's to skip\n\nThe last run before `bea42b79`, run `32262353209`, is the measurement that the\nnew configuration is compared against:\n\n```\n2 failed, 2768 passed, 35 skipped, 2 deselected in 1990.47s (0:33:10)\n\nFAILED nanopynix/tests/rpc/client/test_workerpool.py::test_an_aborted_worker_reports_its_signal\nFAILED nanopynix/tests/rpc/client/test_workerpool.py::test_an_abort_nobody_was_waiting_for_fails_the_close\n```\n\n**Neither of those gets a marker from this issue.** They are now nixidae/nixidae#251. The\nrule here is to exclude a test that fails for a reason it does not own, and\nthese two may own their failure: `ps` reports the signalled child as `R<`,\nwhich is a live process rather than a zombie, so it did not die from the\nsignal. Crash isolation is the subject of nixidae/nixidae#84, and a worker that survives a\n`SIGABRT` on macOS is a defect of the shipped code rather than a limit of the\nplatform.\n\nnixidae/nixidae#251 holds the three readings that fit the evidence and says which of them\nwould make it a defect. **It blocks the removal of `continue-on-error` here**,\nbecause a job cannot become a gate while it carries an unexplained failure.\n"}
{"issue": 249, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdV3o5Q", "before": "## Round two: the marking was right\n\nRun `32269175882`, on `0eea596c`:\n\n| | baseline | round one | **round two** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| failed | 2 | 43 | **1** |\n| errors | 0 | 20 | **0** |\n| passed | 2768 | 2706 | 2707 |\n| skipped | 35 | 36 | **97** |\n| time | 33:10 | 33:11 | **29:39** |\n\nEvery failure and every error of round one is gone, and 61 skips took their\nplace \u2014 the 50 tests and their parameters. Nothing else moved: 2707 passed\nagainst 2706, and the one test that differs is the second `test_workerpool`\none, which passed this time.\n\n`test-local-nix_2_34` of the same run passed, so the 50 markers change nothing\non Linux. `static-checks` passed as well.\n\n**So the capability was one, and the list was right.** No second class of\nfailure appeared behind the first, which was the risk of marking from a single\nrun.\n\n## What is left, and it is not this issue\n\n```\nFAILED nanopynix/tests/rpc/client/test_workerpool.py::test_an_abort_nobody_was_waiting_for_fails_the_close\n  AssertionError: the signalled child was not collected.\n    pid=7141 exit_status=None waited=30.0s\n      7141  7139 R<   .../nanopynix-test-env/bin/python3.14\n```\n\nThat is nixidae/nixidae#251, and **it is intermittent**: two of two failed in the baseline\nand in round one, and one of two failed here. An intermittent failure to reap\nis a different thing from a platform that cannot do it at all, and nixidae/nixidae#251 now\ncarries that.\n\n`continue-on-error` stays until nixidae/nixidae#251 is answered. One unexplained failure is\nexactly what a gate must not carry.\n"}
{"issue": 249, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdV3poA", "before": "## The macOS job is green, and the work of this issue is done\n\nRun `32281706131`, on `6310d467`:\n\n```\n2708 passed, 97 skipped, 2 deselected in 1606.57s (0:26:46)\n```\n\nThe whole arc:\n\n| | baseline | round 1 | round 2 | **round 5** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| failed | 2 | 43 | 1 | **0** |\n| errors | 0 | 20 | 0 | **0** |\n| passed | 2768 | 2706 | 2707 | 2708 |\n| skipped | 35 | 36 | 97 | 97 |\n| time | 33:10 | 33:11 | 29:39 | **26:46** |\n\nThe job is six and a half minutes faster than the baseline, because it no\nlonger runs 50 tests that the platform cannot do and no longer writes to the\nstore of the runner.\n\n**What this issue asked for is in place.** The workaround is gone, the\ncapability that macOS lacks is named once in `LINUX_CHROOT_BUILD`, and the\ntests that need it carry it. No second capability appeared behind the first\nover three runs.\n\n## `continue-on-error` stays, and nixidae/nixidae#251 is why\n\nThis green run is **not** the reason to remove the flag. nixidae/nixidae#251 is an\nintermittent failure of two `test_workerpool` tests, and it fired in five of\nthe six macOS runs of this work. A run that happens to pass is not a job that\npasses.\n\nThe flag comes off when nixidae/nixidae#251 closes. That is the moment this job becomes a\ngate, which is what `lib.nix` has said all along.\n"}
{"issue": 250, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPA0pg", "before": "`test-tsan-nix_2_34` failed in run `32251083468`, job `96061990780`:\n\n```\nFAILED nanopynix/tests/test_concurrent_soak.py::test_soak_inproc[local] - TimeoutError\nFAILED nanopynix/tests/test_concurrent_soak.py::test_soak_rpc[local]\n    - AssertionError: 1 of 43 soaked tests failed under concurrency.\n```\n\n## Two lanes wedged at the same moment, and both were building\n\nThe soak log names the two tests, and it also shows both of them passing\nseveral times earlier in the same run.\n\n| time | lane | test | result |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1.516 | 3 | `test_inproc_build_reports_the_path_it_produced` | ok |\n| 1.598 | 0 | `test_an_opaque_request_builds_nothing_and_says_so` | ok |\n| 1.475 | 3 | `test_inproc_build_reports_the_path_it_produced` | ok |\n| 1.511 | 0 | `test_an_opaque_request_builds_nothing_and_says_so` | ok |\n| **120.383** | **4** | `test_inproc_build_reports_the_path_it_produced` | **fail** |\n| **121.460** | **2** | `test_an_opaque_request_builds_nothing_and_says_so` | **fail** |\n\nSo this is not the slowness of TSan. The same two tests take 1.5 s in the\nsame run, many times, and then two lanes stop for two minutes together.\n\nBoth of the two are the tests of `test_store_metadata_fidelity.py` that\nbuild. The other tests of that file read a derivation, and none of those\nwedged.\n\n## The lead to read first\n\nIssue nixidae/nixidae#128 is the shape to compare against: Nix names the temp-roots lock by\nPID and assumes one `LocalStore` for each process, and this suite makes many,\nso two of them wedge in `flock` forever. That issue is closed. Two lanes that\nstop together, while building, is the same signature.\n\nRead that before reading the sanitizer. The run reports\n`ThreadSanitizer: thread leak` at the end, and a thread leak is a report of\nthe shutdown of the process, not of the hang.\n\n## What is not known\n\n**Whether this happens again.** It is one occurrence. The same job passed on\nthe two runs before this one, `32237291501` and `32250019961`, and the tree\nof this run differs from the tree of `32250019961` by a revert of a test file\nthat these tests do not use.\n\nI have not proven that this failure is unrelated to any change. The evidence\nabove is what the log holds, and the next step is to run the soak seeds again\nand find out whether the pair wedges a second time.\n"}
{"issue": 250, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdV3uDQ", "before": "## It did not happen again\n\nRun `32252814168` is `test-tsan-nix_2_34` alone, dispatched on the same\ncommit that failed, `0bf5e2c5`. It **passed**. Both soak tests ran, and no\nlane wedged.\n\nSo the pair of 120 s stalls is intermittent, and one run does not reproduce\nit. That is not a reason to close this issue: the two stalls were real, they\nwere simultaneous, and the two tests that stalled were the two that build.\n\n**What a later reader needs.** The soak takes a seed, and\n`ciSteps.nix_2_34-tsan` has a `soak-seeds` subcommand that runs a set of\nthem. A run of that, rather than one more run of the default seed, is what\nturns \"it did not happen again\" into a measurement.\n\nLeave the issue open until then, and read nixidae/nixidae#128 first when it does happen\nagain.\n"}
{"issue": 250, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdV3ufg", "before": "## It happened again, with the same signature\n\nRun `32253791321`, job `96070675886`, on `f518b9ac`:\n\n| time | lane | test | result |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 119.551 | 4 | `test_an_opaque_request_builds_nothing_and_says_so` | fail |\n| 121.413 | 7 | `test_inproc_build_reports_the_path_it_produced` | fail |\n\nThe first occurrence was 120.383 s and 121.460 s, in lanes 4 and 2, and it\nwas **the same two tests**. Both times the two lanes stop within two seconds\nof each other, and both times the pair is exactly the two tests of\n`test_store_metadata_fidelity.py` that build. The tests that follow in the\nsame lane report `fail` as well, because the deadline has already passed.\n\n## The record so far\n\n| run | commit | result |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `32237291501` | `c133cacf` | pass |\n| `32250019961` | `d1188c01` | pass |\n| `32251083468` | `0bf5e2c5` | **fail** |\n| `32252814168` | `0bf5e2c5`, dispatched alone | pass |\n| `32253791321` | `f518b9ac` | **fail** |\n\nThree commits, and one of them both passes and fails. So this is\nintermittent, it is about half of the runs, and **no change of these three\ncauses it**: `0bf5e2c5` is a revert, and its tree is the tree of `b3bb2d44`.\n\n## What this makes of the lead\n\nTwo lanes, both building, stopping together, repeatably. Issue nixidae/nixidae#128 is the\nshape: Nix names the temp-roots lock by PID and assumes one `LocalStore` for\neach process, and the soak makes many. The next step is to take a stack of\nthe two wedged lanes rather than to run the seeds again, because the pair now\nreproduces about half the time.\n"}
{"issue": 250, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdV3u5g", "before": "## The stack is already in the log, and it names the wedge\n\n`tests/support/hang_report.py` of issue nixidae/nixidae#73 prints the state at the deadline,\nand the log of job `96070675886` carries it. One thread of the Nix store pool\nis inside a build, and the rest are idle:\n\n```\n- nix-store_1 (daemon=False, alive=True)\n    threading.py:1024 in run\n    concurrent/futures/thread.py:73 in run\n    <@beartype(nanopynix._core._nix_executor._run_interruptible)>:69\n    nanopynix/src/nanopynix/_core/_nix_executor.py:106 in _run_interruptible\n    <@beartype(nanopynix.inproc._impl._run_with_log_context)>:86\n    nanopynix/src/nanopynix/inproc/_impl.py:261 in _run_with_log_context\n    <@beartype(nanopynix._core._objects.CoreStore.build_paths_with_results)>:82\n    nanopynix/src/nanopynix/_core/_objects.py:241 in build_paths_with_results\n```\n\n`nix-store_0`, `nix-store_2` and `nix-store_3` are in\n`concurrent/futures/thread.py:116 in _worker`, which is the wait for the next\npiece of work. So one build is stopped, and the pool is not busy.\n\n**The Python stack ends at the call into the bindings.** The thread is inside\nlibnixstore, in `buildPathsWithResults`, and no Python frame is below that.\nThat is where the two minutes go.\n\nThe three tasks alive are `Session._forward_logs`, `_call_in_runner_task` and\na janus notifier, and none of them is doing work. Nothing waits on Python.\n\n## What this makes of the lead\n\nA build stopped inside Nix, while another lane is also building, is the\nsignature that issue nixidae/nixidae#128 describes: Nix names the temp-roots lock by PID and\nassumes one `LocalStore` for each process, and the soak makes many.\n\n**The next measurement is a native backtrace, and this job already has the\ntool.** `nix/suite-runtime.nix` carries `gdb`, and `mkDarwinTestJob` and the\nLinux test jobs upload `test-gdb-output.log`. A backtrace of `nix-store_1`\nat the deadline names the function in Nix that holds, and that turns the lead\ninto a cause.\n\nThe rpc half of the same run says the same thing from the other side:\n`test_rpc_build_reports_the_path_it_produced` ended in\n`Status.DEADLINE_EXCEEDED`, so the worker process was stopped in the same\nplace.\n"}
{"issue": 250, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdV3wIQ", "before": "## The path-lock lead is wrong, and here is the measurement\n\nThe two tests that wedge build **one derivation**, `BUILT_OUTPUTS_DRV` of\n`nanopynix/tests/test_store_metadata_fidelity.py:261`. Both\n`test_inproc_build_reports_the_path_it_produced` and\n`test_an_opaque_request_builds_nothing_and_says_so` build it, so two lanes\nreach one output path and one `<out>.lock`.\n\n`pathlocks.cc` has no bookkeeping for a second caller in the same process.\n`PathLocks::lockPaths` opens its own descriptor and calls `flock`, and `flock`\nlocks a file description rather than a process, so two threads of one process\ndo block on each other. That is what made nixidae/nixidae#128 look like the shape to copy.\n\n**It serialises correctly, so it is not this.** I ran eight lanes of one\nsession against one fresh derivation, with a builder that takes about ten\nseconds (a shell loop -- `sleep` is not in the sandbox, which is why a first\nattempt measured 0.6 s and proved nothing):\n\n```\nlane_1: status='built'\nlane_0, lane_2 ... lane_7: status='already-valid'\n1 passed in 10.9s\n```\n\nOne lane builds, seven wait on the lock, all eight finish, and the total is\nthe length of one build. No lane is lost and nothing wedges.\n\n## What the evidence still says\n\nThe hang report names **one** thread inside `build_paths_with_results`, and\n`nix-store_0`, `nix-store_2` and `nix-store_3` are idle in `_worker`. So the\npool is not exhausted and two builds are not fighting. One build is stopped\ninside Nix.\n\n## The next thing to look at\n\n`nanopynix_testing.nix_environment` gives every test\n`substituters=[\"daemon\", \"https://cache.nixos.org\"]`, at line 119. So each\nbuild asks the nix-daemon of the machine and then the network before it\nbuilds. A daemon store takes **one** connection unless a caller asks for\nmore, and the soak runs eight lanes.\n\nThat is a hypothesis and not a finding: the log of the failing job holds no\ncurl error and no \"waiting for\" line from the run itself.\n\n## Reproducing it\n\nThe soak passes here in 6.6 s and the job takes over 120 s, so a plain local\nrun cannot show this. `nanopynixVersions.nix_2_34-tsan.shell` fetches Nix\nfrom the cache and builds only the Python packages, so a sanitized shell is\ncheap to get. That is the way to a native backtrace, and it needs no change\nto CI.\n"}
{"issue": 250, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdV3wiA", "before": "## How to run this locally, and what it says so far\n\n`nanopynixVersions.nix_2_34-tsan.shell` is **not** the way. Two things live in\nthe packaged runner and not in that shell:\n\n- `nanopynix/tests.nix:103` exports `TSAN_OPTIONS`, with `halt_on_error=1`,\n  `die_after_fork=0` and the suppressions file.\n- The `LD_PRELOAD` of the sanitizer runtime, which `nix/sanitizer.nix:43`\n  names.\n\nWithout the preload the shell fails at import with `libtsan.so.2: cannot\nallocate memory in static TLS block`. With the preload but without the\nrunner's options it aborts in Boehm, `Signals delivery fails constantly at\nGC nixidae/nixidae#30`, which is the shape of nixidae/nixidae#98 and not this issue.\n\nSo build the step that CI builds, and run the subcommand that CI runs:\n\n```\nnix build --file . ciSteps.nix_2_34-tsan --out-link result\nBACKEND=local ./result/bin/nanopynix-ci soak-seeds\n```\n\nOnly `broad` and `soak-seeds` exist. `soak` does not, and the name in\n`CLAUDE.md` is stale.\n\n## It does not reproduce on a fast machine\n\n20 soak runs, 4 seeds over inproc and rpc and both backends:\n\n```\n20 PASSED\nexit=0\n```\n\nThe slowest lane of the whole set took **15.3 s**, and the job on the runner\nstops at **120 s**. So this machine is far enough ahead of a GitHub runner\nthat the composition alone does not produce the wedge.\n\n## What that leaves open, and the test that separates it\n\nTwo readings survive, and they need different corrections:\n\n1. **A genuine wedge.** Something stops, and the deadline reports it.\n2. **A deadline that is too tight for a loaded runner.** The build tests are\n   the slowest in the roster, and a slow enough machine crosses 120 s.\n\nReading 2 has an argument against it already: the same two tests finish in\nabout 1.5 s earlier in the same run, and an 80-fold spread inside one run is\nnot load.\n\nThe measurement that separates them is to run the same step under a\nconstrained processor count, which is what a runner is. That is running now.\n\n## A native backtrace needs a decision\n\n`/proc/sys/kernel/yama/ptrace_scope` is `1` here, so gdb attaches to its own\ndescendants alone. Attaching to a soak that another shell started needs\neither `ptrace_scope=0`, which is root, or `prctl(PR_SET_PTRACER)` from the\nprocess under test. Neither is in place, and that is why this comment carries\nno native stack.\n"}
{"issue": 250, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdV3xXg", "before": "## Only the `local` backend wedges, in both runs\n\nBoth jobs run the soak against `local` and against `daemon`. The failures:\n\n```\njob 96061990780:  FAILED ... test_soak_inproc[local]\n                  FAILED ... test_soak_rpc[local]\njob 96070675886:  FAILED ... test_soak_inproc[local]\n                  FAILED ... test_soak_rpc[local]\n```\n\n`test_soak_inproc[daemon]` and `test_soak_rpc[daemon]` are in both logs and\npass in both. Four failures, and every one is `local`.\n\n**That is a discriminator, and it moves the lead.** A `daemon` store does its\nstore work in another process, so the wedge needs a `LocalStore` that this\nprocess owns. The path lock is not that -- it is a file, and a daemon takes\nit just the same, from the daemon process.\n\n## Which puts the temp-roots lock back, and this time it is the right half\n\nIssue nixidae/nixidae#128 is `two LocalStore objects in one process deadlock on the PID-named\ntemp roots lock`. I read that earlier as the path lock and disproved the path\nlock. The temp-roots lock is the other half of it, and it fits every\nobservation here:\n\n| observation | temp-roots lock |\n| --- | --- |\n| `local` fails, `daemon` passes | only a store this process owns writes temp roots |\n| the two tests that wedge are the two that build | a build takes a temp root for its output |\n| one thread inside `build_paths_with_results` | that is where the root is taken |\n| intermittent | it needs two stores to overlap |\n\nMy eight-lane experiment did not reproduce it because every lane used **one**\nstore. The condition of nixidae/nixidae#128 is **two** `LocalStore` objects, and the roster\ncarries tests that make exactly that:\n`test_two_store_roots_get_a_local_store_each` and\n`test_two_handles_on_one_local_store_share_the_temp_roots_file` both appear in\nthe notes of a soak run.\n\nSo the composition to try is a build in one lane while another lane makes a\nsecond local store, rather than eight lanes on one store.\n\nThe wait channel that `a5bcda0e` adds separates the two remaining readings on\nthe next failure: `locks_lock_inode_wait` names a file lock, and `futex_wait`\nnames something inside the process.\n"}
{"issue": 250, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdV3x0g", "before": "## This is a recurrence of nixidae/nixidae#128, and nixidae/nixidae#128 names both the cause and the fix\n\nI cited nixidae/nixidae#128 loosely above. Read properly, it is the same failure as this one,\nand it carries more than a lead.\n\n**The body of nixidae/nixidae#128 is the original report, and the title is the conclusion it\nreached.** The body describes `test_soak_inproc[local]` reaching the 120 s\ndeadline in `test-nogc-nix_2_34`, where the pair normally costs about ten\nseconds. That is this failure, at a different job.\n\nIt also rules out what I re-ruled out here, and by the same kind of\nmeasurement: \"Not a slow runner alone. The main suite of the failing attempt\ncost 722.09s against 678.89s on the re-run, which is about 6 percent. That\ndoes not turn 9 seconds into 122.\" My two-processor run says the same thing\nfrom the other side.\n\n**The cause it settled on is the temp-roots lock**, which is what its title\nsays, and the closing comment names the correction:\n\n> The fix is nixidae/nixidae#183: give the process one `Store` for each resolved store\n> reference. That issue states the decisions the change needs, and the 30-run\n> measurement that says the race is closed.\n\n## So the question is what nixidae/nixidae#183 does not cover\n\nnixidae/nixidae#183 deduplicates by **resolved store reference**. Two `LocalStore` objects\nthat point at one state directory are what deadlock, and the dedup stops that\nwhenever the two arrive by the same reference.\n\nTwo ways it would not stop it, and both are guesses until the wait channel\nsays otherwise:\n\n1. **Two spellings of one store.** A reference that differs as a string and\n   resolves to one state directory gives two objects and one temp-roots file.\n2. **A recycled directory name.** pytest reuses a `tmp_path` name after it\n   deletes the old one, so a store reference is not a stable identity across\n   tests. A cache keyed on the reference can hand back a store of the previous\n   directory, or hold two for one path.\n\n## What differs from nixidae/nixidae#128, and why it matters\n\n| | nixidae/nixidae#128 | this issue |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| stalled in | `eval_flake` | `build_paths_with_results` |\n| job | `test-nogc-nix_2_34` | `test-tsan-nix_2_34` |\n| which test held it | **not in the log**, and nixidae/nixidae#128 asked for it | named: the two build tests of `test_store_metadata_fidelity.py` |\n\nnixidae/nixidae#128 closed with two things missing, and the first was \"the log does not say\nwhich roster test was in `eval_flake`\". That gap is closed here. The second\nwas that a stopped Nix call cannot say where it is. `a5bcda0e` answers that\none without a debugger: `locks_lock_inode_wait` would confirm the lock that\nnixidae/nixidae#128 settled on, and `futex_wait` or a socket wait would say nixidae/nixidae#183 left\nsomething else open.\n"}
{"issue": 250, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdV3yWA", "before": "## Both gaps I guessed at in nixidae/nixidae#183 are already closed\n\nI read the cache rather than kept guessing.\n`nanopynix-bindings/src/nix_store.cpp:314`, `open_store_shared`:\n\n```c++\n// `weakly_canonical`, so two names for one directory give one key. The\n// directory does not exist yet on the first open, and that function\n// accepts a path that is not there.\nstd::filesystem::path state_dir =\n    std::filesystem::weakly_canonical(std::filesystem::path(local->stateDir.get()));\n```\n\nThat answers the first guess: **two spellings of one store give one key**, and\nthe comment says so.\n\nThe second guess was a recycled `tmp_path` name. The cache holds an open\ndescriptor on the state directory and the identity of that directory, and\n`cached_store_is_current` at line 304 checks it:\n\n```c++\nif (fstat(entry.state_dir.get(), &held) != 0 || held.st_nlink == 0)\n    return false;\nstruct stat now{};\nif (stat(path.c_str(), &now) != 0)\n    return false;\nreturn now.st_dev == entry.dev && now.st_ino == entry.ino;\n```\n\nA directory that was deleted has `st_nlink == 0`, and a directory that came\nback under the same name has another inode. Either way the entry goes and a\nnew store opens. **A store reference is not the identity; the inode is.**\n\nThe cache also keeps a `LocalStore` alone, and the comment gives the reason:\n\"A store that is not a `LocalStore` takes no temp-roots lock, and it stays out\nof the cache.\"\n\n## What that leaves\n\nTwo `LocalStore` objects on one state directory is the mechanism nixidae/nixidae#128 settled\non, and nixidae/nixidae#183 closes it by identity rather than by name. So this issue is\nprobably **not** that mechanism coming back.\n\nThe weight moves to the other two readings of the wait channel. If the next\nfailure says `locks_lock_inode_wait` then a file lock is still involved and\nthis analysis is wrong somewhere. If it says `futex_wait` or names a socket,\nthen nixidae/nixidae#183 is intact and the cause is elsewhere -- and the `local`-only\nsignature still has to be explained by whatever that is.\n"}
{"issue": 250, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdV3y1A", "before": "## Four more samples, all green, and a correction to the rate\n\nI dispatched `test-tsan-nix_2_34` alone three times on `a5bcda0e`, and the\npush of that commit ran it once more. All four passed, so no wedge met the\nnew wait-channel report.\n\nThe whole record:\n\n| run | commit | result |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `32237291501` | `c133cacf` | pass |\n| `32250019961` | `d1188c01` | pass |\n| `32251083468` | `0bf5e2c5` | **fail** |\n| `32252814168` | `0bf5e2c5` | pass |\n| `32253791321` | `f518b9ac` | **fail** |\n| `32262353209` | `a5bcda0e` | pass |\n| `32263169541` | `a5bcda0e` | pass |\n| `32263184843` | `a5bcda0e` | pass |\n| `32263200716` | `a5bcda0e` | pass |\n\n**\"About half of the runs\" was wrong, and I wrote it several times above.**\nIt came from the first three runs, where two failed. Over nine runs the rate\nis two, which is closer to one in five. A three-run sample was not a rate.\n\n**The two commits since the last failure do not touch this.** `f518b9ac`\nchanges `test_namespace.py`, which is **not in the soak roster** -- a full\nlocal soak names it zero times. `a5bcda0e` adds a report that runs at a\ndeadline and nothing else. So these four passes are sampling, and not a\nrepair.\n\n## Stopping the dispatches\n\nFour green runs do not undo two red ones. The wedge happened, twice, with the\nsame two tests and the same timing both times. Dispatching until it recurs\nspends CI on a one-in-five event, and the report now waits in the tree for the\nnext natural occurrence. That is the cheaper way to catch it.\n\n## What is settled, so the next reader starts here\n\n| ruled out | how |\n| --- | --- |\n| the path lock | 8 lanes on one derivation serialise: one `built`, seven `already-valid`, 10.9 s |\n| a deadline that is too tight | 2 processors move the slowest lane 15.3 s \u2192 13.8 s, and nixidae/nixidae#128 measured 6% |\n| the `LocalStore` dedup of nixidae/nixidae#183 | the cache keys on `weakly_canonical` state dir and checks `st_dev`/`st_ino` against a held descriptor |\n\nAnd two constraints that any explanation has to meet:\n\n- **`local` only.** All four failures are `[local]`; `[daemon]` ran and passed\n  each time. The daemon backend is a `unix://` `UDSRemoteStore`, so the build\n  runs in the managed daemon process and this process holds a socket. A wedge\n  inside libnixstore can only show on `local`.\n- **Not the sanitizer.** nixidae/nixidae#128 is the same failure in `test-nogc-nix_2_34`. So\n  this is altered timing in a shared process, and not TSAN.\n\nTogether those favour something inside the process over a file lock, which is\nwhat `futex_wait` in the new report would say.\n"}
{"issue": 258, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPBoZA", "before": "## What happens\n\n`nanopynix/tests/inproc/test_inproc_multithreaded.py::test_inproc_one_evaluator_extracts_and_builds_fifty_derivations`\nfailed on the macOS job of run 32319131923:\n\n```\nnanopynix/tests/inproc/test_inproc_multithreaded.py:467: in test_inproc_one_evaluator_extracts_and_builds_fifty_derivations\n    assert first_wave_spread < seconds * 4, (\nE   AssertionError: fewer than 25 builds were dispatched concurrently (first-wave spread 9.208s)\nE   assert 9.208278291999932 < (2 * 4)\n```\n\nThe test builds 50 sleep derivations and reads the start time of each build.\nIt asserts that the tightest cluster of 25 start times spans less than 8\nseconds. The job measured 9.208 s, which is 15 % over the margin.\n\n## What the assertion measures\n\nIt measures dispatch concurrency, and not total time. `max-jobs=25` permits 25\nbuilds in one wave, so a spread of 9.2 s says the 25th build started 9.2 s\nafter the first one. The comment above the assertion gives the reason for this\nshape: a budget on the total time was fragile, because the setup of a fork and\na sandbox costs real time even when the builds are concurrent.\n\n## A hypothesis: the two failures of that job are one failure\n\nThe same job also failed `test_an_aborted_worker_reports_its_signal`, which is\nissue nixidae/nixidae#243. That test leaves a signalled child that macOS does not collect, and\nthe diagnostic of nixidae/nixidae#243 names the two programs that hold it: `ReportCrash` and\n`spindump`. `spindump` samples every process on the machine. A sampler of that\nkind takes CPU from the 25 forks that this test measures, and this test runs\nlater in the same job.\n\nSo nixidae/nixidae#243 may be the cause, and this may be its second cost.\n\n## Is the commit that first showed it the cause?\n\nNot proven either way. The commit is 36a68be1, and the three macOS jobs before\nit passed. That commit changed `test_support.shell_pty`, the packaging of\n`test-support`, the tests of `completion-spike`, one ledger under `tests/`, and\none row of the completion table of `pynix`. `nanopynix/tests/inproc/` imports\nnone of those. The commit also puts `pyte` in the test environment, which is\none more package in the closure and not a change of behaviour.\n\nThat is an argument, and not a proof. The measurement below is what settles it.\n\n## What to do\n\nTake one of two answers, with a measurement and not with a guess:\n\n- Read the first-wave spread of this test on the macOS job over several runs.\n  Raise the margin only if the passing runs sit close to 8 s, which would show\n  that the margin was always thin.\n- Or stop nixidae/nixidae#243 leaving a crash reporter that runs, which removes the load.\n\nDo not raise the margin without the measurement. A margin that goes up each\ntime it fails states nothing.\n\n## Where\n\n- `nanopynix/tests/inproc/test_inproc_multithreaded.py:467`\n- Run 32319131923, job `test-darwin-nix_2_34`\n\nRefs nixidae/nixidae#243\n"}
{"issue": 258, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdV4tbw", "before": "## A second sample: it did not recur\n\nTwo macOS jobs have run since this issue opened, 32319696900 and 32341018919.\n`test_inproc_one_evaluator_extracts_and_builds_fifty_derivations` passed in\nboth. So the failure is occasional and not a new floor.\n\nThat fits the hypothesis this issue opened with. nixidae/nixidae#243 failed in the run that\ncarried this failure, and it did **not** fail in run 32319696900, which is the\none run where the macOS job passed completely. The load of the crash reporter\nthat nixidae/nixidae#243 leaves behind is still the first thing to test.\n\n**One sample is not a measurement.** Do not raise the margin yet. The spread of\nthe passing runs is what says whether 8 s was ever the right number, and that\nvalue is in the JUnit report of each job rather than in this comment.\n"}
{"issue": 278, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPDsTw", "before": "Shell completion for `pynix` currently takes approximately 100\u2013115 ms for each keypress in an interactive shell.\n\nThe implementation modules (`pynix._impl`) are already deferred behind a lazy `__getattr__` table (issue nixidae/nixidae#158) and `argcomplete` is imported only when `_ARGCOMPLETE` is set (issue nixidae/nixidae#253). However, profiling shows that startup latency remains in `import pynix`, `build_parser(Pynix)`, and `argcomplete`.\n\nThis umbrella issue tracks optimizations to reduce `pynix` completion invocation latency."}
{"issue": 278, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdV7Ozg", "before": "Note for whoever reads this tree next: the remaining import-cost work here is\ndeferred until the `py315` branch lands.\n\nThat branch brings nanopynix to Python 3.15, which has lazy imports. An\noptimisation written against 3.14 would be work done twice, and some of it\nwould be undone.\n\nThat covers nixidae/nixidae#285 and nixidae/nixidae#281, which are both import cost. nixidae/nixidae#285 carries the\nmeasurement, so it does not have to be taken again: the completion is 493 ms\nagainst 31 ms for `nix`, and `-X importtime` puts 516 ms of that in module\nloading across 468 modules. Evaluating the file is not the cost.\n\nnixidae/nixidae#282 is measured and needs no change -- `-B` moves no syscall, because the\nstore already holds a valid `.pyc` for every module and is read-only. That\ncomment recommends closing it.\n\nThe one item that stands on its own whatever Python is under it is nixidae/nixidae#287:\n`inproc/_impl.py` imports `nanopynix_proto.nix.common` at module level for two\nenums, which costs 84.5 ms of betterproto2 and protobuf descriptor pools that\nan in-process evaluation never uses.\n"}
{"issue": 285, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdV8LIQ", "before": "Deferred, and the reason is in another branch. **Do not pick this up as an\nimport-cost task.**\n\nThe `py315` branch brings nanopynix to Python 3.15, which has lazy imports.\nThat changes the shape of this problem, so an optimisation written against\n3.14 would be work done twice, and some of it would be work undone. Wait for\nthat branch.\n\nThe measurement is here so that it is not taken again.\n\n**Where the time goes today, on the release build, for\n`pynix build --file F --attr b<TAB>` against a nine-line Nix file:**\n\n| program | per invocation |\n| --- | --- |\n| `pynix` | 493 ms |\n| `nix`, same file and prefix | 31 ms |\n\nBoth answer the same candidate. The figure in the issue body was 1.9 s, so it\nhas improved by a factor of four; the gap to `nix` is still 16 times.\n\n**Almost all of it is import.** `python -X importtime` over the same\ninvocation reports **516 ms of self time across 468 modules**, against a 493 ms\ntotal. The three areas the issue body names -- store initialisation, the\nevaluator import chain, and attribute traversal -- are not three areas. The\nevaluation of that file is not the cost. Loading the code that evaluates it is.\n\nThe heaviest cumulative entries:\n\n```\n176.0 ms  pynix._impl.settings          (pydantic_settings 91.0, pydantic.dataclasses 60.8)\n 98.6 ms  nanopynix.inproc._impl\n 84.5 ms  nanopynix_proto.nix.common    (betterproto2 55.4, protobuf descriptor pools 10.3)\n 58.3 ms  pynix\n 56.4 ms  anyio\n 44.9 ms  nanopynix.stores\n```\n\n**One of those is a dependency that this path does not use.** A completion\nopens an `inproc.Session`, and `nanopynix/src/nanopynix/inproc/_impl.py:23`\nimports `nanopynix_proto.nix.common` at module level for two enums. That pulls\nbetterproto2 and the protobuf descriptor pools, which is 84.5 ms of the 493 --\nabout a sixth -- for RPC wire types that an in-process evaluation never sends.\nIssue nixidae/nixidae#287 is that dependency, and it is the one part of this that stands on\nits own whatever Python version is under it.\n\nProbe: `-X importtime` on `pynix-env/bin/python3.14` with the argcomplete\nenvironment set, running `from pynix import main; main()`.\n"}
{"issue": 287, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdV8abQ", "before": "Deferred with the rest of the import-cost work, until the `py315` branch lands.\n\nPython 3.15 has lazy imports, and the mechanism of this issue is a top-level\n`import grpclib` that betterproto2 generates into the service stubs. A lazy\nimport defers exactly that, so an isolation written against 3.14 would be\nwork done twice.\n\nCorrecting a pointer I left on nixidae/nixidae#285: that comment named this issue as the one\npart of the tree that stands whatever Python is underneath. That is wrong. The\nnumber it carries belongs here, and the fix does not.\n\nThe number, measured on 2026-08-25 on the release build: a completion that\nopens an `inproc.Session` pays **84.5 ms** for `nanopynix_proto.nix.common`,\nout of 493 ms for the whole invocation. `nanopynix/src/nanopynix/inproc/_impl.py:23`\nimports it at module level for two enums, `LogLevel` and `RequestFinalized`.\n\nThe architectural half of this issue does not expire with the Python version:\nan in-process evaluation sends nothing on a wire, and it should not name a\nwire package. Keep that when the branch lands. It is the latency argument that\n3.15 answers, not the layering one.\n"}
{"issue": 315, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPHUIg", "before": "Issue nixidae/nixidae#310 found that Nix 2.35 caches directory descriptors up to\n`min(4096, RLIMIT_NOFILE / 8)`, and 0f81b750 pins the soft limit of the test\nprocess so the cache holds at most 256. Measured on `pynix/tests` with Nix\n2.35 and `NANOPYNIX_TEST_FAITHFUL_SESSIONS=1`, that took the peak from 628\ndescriptors to 337.\n\nA smaller term remains. At the end of the capped run, 122 of the 317 open\ndescriptors were handles on the shared store directory -- about one for every\nfour tests, and it only ever rises inside a run.\n\n## What is known\n\n- **It is a live strong reference, not a reference cycle.** A throwaway plugin\n  called `gc.collect()` after every test of `test_search.py` and counted\n  before and after. `before == after` on all 49 rows, while `collected` ran\n  from 0 to 10916 objects. A collection per test would buy nothing.\n- **The store itself is not retained.** The end-of-run histogram shows 122\n  handles on the store directory and one `db.sqlite`. A `LocalStoreAccessor`\n  holds `ref<LocalFSStore>`, so 122 of those would show 122 sqlite handles.\n  What is retained is a bare `PosixDirectorySourceAccessor` rooted at the\n  store directory.\n- **A bare round leaks nothing.** Open a session, a store and an evaluator,\n  evaluate, close all three: flat at 8 descriptors over 8 rounds, with and\n  without a `builtins.readFile` of a path in the store. A flake with no\n  inputs: flat at 14 over 6 rounds. So the retention needs something the\n  probes do not do yet.\n\n## Where to look next\n\nA global cache inside Nix that keeps a `SourcePath` fits, because a\n`SourcePath` holds a `ref<SourceAccessor>`.\n\n`nanopynix-bindings/src/nix_store.cpp:676` is worth reading first.\n`source_path_from` builds a fresh accessor for each call on Nix 2.35\n(`makeFSSourceAccessor`) where 2.34 takes another route\n(`PosixSourceAccessor::createAtRoot`), and on 2.35 each such accessor carries\nits own directory-descriptor cache.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nThe descriptor count of a whole `pynix/tests` run with faithful sessions does\nnot rise with the number of tests. A self-check that records the count is\nenough; `test_support.hang_report.open_descriptors` already produces it.\n"}
{"issue": 315, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdV_7kA", "before": "The leak is measured, and there is a six-line reproducer.\n\nA temporary probe in `~PyEvalState` records the reference counts around the\ndestruction of the evaluator: it captures `weak_ptr`s to `state->storeFS` and\n`state->rootFS`, calls `state.reset()`, and then reports what survives.\n\n**`pynix eval --file F --attr alpha`, eight rounds in one process:**\n\n```\nstate=1 store=5  storeFS_before=3 rootFS_before=8 storeFS_after=1 rootFS_after=1 store_after=3\nstate=1 store=6  ...                                                             store_after=4\n...\nstate=1 store=12 storeFS_before=3 rootFS_before=8 storeFS_after=1 rootFS_after=1 store_after=10\n```\n\nThree facts in that table.\n\n- `state=1` -- the `EvalState` really is destroyed. The wrapper is not the\n  holder, and neither is anything on the Python side.\n- `storeFS_after=1` and `rootFS_after=1` -- **exactly one strong reference to\n  each accessor survives the evaluator, every round.** `rootFS` is\n  `makeUnionSourceAccessor({getFSSourceAccessor(), storeFS})` in impure mode,\n  so one retained `rootFS` retains `storeFS` under it.\n- `store_after` rises by one per round -- and `storeFS` mounts\n  `store->getFSAccessor(...)`, which is a `LocalStoreAccessor` holding\n  `ref<LocalFSStore>`. So the rising store count and the 122 descriptors of\n  this issue are one leak seen at two levels.\n\nThe descriptors do not rise in this loop, because it runs on 2.34. Issue nixidae/nixidae#310\nestablished that 2.35 is where a `PosixDirectorySourceAccessor` keeps an open\ndirectory fd. **The leak is not 2.35-only; the descriptor cost is.**\n\n**The reproducer is a path read, and nothing smaller.** Six rounds of session\nplus store plus evaluator, closing all three, through `nanopynix.inproc`:\n\n| what the round evaluates | `storeFS_after` | `rootFS_after` | `store_after` |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| nothing at all | 0 | 0 | 2, flat |\n| `1 + 1` | 0 | 0 | 2, flat |\n| `builtins.readFile <path>` | 1 | 1 | 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 |\n| `ev.file(F)` then an attribute | 1 | 1 | rises |\n\nSo constructing an evaluator leaks nothing, and evaluating an expression that\ntouches no path leaks nothing. Reading one path through `rootFS` leaks one\nowner of `rootFS`, permanently. That is why every earlier probe came back flat:\ncomment 3 measured a read of the *physical* path, which the union answers from\nthe global root accessor, and the logical-path row read a path that was added\nto the store rather than one under the evaluator's own root.\n\n**What the holder is not.** Every cache of `EvalState` that could hold a\n`SourcePath` -- `srcToStore`, `importResolutionCache`, `fileEvalCache`,\n`inputCache` -- is a `make_ref` in the constructor initialiser list\n(`src/libexpr/eval.cc:302-306`), so each dies with the state. There is no\nglobal registry of accessors in either version. `nanopynix-bindings` holds no\naccessor of its own.\n\n**The hypothesis that fits.** A `shared_ptr<SourceAccessor>` copy that ends up\ninside a Boehm-allocated object is never released, because Boehm runs no\ndestructor. One such object per evaluator gives exactly one permanent extra\nreference per evaluator, no Python owner, no global registry, and nothing at\nall when no path is evaluated. It also predicts that a Boehm collection does\nnot help, which matches the `gc.collect()` result in comment 1.\n\nThat prediction is testable with what this repository already builds:\n`nanopynixVersions.nix_2_34-nogc`. The same reproducer against a Nix built\nwithout Boehm answers it -- if `storeFS_after` is 0 there, the holder is a\nBoehm-allocated object and the fix is to stop putting a `shared_ptr` in one.\n\nProbes: `fdcmd.py` (the command layer) and `fdbare.py` (the four rows above).\nThe `~PyEvalState` instrumentation is temporary and is not committed.\n"}
{"issue": 317, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPHhxQ", "before": "Agents committed build output and agent-workflow notes to this repository.\nCommit `6bbbecf6` closes the hole that admitted the most recent batch. This\nissue holds the part that is not done: the same files are still in the history\nthat `origin` carries.\n\nScanned on 2026-08-25, over every reachable ref.\n\n## The junk splits into two populations\n\n**Population 1 reaches no remote.** jj's ~5874 `refs/jj/keep/*` refs and its\n~5566-operation log pin about 1.9 MiB that no bookmark has ever carried:\n`.jjconflict-side-N/` and `.jjconflict-base-N/` trees in 133 commits,\n`nanopylix-bindings/build/` CMake and ninja output including nanobind `.o`\nfiles, `docs/_build/`, `.test-runs/`, `.direnv/`, `__pycache__/`, and root\nfiles named `nothing`, `np-link` and `JJ-CONFLICT-README`.\n\nA plain `jj git clone` drops all of it, because a clone takes bookmarks and\nnot keep-refs: `.git` goes from 18M to 9.5M and `.jj` from 114M to 316K.\n`git push` never sends any of it. **This population is out of scope.** Local\nsize does not matter.\n\n**Population 2 is in branch history, so `origin` has it.** That is the work\nhere.\n\n## The denylist\n\nVerified against all 13 branches:\n\n```\ncoverage.xml\njunit.xml\ngc-thread-debug.log\ntest-gdb-output.log\nnanopynix-test-store-paths.txt\nglob:tsan-output-*.log\nglob:soak-*.json\ndag_error.txt\nqueries_error.txt\ntest_error.txt\ntodo/\nai/\nresearch/\nnotes/\nplans/\n.opencode/\n.gemini/\nprogress.md\nrecurring.md\nopencode.json\nREASONIX.md\nGEMINI.md\n```\n\nThe first ten are run artifacts. `ci/steps.nix` writes them into the\nrepository root at lines 257, 276 and 325, CI uploads each one as an artefact,\nand nothing reads one back. The rest are the agent-workflow notes that nixidae/nixidae#166\nremoved from the tree.\n\n`notes/` matches at the root only, so `pynixd/docs/notes/` survives. This was\nchecked.\n\n## Use git-filter-repo, not jj\n\n**The jj recipe does not work here.** `jj squash --from <deleting commit>\n--into <adding commit> <paths>` cancels one add against one delete. It works\non a toy history. On this repository it left **419 conflicted commits** and the\njunk was still present, because a file with two add/delete windows, or a\ndirectory edited over four months, does not have that shape.\n\n`git-filter-repo --paths-from-file <list> --invert-paths` over a fresh clone\nof the 13 branches was verified exact:\n\n- 140 paths removed, **every one on the denylist, zero collateral**\n- **zero files changed or added** on any of the 13 branch tips\n- pack 13.56 MiB to 8.60 MiB\n- 13 commits pruned from `develop`, each one bookkeeping whose whole content\n  was agent notes: \"chore: move completed todos to completed/\n  subdirectories\", \"ai plan\", \"recurring AI tasks\", \"chore(repo): remove the\n  todo folder, which GitHub issues replaced\"\n\nThe live tree also loses `REASONIX.md`, a root symlink to `AGENTS.md` that\nnothing reads. One line of `AGENTS.md` names it.\n\n## What a rewrite costs\n\n| | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| signed commits on `develop`, of 300 | 87 | 0 |\n| jj `change-id` headers, of 200 | 197 | 0 |\n\nBoth losses are unavoidable, and no tool avoids them: the commit content\nchanges, so the hash changes, and a signature over the old hash cannot\nsurvive. `change-id` is a commit header, and `git fast-export` drops it.\n\nAll 13 branches get new SHAs, so the push is a force-push. There were no open\nPRs. The six jj workspaces need re-pointing.\n\nFour of this repository's own commit SHAs are cited as evidence and go stale:\n\n- `99f74d82` in `ci/steps.nix:231` and `ci/steps.nix:237`\n- `9b28f36d`, `0349648a` and `7ec0114a` in `docs/collector-and-threads.md`\n\nThe SHAs in `default.nix` and in `pynixd/docs/notes/reentrancy.md` name\nnixpkgs and Nix upstream, so a rewrite does not touch them.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Decide whether to rewrite, given the cost of the signatures and the\n      change-ids.\n- [ ] If yes: run `git-filter-repo` with the denylist above, and check that\n      the removed set equals the denylist and that no branch tip gains or\n      changes a file.\n- [ ] Force-push the branches that the decision covers.\n- [ ] Correct the four stale SHA references above, or add a note that says\n      they name the history before the rewrite.\n- [ ] Re-point or recreate the six jj workspaces.\n- [ ] Add a self-check in `tests/meta/` that fails when a tracked file matches\n      the denylist, and that keeps `.gitignore` and `nix/clean-source.nix` in\n      agreement. Prose did not stop the last batch, and `AGENTS.md` already\n      says a convention a machine can check belongs there.\n\n## Notes for whoever picks this up\n\nBuild the path inventory with `git log --all -m --pretty=format: --name-only`.\nWithout `-m` it skips merge commits and misses 848 paths, because this\nrepository is a merge of two histories. `git rev-list --objects` is not a\nsubstitute: it dedupes by blob, so a path whose content matches another path\ndoes not appear.\n"}
{"issue": 320, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPH1Bg", "before": "## What is wrong\n\n`pynix search` cannot find a package that lives in a package set.\n`python3Packages.requests`, `haskellPackages.aeson`, `nodePackages.typescript`\nand `perlPackages.*` are all absent, and a reader who searches for one gets\nnothing.\n\n`pynix/src/pynix/_packages.py` already states the limitation, at the end of its\nmodule docstring. Nothing tracked it, so this issue does.\n\n## The measurement\n\nTwo package caches on one developer machine, `~/.cache/pynix/packages/`:\n\n| nixpkgs | packages | with a dotted attribute |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `6xivzi43\u2026-source` | 24 571 | 0 |\n| `ykxf7hip\u2026-source` | 24 981 | 0 |\n\nNot one record in either cache names an attribute below the top level. Hydra\npublishes 148 251 packages for one release, and the docstring of that module\nsays the nested sets are most of the difference.\n\n## Why\n\n`_COLLECT_PACKAGE_METADATA` is one Nix function, and its last line is:\n\n```nix\nbuiltins.filter (x: x != null) (lib.mapAttrsToList entry pkgs)\n```\n\n`entry` returns `null` for anything `lib.isDerivation` rejects, and a package\nset is not a derivation. So the walk visits `pkgs` once, keeps the derivations\nit finds there, and drops every set without looking inside it.\n\n`nix search` does look inside. Nix walks a set that carries\n`recurseForDerivations`, which is the marker nixpkgs puts on exactly the sets\nthis issue is about.\n\n## What makes it more than a one-line change\n\n**The cost.** The top-level walk alone is 17.6 s and 2.08 GB at peak, measured\non nixpkgs unstable and recorded in the module docstring. Six times the records\nis not six times the cost -- evaluating `python3Packages` forces a great deal\nthat the top level never touches -- so the first thing this issue needs is that\nnumber, before any decision about what to keep.\n\n**Where to stop.** `recurseForDerivations` is the answer nixpkgs itself gives,\nand following it is what `nix search` does. Depth still needs a rule:\n`pkgsCross`, `pkgsStatic` and `pkgsi686Linux` each carry a whole nixpkgs, and a\nwalk that enters them evaluates the tree many times over.\n\n**The cache.** A walk that costs minutes rather than seconds changes what the\ncache is for. `~/.cache/pynix/packages/<hash>-source.json` is keyed by the\nstore path of the nixpkgs, so it is already correct across versions; what\nchanges is that a cold cache stops being something a reader waits through.\n\n**The ranking.** `_package_search.py` ranks on `record.attr` and\n`record.pname`. `PackageRecord.attr` is documented as \"the attribute path under\nthe top level\", so a dotted value fits the field. What needs thought is that\n`python3Packages.requests` and `python312Packages.requests` are the same\npackage twice, and a reader who searches `requests` should not get one screen\nof aliases.\n\n**The join with issue nixidae/nixidae#295.** That issue adds `programs.sqlite` as a second\nsource, and `_package_search.py` joins it on `record.attr`. Hydra keys that\nfile by the same attribute path, so the join should still hold -- worth\nconfirming rather than assuming.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- `pynix search requests` finds `python3Packages.requests`.\n- The walk enters a set that carries `recurseForDerivations`, and no other.\n- The rule that bounds the depth is written down, with the sets it excludes and\n  why.\n- The cost of a cold walk is measured and recorded beside the existing 17.6 s\n  and 2.08 GB, so a reader can see what the change bought and what it cost.\n- A reader searching a name that many package sets carry gets a readable\n  screen, and the rule that makes it readable is stated.\n"}
{"issue": 323, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPII0w", "before": "## What happens\n\nA pytest process that runs `pynix/tests` **before** `nanopynix/tests` trips the\nsetting-leak guard of nixidae/nixidae#321:\n\n```\nthis test left a Nix setting behind in the pytest process, where every test\nafter it reads the value. Mark it `@pytest.mark.forked`, ... or put the\nsetting back.\n  substituters: 'https://cache.nixos.org/' -> 'daemon https://cache.nixos.org'\n```\n\nThe guard names\n`nanopynix/tests/bindings/test_store_empty_path.py::test_empty_path_raises_instead_of_aborting[local-add_perm_root]`,\nwhich is the first test whose own before/after differ. That test creates a GC\nroot and has nothing to do with substituters, so the name is where the change\nbecame visible and not where it came from.\n\n## Three runs that locate it\n\nMeasured on `develop` at `6a9c4b49`, same tree each time:\n\n| order | result |\n| --- | --- |\n| `pynix/tests/test_copy.py nanopynix/tests/bindings/test_store_empty_path.py` | **1 error**, the guard above |\n| `nanopynix/tests/bindings/test_store_empty_path.py pynix/tests/test_copy.py` | 35 passed |\n| `pynix/tests/test_columns.py nanopynix/tests/bindings/test_store_empty_path.py` | 38 passed |\n\nSo the direction is pynix -> nanopynix, and the pynix side has to open a\nsession or a store for it to happen. `test_columns.py` opens neither.\n\n`test_copy.py` is not special. This started as a diagnosis of\n`pynix/tests/test_registry.py`, and swapping in `test_copy.py` reproduced it\nunchanged, which is what proved the registry work was not the cause.\n\n## Why CI is green\n\n`pytest.ini` line 15:\n\n```\ntestpaths = tests nanopynix/tests nanopynix-helpers/tests libpynix/tests pynix/tests pynix-lsp/tests\n```\n\n`nanopynix/tests` comes before `pynix/tests`, so a repository run always takes\nthe order that passes. The fragility is real and the roster hides it.\n\n## Why it is worth fixing rather than documenting\n\nA mixed selection by hand is the normal way to check two files after a change,\nand this makes such a run report a failure that a full run does not. The\nreport also names an innocent test, so the first reading of it is a wrong\nlead. That cost a diagnosis once already.\n\n## What is not known\n\n**Which pynix fixture writes the value.** The three runs above locate the\nside and not the line. `'daemon https://cache.nixos.org'` is a store URI\nprepended to the substituter list, so the candidates are the shared session of\n`pynix/tests/_shared_sessions.py` and the store fixtures of\n`nanopynix_testing`. I have not read either far enough to say."}
{"issue": 332, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPJGFg", "before": "## What happens\n\n`test-asan-git` fails one test on Nix 2.36:\n\n```\nFAILED nanopynix/tests/test_scalar_accessor_semantics.py::test_deep_conversion_of_a_true_cycle_raises_instead_of_crashing[local]\n==13933==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: SEGV on unknown address 0x03e90000366d\nAddressSanitizer: nested bug in the same thread, aborting.\n= 1 failed, 2461 passed, 30 skipped, 2 deselected, 1 xfailed in 1455.82s\n```\n\n2.34 and 2.35 still pass this test under ASAN.\n\n## The measurement\n\n`ciSteps.git-asan`, on the cyclic value the test builds\n(`let x = { y = x; }; in x`, then `to_python()`).\n\nFirst, `max-call-depth` does not decide it:\n\n| build | 500 | 10000 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 2.36, no sanitizer | raises `max-call-depth` | raises `max-call-depth` |\n| 2.36, ASAN | stack-overflow | stack-overflow |\n\nThe setting reaches the evaluator, which reports `500` back, and it changes\nneither outcome. So the depth of the walk is a property of the value, and\nthe only variable is the size of a frame. ASAN names the frame:\n\n```\nSUMMARY: AddressSanitizer: stack-overflow ../value-to-json.cc:53 in operator()\n```\n\nSecond, and this is the part that decides what to do: **more stack does not\nhelp.** With `NIX_EVALUATOR_STACK_SIZE` raised, on the same value:\n\n| evaluator stack | result |\n| --- | --- |\n| 60 MiB (current) | `stack-overflow ../value-to-json.cc:53` |\n| 200 MiB | `ASan is ignoring requested __asan_handle_no_return` |\n| 400 MiB | `ASan is ignoring requested __asan_handle_no_return` |\n\nThat is the sweep `_nix_executor.py` already records for the two\n`_ASAN_CANNOT_UNWIND` tests of nixidae/nixidae#100: below about 120 MiB the instrumented\nframes exhaust the stack, and at or above it the counter fires and then ASAN\nfails on its own `__asan_handle_no_return`.\n\n## What this means\n\nThis test is not a new defect. It is the third instance of nixidae/nixidae#100, and 2.36\nchanged only **which of the two failures arrives first**. The root cause is\nthat ASAN cannot unwind the `max-call-depth` exception, which is inside\nlibasan and is not a behaviour of Nix or of nanopynix.\n\nAn earlier revision of this issue proposed deriving a larger stack for the\nsanitizer builds. The sweep above refutes that: raising the stack moves the\nfailure rather than removing it, exactly as nixidae/nixidae#100 recorded.\n\n## What this repository does now\n\n`nanopynix/tests/test_scalar_accessor_semantics.py` marks the test with\n`nix_known_issue(exclude=(\"2.36\",), sanitizer=\"asan\")`. Both bounds, because\nthe failure needs both: `known_issue_skip_reason` skips it on 2.36 under\nASAN and runs it on 2.34 under ASAN, on 2.35 under ASAN, and on 2.36 with no\nsanitizer.\n\nThe version bound is deliberate rather than tidy. 2.34 and 2.35 still pass,\nso a bare `sanitizer=\"asan\"` would drop ASAN coverage this repository still\nhas.\n\n## What is left\n\n- The marker matches a version **prefix**, so the day `nixVersions.git`\n  becomes 2.37 the test runs again and fails again. nixidae/nixidae#100 records the same\n  limitation. A marker that can say \"2.36 and later\" retires it, and\n  `tests/meta/` gains the self-check that nixidae/nixidae#100 asks for.\n- The three marked tests come back when libasan can unwind a `longjmp`-free\n  throw on a large thread stack. That is upstream, and worth a link here when\n  someone finds the report."}
{"issue": 332, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWB7fw", "before": "The `nix_known_issue` mark alone was not enough. CI run 33625063861 showed\nthe main ASAN suite green (2461 passed, 0 failed) and then the soak crashed\non the same test.\n\nThe soak roster reads the source marks before a Nix runtime exists, so\n`nix_known_issue` never reaches it. `_another_platform_only` in\n`nanopynix_testing/soak.py` already documents that for `nix_version`,\n`nix_capability`, `nix_sanitizer` and `nix_known_issue` together.\n\nLane 0 started\n`test_deep_conversion_of_a_true_cycle_raises_instead_of_crashing` and never\nfinished it:\n\n```\nAddressSanitizer: SEGV on unknown address 0x03e900003c17\n    nixidae/nixidae#32 operator() ../value-to-json.cc:53\n    nixidae/nixidae#33 ... printValueAsJSON ... eval-inline.hh:232\n```\n\nThe dead process ended all 65 peers of that run. The soak passed on\n`test-local-git`, `test-nogc-git` and `test-ubsan-git`, so the crash is\nASAN-only, which matches the rest of this issue.\n\nThe test now has a `DENYLIST` entry beside the two neighbouring entries of\nthe same module, which state the rule it follows: a test whose failure mode\nis a dead process cannot share a lane on any build."}
{"issue": 333, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPJMvg", "before": "## Correction to the first version of this issue\n\nThe first version of this issue named the nanobind leak report as the\nfailure and called the cause unknown. That was a misreading of the log. The\nleak report is a symptom of the exit path, and the cause is in the two lines\nabove it.\n\n## What happens\n\n`test-darwin-nix_2_34` failed 13 tests in run 33630828303. Ten are the\n`building using a diverted store is not supported on this platform` set that\nalso failed in run 32977383269 on 2026-08-26. One is nixidae/nixidae#243. The two below are\nnew since that run:\n\n- `pynix/tests/test_develop.py::test_develop_runs_a_command_in_the_environment`\n- `pynix/tests/test_develop.py::test_develop_reports_the_exit_status_of_its_command`\n\nBoth assert on the exit status of a `pynix develop` child. The child's stderr\nholds the answer, in this order:\n\n```\ndrv_path='/nix/store/h79i...-pynix-develop-plain-1-env.drv' event='pynix develop building the environment'\nerror: building using a diverted store is not supported on this platform\nnanobind: leaked 1 instances!\n - leaked instance 0x111321ee8 of type \"nanopynix_bindings.signals.InterruptToken\"\nnanobind: leaked 8 types!\n - leaked type \"nanopynix_bindings.signals.InterruptToken\"\n - leaked type \"nanopynix_bindings.expr.EvalState\"\n ...\nnanobind: leaked 109 functions!\n```\n\nSo these two join the ten. The same macOS limitation stops them, and the\n`BeartypeClawDecorWarning` above it in stderr is noise that the passing runs\ncarry too.\n\n## Why they are new\n\n`c2ba3cd1` moved `get-env.sh` out of `pynix` and took it from the linked Nix\ninstead. The `-env` derivation is content-addressed over that script, so its\npath changed. Whatever let these two tests pass in August no longer applies to\nthe new path. The ten that already failed were never affected by this.\n\n## The second thing, which is real but is not why the test fails\n\n`pynix` exits with an `InterruptToken` instance alive, and that one instance\nholds its type, which holds the module. Hence 8 types and 109 functions in the\nreport. Counts of `nanobind: leaked` in the job logs:\n\n| log | count |\n| --- | --- |\n| `test-darwin-nix_2_34`, run 32977383269 (2026-08-26) | 0 |\n| `test-darwin-nix_2_34`, run 33630828303 (2026-09-02) | 16 |\n| `test-asan-git`, run 33627838658, same tree | 0 |\n\nIt appears on the error path only, so the ten older failures did not print it\nin August and print it now. Worth fixing on its own: an `InterruptToken` should\nnot outlive interpreter shutdown. It is not a reference counting defect that\naffects a successful run.\n\n## Scope\n\n`test-darwin-nix_2_34` carries `continue-on-error: true`, so run 33630828303\nreports success. The three tests named here all pass on Linux on that tree,\nmeasured in the dev shell: 3 passed."}
{"issue": 335, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPJZAQ", "before": "Design, not yet built. Reviewed across two sessions: this one against `ekn`'s\napply machinery, and the solid-kubernetes session against the tree that has\nthe pain and will consume it.\n\n## The constraint everything else follows from\n\n**A secret must never enter Nix.** Nix can write an evaluated string to\n`/nix/store`, which is world-readable and persists -- and `ekn.cacheTo`\npushes that closure to a binary cache. So there is no `builtins.getEnv`, no\nimport-from-derivation, and no reading a file into a Nix string anywhere in\nthis design.\n\n**Nix contributes exactly one thing: a reference.** `ekn.envSeed \"VARNAME\"`\nis a helper that returns a string. Nix does not know what an environment\nvariable is, does not read one, and has no pipeline stage that resolves one.\n\n**Everything else is `ekn` Python, after rendering is finished.** Finding\nreferences, the pre-flight, the existence check, substitution, annotations\nand redaction all happen against a manifest that already exists.\n\n## What this is for\n\nTwo credentials, and only two. Both are **seeds**: the cluster cannot invent\nthem, and once they exist in the cluster the operator throws the copy away.\n\n1. **ArgoCD repository read credentials.** ArgoCD cannot sync anything until\n   it can read the repository.\n2. **External Secrets Operator credentials** for the external secret store.\n   Every other secret in the cluster then lives in that store, or is\n   generated into it.\n\n**The concrete driver.** solid-kubernetes' nixlab2 bootstrapped cleanly --\nsixteen barriers, exit 0, nodes Ready, every workload running -- and could\nnot sync, because ArgoCD had no repository credential. Nothing in the apply\nsaid one was expected. That was found about twelve hours later, by reading\nan Application's status conditions.\n\n## Why not ksops\n\nIts cost is not the encryption. It is `kubernetes.rawFiles`.\n\nA SOPS whole-document MAC does not survive the\n`kubernetes.resources` -> `generated` round trip: Nix alphabetizes keys and\nmodules add sibling fields, and both invalidate the MAC for a consumer that\ncannot skip it. `ekn kubeapply` can skip it (`--ignore-mac`, each field's own\nAES-GCM tag still verifies). ArgoCD's ksops-enabled repo-server cannot. So\nthose objects go in `kubernetes.rawFiles` and are read as bytes.\n\nAn object there is outside the module system. No option touches it. No\nassertion sees it. No transformer reaches it. Every future cluster-wide rule\nhas the same hole.\n\nThe apparatus that buys: a ksops sidecar in argocd-repo-server, an\nin-cluster age identity through `kubernetes.sopsAgeIdentities`, `.sops.yaml`\nrecipient registration, a `sops updatekeys` re-key step, and a kustomize\nbuild. For two seeds.\n\nA value substituted at apply time has no MAC, so a seeded Secret goes back\nthrough the normal pipeline and is key-alphabetized harmlessly.\n`kubernetes.rawFiles` itself stays -- it is plumbed through `gitops.py` and\n`eval.py` as a general escape hatch for anything that must reach git\nbyte-identical.\n\n## The shape: a marked field on an ordinary resource\n\nA seeded object is **mostly ordinary configuration with a credential in one\nfield**. solid-kubernetes' live ArgoCD repository credential is the shape to\ndesign against:\n\n```json\n{\"kind\": \"Secret\",\n \"metadata\": {\"name\": \"repo-creds\", \"namespace\": \"argocd\",\n              \"labels\": {\"argocd.argoproj.io/secret-type\": \"repository\"}},\n \"stringData\": {\"type\": \"...\", \"url\": \"...\", \"username\": \"...\", \"password\": \"...\"}}\n```\n\nFour `stringData` keys, one of them secret, plus a label ArgoCD requires for\ndiscovery. So this must stay an ordinary `kubernetes.resources` Secret with\none marked field. A metadata-only declaration -- \"tell ekn the name,\nnamespace and keys and let it build the Secret\" -- was considered and\nrejected: it re-grows namespaces, labels and non-secret fields, which is\nreinventing Kubernetes resource options.\n\n```nix\nkubernetes.resources.argocd.Secret.repo-creds = {\n  metadata.labels.\"argocd.argoproj.io/secret-type\" = \"repository\";\n  stringData = {\n    type = \"git\";\n    url = \"https://gitlab.example.com/group/repo.git\";\n    username = \"gitlab-ci-token\";\n    password = ekn.envSeed \"SOLID_ARGOCD_REPOCREDENTIALS_PASSWORD\";\n  };\n};\n```\n\nNobody hand-writes these; a module does, and the environment file says\nnothing at all.\n\n### The reference is a string\n\n`ekn.envSeed \"VAR\"` returns `\"$ekn:env:VAR\"`. Not an attrset.\n\nThis matters, and it was measured against easykubenix `4454d03b` rather than\nreasoned. An attrset marker does survive the merge untouched --\n`kubeValueType`'s `objectType` branch accepts any attrset that is not a list\nmarker -- so `{\"__eknEnv\":\"VAR\"}` reaches the manifest intact. But\n`stringData` is `map[string]string`, so that manifest is **schema-invalid**,\nand `ekn validate` pipes `internal.manifestJSONFile` straight into\n`kubeconform` in shell (`easykubenix/validation.nix:189`), outside Python.\nSkipping the object would not have helped: kubeconform reads the file\nwhether or not an object is applied.\n\nA string sentinel is schema-valid, so `kubeconform` passes, the throwaway\napiserver accepts a Secret whose password is the literal `$ekn:env:...`,\nand `bootstrapValidation` keeps working on a machine with no credentials.\nNo stub, no skip list, no flag.\n\nThe sentinel must be unmistakable, since matching is by prefix.\n\n## Two output lists\n\n```\nkubernetes.objects (attrs)  ->  kubernetes.generated\nkubernetes.generated        ->  kubernetes.generatedExportable\n```\n\n- **`generated`** -- every object, and may hold unresolved seed references.\n- **`generatedExportable`** -- `generated` minus any object holding one.\n\n**The rule is who applies it, not what it contains:**\n\n> `generated` is for `ekn`, which can resolve a seed reference.\n> `generatedExportable` is for anything that hands objects to a different\n> applier.\n\nThat is two consumers today: GitOps targets (`ekn commit`, which ArgoCD then\nsyncs) and `kluctl.nix`. Both already have exclusion machinery to hang it\non. `ekn validate` deliberately stays on `generated` -- a string sentinel is\nschema-valid, so there is nothing to exclude.\n\nNaming states the contract. \"Filtered\" does not say filtered of what, and\nsomeone will guess wrong at 2am.\n\n### Which outputs take which list\n\nThe same test splits the manifest outputs, and the line already exists in\nthe tree: `internal.*` is ekn's own plumbing, the `manifest*` attributes in\n`default.nix` are what a user builds and hands to something.\n\n- **`internal.manifestJSONFile` stays on `generated`.** `ekn validate` and\n  `ekn _applyManifest` read it, and `ekn.cachePackage` pushes its closure.\n  All three are ekn or ekn-adjacent, kubeconform passes on a sentinel, and\n  the closure holds variable *names*, never values.\n- **The public outputs come from `generatedExportable`** -- `manifestAttrs`,\n  `manifestJSON`, `manifestJSONFile`, `manifestYAML`, `manifestYAMLFile`,\n  `manifestYAMLList`, `manifestYAMLFileList`, `manifestYAMLDir`. Each exists\n  to be fed to an applier; `manifestYAMLDir` is the clearest case, since a\n  namespace/kind/name.yaml tree has exactly one use. kubectl accepts JSON\n  too, so the JSON outputs get the same treatment as the YAML ones.\n- **`ekn render` needs no change.** It resolves `kubernetes.generated`\n  (`ekn/src/ekn/eval.py:493`), so it already shows the sentinel.\n\nThat last one matters more than it looks. `ekn render` is where somebody\ngoes when a bootstrap misbehaves, and an object that has been filtered out\nis indistinguishable from one that was never declared -- which is the\nnixlab2 failure exactly: not a wrong value, an absent one, found twelve\nhours later. A sentinel holds no secret, so showing it tells the reader\nwhich field is seeded and from which variable. Outputs that feed an applier\ndrop the object; output a person reads keeps it, sentinel visible.\n\nThe two `manifestJSONFile`s then differ in content, which is honest rather\nthan confusing: one is what ekn applies, the other is what you may hand to\nsomething else. Both need to say so.\n\n**Gate the predicate on kind.** Asking each object \"do you hold a seed\nreference\" is a deep walk. Scope is Secrets only, so test that first:\n\n```nix\nlib.filter (o: !(o.kind == \"Secret\" && hasSeedRef o))\n```\n\nThat short-circuits on a string compare for all but a handful of objects.\nWithout it, a 749-object environment pays a full recursive traversal --\nexactly the class of cost issue nixidae/nixidae#334 exists to measure.\n\n**Assert, so nobody has to remember which list.** No object reaching a\nGitOps target or kluctl may hold a seed reference. Wrong-list use then fails\nat evaluation with a message naming the object, rather than ArgoCD quietly\napplying a Secret whose password is the literal `$ekn:env:VAR`. It reuses\nthe same predicate the filter needs, so it is a few lines.\n\nThis is the precedent, and it is exact. `kluctl.nix` already excludes\nobjects it cannot resolve:\n\n> kluctl has no SOPS awareness at all -- it would apply the raw,\n> still-encrypted `sops:` blob as the object's literal content. Any object\n> carrying one is meant for a ksops/`ekn kubeapply`-style decrypt-at-apply\n> path instead [...] so exclude it here unconditionally.\n\nNix testing for a marker it cannot resolve, to route the object away from a\npath that would apply it literally, is established here. That is not Nix\nknowing about environment variables.\n\n## Apply-time behaviour\n\n`ekn` walks `generated` for sentinels, then checks the cluster. Four cases,\nexactly one of which aborts:\n\n| | variable set | variable unset |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **not in cluster** | substitute and create | **abort**, listing every missing variable at once |\n| **in cluster** | compare; equal is a no-op, different substitutes and updates, logging the name and never the value | skip, log that it was left alone |\n\n**\"In cluster + unset\" is the steady state, not an edge case.** A seed is\nexported once, applied once, and dropped. Every apply after the bootstrap\ntakes that cell.\n\n**\"In cluster + set\" is the exception**, and needs no policy beyond honesty.\nComparing makes a variable left exported with the same value silent and\nfree, and a different value a deliberate act that says so in the log. ekn\nalready fetches the object for the existence check, and\n`ekn.sops.ensure_age_identities` already base64-decodes a live Secret key.\n\nAborting on a set variable was considered and dropped. An environment\nvariable must not silently change what a command does -- an argument for\ncomparing and logging, not for refusing.\n\n**Annotations are stamped by Python at apply time**, one per seed:\n`ekn.dev/env-0`, `ekn.dev/env-1`, each naming a variable. Numbered rather\nthan one JSON blob, because a blob is opaque to `kubectl` and to `grep`,\nwhile numbered keys make \"which live resources are seeded, and from what\" a\nscan against the cluster with no re-render. `_with_discriminator_label`\n(`ekn/src/ekn/apply.py:207`) already stamps `ekn.dev/discriminator` exactly\nthis way, so seeds follow a pattern that exists.\n\n## Three things that must be structural\n\n**`kubernetes.crds` needs its own guard, and it is not redundant.** The\nexportable predicate short-circuits on `o.kind == \"Secret\"`, so a\nCustomResourceDefinition never matches it. A reference sitting in\n`kubernetes.crds` is therefore invisible to the exclusion *and* to the\nassertion that rides the same predicate -- and since\n`allGenerated = generatedWithEkn ++ checkedCrds`, it still reaches\n`generated`, and from there a GitOps target, and gets applied literally with\nnothing anywhere having had a chance to notice. **The crds guard is the only\nthing covering the hole the Secret-only scope opens.**\n\nThe two compose cleanly. `checkedCrds` throws while `generated` is being\nbuilt, so a seeded CRD never reaches the assertion at all; the kind-gated\npredicate covers the objects path, and this covers the crds path.\n\nIt is also nearly free. `checkedCrds` already runs `lib.hasMarker` over\nevery CRD on every evaluation, and in the ordinary case -- no marker -- that\nwalk already visits every node. Adding a string test to the same walk is one\nmore comparison per node, not a second pass. Note `hasMarker` scans for\n`_type` and a sentinel is a plain string, so it will not see one today.\n\nThe message should follow `hasMarker`'s: name the CRD, say `kubernetes.crds`\ngoes around the pipeline on purpose, and say a seeded value belongs in a\nSecret in `kubernetes.objects`.\n\nHow it would happen: not by hand. In solid-kubernetes every\n`kubernetes.crds` assignment is `rendered.crds` from a Helm chart. The path\nis somebody putting a reference into a chart's values and the chart\ntemplating it onto the CRD side of the split. Unlikely, not zero, and silent\n-- which is the whole argument.\n\n\n\n**`stringData` only.** A Secret's `data` is base64. Substituting a raw\nvariable there produces garbage that applies cleanly and fails at runtime.\nRestrict the reference to `stringData`, or base64 the value, and say which.\n\n**\"Skip\" must mean skip.** `ssa_apply` (`ekn/src/ekn/apply.py:140`) issues a\nreal `application/apply-patch+yaml` with `force=true` under field manager\n`ekn`. Server-side apply **deletes fields the same manager previously owned\nand then omits**. So \"in cluster + unset\" must drop the object from the\napply set entirely. Implemented as \"apply the manifest with the field\nabsent\", the second `ekn kubeapply` empties the credential. This is the\nsharpest trap in the design, because that cell is the steady state -- get it\nwrong and the credential survives exactly one apply. Applying the rendered\nsentinel over a live seed is equally destructive.\n\n## `ekn clusterdiff`\n\n`cluster_diff` calls\n`ssa_apply(desired_obj, field_manager=field_manager, dry_run=True)`\n(`ekn/src/ekn/clusterdiff.py:125`) and diffs the prediction against live.\nNothing persists, so this destroys nothing -- but the rendered side holds\nthe sentinel and the live side holds the credential, so a seeded Secret\nwould diff dirty forever. A diff tool with a permanent false positive is one\npeople stop reading.\n\n**It follows the same rule the apply does, keyed on the variable:**\n\n- **unset** -- skip. An apply would not update or remove it either, so there\n  is nothing to report.\n- **set** -- substitute, then diff. An apply *would* act, so the diff should\n  say so.\n\nThis looks like the `SOLID_BOOTSTRAP` smell -- a command's behaviour\ndepending on an exported variable -- and is not. `clusterdiff` answers \"what\nwould this apply change\", and the apply's own behaviour depends on the\nvariable, so tracking it is what makes the answer true rather than what\nmakes it unpredictable.\n\n**Redaction keys on the seeded field path, not on \"this is a Secret.\"** It\nis the *predicted* side that leaks: live Secrets come back base64-encoded in\n`data`, but the predicted object holds the substituted plaintext, and\n`_dump(predicted)` would render it to the terminal. Report that the field\ndiffers; never either side's value.\n\n## UX requirements from the consuming tree\n\n**Variable names are the option path, uppercased** -- derived by convention,\noverridable. `SOLID_ARGOCD_REPOCREDENTIALS_PASSWORD` is ugly, but it is a\nvariable an operator exports twice in a cluster's life, so ugly is free and\nirreversible is not. Two clusters needing the same token then name it the\nsame variable without coordinating.\n\n**The error carries the variable name and where it lands.** With that\nconvention the variable name *is* the breadcrumb back to the option:\n\n```\nSOLID_ARGOCD_REPOCREDENTIALS_PASSWORD is not set\n(argocd/repo-creds, stringData.password)\n```\n\nEvery missing variable at once. Bootstrapping a cluster with two seeds must\nnot be two failed applies. An author who overrides the name to something\nprettier accepts a less self-explaining error, knowingly.\n\n**`ekn secrets`: ask what a cluster needs before touching it.** Read-only,\nlisting variable name, target object and field, and -- when a kubeconfig is\nreachable -- whether the object already exists. That turns \"bring up a new\ncluster\" into a checklist readable while the VMs boot, and it is what makes\ndropping the variable a decision rather than a hope, when it answers \"in\ncluster, no variable needed\".\n\n**It must see into a nested bootstrap instance.** The credential is needed by\nthe instance hardest to introspect: ArgoCD lives in\n`gitOps.units.bootstrap.modules`, a separate easykubenix instance whose\nobjects are deliberately absent from the parent's `generated`. Anything\nenumerating seeds has to walk `gitOpsTargets.<name>.instance.config` too, or\nit reports zero seeds for the one environment that has them.\n\n**Report what landed.** Per seed after an apply: created / updated /\nunchanged / left alone. Names only, never values.\n\n**No new file, directory convention, or required step.** The entire win over\nksops is that bootstrapping becomes\n`SOLID_ARGOCD_REPOCREDENTIALS_PASSWORD=... ekn kubeapply --target bootstrap`\nand nothing else. `ekn secrets` as a read-only question is fine; as a\nrequired step it is ksops with different letters.\n\n## Scope notes\n\n**Secrets only.** Both seeds are Secrets. \"Any resource\" is speculative\ngenerality; there is no non-Secret case, and the kind gate above depends on\nthis.\n\n**Which apply path.** `ekn kubeapply --target bootstrap`. ArgoCD is created\nin that apply, so credential 1 has to land in the same one, before its first\nsync.\n\n**Pruning.** `prune=False` is already the `ekn kubeapply` default against a\nreal cluster, so \"never pruned\" is nearly free. `ensure_age_identities`\nalready shows the mechanism: it applies through `apply_one` directly and\nskips discriminator labelling, and the prune scan selects on that label. It\nmatters if anyone passes `--prune`, where the scan is cluster-wide\n(`namespace=kr8s.ALL`).\n\n**ekn is ours.** It is our own Python tool and may do any reasonable work at\napply time. Nothing here should be shaped by what ksops or kustomize would\nhave forced.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- \"Exists\" is not \"correct\". A half-finished earlier run leaves a Secret with\n  an empty key, and an existence check locks that in silently. Check the\n  named keys are non-empty, and treat a live value equal to the sentinel as\n  absent?\n- Allow a command as well as a variable\n  (`ekn.envSeedCommand \"pass show argocd/repo\"`)? Same pre-flight, same seed\n  property, one extra branch -- and the operator never exports plaintext\n  into a process environment, which is readable through `/proc` by the same\n  user and lands in shell history when typed.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- **External Secrets Operator.** Not an alternative -- it is the\n  destination. Credential 2 exists to seed ESO, and every other secret then\n  lives in the external store. ESO cannot solve its own bootstrap: its\n  SecretStore needs a credential, which is what this provides.\n- **A metadata-only declaration, with ekn building the Secret.** Rejected on\n  the repo-credential shape above: it reinvents Kubernetes resource options.\n- **A separate `kubernetes.seeds` option holding whole objects**, parallel to\n  `kubernetes.crds`. Gets apply-only by construction, but splits a module's\n  objects across two options. The exportable list plus an assertion recovers\n  the same guarantee while seeds stay ordinary resources.\n- **`builtins.getEnv` to filter objects by whether a variable is set.**\n  Presence-only, so no secret enters the store -- but pure evaluation always\n  returns `\"\"`, so behaviour differs between `nix build`, flakes and\n  `--impure`, and `manifestJSONFile`'s hash would depend on the environment,\n  poisoning the cache `ekn.cacheTo` pushes to.\n- **sops with the age key handed in by environment.** Keeps ciphertext in the\n  repository, for strictly more moving parts.\n- **A `kubectl create secret` bootstrap script.** Outside the module system,\n  which is the cost this issue opens with.\n\n## Revision history\n\n- **Scope came from a census; it should have come from intent.** The SOPS\n  files in solid-kubernetes' nixlab tree are compatibility with the non-Nix\n  architecture in that repo's `is/` tree -- legacy, not a pattern to size\n  against.\n- **Rotation was never the use case.** A seed is thrown away once it is in\n  the cluster, so the unset column is the normal steady state.\n- **A hand-written object annotation became a value marker**, which surfaced\n  the `ekn validate` problem.\n- **The Nix render pass was removed entirely.** An earlier revision had Nix\n  walking objects to rewrite markers and derive annotations; two sessions\n  then argued about where in the pipeline it should sit. Wrong axis: Nix\n  produces a reference and nothing else, and Python does the rest after\n  rendering. The annotations moved to apply time, where the discriminator\n  label already lives.\n- **The reference became a string**, which is what makes the manifest\n  schema-valid and keeps `kubeconform` and `bootstrapValidation` working\n  untouched.\n- **Two derived output lists** replaced hand-maintained exclusions, with an\n  assertion so the choice cannot be got wrong silently.\n- **The manifest outputs were split** by whether an applier or a person is\n  on the other end, and the `kubernetes.crds` guard was added -- not as\n  insurance, but because the kind-gated predicate makes a CRD invisible to\n  both the exclusion and the assertion.\n"}
{"issue": 344, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPKU6Q", "before": "nixkube has three tiers of test and no written rule about which belong on a\nGitHub-hosted runner. Worth stating, because the answer is currently implicit\nin where each one happens to be wired.\n\nToday:\n\n| suite | where | runs on GHA? |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `checks.all` | `checks.nix` | yes, via `check` |\n| `test-kind-cache` / `test-kind-nocache` | `ci.yaml` | yes |\n| `tests/nixos/integration.nix` | `test-nixos.yaml` | only on `cidev`, `continue-on-error: true` |\n| `nix/kubeapply` (User-Mode Linux) | `checks.nix`, outside `checks.all` | no |\n| `nix/uml/*` | \u2014 | no |\n\nSo the User-Mode Linux tests are already out, and the NixOS test is on a\nbranch nobody pushes with its failures ignored. That is a policy nobody wrote\ndown, which means the next person to add a heavy test has nothing to follow.\n\n## What to decide\n\n- A GHA-hosted runner is 2 cores and a 6-hour cap. Booting a control plane\n  under UML is minutes of CPU per run and was deliberately kept out of\n  `checks.all` for that reason (see `nix/kubeapply/default.nix`'s header).\n  Keep it out of GHA too, and say so where someone will read it.\n- `test-nixos.yaml` being `continue-on-error: true` on a branch that is never\n  pushed means it is not a gate. Either make it one somewhere it can pass, or\n  delete it \u2014 a workflow that cannot fail teaches a reader that it is\n  covered when it is not.\n- Name where the excluded suites *do* run, so \"not in CI\" does not read as\n  \"not run\". Right now that is a person's machine.\n\nNot urgent, and not a blocker for nixidae/nixidae#339. Worth doing while the CI is being\nfreshened up rather than after, since the answer shapes what the workflows\nshould contain."}
{"issue": 347, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPKoCw", "before": "`pytest-agent/` is a pytest plugin: minimal terminal noise, full per-test\ndetail written to `.pytest-agent/`. 34 modules, ~8900 lines, its own\n`pyproject.toml`, `LICENSE`, `README.md`, `SKILL.md`, `TODO.md` and `tests/`.\nIts only dependencies are `pytest>=8` and `pyinstrument` \u2014 nothing from\nnanopynix.\n\n## Why it is the one with users outside this repo\n\nUnlike the other two extractions, this one is already in use across the\numbrella. easykubenix's test runs go through it, and its guard against piped\noutput shows up in every session:\n\n```\n[pytest-agent] auto-activated: found CLAUDECODE in the environment\n[pytest-agent] run 97, pid ...: writing full per-test detail to: .pytest-agent/runs-0097\n[pytest-agent] done in 61.7s -- 353 passed, 0 failed, 0 error, 0 skipped\n```\n\nEach consumer currently reaches it through whatever path it has to nanopynix,\nwhich makes a plugin about running tests look like a dependency on a Nix\nevaluation library.\n\nIt is also the most plausibly *general* of the three: nothing in it is about\nNix, Python-in-Nix, or this umbrella. It is the piece most likely to be useful\nto someone who has never heard of nanopynix.\n\n## Shape\n\n- new repo, keep `src/pytest_agent`, its tests, `SKILL.md` and `TODO.md`\n- `nix/sources.nix` in the umbrella gains an entry, and every consumer takes\n  it from there\n- `requires-python = \">=3.10\"` here is already lower than nanopynix's floor,\n  which is a sign it was written to stand alone\n- decide whether it goes to PyPI. It is a pytest plugin, so `pip install\n  pytest-agent` is how most people would want it, and that is a different\n  commitment from a Nix source entry\n\nSibling extractions: ghanix (Lillecarl/nanopynix#296) and grpclib-transports\n(#297). Order: ghanix first \u2014 smallest, and nixkube is already waiting for\nit \u2014 then this, then grpclib-transports."}
{"issue": 351, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPLEsQ", "before": "`umlTest` runs a kubeadm control plane, and kubeadm runs its kubelet in the\nhost mount namespace. Issue nixidae/nixidae#349 was a bug that only appears when the kubelet\nhas its own mount namespace, and `umlTest` passed throughout \u2014 not because the\nmanifests were right, but because no test with a host-namespace kubelet can\nobserve that class at all.\n\nMeasured on Talos v1.13.9 by the solid-kubernetes session:\n\n    host pid 1  mnt:[4026531832]   /var/lib/nix-csi/nix/store -> 242 entries\n    kubelet     mnt:[4026532779]   /var/lib/nix-csi/nix/store ->   0 entries\n\nThe kubelet performs a `subPath` bind itself, in its own namespace. Anything\nit cannot see there, it binds as an empty directory.\n\n## What would catch it\n\nRunning the UML kubelet in its own mount namespace, with only\n`/var/lib/kubelet` bind-mounted in \u2014 which is the shape Talos, k3s in a\ncontainer, and any other distribution that containerises the kubelet all\nshare. That single difference should reproduce nixidae/nixidae#349 as it stood.\n\nThis is a harness change, not a manifest change, and it is not small. Filing\nit so the gap is recorded rather than rediscovered.\n\n## The cheap partial, worth doing first\n\nAn eval-time assertion that no `volumeMount` with a `subPath` refers to a\n`hostPath` volume. That encodes the distinction the measurement drew:\n\n- **hostPath + subPath** needs the kubelet to see the host tree. Fragile.\n- **CSI + subPath** resolves under `/var/lib/kubelet`, which a containerised\n  kubelet must have or it cannot function. Fine.\n\nIt would have failed nixidae/nixidae#349 at eval time, on any machine, in a second. It does\nnot replace the harness work, because it only catches the shape we already\nknow about."}
{"issue": 356, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPLnNQ", "before": "`build-amd64` and `build-arm64` both stop dead on the same derivation and print\nnothing until `timeout-minutes` kills them. Measured on run 34477979957, commit\n`5603baa6`:\n\n| job | last line | cancelled | silent for |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| build-amd64 | 12:41:59 | 13:26:24 | 44 min |\n| build-arm64 | 12:41:54 | 13:42:21 | 60 min |\n\n## It is the nri-wait build\n\nThe last thing each builder announced is `nri-wait-0.1.0.drv`:\n`28a1pz6akwrdi5qn4jk420pv52mx4zzi` on x86_64,\n`mdh9rb0qp2hyx5vqnjvdq9h9a02w5x49` on aarch64.\n\narm64 settles it. That job runs `--max-jobs 1`, so nix can build one thing at a\ntime, and `building '<nri-wait>.drv'...` is the **very last line of the step** --\nno `copying path` after it, nothing in flight. nix was doing exactly one thing\nfor sixty minutes.\n\namd64 emitted four more `copying path` lines within 1.3 seconds and then also\nwent quiet.\n\n## What it is not\n\n**Not a stalled substituter.** nix's default `stalled-download-timeout` is 300s.\nNothing failed in 44 minutes, so the downloads that were in flight finished.\n\n**Not the package.** The identical derivation\n`28a1pz6akwrdi5qn4jk420pv52mx4zzi` builds outside GHA in six seconds:\n\n    nri-wait> ============================== 4 passed in 6.03s ===============================\n\nSo the cause is in the runner environment, and it is still unknown. Every test\nin `pkgs/nri-wait/tests/test_wait.py` waits on a ZeroMQ `ipc://` socket, which\nis the obvious suspect, but `wait_for_completion` closes its socket on every\nexit path and the tests do not leak a context.\n\n## Why nothing said so\n\nNone of the four `nix build` steps in the two build jobs passed\n`--print-build-logs`, so `building '<drv>'...` was all either job could give.\nIt names a derivation and no phase.\n\n## Landed so far\n\n- `7a615af1` adds `--print-build-logs` to the four build steps, so the next\n  occurrence names the phase.\n- `0644336a` adds `pytest-timeout` to nri-wait with `--timeout=60`. Every wait\n  in that suite is bounded by `TIMEOUT=1`, so 60s is a hundredfold margin and\n  reaching it means stuck rather than slow. A hang then fails with the stack of\n  the thread that stopped, instead of burning the job timeout.\n\nBoth commit messages say \"See nixidae/nixidae#354\" by mistake. nixidae/nixidae#354 is closed and is about the\npush uploading 5 GiB; this is a different failure. This issue is the right\nreference."}
{"issue": 356, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWE8fw", "before": "## Root cause found and fixed\n\nReproduced locally, with the exact error:\n\n    zmq.error.ZMQError: ipc path \"<...>/pytest-of-runner/pytest-0/\n    test_silence_fails0/wait-pub.sock\" is longer than 107 characters\n    (sizeof(sockaddr_un.sun_path))\n\nThe chain:\n\n1. `.github/actions/setup-nix` uses `nixbuild/nix-quick-install-action`, which\n   installs nix **single-user with no daemon**. A build therefore runs under the\n   runner's TMPDIR rather than in the daemon's `/build`. Measured, the same\n   socket path is **77 bytes sandboxed and 125 bytes on the runner**.\n2. `socket.bind()` raises, because 125 is over the 107 a unix socket path holds.\n3. It raises inside the `pub` fixture **before its yield**, so pytest runs no\n   teardown and the PUB socket and its `zmq.Context` leak.\n4. `zmq_ctx_term` blocks for ever on a context whose sockets are still open.\n   pytest had already printed `1 error in 0.03s`; the interpreter then never\n   exits, and the job sits there until `timeout-minutes` kills it.\n\nThat is why it looked like a hang with no output: the tests had finished and\nreported before the process wedged.\n\n## What was ruled out on the way\n\n| variable | verdict | how |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| architecture | not it | aarch64 builds and passes locally under qemu; both arches hung on GHA |\n| derivation content | not it | identical drv hashes local and CI |\n| stalled substituter | not it | nix's 300s `stalled-download-timeout` never fired in 44 min |\n| flaky race | not it | 40/40 clean runs locally |\n\n## Fixed in 7de0bf26\n\nThe socket now goes in a short directory of its own instead of under pytest's\n`tmp_path`, with `/tmp` as the fallback for a TMPDIR too long to hold one at\nall. Every context is closed with `destroy(linger=0)` from a `finally`, so a\nfailure here is a failure and never a hang.\n\nVerified at the exact GHA path length, at 143 bytes, and on both architectures.\n\n## Note on the earlier commits\n\n`pytest-timeout` (0644336a) does **not** catch this \u2014 the hang is after pytest\nreports. It stays as a guard against a test that hangs the ordinary way.\n`--print-build-logs` (7a615af1) is still worth having. Both of those commits\nreference nixidae/nixidae#354 by mistake; this issue is the right one.\n\n## Still worth considering\n\nSwitching to `cachix/install-nix-action` would restore the daemon and the\nsandbox. That would have prevented this class of failure outright, and it makes\nCI builds hermetic rather than sensitive to the runner's filesystem layout."}
{"issue": 357, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPLt-A", "before": "Design, not yet built. Investigated across one session: the `ekn` apply\nmachinery, the bootstrap nested-instance pattern, and what terranix and\nOpenTofu actually provide.\n\n## What this is for\n\nA cluster's life is three stages, and today only the middle one has a home:\n\n1. **Infra tofu** provisions the cluster (cloud provider, k3s/eks/talos\n   resources). Its outputs: a kubeconfig, and cluster facts (endpoint,\n   OIDC issuer URL, subnets).\n2. **easykubenix** applies the Kubernetes configuration against that\n   kubeconfig.\n3. **App tofu** targets applications *inside* the cluster: a Keycloak\n   realm via the keycloak provider, a Grafana org via the grafana\n   provider. It reads state from the cluster itself -- client secrets an\n   ExternalSecret synced in, an admin password -- through tofu data\n   sources, and needs the same kubeconfig to do it.\n\nThree states, three applies, one pipeline. The ask: easykubenix and `ekn`\nown all three, so the shared facts live once and the artifacts (first of\nall the kubeconfig) move themselves.\n\n## The constraints everything else follows from\n\n**A runtime value must never enter Nix.** Same rule as nixidae/nixidae#335, one layer\nup. Tofu outputs are known only after apply; a kubeconfig is a\ncredential. Neither reaches `/nix/store`. Nix contributes structure and\nnames; `ekn` resolves values after rendering, against state that already\nexists.\n\n**Tofu state must never enter Nix either.** State is mutable, locked,\nand machine-local. Nix holds generated `config.tf.json` and nothing\nelse. A tofu workspace (state, lock file, `.terraform/`) lives outside\nthe store, default `.ekn/tofu/<stage>/`, overridable per stage.\n\n**Each tofu stage is its own tofu state.** Two stages must never merge\ninto one config or one workspace -- a provider set per stage, a lock\nfile per stage, a `plan`/`apply` per stage. This is what forces the\nmodule-system shape below.\n\n## Module-system shape: one entry point, nested per-stage evals\n\nThe open question was separate `evalModules` invocations per stage, or\none `evalModules` with submodules for tofu -> kube -> tofu. Answer: one\n*entry point*, separate *evals* under it -- which is what\n`deployment.units.<name>.modules` already is, so the pattern exists.\n\n```nix\nterranix.stages.cluster = {\n  modules = [ ./infra/hcloud.nix ];   # produces config.tf.json\n};\nterranix.stages.apps = {\n  modules = [ ./apps/keycloak.nix ];\n  needs.kubeconfig = \"cluster\";\n};\n```\n\n- `terranix.stages.<name>` is an `attrsOf submodule` on the outer tree.\n  Each stage evaluates as its **own** module-system invocation into a\n  freeform Terraform-JSON attrset. Freeform because provider resources\n  are untypable from here, and separate because of the state rule.\n- The stage option tree speaks Terraform JSON: `resource`, `data`,\n  `provider`, `variable`, `output`, `locals`, `terraform`, `module`,\n  `import`, `moved`, `removed`, `ephemeral`. That is terranix's schema\n  convention, so **upstream terranix modules import as-is**.\n- Shared cluster facts -- subnetting, OIDC, names -- are ordinary\n  options on the outer tree, declared once and read by all three sides\n  through the module system. Stage modules see the outer config the way\n  a bootstrap unit sees `parent`. Same rule as there: read the parent's\n  *inputs*, never its rendered outputs.\n- The Kubernetes side reads the same options. One entry point means a\n  mismatch (service CIDR declared differently in tofu and consumed\n  differently in manifests) is a module-system error at eval time, not\n  a drifted runtime discovered at apply time. Separate top-level evals\n  reduce that to discipline.\n\n### Why not the alternatives\n\n**One flat evalModules** where tofu resource attrs merge into the\n`kubernetes` tree: two tofu stages would merge into each other's\n`config.tf.json`, and provider keys would collide with kind names. The\nstate rule forbids it.\n\n**Three independent evals** (terranix CLI for the tofu stages,\neasykubenix for the middle) glued by shell: it is exactly the artifact\njuggling this exists to remove, and the shared facts are consistent only\nbecause someone remembers to be consistent.\n\n**Depends-on-upstream-terranix as a library**: terranix is GPL-3.0; this\nrepository is Apache-2.0. The core being reused is small -- an\n`evalModules` over ~12 top-level options, a null-stripping sanitize, a\nwhitelist of Terraform's JSON keys, roughly 60 lines -- and the schema\nit speaks is Terraform's, not terranix's. Ship our own core under\nApache-2.0, byte-compatible in output, and upstream modules still work\nas plain imports.\n\n## The handoffs\n\n### kubeconfig: tofu output -> kubeapply\n\nContract: a stage declares what it hands over, not how the next stage\nfetches it.\n\n```nix\nterranix.stages.cluster = {\n  modules = [ ./infra/hcloud.nix ];\n  provides.kubeconfig = \"kubeconfig\";  # name of a tofu output; default\n};\n```\n\nAfter `ekn tofu apply --stage cluster`, `ekn` runs `tofu output -json`,\nextracts that output, parses it as a kubeconfig (fail fast if the\nprovider emitted something else), writes it to a runtime file, mode\n0600, outside the store, and remembers it for this run.\n\n`ekn kubeapply` gains `--kubeconfig <file>` (or picks up the remembered\none); kr8s already takes a kubeconfig path and `validation.py` already\nshows the pattern of pointing it at a file. `KUBECONFIG` env and the\nambient context keep working for clusters tofu did not provision --\nthe plumbing is additive.\n\nA pre-flight before kubeapply: parse the kubeconfig, one version ping\nwith a short backoff. A tofu apply that returns while the control plane\nis still coming up should retry for seconds, not fail the pipeline on a\nrace.\n\n### app tofu: kubeconfig -> tofu variables, cluster reads stay in tofu\n\nA stage that `needs.kubeconfig = \"cluster\"` receives it as a tofu\nvariable. The stage's config declares `variable \"kubeconfig\" {}` and\nfeeds its provider:\n\n```nix\nprovider.kubernetes.config_path = var.kubeconfig;  # or client-config block\ndata.kubernetes_secret.keycloak_admin.metadata.name = \"keycloak-admin\";\nprovider.keycloak.client_id = \"admin-cli\";\nprovider.keycloak.password   = data.kubernetes_secret.keycloak_admin.data.password;\n```\n\n**ekn does not read cluster values for tofu.** Tofu has data sources;\nthat is the right tool and it already has the kubeconfig. ekn hands over\nthe file and gets out of the way. (If some provider genuinely cannot\ndata-source a value, that is a phase-3 question -- see below.)\n\n### cluster facts into manifests: seeds, only if needed\n\nFacts known statically (endpoint shape, OIDC issuer URL pattern,\nsubnets) live in shared options and flow at eval time -- no mechanism\nneeded. A fact that exists only after an apply and must land *inside* a\nmanifest (an LB IP into a ConfigMap, say) gets the nixidae/nixidae#335 treatment: an\n`ekn.tofuSeed \"<stage>.<output>\"` placeholder, resolved by `ekn` at\napply time from the outputs that apply recorded. Phase 2; build it when\na real manifest needs it, not before.\n\n## What `ekn` gains\n\n- `ekn tofu plan|apply --stage <name>`: materialize that stage's\n  `config.tf.json` into its workspace, `tofu init`, then\n  `plan`/`apply`, capture `tofu output -json`. `tofu` on PATH, like\n  `kubectl` and `git` -- the operator's tool with the operator's\n  credentials, not an ekn closure entry. Revisit only if that hurts.\n- `--kubeconfig` on `ekn kubeapply`, plus the remembered-kubeconfig\n  handoff above.\n- Output logging redacts `sensitive: true` values, the way `seeds.py`\n  redacts seed values today.\n- Phase 2: `ekn deploy` -- the whole pipeline in dependency order\n  (`needs` on stages; the cycle check reuses the deployment-unit\n  dependency machinery). Phase 1 keeps the three commands explicit; the\n  value is in the handoffs, not in the sugar.\n\n## Pure-Nix surface, same layering as the manifests\n\n`terranix.stages.<name>` also exports as plain files --\n`terranixConfigurations.<name>` builds `config.tf.json` -- so `nix\nbuild` plus a manual `tofu apply` works with no `ekn` at all. The CLI\nadds orchestration and apply-time resolution on top; it never gates the\npure output. This mirrors `manifestYAMLFile` vs `ekn kubeapply`.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe tf.json generation is pure Nix: eval gates, same as the doc\nexamples. The orchestration plumbing gets a fake `tofu` -- a script that\nrecords invocations and emits canned `output -json` -- so the handoff\nwiring is testable without a cloud provider. The kubeconfig pre-flight\nand the apply order against a real cluster stay manual, like the\nbootstrap flow today; the ULM kubeapply gate covers the middle stage.\n\n## Phase plan\n\n1. `terranix.stages.<name>` (own Apache-2.0 core, terranix-compatible\n   schema), pure-Nix tf.json exports, `ekn tofu plan|apply`,\n   kubeconfig handoff, `--kubeconfig` on kubeapply, docs example.\n2. `ekn deploy` orchestration over `needs`; `ekn.tofuSeed` if a\n   manifest needs a post-apply fact.\n3. (Open) shared upstream-able modules (subnet/OIDC helpers); (open)\n   ekn reading cluster values into tofu variables if some provider\n   cannot data-source them.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- Option-tree name: `terranix.stages` vs `tofu.stages`. The tree speaks\n  Terraform JSON and stays compatible with upstream terranix modules;\n  the name should say which ecosystem a module comes from, and\n  `terranix` is the one people search for.\n- Default workspace location (`.ekn/tofu/<stage>/` in the working copy\n  vs `XDG_STATE_HOME`).\n- Remote backends (s3, kubernetes secret) are just `terraform.backend`\n  in the generated JSON -- no ekn involvement planned; state locking\n  stays tofu's problem, documented as such."}
{"issue": 359, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPL7eQ", "before": "nixkube ships Nix store paths into pods. The other half is missing: the host\nitself. A node that runs NixOS could get its configuration from nixkube, the\nway it now gets its CSI mounts. This issue sketches how. It is a sketch, not a\nplan; each step can become its own issue.\n\nNo flakes. Evaluation goes through `nix build --file`, the same path a\n`nixExpr` volumeAttribute already takes (`pkgs/nixkube/src/nix/build.py`).\n\n## The Node object is the source of truth\n\nLabels and annotations on the Node object say what the machine should be.\nThe node daemon reads them, evaluates a NixOS configuration and makes the\nhost match. The node's name maps to the NixOS hostname.\n\nThe sketch of the contract:\n\n    nixkube.io/nix-expr   annotation   a Nix expression, evaluated with the\n                                       node's identity as its argument\n    nixkube.io/toplevel   annotation   a prebuilt store path, pinned\n    labels                --           coarse facts: role, environment,\n                                       kubernetes.io/arch\n\nLabels have a restricted charset, annotations are free-form and large. So the\nexpression lives in an annotation, and the selectors that switch on it live\nin labels. The expression receives the node's identity as data:\n\n    { name, labels, annotations, system }:\n      ...config.system.build.toplevel\n\nNothing about the machine's configuration lives in a lock file. The Node\nobject is the input, and kubectl is the interface.\n\n## The pipeline, per node\n\nThe node daemon already runs as root on every node, watches the API through\nkr8s and has `/host` mounted (`kubenix/daemonset.nix`). A new reconciler in\nit runs this state machine:\n\n1. **Watch.** The daemon watches its own Node object. A label or annotation\n   change is new desired state.\n2. **Evaluate and build.** Evaluate the expression, build\n   `config.system.build.toplevel` for the node's system, substitute it into\n   the node's store through the existing pynixd path. An annotated store\n   path skips the evaluation.\n3. **Decide.** Compare the new toplevel with `/host/run/current-system`.\n   Three action classes: activate only, cordon first, reboot. Which class a\n   change belongs to comes from a diff of the kernel and systemd paths\n   between the current system's closure and the new one, or from a small\n   manifest the evaluation emits beside the toplevel.\n4. **Cordon, and drain when needed.** Patch `spec.unschedulable`. Evict\n   pods only when kubelet, the runtime, systemd or the kernel change; a\n   pure configuration change should not churn workloads. Drain goes through\n   the eviction API, honours PDBs, and has a timeout; on timeout, stop\n   before the switch.\n5. **Switch.** Run the new toplevel's `bin/switch-to-configuration switch`\n   against the host. It has to talk to the host's systemd, so the daemon\n   needs hostPID or nsenter; `/host` is already mounted\n   (`kubenix/daemonset.nix:369`). The toplevel lives in the store the\n   daemon already serves.\n6. **Reboot when needed.** Keep the node cordoned and drained, reboot the\n   host, wait for the daemon to come back and the Node to be Ready, then\n   continue.\n7. **Canary.** Schedule a probe Pod onto the cordoned node with a toleration\n   for `node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable:NoSchedule` and a nodeSelector for\n   this node. It checks that the kubelet answers, that `nixos-version`\n   matches the new generation, and that a CSI mount of a path from the new\n   toplevel works.\n8. **Uncordon.** `spec.unschedulable=false`. A failed canary keeps the node\n   cordoned, writes the failure into an annotation and raises an event.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- **Who runs the reconciler.** Node-local in the daemon, or a central\n  controller. Node-local fits the one-daemon design and survives a broken\n  control-plane machine. A controller can evaluate once for many nodes.\n- **Rollback.** Nix generations and `bootctl set-default` exist on the\n  host. A `nixkube.io/last-good` annotation pins the previous toplevel.\n  The canary's failure path should be able to drive it.\n- **Where the expression gets nixpkgs from.** A source tree nixkube ships,\n  the cache, or a path in another annotation.\n- **Trust.** Whoever can patch Node annotations can configure the machines.\n  That is a bigger grant than it looks, and belongs with the survey in nixidae/nixidae#355.\n- **Testing.** The integration test runs on kind, whose hosts are not\n  NixOS. Testing this needs a NixOS guest with a kubelet; see nixidae/nixidae#26 and nixidae/nixidae#360.\n"}
{"issue": 360, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPMB9Q", "before": "Today the node daemon works against a store nixkube owns on the host's disk,\nat `nixkube.hostMountPath`, which defaults to `/var/lib/nix-csi`\n(`kubenix/options.nix:85`). On a host that runs NixOS there is already a\nstore at `/nix`, with its own daemon, its own cache config, and the system\nprofile the machine boots. nixkube should be able to run against that store\ninstead. Supporting it is one thing; being able to test it is the other.\n\n## Why\n\n- One store on the machine, not two. Builds and substitutions land where the\n  host can see them, and host services and pods share paths.\n- It is the store `current-system` lives in, which is what the NixOS host\n  management sketch builds on (see the issue about node labels driving\n  builds, switches, drains and reboots).\n- Cache, trust and remote-building settings come from the host's own\n  nix.conf instead of a second one nixkube ships.\n\n## What changes\n\n- `hostMountPath = /` becomes a supported mode rather than the untested\n  thing it is today; the volumes are already hostPaths\n  (`kubenix/daemonset.nix:346`).\n- The daemon talks to the host's nix-daemon socket instead of the one in\n  the container, or runs its builds against the host store directly.\n- GC coordination is the footgun. nixkube installs GC roots for the paths\n  pods mount (`pkgs/nixkube/src/nix/gc.py`); against the host store those\n  roots have to land where the host's GC honours them, or a host GC deletes\n  paths live pods still mount.\n- Trust and signing are the host's call: the host nix.conf decides\n  substituters and allowed-users. The node daemon currently ships\n  `allowed-users = *` for its own store (`kubenix/config.nix`); on the host\n  store it must not.\n- `kubenix/undeploy.nix:19` already refuses to undeploy an `/nix`-prefixed\n  hostMountPath, so the option is half-envisioned today and only that.\n\n## How to test\n\nCI hosts are not NixOS and kind does not change that, so the test needs a\nNixOS guest with a kubelet inside. The umbrella has the tool for it:\nuser-mode-nixos runs NixOS integration tests on user-mode Linux, no KVM, so\nthe test can be a derivation.\n\nThe test asserts, on a NixOS guest:\n\n- a pod's build lands in the guest's `/nix`, and the host's `nix store ls`\n  sees it,\n- a CSI mount binds straight from the guest store instead of copying\n  across stores,\n- the node daemon's GC roots keep a mounted path alive across a host GC,\n- the daemon substitutes through the host's configured cache.\n\nRelated: nixidae/nixidae#26 (NixOS tests), nixidae/nixidae#351 (umlTest cannot see the containerised-kubelet\nclass, because its kubelet is on the host -- this issue needs the kubelet in\nthe guest).\n"}
{"issue": 360, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWFbdQ", "before": "Host store is now tested on CRI-O and on a node that is not NixOS (nixkube 36788ac0):\n\n- `umlMatrix.uml-crio-hoststore`: all nine phases pass.\n- `ciMatrix.hoststore`: kubenixCI2 with host store on a NixOS guest. `nix-node-host` serves the node, and all jobs and refusals pass.\n- `ciMatrix.hoststore-foreign`: the same with /etc/NIXOS removed. `nix-node-separate` serves the node, and all jobs and refusals pass.\n\nStill open: NRI on composefs, and nixkube#75."}
{"issue": 365, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPMkdQ", "before": "`nodeEnv` and `cacheEnv` are keyed by whatever nixpkgs the umbrella held at\nevaluation time, and nothing fixes which umbrella that is. So CI's published\npaths and a consumer's rendered paths agree only by coincidence.\n\nCarl's requirement, 2026-09-14: nixkube must provide its own nixpkgs pin. It\nmay be the umbrella's, but it must not be something a consumer can override,\nbecause otherwise bootstrapping cannot be guaranteed.\n\n## Where it comes from\n\n`kubenix/options.nix:228` -- `nixkube.pkgs` defaults to `sources.nixpkgs`\nand is `internal = true`. Every `csiPkgs` derivation is built from it.\n\n`nix/sources.nix:47` -- nixkube has no lock of its own. The umbrella owns\nevery source, and outside the umbrella nixkube fetches it:\n\n    wire = if inUmbrella then ../../nix/wire.nix\n           else (builtins.fetchTree (builtins.parseFlakeRef \"github:nixidae/nixidae\")).outPath\n                + \"/nix/wire.nix\";\n\nThat fetch names **no rev**. CI builds standalone, so CI takes the nixidae\ndefault branch as it stands at that moment. A consumer inside the umbrella\ntakes their own checkout's pin.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nA node cannot build `nodeEnv` for itself in any useful sense: it is a\n`buildEnv`, so `allowSubstitutes = false` and a cache is the only source\nbesides pynixd. If the consumer's hash differs from the one CI published,\nthe cache answers 404 for a path that is genuinely built and published --\njust a different one.\n\nThis was latent on 2026-09-14 rather than the cause: the outage that day was\nCI not publishing at all (a `timeout-minutes` key on a composite-action\nstep). The pin problem survives that fix.\n\n## Two shapes\n\n**Direct pin.** nixkube carries `rev` + `narHash` for nixpkgs in its own\ntree; `nixkube.pkgs` resolves from that and becomes `readOnly`. Fewest\nmoving parts, and it satisfies the requirement literally. Costs a consumer a\nsecond nixpkgs in the closure, and a bump command nixkube owns.\n\n**Pinned umbrella.** nixkube pins a nixidae *rev* and takes nixpkgs from it,\nin-umbrella or not. One source of truth, but it creates a pin cycle --\nnixidae locks nixkube@X, nixkube@X pins nixidae@Y -- where bump order\nmatters.\n\nI lean to the direct pin. The discriminating question is whether one\nlockfile is worth more than avoiding the cycle.\n\n## Related\n\nWhatever shape wins, `nixkube.pkgs` should stop being `internal` or should\nbecome `readOnly`, so the guarantee is visible rather than conventional.\n\nA second unpinned input was found in the same audit and is already fixed:\nthe CI test workload built from `ref = \"nixos-unstable\"` with no rev.\n\nSee also nixidae/nixidae#363, where a consumer's nodes could not find `nodeEnv`."}
{"issue": 366, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPMrzA", "before": "`nixkube.push` gates the `unsafeDiscardStringContext` transformer\n(`kubenix/options.nix:304`) and nothing else. Three things about it do not\nmatch what it is for.\n\n- `internal = true`, so a consumer cannot discover it or read it in the docs.\n- It defaults to `false`, which means the discard is **on** by default.\n- It is named for a use case, not for what it does.\n\nCarl's statement, 2026-09-14: the discard exists so a deployer need not build\nnixkube for a foreign architecture, given that nixkube's paths should be on\ncachix. It is an optimisation and should be toggleable, and by default\nmanifests should **not** have their string context cleaned.\n\n## What being on by default costs\n\n`ekn.cachePackage` defaults to the manifest and relies on string context to\ncarry referenced paths. Measured on `kubenixApply`:\n\n    store paths named as text in manifest.json   4   (node and pynixd envs, both arches)\n    paths in its closure                         1   (itself)\n\nSo `ekn deploy` reports a successful cache push and seeds none of the paths a\nnode needs to boot. That is invisible from the deploy side, which is what\nmakes it worth fixing rather than documenting.\n\n## Why the default cannot simply be flipped today\n\n`buildEnv` sets `allowSubstitutes = false`\n(`pkgs/build-support/buildenv/default.nix:142`), so a context-carrying\nforeign-architecture environment is **built**, never fetched, even when a\ncache holds that exact output. Proven rather than inferred, with binfmt\ndisabled:\n\n    nix build <aarch64 nodeEnv> --store <empty> --option extra-platforms \"\"\n    error: Cannot build '...-nodeEnv.drv'\n           Reason: platform mismatch\n           Required system: 'aarch64-linux'\n           Current system: 'x86_64-linux'\n\nThe aarch64 *dependencies* substitute normally; only the `buildEnv` output\nrefuses. This is exactly the case the discard avoids.\n\n## Two ways to remove that obstacle\n\n**1. `allowSubstitutes = true` on the environments.** Line 142 reads\n`derivationArgs.allowSubstitutes or false`, so it is overridable. Precise,\nneeds no consumer configuration. Costs a one-time change of those derivation\nhashes, so the new paths must reach cachix before nodes roll -- otherwise it\nreproduces the outage of 2026-09-14.\n\n**2. `always-allow-substitutes = true` in the deployer's nix.conf.** Same\nderivation, same empty store, binfmt off:\n\n    (default, false)  -> error: platform mismatch\n    (true)            -> copying path '...m075kkzq-nodeEnv' from 'https://nix-csi.cachix.org'\n\n**No derivation change and no hash change at all.** It is a machine-wide\nsetting, so it also permits fetching other cheap-to-build local outputs,\nwhich is a mild and deliberate trade.\n\nOption 2 unblocks a deployer today with one line. Option 1 makes the\nbehaviour correct for everyone without asking them to configure anything.\nThey are not exclusive: 1 is the fix, 2 is what makes 1 unnecessary to rush.\n\n## Then\n\nWith either in place, the default can keep string context, `nixkube.push`\ncan stop being internal, and the discard becomes the opt-in escape hatch it\nwas meant to be. A name saying what it does would help.\n\nSee nixidae/nixidae#363 for the cycle this sits beside, and nixidae/nixidae#365 for the pin."}
{"issue": 369, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPNAeg", "before": "`ci.yaml` is 409 lines over nine jobs. It already calls **22 distinct Nix\nattributes** \u2014 `arch-audit`, `assert-cached`, four assertion gates,\n`treefmt`, three deployment scripts, three push scripts, the images,\n`sourcesAreLocked`, `ci-debug`, and so on. So the shape is right: CI runs\nNix. What is missing is that nothing collects those, so the workflow is the\nonly place that knows what CI does.\n\nRanked by value, most first.\n\n## 1. No aggregate, so CI cannot be run locally\n\neasykubenix has `checks.nix` and `nix build --file ./checks.nix all`.\nnixkube has no `checks.nix`. Its `check` job is ten steps: pyright, four\neval gates, treefmt, and the rest. A contributor cannot run what CI runs\nwithout reading the YAML and copying six commands.\n\nAdding `checks.all` would make the `check` job one step, and make \"does this\npass CI\" answerable before pushing. It also gives the eval gates one home\ninstead of a list that only exists in YAML.\n\n## 2. The two kind jobs are near-duplicates\n\n`test-kind-cache` (11 steps) and `test-kind-nocache` (10) differ in three\nthings: `kubenixCI1` versus `kubenixCI2`, one extra \"wait for the pynixd\npod\" step, and a comment on the clean-runner step. Everything else is\nidentical, including the two hand-written `kubectl` lists.\n\nA `strategy.matrix` over the variant, or a composite action taking the\ninstance name, removes about ninety lines and makes the two provably the\nsame test.\n\nThis duplication has already cost something: see nixidae/nixidae#368, where a workload is\nwaited on in neither copy.\n\n## 3. Lists Nix already knows, maintained by hand\n\n- The `arch-audit` argument list. I added `push-citest` to it by hand this\n  week; nothing would have failed if I had forgotten.\n- The `kubectl wait` and `kubectl delete` job lists, in both kind jobs \u2014\n  four places. nixidae/nixidae#368 is the drift this produced.\n\nBoth are derivable from the instances themselves.\n\n## 4. `ci-debug` throws away its own errors\n\n    nix run --file . ci-debug 2>/dev/null || true\n\n`|| true` is right: a debug step must not replace the real failure. The\n`2>/dev/null` is not \u2014 it hides why `ci-debug` itself failed, and a debug\ntool that fails silently is worse than none. Issue nixidae/nixidae#342 was this exact class\non `push-ci2`.\n\n## 5. `test-nixos.yaml` runs on a branch nobody uses\n\nTriggers are `push` to `cidev` and `workflow_dispatch`. It is 61 lines with\nits own installer configuration, which had to be fixed separately this week\nbecause it did not share `setup-nix`. Either it is a real gate and should\nrun on develop, or it is dead and should go.\n\n## 6. Small ones\n\n- `softprops/action-gh-release@v1` \u2014 actionlint reports the runner as too\n  old to run on GitHub Actions.\n- `if: env.CACHIX_AUTH_TOKEN != ''` is repeated; fine at two, worth a job\n  level `env` if it grows.\n\n## What this is not\n\nThe job graph itself is sound: `check` and the two builds run in parallel,\n`build-manifests` fans in, both kind tests fan out, `release` gates on all\nfour. Concurrency with `cancel-in-progress` is right for a branch. None of\nthat needs changing."}
{"issue": 369, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWGjXQ", "before": "Items 1 and 4 are done, on develop at e9a155aa. Item 3 was done by the\nghanix port. Items 2 and 5 are the ones left, and 5 is a question rather\nthan work.\n\n## 1 \u2014 no aggregate: done\n\n`nix build --file ./checks.nix all` runs what CI runs. The `check` job\nbuilds that one attribute instead of eight, so it went from ten steps to\nsix.\n\nIt holds exactly what the workflow held, so this is a refactor and not a\nwidening. Two gates stay outside because a derivation cannot have what\nthey need: pyright wants the dev shell, and treefmt is checked with\n`git diff` against a working tree.\n\nOne property that was not designed and is worth keeping: the set checks\nitself. `ciWorkflowCheck` is in it, and it asserts the committed YAML\nmatches `ci/workflows/`, while the workflow's `check` job builds the set.\nSo editing the workflow source without rendering it fails the aggregate.\nIt did, while this was being written.\n\n**`umlImagesMatch` is the obvious next addition.** It is a real gate --\n\"do these image tarballs carry the tags the DaemonSet asks for\" -- and CI\nhas never run it. Adding it would be a widening rather than a refactor,\nwhich is why it is not in this change.\n\n## 4 \u2014 `ci-debug` discarding its own errors: done\n\n`|| true` stays, because a debug step must never replace the real\nfailure. `2>/dev/null` is gone.\n\n## 3 \u2014 hand-maintained lists: done by the ghanix port\n\nThe `arch-audit` argument list and the two `kubectl` job lists are Nix\nvalues in `ci/workflows/ci.nix` now. The workload lists are two bindings,\n`deployedJobs` and `assertedJobs`, sitting next to each other -- which\nmakes nixidae/nixidae#368 visible rather than derivable: four jobs are deployed and\ndeleted and never waited for.\n\nThey are still written by hand rather than derived from the instances.\nDeriving them is worth doing and is a smaller job now that there is one\ncopy instead of four.\n\n## 2 \u2014 the two kind jobs: still open, and now cheaper\n\nThe port already shares them: both come from one `testKind` function\ntaking the instance name and any extra readiness waits, so the ninety\nduplicated lines are gone from the source. What remains is that they are\nstill two rendered jobs from one function rather than a\n`strategy.matrix`, which is a smaller and more cosmetic change than it\nwas when they were two hand-written copies.\n\n## 5 \u2014 `test-nixos.yaml` on `cidev`: a question for @Lillecarl\n\nUnchanged, and it is a decision rather than a task. It is ported to\nghanix and shares nothing else. Either it is a real gate and should run\non develop, or it is dead and should go. I have no evidence either way --\nit has not run in the period I can see."}
{"issue": 382, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPOb2g", "before": "Measured 2026-09-15 on a freshly rebuilt cluster (Talos v1.14.0, 4 workers).\n\n`nixkube-demo-nri` starts at the same time as the nix-node DaemonSet. On a\ncold cluster it loses that race: the container cannot exec its own shell,\nbecause the store path is not on the node yet.\n\n```\nfailed to create containerd task: ... unable to start container process:\nexec: \"/nix/store/4s514kmhnmncvcsvjh3d17y7y0psbyc1-busybox-1.37.0/bin/sh\":\nno such file or directory\nExit Code: 128, Reason: StartError\n```\n\n`backoffLimit` is 2, so all three attempts burn inside about six minutes --\nevery one of them before nix-node is ready. The Job then goes `Failed`\npermanently and never retries.\n\n### The NRI mechanism is fine\n\nThis is not an injection bug. nix-node's own log shows the plugin connected\nand processing events the entire time:\n\n```\n{\"event\": \"cri_socket_discovered\", \"socket\": \"/run/containerd/containerd.sock\"}\n{\"container_count\": 29, \"event\": \"cri_connected\"}\n```\n\n### Proved by re-running it\n\nDeleting the Job so it is recreated, with nothing else changed:\n\n```\njob: Running  0/1\njob: Complete 1/1      (13 seconds)\n```\n\nIt passes the moment it is not racing.\n\n### Why this is worth fixing rather than ignoring\n\nThe failed Job holds the `nixkube` ArgoCD Application at **Degraded\nindefinitely, on every fresh cluster**. That is a false alarm in the one\nplace an operator looks to decide whether nixkube is healthy, and it trains\npeople to ignore a Degraded nixkube -- which is exactly the signal that\nshould not become noise.\n\n`nixkube-demo-csi` has no such problem: it Completed 1/1 on the first\nattempt, because a CSI mount blocks until the volume is ready instead of\nexec'ing a path that may not be there yet. The NRI demo is the only one\nthat can start before its precondition exists.\n\n### Possible fixes, in rough order of preference\n\n- Give the demo an initContainer that waits for the store path, so it\n  blocks instead of failing. This mirrors what the CSI demo gets for free.\n- Raise `backoffLimit`, and/or set `activeDeadlineSeconds`, so retries\n  outlast a cold start.\n- Make the demo depend on nix-node readiness explicitly.\n\nPossibly related to nixidae/nixidae#368 (workloads deployed but never asserted): a demo that\ncannot fail CI and a demo that always fails on a cold cluster are two halves\nof the same gap -- nothing checks that these actually passed for the right\nreason."}
{"issue": 387, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPO9kw", "before": "## What happens\n\n`tests/test_eval.py::TestEknModule::test_marking_an_object_with_no_reference_is_rejected`\nin easykubenix asks Nix for a value the module system throws on, and expects\n`nanopynix.NixError`. On a GitHub runner it gets:\n\n```\nnanopynix.exceptions.WorkerDiedError: Protocol error\n```\n\nwith this captured stderr:\n\n```\nFailed to handle cancellation\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \".../grpclib/server.py\", line 436, in request_handler\n    with deadline_wrapper, wrapper:\n  File \".../grpclib/utils.py\", line 70, in __exit__\n    raise self._error\ngrpclib.exceptions.StreamTerminatedError: Connection lost\n\nWARNING asyncio:unix_events.py:727 pipe closed by peer or os.write(pipe, data) raised exception.   (x7)\n```\n\nThe pipe between client and worker closes while the call is in flight. Seven\nwrites fail after it.\n\n## What is already ruled out\n\nThis was first reported as nixidae/nixidae#384, because the close tripped a second defect:\n`pump` kept feeding a connection that `grpclib.protocol.Connection.close` had\nalready stripped, which raised `AttributeError` on `_frame_dispatch_table`.\n`deb42e67` fixed that, and run 34964559464 confirms it \u2014 zero occurrences of\nthat name, and one clean exception in place of an `ExceptionGroup` of two. The\nclose itself is unchanged, so it was never caused by the pump.\n\nAlso ruled out while chasing nixidae/nixidae#384: h2 version skew (4.3.0 on both sides, line\nfor line), beartype instrumentation (easykubenix installs no hook), a\nfree-threaded interpreter (`sys._is_gil_enabled()` is True, `Py_GIL_DISABLED`\nis 0), and pytest-agent (three full runs with it disabled).\n\n## What makes it specific\n\n- **It is deterministic on the runner and absent here.** Three CI runs out of\n  three, byte-identical counts (`1 failed, 404 passed, 1 xfailed`). On this\n  workstation: the whole suite through `nix-shell --run` exactly as CI runs it\n  (405 passed), the suite against a dev env rebuilt on the fixed nanopynix\n  (401 passed, and the 4 are the local sops/age gap), 15 runs of the test\n  alone, 6 pinned to one core. No occurrence.\n- **Other throwing tests pass.** `iPv6ServiceOnIPv4ClusterThrows` and\n  `pinnedIPv4OutsideCidrThrows` are in the same file and expect the same\n  exception type. Only `envSeededWithoutReferenceThrows` does this.\n\n## The hypothesis worth testing first\n\nThe error this one produces is a module-system throw carrying a long message.\ngRPC puts that message in a trailer header, and HTTP/2 bounds a header list.\nA message that fits on one machine and not on another would close the stream\nexactly here, and would explain why only this throw does it and only on a\nrunner.\n\nMeasuring the length of the rendered error, and comparing it against the\n`SETTINGS_MAX_HEADER_LIST_SIZE` the transport negotiates, is the cheap first\nstep. `grpclib_transports.protocol.TransportTuning` already tunes the frame\nand window sizes and says nothing about the header list."}
{"issue": 395, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPPzMw", "before": "Carried over from `pynixd/todo/compare_build_decomposition.md`, which issue Lillecarl/nanopynix#131\nremoved. The text below is that file.\n\n---\n\nWe should investigate how to build a test that first builds a closure with\n`--builders` (let's Nix decompose derivations into BasicDerivations for\nBuildDerivation) and record the BasicDerivations we receive, cleans the store\nand then builds with `--store` and records the pynixd generated\nBasicDerivations and compares them with eachother. The goal would be to make\nthem identical, but since the build decomposition tests work we should be\nreasonable and not break pynixd to chase a ghost.\n\n## Todo\n\nBuild tests to compare pynixd vs Nix generated BasicDerivations.\n\n## Tips\n\nI'm perfectly fine with using some global-style variable like\n`WRITE_BASICDERIVATION = Path(\"/somewhere\")` that enables writing\nBasicDerivations to file to be able to hook into where they're received and\ncreated since otherwise it'd be borderline impossible to achieve this.\n\n## Goals\n\n- Try to make them identical within reason.\n- Have a reliable way to compare them.\n\n---\n\nRelated: Lillecarl/nanopynix#131 brought pynixd into this repository.\n\n---\n\nMoved from Lillecarl/nanopynix#133. Labels there: enhancement, priority: medium, phase-3: tests, size: M."}
{"issue": 396, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPP6Wg", "before": "Carried over from `pynixd/todo/test_resilience.md`, which issue Lillecarl/nanopynix#131 removed.\nThe text below is that file.\n\n---\n\nImplement functional tests for failure scenarios and edge cases in the daemon\nprotocol.\n\n## Scenarios to test\n\n- **Backend disconnection**\n  - What happens if a backend store disconnects mid-operation?\n  - What happens if a build is in progress when the backend dies?\n- **Interrupted NAR transfers**\n  - Verify the \"abrupt close\" failure signalling for NAR transfers that fail\n    after the `200 OK` header is sent.\n- **Protocol handshake mismatch**\n  - Verify that pynixd correctly rejects, or downgrades, a client or a backend\n    with an incompatible protocol version.\n- **Invalid requests**\n  - Verify that malformed data sent by a client does not crash the server.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- [ ] The server stays stable and serves other clients after a failure.\n- [ ] A failed operation reaches the client as a clean protocol error, where\n      that is possible.\n- [ ] No connection and no file handle leaks during a failure.\n\n---\n\nRelated: Lillecarl/nanopynix#131 brought pynixd into this repository.\n\n---\n\nMoved from Lillecarl/nanopynix#134. Labels there: enhancement, priority: medium, phase-3: tests, size: M."}
{"issue": 402, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPQizA", "before": "## What was measured\n\n`pytest` in `pynixd/` on darwin (aarch64-darwin, Nix 2.34.7):\n\n```\n157 of 158 functional failures share one root cause:\n\nRuntimeError: Managed daemon socket not accepting connections at\n/tmp/pynixd-session-stores/local/nix/var/nix/daemon-socket/pynixd-nix within 5s\n  stderr: unexpected Nix daemon error: error: the path \"/tmp\" is a symlink;\n  this is not allowed for the Nix store and its parent directories\n```\n\nOn darwin `/tmp` is a symlink to `/private/tmp`. `STORE_PREFIX` is\n`/tmp/pynixd-stores`, so every store this suite makes sits under a symlink and\nno `nix-daemon` will start on it.\n\nThe same tree in the `vzrun` Linux VM: **321 passed, 96 skipped, 4 xfailed, 7\nxpassed**, 21 failed (`test_ca_ops.py`, `test_sftp_server.py`) and 21 errors\n(`test_drv_parser.py::TestLiveProbes`, which wants `nixpkgs` on `NIX_PATH`).\nAll of those are separate and pre-existing \u2014 the same two files give an\nidentical 20 failed / 3 passed on a baseline tree.\n\n## Why it is not just a prefix change\n\nMoving the prefix to `/private/tmp` fixes the symlink, but not the class of\nproblem. darwin does not support a chroot store at all:\n\n```\nbuilding using a diverted store is not supported on this platform\n```\n\n`tests/differential/` already states this and skips the whole directory with\nthat reason. The functional suite states nothing and fails 157 times.\n\n## What to do\n\npynixd is platform-independent apart from the store underneath it, so a darwin\nrun should say \"probably works\" rather than nothing:\n\n- Run the subset that needs no chroot store against the system store.\n- Mark the rest so darwin **skips** with the reason, the way\n  `tests/differential/conftest.py` does. A skip that names the platform\n  limitation is information; 157 identical socket timeouts are not.\n\nA collection-time marker is what the differential suite uses, and it has to be\ncollection-time: the parent conftest's session-scoped autouse `pynixd_server`\nfixture starts a chroot-store daemon before any fixture of a subdirectory\nruns, so a fixture-level gate is too late. `tests/differential/conftest.py`\n`pytest_collection_modifyitems` is the shape to copy.\n\n## Related\n\n- Lillecarl/nanopynix#159 \u2014 `no_pynixd` is declared in `pytest.ini` and honoured nowhere. A test\n  that must not have a pynixd server is the same kind of gate.\n\n\n---\n\nMoved from Lillecarl/nanopynix#161. Labels there: bug."}
{"issue": 407, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPREnA", "before": "`4622ab4b` (Lillecarl/nanopynix#166) made pynixd record when each store path was last referenced, in `PynixdPathAccess` and in `ValidPaths.registrationTime`. The data is collected and correct. Nothing reads it.\n\n`LocalStoreDB` exposes two methods, and neither has a caller:\n\n- `query_paths_not_referenced_since(max_age_seconds)` \u2014 the paths nothing has named for that long.\n- `prune_path_access()` \u2014 drop the rows for paths the store no longer holds. Without a caller the table grows for ever, because a path the garbage collector deletes leaves its access time behind.\n\nThis is the same shape as the defect Lillecarl/nanopynix#166 corrected. `query_stale_paths` was declared and called nowhere, beside `mark_path` and `mark_paths`, which were also called nowhere. A method with no caller reads as a working feature and is not one, so this issue exists to stop the pattern repeating one layer up.\n\n## What to decide\n\n**Where the collector runs.** `instance.py` already starts a GC loop (`gc_loop_started`, one hour). Whether the LRU pass belongs in that loop, in `pynixd_collect_garbage`, or in a command of its own is open.\n\n**What it deletes, and what protects a path in use.** A path nothing has referenced for N days is a candidate, not a verdict. `registrationTime` refreshing already gives stock `nix-collect-garbage --delete-older-than` the same behaviour, so a first version could do nothing but prune the table and report what it would delete.\n\n**A path with no row has never been referenced through pynixd.** `query_paths_not_referenced_since` does not report it, on purpose: \"never seen\" and \"seen long ago\" are different questions, and only the second one is safe to collect on this evidence. A collector needs its own answer for the first, and the answer is probably the age of the pynixd deployment.\n\n**Pruning has to run before any deletion reads the table**, or a path that Nix deleted and that a later path reused the name of could carry a stale time. The key is the path text, so this is a leak rather than a correctness fault, but the order still matters.\n\n## Related\n\n- Lillecarl/nanopynix#166 \u2014 the collection, and why the column alone was not enough.\n- nixidae/nixidae#46 \u2014 the per-operation cost. The marking added to every request cycle there is not measured yet.\n\n---\n\nMoved from Lillecarl/nanopynix#170. Labels there: enhancement."}
{"issue": 410, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPRbBw", "before": "## The gap\n\n`pynixd/tests/differential/` compares a **build** through pynixd against the\nsame build through nanopynix. It does not compare the **codecs**. pynixd\nwrites its own ATerm serialiser, its own `hashDerivationModulo`, its own\n`makeOutputPath` and its own derivation parser, and nothing states that any of\nthe four agrees with libnixstore.\n\nIssue Lillecarl/nanopynix#179 needed `hashDerivationModulo`, and the check that it is right was\nbuilt by hand:\n\n> For an input-addressed output, `makeOutputPath(name, hash, drvName)` must be\n> the path that the derivation states.\n\nThat oracle answered for 235 derivations of one machine and **skipped 165**.\nIt cannot reach a fixed-output derivation, because the path comes from the\ncontent hash, and it cannot reach a floating one, because there is no path in\nthe derivation to compare with. So the two branches of\n`Derivation.hash_derivation_modulo` that a machine can check are the ones the\noracle cannot see.\n\nThe check also lives in the commit message and in\n`tests/unit/test_drv_hash.py`, which pins two derivations that\n`nix-instantiate` wrote once. Two is not a corpus.\n\n## What nanopynix already gives\n\n- `Store.read_derivation(path) -> Derivation` \u2014 libnixstore parses the ATerm\n  and returns the structured form. That is an oracle for\n  `pynixd.drv_parser.parse_drv`.\n- `py_store_impl.cpp` calls `nix::parseDerivation` on ATerm text that a Python\n  store supplies, so the parser can read what pynixd writes.\n- `hashDerivationModulo` and `makeOutputPath` are in libnixstore and\n  `write_dev_shell_derivation` uses both. Neither is a binding yet.\n\n## The work\n\n1. Add a binding for the output hashes of a derivation, which is\n   `staticOutputHashes` of Nix. One call, no state.\n2. Add a differential module that walks a corpus of real derivations and\n   states four things for each one:\n   - `parse_drv` reads the same fields libnixstore reads.\n   - `Derivation.unparse` writes text libnixstore parses back to the same\n     derivation.\n   - `drv_hash.output_hashes` gives the hashes `staticOutputHashes` gives.\n   - `_make_output_path` gives the paths the derivation states.\n3. Take the corpus from the store of the machine, and skip when it is empty.\n\n## Not this issue\n\n**pynixd must not depend on nanopynix at run time.** `tests/differential/\nconftest.py` states the rule and the reason: the shipped proxy is pure Python\nand links no C++. A binding is the oracle of a test, and it stays there.\n\nThis is also why the duplication is not a fault to remove. Two independent\nimplementations that agree are evidence. One implementation that calls the\nother is not.\n\n\n---\n\nMoved from Lillecarl/nanopynix#181. Labels there: enhancement."}
{"issue": 412, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPRoZw", "before": "pynixd matches the bytes of \\`nix-daemon\\` on the wire. That is the measure\ntoday, and it is the right measure while the functional suite of Nix is the\ntest. It also means that pynixd copies decisions of Nix that are wrong.\n\nNix is not perfect. C++ limits what its authors can do easily, and Python does\nnot carry the same limits. A comment that reads as if Nix is the specification\nhides the difference between \"pynixd does this because it is right\" and\n\"pynixd does this because the parity run compares the bytes\".\n\n## The convention\n\nMark each such place with a comment that starts with the literal tag\n\\`NIX-DEFECT (#TRACKING):\\`, and give four parts:\n\n1. the mechanism in Nix, with the file and the line;\n2. what the mechanism gets wrong;\n3. what pynixd could do instead;\n4. why pynixd still copies it.\n\n\\`tests/meta/test_nix_defect_markers.py\\` checks the shape of each marker and\nlists them.\n\n## Why a mode is not the answer yet\n\nA \"Nix-correct mode\" and a \"correct-result mode\" split every behaviour in two,\nand the parity run can prove only one of them. The divergence surface is the\nwire, and not the goal system: pynixd already schedules, caches and dedupes\nits work differently, because none of that reaches the client.\n\nA flag earns its place when one entry of this list has a measured cost. Scope\nthe flag to that entry.\n\n## The list\n\nEach marker in the source is one item. This issue collects them, and a comment\nbelow records each new one.\n\n---\n\nMoved from Lillecarl/nanopynix#191."}
{"issue": 415, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPR82w", "before": "`QueryMissing` of pynixd leaves the root content-addressed derivation out of\n`willBuild`, and `nix-daemon` names it. A `streams build` run against Nix 2.34\nholds eight of these, and every one is in the `ca` suite.\n\n## What the recording says\n\nThe shortest pair, at `daemon-4/conn-0004.wire`:\n\n```\noperation 7 QueryMissing response.will_build\n    daemon: ['.../7w8wxj6xqzkfcnzf747z0ichynbi9rhq-rootCA.drv']\n    pynixd: []\n```\n\nA longer one, at `daemon-4/conn-0002.wire`, shows that pynixd drops that one\nentry and keeps the rest:\n\n```\noperation 15 QueryMissing response.will_build\n    daemon: ['...-transitively-dependent.drv', '...-rootCA.drv', '...-dependent.drv']\n    pynixd: ['...-transitively-dependent.drv',                   '...-dependent.drv']\n```\n\n**The derivation that goes missing is `rootCA.drv` in every case.** That is the\nroot content-addressed derivation of the `ca` fixture, so the answer is wrong\nfor the derivation that has no input derivation of its own.\n\n## What follows from it\n\nThe client reads the answer and takes another road. `daemon-4/conn-0002.wire`\nagain:\n\n```\noperation 18   daemon: QueryPathInfo (26)     pynixd: BuildPaths (9)\noperation 19   daemon: BuildPaths (9)         pynixd: QueryDerivationOutputMap (41)\noperation 20   daemon: QueryDerivationOutputMap (41)  pynixd: AddTempRoot (11)\nthe client sent 13 operations to the daemon and 12 to pynixd\n    daemon: ['AddIndirectRoot']\n    pynixd: []\n```\n\nThe client skips one `QueryPathInfo`, so every operation after it moves by\none place, and it sends one operation fewer. `_compare_operation` states the\nrule that this meets: the client sends the request, so a difference in a\nrequest means an earlier answer of pynixd sent the client down another path.\n\n## What it does not break, yet\n\n`ca:build` passes. So this is a difference of the wire and not a failure of\nthe suite today, and `printMissing` of `nix build --dry-run` is the command\nthat reads the answer directly.\n\n## Where to look\n\n`pynixd/docs/notes/querymissing.md` holds the algorithm of\n`Store::queryMissing` at `misc.cc:102-315`, and it does not cover a\ncontent-addressed derivation at all. The note says a derivation goes in\n`willBuild` when no substituter holds its output. A content-addressed output\nhas no output path until the derivation resolves, so the question \"does a\nsubstituter hold it\" needs the realisation and not the path, and that is the\nfirst place to read.\n\nIssues Lillecarl/nanopynix#182 to Lillecarl/nanopynix#185 corrected four content-addressed defects of the build\nroad. This is the same subject on the query road, and none of the four\ntouched `QueryMissing`.\n\n## How to measure it again\n\n```\nNIXFT_WORK=/tmp/nixft-234 nanopynix-nixft-nix_2_34 streams build\ngrep -A 3 'QueryMissing response.will_build' /tmp/nixft-234/streams/report.txt\n```\n\nRead issue Lillecarl/nanopynix#202 first. Six of the 33 difference lines of that report are the\npid of the client and say nothing, so the report reads as noisier than it is.\n\n\n---\n\nMoved from Lillecarl/nanopynix#203."}
{"issue": 416, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPSCyw", "before": "## What this tracks\n\nIssue nixidae/nixidae#52 tracks each place where **Nix is wrong**. This issue tracks the\nother kind: a place where Nix is right, or where neither answer is wrong, and\npynixd answers differently on purpose.\n\nThe two need separate registers because the follow-up differs. An entry of\nnixidae/nixidae#52 can become a report on the fork. An entry of this list never does. It is\na decision of pynixd about itself, and the only question it carries is whether\nthe decision still earns its cost.\n\n## The convention\n\nMark each such place with a comment that starts with the literal tag\n`NIX-DEVIATION (#TRACKING):`, and give four parts:\n\n1. the mechanism in Nix, with the file and the line, in back quotes;\n2. what pynixd does instead;\n3. why the difference is worth its cost;\n4. what a reader would have to measure to reverse the decision.\n\n`tests/meta/test_nix_defect_markers.py` reads the shape of both tags.\n\n## The first entry\n\n`BuildPathsWithResultsGoal._build_slots` states one already, in prose. Four\nfixed-output derivations all give the wrong hash, with `-j1`. Nix makes every\ngoal first and gives the one builder slot to the first goal by name, so it\nnames x1. pynixd prepares its goals as coroutines, so the goal that reaches\nthe queue first takes the slot. The correction is a barrier before the first\nbuild of every request, and pynixd does not take it.\n\nNix is not wrong there. Its order is an effect of how it makes goals, and the\nresult of the build is the same either way. `build.sh:167` reads the number of\n`error:` blocks, so the suite sees the difference.\n\n\n---\n\nMoved from Lillecarl/nanopynix#206."}
{"issue": 419, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPSV2Q", "before": "## The measurement\n\nThe whole functional suite, both arms, one serial run each, Nix 2.34.8 on\nLinux, at `62ed5ce6`. `nanopynix-nixft-nix_2_34 all`.\n\n**Correctness is not the problem.** The two arms agree on everything the\nsuite can see:\n\n| arm | OK | SKIP | FAIL |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| control (`nix daemon`) | 159 | 36 | 12 |\n| pynixd | 158 | 36 | 13 |\n\nOne regression, `main:store-info`, which the README records as permanent and\ndeliberate.\n\n**The cost is the problem.** Over the 158 tests that pass in **both** arms:\n\n```\ncontrol 263 s     pynixd 959 s     3.64x     696 s of overhead\n```\n\n- median ratio, over the 135 tests that take more than 0.5 s: **4.12x**\n- 48 tests are slower by more than 5 s\n- exactly **1** test of 158 is faster through pynixd\n- the 20 worst tests hold 232 s, which is 33% of the overhead, so this is\n  spread across the suite rather than concentrated in a few tests\n\n### The 20 largest gaps\n\n| gap | control | pynixd | test |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| +25.3 s | 7.1 | 32.4 | `ca:signatures` |\n| +24.2 s | 6.7 | 31.0 | `ca:nix-copy` |\n| +18.3 s | 4.1 | 22.4 | `ca:build-cache` |\n| +16.8 s | 2.4 | 19.2 | `main:impure-env` |\n| +16.0 s | 3.2 | 19.1 | `ca:build` |\n| +12.8 s | 2.6 | 15.4 | `main:fetchClosure` |\n| +12.7 s | 2.1 | 14.8 | `main:build-dry` |\n| +11.3 s | 3.2 | 14.5 | `ca:post-hook` |\n| +9.2 s | 2.9 | 12.1 | `main:post-hook` |\n| +9.1 s | 1.7 | 10.8 | `main:export` |\n| +8.3 s | 3.1 | 11.4 | `ca:nix-shell` |\n| +8.2 s | 9.0 | 17.2 | `main:fixed` |\n| +7.9 s | 2.2 | 10.1 | `ca:gc` |\n| +7.8 s | 3.8 | 11.7 | `main:nix-profile` |\n| +7.8 s | 1.7 | 9.5 | `main:nix-copy-ssh-ng` |\n| +7.7 s | 1.9 | 9.5 | `ca:multiple-outputs` |\n| +7.5 s | 1.0 | 8.5 | `main:brotli` |\n| +6.9 s | 4.3 | 11.2 | `main:remote-store` |\n| +6.9 s | 1.3 | 8.2 | `dyn-drv:recursive-mod-json` |\n| +6.9 s | 1.4 | 8.2 | `main:logging` |\n\n## Why this is worth an issue of its own\n\n**One instance of this class is already fixed, and it was found by accident.**\nIssue Lillecarl/nanopynix#287: every failed build waited a 2.0 s deadline in\n`_wait_for_local_paths` for an output that a failure means cannot appear. It\nturned up while proving something unrelated about issue Lillecarl/nanopynix#196, and it took\n`main:build` from 23.03 s to 9.05 s on its own.\n\nA 696 s total says there are more like it. `main:build` is still 2.73 s\nagainst 8.03 s after that fix, and it does not even appear in the table above.\n\n**Nothing measures this.** The functional suite records a verdict per test and\n`compare.py` reads the verdicts. The durations are in\n`build/meson-logs/testlog.json` of each arm and nobody subtracts the two\ncolumns.\n\n## Where to start\n\n`ca:signatures` and `ca:nix-copy` are the two largest, and neither is\ndominated by builds -- they are store operations and copies. That points at a\nper-operation cost rather than a per-build one, which would also explain why\nthe overhead is spread so evenly rather than concentrated.\n\nThe method that found Lillecarl/nanopynix#287 applies directly, and\n`project_nixft_run_and_read_one_test` records it: run one test with\n`PYNIXD_LOG_LEVEL=DEBUG`, read the structured log out of\n`build/meson-logs/testlog.txt`, and look for gaps in the timeline where\nnothing happens. Lillecarl/nanopynix#287 showed as 226 identical `IsValidPath` statements 26 ms\napart and nothing else.\n\n## What this is not\n\nThis is not an argument to match `nix-daemon` byte for byte or microsecond for\nmicrosecond. pynixd caches, schedules and removes duplicate work, and it is\nallowed to cost more where it buys something. The question this issue asks is\nnarrower: **which of those 696 seconds buys anything?**\n\n`durations.py` for the table above:\n\n```python\nrows = [(p[n][1] - c[n][1], c[n][1], p[n][1], n)\n        for n in control if n in candidate\n        and control[n][0] == \"OK\" == candidate[n][0]]\n```\n\nreading the two `testlog.json` files that `nixft` saves.\n\n---\n\nMoved from Lillecarl/nanopynix#290."}
{"issue": 419, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWMvjg", "before": "## Measured: it is per-request, not a slow path\n\nThe gate from nixidae/nixidae#48 records the duration of every test in both arms, so the data was already there. At `fc88ad6c`, `JOBS=4`, 140 tests that pass in both arms and take over half a second:\n\n```\ncontrol  241.2 s of test time\npynixd   542.8 s\nmedian ratio  2.52x\nmedian extra  1.6 s per test\ntotal extra   282.5 s\n```\n\n**The ratio is uniform, not concentrated.** No single test carries the cost \u2014 the worst absolute offenders add 4 to 10 seconds each and there are a dozen of them.\n\nThe worst *ratios* are what point at the mechanism:\n\n| ratio | control \u2192 pynixd | test |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 4.27x | 1.8 \u2192 7.8 | `build-dry` |\n| 3.97x | 2.3 \u2192 9.0 | `impure-env` |\n| 3.72x | 1.2 \u2192 4.5 | `logging` |\n| 3.67x | 1.0 \u2192 3.8 | `check-reqs` |\n| 3.52x | 1.5 \u2192 5.2 | `nix-copy-ssh-ng` |\n| 3.40x | 1.5 \u2192 5.1 | `export` |\n\n`build-dry` builds nothing. `check-reqs` and `logging` build almost nothing. These are tests that do **many small daemon operations**, and they are the worst. Meanwhile the data-moving tests are middling: `nix-copy` is 2.63x and `signatures` 2.68x, both below the 2.52x median's neighbourhood rather than above it.\n\nSo the cost scales with the **number of requests**, not with bytes moved and not with builds run. That rules out the NAR path and the build scheduler as the main term, which is where I would have looked first.\n\n## What this does not say\n\nIt does not name the per-request term. The candidates I can see, none measured:\n\n- a round trip pynixd adds that `nix-daemon` does not\n- the decode-re-encode of every request and response, where `nix-daemon` reads a request once\n- per-request allocation in the wire models\n- logging: `pynixd` writes a structlog event per operation, and `logging` being 3.72x is at least suggestive\n\nThe next instrument is a per-operation count and time, not another end-to-end number. `tests/parity` already records both wire streams for one workload, so the request count per test is available from a recording without new code.\n\nThe 3.6x in the title was measured at `JOBS=1` as I understand it; 2.5x here is at `JOBS=4`, so the two are not directly comparable and neither replaces the other."}
{"issue": 419, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWMwaA", "before": "Two follow-ups on the operation numbers above.\n\n**`QueryMissing` spawns nothing.** 56 ms with almost no spread looked like one `nix` exec per call, so I checked: `pynixd/goals/query_missing.py`, `goals/ensure.py`, `goals/engine.py` and `goals/build_derivation.py` hold no `subprocess`, no `create_subprocess` and no use of `nix_bin`. `QueryMissingPlanGoal._run` is a concurrent closure walk in an `anyio` task group, and each classification asks the upstream daemon over the socket. So the cost is round trips, and the plain daemon does the same walk against its own database in process. That is the shape to measure next, and it is a property of proxying rather than a defect.\n\n**Daemon start now has its own issue, nixidae/nixidae#79.** It carries the direct measurement of the 0.72 s, the debug-log breakdown of where it goes, the import profile, and the levers with what each one is worth \u2014 including one measured negative, so nobody runs it twice.\n"}
{"issue": 421, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPSi8w", "before": "Every `ekn deploy` should write a small metadata file describing the deploy itself, and ship it in both the source branch and the deploy branch.\n\n## What to record\n\n- **Rendering time** \u2014 wall-clock time to render (and, if it is cheap to separate, per-phase: eval, chart render, write).\n- **Hostname** \u2014 which machine produced the deploy. This is what you reach for when a deploy branch looks wrong and you want to know where it came from.\n\n## Where it goes\n\nA meta file (one per deploy, or one per unit \u2014 whichever matches how the manifests are laid out) committed to:\n\n- the **deploy branch**, so the rendered tree self-describes who rendered it and how long it took, and\n- the **source branch**, so the history of render times per host is reviewable and diffable next to the source that produced it.\n\n## Nix call stats\n\nEvaluation/call statistics (see nixidae/nixidae#334) would be valuable in the same file, but only if collecting them does not slow the deploy down. Measure the cost of collecting them first; if it is anything but negligible, leave them out of the per-deploy file and keep them in the benchmarking workflow nixidae/nixidae#334 describes instead.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- Format: plain JSON? An `ekn`-versioned schema so tooling can read it later?\n- One file per deploy or per unit?\n- Does writing to the source branch mid-deploy complicate the commit flow `ekn commit` already owns?"}
{"issue": 424, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPS2aw", "before": "Three live classes carry the name `DrvOutput`:\n\n| Class | What it holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| `nix_daemon_protocol.drv_output.DrvOutput` | a `WireScalar`: `<algo>:<hash>!<output>`, the id of a `Realisation` |\n| `pynixd.store_path.DrvOutput` | the same identifier, as four fields, with no wire codec |\n| `pynixd.drv_parser.DrvOutput` | one output entry of a parsed `.drv`: name, path, hash |\n\nThey are not equal, they do not hash alike, and `from pynixd.serde import\nDrvOutput` gives the first one while `from pynixd.store_path import\nDrvOutput` gives the second.\n\nFound while typing the test suites: four test files import one of the\npynixd classes and construct a `Realisation`, which declares the\nprotocol one. The fix there was `DrvOutput as SerdeDrvOutput`, which is\nthe spelling `SerdeDerivedPath` already set for the same collision on\nanother name. The alias hides the question rather than answering it.\n\nThis is the shape of nixidae/nixidae#392 (two `StorePath` classes) one name over, and nixidae/nixidae#422\nis the same kind of leftover naming. The work is to decide which of the\nthree is the identifier, give the other two names that say what they\nare, and drop the `Serde*` aliases that only exist because the names\ncollide."}
{"issue": 424, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWNVbA", "before": "## Correction: there are two classes, and the problem is worse than three names\n\n`pynixd.drv_parser` defines no `DrvOutput`. Line 46 is\n`from .store_path import DrvOutput, StorePath` \u2014 a re-export. Only two classes\ncarry the name:\n\n```\n$ grep -rn \"^class DrvOutput\" --include=\"*.py\" .\npynixd/store_path.py:17:class DrvOutput:\nnix-daemon-protocol/src/nix_daemon_protocol/drv_output.py:21:class DrvOutput(WireScalar):\n```\n\nSo this is not a three-way naming decision. **It is one class doing two jobs**,\nwhich is a harder problem than the one the issue states.\n\n## The two jobs\n\n`pynixd.store_path.DrvOutput` is built two ways, and the field sets are\ndisjoint:\n\n| built from | `hash_algo` / `hash_value` | `path` |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `DrvOutput(\"<algo>:<hash>!<out>\")` \u2014 a realisation id | the `hashDerivationModulo` | forced to `\"\"` (line 65) |\n| `parse_outputs()` \u2014 an ATerm `(name, path, hashAlgo, hash)` | the **output hash**, or empty | the output store path |\n\nMeasured against real derivations:\n\n```\nplain  name='out' hash_algo='' hash_value='' path='/nix/store/ak2i...-plain'\nfixed  name='out' hash_algo='sha256' hash_value='0000000000000000' path='/nix/store/ap9h...-fixed'\n```\n\nFor `fixed` those hash fields are the `outputHash` I supplied, not\n`hashDerivationModulo`. So **the class docstring is false for every instance\nthe parser makes**: \"The hash is the derivation's `hashDerivationModulo`\" is\ntrue only of the id form.\n\n## Two consequences, both measured\n\n**A parsed output stringifies into a realisation id that means nothing.**\n\n```\nparser plain: str=':!out'\n```\n\nAny code that turns a parser-made `DrvOutput` into a realisation lookup asks\nfor `:!out`.\n\n**An id compares equal to a parsed output.**\n\n```\nDrvOutput(\"sha256:0000!out\") == DrvOutput(hash_algo=\"sha256\", hash_value=\"0000\",\n                                          output_name=\"out\", path=\"/nix/store/bbb-fixed\")\n->  True\n```\n\n`__eq__` (line 102) reads `hash_algo`, `hash_value` and `output_name` and\nignores `path`. So the two concepts are `==` while meaning different things,\nand a set or a dict keyed on them merges them silently. That is the same shape\nas nixidae/nixidae#392, except that there the two classes were *not* equal and here they are \u2014\nwhich is the harder direction to find.\n\n## What this changes about the work\n\nThe issue says \"decide which of the three is the identifier\". There is no such\nchoice: `nix_daemon_protocol.drv_output.DrvOutput` is the identifier, it has\nthe wire codec, and it is the only one the protocol declares.\n\nThe work is instead:\n\n1. **Split** `pynixd.store_path.DrvOutput` into the identifier and the parsed\n   output. The parsed one is not an identifier at all \u2014 give it a name that\n   says so (`DerivationOutput`, beside `Derivation` in `drv_parser`) and drop\n   `path` from the identifier, which already forces it empty.\n2. **Then** merge the identifier into `nix_daemon_protocol`, the way nixidae/nixidae#392 did for\n   `StorePath`. That is what makes `SerdeDrvOutput` unnecessary \u2014 4 references.\n\nStep 1 is the one that fixes a defect rather than a name. 201 references across\n37 files, and `__eq__` changing meaning is the risk, so it wants its own change\nwith the field access audited rather than a rename pass.\n\nI have not started it. `SerdeDerivedPath` (9 references) is the same question\none name over \u2014 `pynixd.derived_path.DerivedPath` and the protocol's are also\ntwo classes \u2014 and is probably the same commit's shape."}
{"issue": 428, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWN1_g", "before": "> **Superseded \u2014 the suspect named here is the wrong one.** Both UML\n> userspaces panic, so the seccomp mode is not the variable; the cause is\n> io_uring. See the comment below. The reproduction and the upstream\n> survey stand; the diagnosis does not.\n\n## Reproduced, and the suspect has a name\n\nThe panic reproduces on demand with pynixd's suite under `seccomp=auto`.\nIdentical signature to the report, with the faulting process named:\n\n```\nKernel panic - not syncing: Kernel mode fault at addr 0xa0, ip 0x60255d69\nCPU: 0 UID: 1000 PID: 441 Comm: .pytest-wrapped Not tainted 7.2.5 nixidae/nixidae#373-NixOS\nCall Trace:\n [<60228351>] ? folio_remove_rmap_ptes+0x41/0x100\n [<602132b7>] ? __zap_vma_range+0x7e7/0x11b0\n [<60213fe5>] ? unmap_vmas+0x65/0xa0\n [<6023362b>] ? vms_complete_munmap_vmas+0xfb/0x260\n [<6021e955>] ? sys_munmap+0x15/0x30\n [<6005cf98>] ? handle_syscall+0x88/0xc0\n [<6006fc69>] ? userspace+0x1f9/0x7c0\n```\n\n`handle_syscall` under `userspace()` is the guest kernel servicing a\n`munmap` for the stub.\n\n## The suspect: UML's SECCOMP userspace\n\nGuests boot `seccomp=auto` and take it -- `Checking that seccomp filters\ncan be installed...OK`. That mode is newer than the ptrace one and does\nnot manage memory the same way.\n\nRead out of the 7.2.5 source rather than assumed:\n\n- `arch/um/kernel/skas/stub_exe.c` gates only the instruction pointer.\n  `STUB_MMAP_NR` and `__NR_munmap` then fall through to\n  `SECCOMP_RET_ALLOW` -- no check on their arguments.\n- UML's own `--help` says the filter \"is not (yet) restrictive enough to\n  prevent userspace from reading and writing all physical memory\".\n- `arch/um/os-Linux/skas/mem.c` branches on `using_seccomp`, and the\n  seccomp side carries an extra `syscall_fd_map` handed to the stub.\n\n## Upstream has no fix to pick up\n\n- [`um: skas: harden the seccomp userspace stub`][1] (June 2026) would\n  route mmap/munmap through `SECCOMP_RET_USER_NOTIF` and validate against\n  the guest page table. **Dropped** -- 11-30% overhead; on 1 July 2026 the\n  author agreed to abandon it.\n- [`pidfd_mmap()`/`pidfd_munmap()`][2] (July 2026) replaces it: the monitor\n  installs mappings directly, \"the stub never touches the fd or executes\n  mmap/munmap\". Still an **RFC**. It states the ptrace mode is unchanged.\n\nSo no newer kernel helps: 7.2.5 is already `linuxPackages_latest` here and\nthe fix is not merged anywhere.\n\n**Caveat.** Both series are framed against a *hostile* stub. pytest is not\nhostile. The same machinery could corrupt a well-behaved guest through an\nordinary bug, but upstream does not claim that, so this is a mechanism\nworth testing, not a diagnosis.\n\n[1]: https://ratatoskr.run/linux-um/2026/06/17156707/t\n[2]: https://ratatoskr.run/linux-um/2026/07/17244322/t\n\n## The lever\n\n`boot.uml.seccomp` (`auto` | `on` | `off`) now exists; `off` is the ptrace\nuserspace. Cost per the note in `backend.py`: a few percent of throughput\nand about five seconds of boot.\n\n## Trials\n\nRunning now: 5 per mode, alternating, panic detected from the log so a\ndead guest costs seconds rather than a 45-minute timeout. Standing at\n`auto` 1/1 PANIC. The table follows in a second comment.\n\n## Unrelated, found while measuring this\n\nA run killed by a signal used to orphan its guests -- one such UML kernel\nhad been using a whole core for 31 hours. Fixed: catchable signals unwind\ninto the existing teardown, and `PR_SET_PDEATHSIG` covers `SIGKILL`.\nMeasured at 0 survivors from 2 (uml) and 4 (qemu) processes, on TERM and\non KILL.\n\n"}
{"issue": 436, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPUFQw", "before": "## Summary\n\nThere is no way to see how large a node's Nix store has grown without\nshelling into the node. nixkube exports no metrics at all today -- no\n`prometheus_client`, no HTTP listener, nothing under `pkgs/nixkube/src/`\nthat reports a number. The only readings I could take were\n`talosctl usage` against each node by hand.\n\nA gauge for the node store size, per node, would make store growth\nvisible to whatever already scrapes the cluster.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nMeasured on a four-node Talos cluster, 2026-09-17:\n\n| node | node store |\n| --- | --- |\n| worker-0 | 1.59 GB |\n| worker-1 | 1.95 GB |\n| worker-2 | 2.31 GB |\n| worker-3 | 4.37 GB |\n\nOn Talos the store lands on the EPHEMERAL volume, shared with the\ncontainer image store and the kubelet. The kubelet collects its own\nimages at a threshold; the store is not a container image, so the kubelet\nhas no lever on it and no metric covers it. `node_filesystem_avail_bytes`\nshows the filesystem filling but cannot say which share is the store.\n\nThat gap is how nixidae/nixidae#435 stayed invisible for 33 hours. The store grew, the\nfilesystem alert fired against `/var`, and nothing pointed at the store\nas the growing part.\n\n## The expensive part, and a way around it\n\nA `du`-style walk over a multi-GB store is not something to do on a\nscrape interval.\n\n**The cheap route: the store already knows.** `nix path-info --store local\n--all --json` returns `narSize` for every path, straight out of the Nix\ndatabase, with no filesystem walk. `_run_gc_cycle` already makes exactly\nthat call once per GC cycle (`gc_task.py:51`) and reads `registrationTime`\noff it. So the size can be a by-product of work the daemon already does,\non a cadence that is already slow, at no extra cost:\n\n```python\nstore_size = sum(e.get(\"narSize\", 0) for e in path_info if isinstance(e, dict))\n```\n\nCaveat worth stating in whatever exports it: `narSize` is the NAR size,\nnot bytes on disk. It ignores filesystem block overhead and any hardlink\ndedup, so it is an approximation rather than a `du`. For watching growth\nand catching a runaway that is the right trade -- the trend is what\nmatters, not the last megabyte.\n\n**If a true on-disk figure is wanted**, then the request is: walk\nasynchronously, at low priority, and cache the result. Concretely --\n`os.scandir` in a thread so the event loop keeps serving CSI and NRI,\n`os.nice` and `ionice` (or `IOPRIO_CLASS_IDLE`) on that thread so it\nyields to real work, a TTL cache so a scrape never triggers a walk, and a\nrefresh interval measured in minutes. `cachetools` is already a\ndependency, so the caching needs nothing new.\n\nEither way the cached value should be what a reader sees, never a walk\nstarted by the read.\n\n## Transport\n\nThere is no HTTP server in the daemon today, so a `/metrics` endpoint\nmeans a new dependency and a new port. Alternatives that avoid both, in\ncase either fits the design better:\n\n- a node annotation or a CR status field, written on the same slow\n  cadence, scraped by whatever already reads Kubernetes objects;\n- a file the existing node-exporter textfile collector picks up, which\n  costs no dependency at all and inherits the node-exporter's scrape.\n\nNo preference from me -- the number being visible somewhere is the ask.\n\n## Suggested metric shape\n\n```\nnixkube_node_store_bytes{node=\"...\"}            current store size\nnixkube_node_store_paths{node=\"...\"}            path count, free from the same call\nnixkube_node_store_last_gc_timestamp{node=\"...\"} when a GC cycle last completed\n```\n\nThe last one is not about size, but it is the metric that would have\nsurfaced nixidae/nixidae#435 on day one: a GC timestamp that stops advancing says the\ncollector is stuck, which no size gauge can say on its own.\n\n## Context\n\nFiled alongside nixidae/nixidae#435, which is the reason the store on these nodes grew in\nthe first place. Versions: nixkube `develop` as of 2026-09-16, Talos\nv1.14.0, Kubernetes v1.36.0, IPv6-only cluster.\n"}
{"issue": 436, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWO21Q", "before": "## Done, the cheap route, in `4b51ffbe`\n\nThree series, exactly as you scoped them, all as a by-product of the\n`nix path-info --all --json` the GC cycle already makes:\n\n```\nnixkube_store_nar_bytes                      3072\nnixkube_store_paths                          2\nnixkube_gc_last_success_timestamp_seconds    1.789602613e+09\n```\n\nRead out of a real exposition, not only out of the registry.\n\nNo filesystem walk, no new call, no work on the scrape. The values move on the\nGC cadence, which is already slow.\n\n## Two deviations from your spec, both deliberate\n\n**`nixkube_store_nar_bytes`, not `nixkube_node_store_bytes`.** Your caveat is\nright and larger than it looks. Measured on this workstation: summing\n`narSize` over `ValidPaths` answered **952 GB for a file system of 268 GB**,\nbecause NAR sizes add without the hard links and the dedup the store on disk\nhas. The series now sits beside `nixkube_store_filesystem_size_bytes`, so a\nname reading \"store bytes\" invites subtracting one from the other and getting\nnonsense. The name says `nar` for that reason, and the help string says\n\"which over-counts disk use\". Rename it if you prefer yours -- the number is\nidentical.\n\n**No `node=\"...\"` label.** The scrape's target labels already carry the node\nunder Kubernetes service discovery, and a label that duplicates one of those\nis the usual way to end up with two spellings of the same node.\n\n## Transport was already there\n\n`/metrics` on port 9099 landed in `fdf764da`, with `prometheus.io/scrape`\nannotations behind `nixkube.metrics.annotations`. So the textfile-collector\nand node-annotation alternatives you offered are not needed.\n\nAlso there from that commit: `nixkube_store_filesystem_size_bytes` and\n`..._available_bytes`, from one `statvfs` on the scrape. That answers a\ndifferent question from the two above -- how full the disk is, rather than how\nbig the store is -- and the pair is what lets a dashboard say which share of\n`/var` the store holds. The `statvfs` costs 0.010 ms against 584 ms for the\nNAR sum over 141,749 paths, which is why it is the one read on the scrape.\n\n## On `nixkube_gc_last_success_timestamp_seconds`\n\nAgreed that this is the one that matters. It starts at 0 rather than absent,\nso an alert reads `== 0 or time() - it > N`, the way `node_boot_time_seconds`\nis used. A failed cycle leaves it alone: it says when collection last worked,\nnot when it last ran.\n\n`nixkube_gc_cycles_total{result=\"ok\"}` carries the same signal as a rate, and\n`gc_loop` now also logs `gc_stalled` at error level after\n`GC_STALL_CYCLES` (3) intervals with no completed cycle.\n\nThe four defects that made those cycles never complete are in nixidae/nixidae#435, fixed in\n`f3c0d403` and `e0e99491`.\n\nNot verified on a cluster -- this is a workstation with a unit suite. The\nfour-node reading is yours to repeat."}
{"issue": 436, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWO3eg", "before": "Already implemented on `develop` at `97b3b3fb` \u2014 raised by the nixkube session, verified here against the source rather than taken on trust.\n\n`metrics.py` carries what this issue asked for:\n\n- `nixkube_store_nar_bytes` and `nixkube_store_paths`, set from `_record_store_size` out of the `narSize` already present in the hourly `path-info` answer. That is the cheap route proposed above, with no filesystem walk added.\n- `nixkube_gc_last_success_timestamp_seconds` \u2014 the \"when did GC last work\" gauge, which is the one that would have surfaced nixidae/nixidae#435 on day one.\n- Beyond the ask: `nixkube_gc_cycles_total`, `nixkube_gc_paths_deleted_total`, `nixkube_gc_cycle_duration_seconds`, and `nixkube_store_filesystem_size_bytes` beside the NAR figure.\n\nThe `narSize` caveat I raised is recorded upstream with a number I did not have: `metrics.py:115` notes that `sum(narSize)` over `ValidPaths` answered 952 GB on a 268 GB filesystem. That is a better statement of the caveat than mine \u2014 it is not a small error, and the filesystem gauge beside it is the right answer to it.\n\nLeaving this open for whoever closes it against a release. Happy to post a scrape from a four-node Talos cluster once the fixed build is deployed there."}
{"issue": 437, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPULeA", "before": "## Summary\n\n`ekn deploy`'s pre-git-push cache push is silent while it runs and says\nalmost nothing when it finishes. The whole report is one line, after the\nfact:\n\n```\npushed /nix/store/890ckv...-nixkube-node-paths to ssh-ng://nix@pynixd.lab2.ch.se.eu.org\n```\n\nNo path count, no byte count, and nothing at all between \"starting\" and\n\"done\". On a fast link that is fine. On a slow or broken one the deploy\nis indistinguishable from a hang, and there is no way to tell afterwards\nwhether it moved 3 paths or 300.\n\n## Why it matters here\n\nThe push runs *before* the git commit that triggers GitOps sync, and it\nhas to succeed, because CSI-mounted store paths named in the manifests\nabout to be applied must already be substitutable. So it is on the\ncritical path of every deploy, and it is the step most likely to be slow:\nit is the only one that talks to a machine over the network.\n\nConcretely, I spent an evening on a suspected slow push of an 801 MiB\nclosure and could not tell from the output whether the closure was being\nstreamed whole or whether the destination already had most of it. The\nanswer was available in one second from outside:\n\n```\n$ nix path-info --store ssh-ng://nix@<cacheTo> <paths>\n```\n\n3 of 19 paths missing, 184 MiB. The deploy knew this and did not say it.\n\n## What would help, in order of value\n\n1. **Say what is missing before sending it.** `queryValidPaths` against\n   the destination is what `copy_closure` does first anyway, so the\n   numbers already exist inside the call. Logging \"3 of 19 paths,\n   184 MiB\" before the transfer starts turns a silent wait into a\n   progress expectation.\n2. **Say what was sent when it finishes.** Path count, bytes and elapsed\n   time, next to the destination URI.\n3. **Some sign of life during a long transfer.** A periodic line, or\n   whatever nanopynix can surface from Nix's own progress callback.\n\nNone of this changes behaviour. It is the difference between a deploy\nthat looks hung and a deploy that says it is copying 184 MiB.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `substitute_on_destination` already defaults to `True` and\n  `_push_ekn_cache` takes the default, so the destination does get the\n  chance to fetch from its own caches. This request is only about\n  reporting.\n- `cache_timeout_sec` already bounds the copy (issue nixidae/nixidae#381), so the failure\n  mode is \"eventually fails\" rather than \"hangs for ever\". The gap is\n  that nothing distinguishes the two while it is happening.\n\nVersions: `ekn` 0.1.0 as packaged in the dev shell of a downstream repo,\nnanopynix in-process store, destination `ssh-ng://`.\n"}
{"issue": 449, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPVZ6A", "before": "`pynixd daemon` needs 0.72 s before a client can connect. Nix's own daemon binds its socket within one 0.1 s poll, every time. Issue nixidae/nixidae#419 measures what that costs: Nix's functional suite starts a daemon 409 times, so the difference is 257.5 s of the 329.1 s that pynixd adds to the suite. That is 78% of the whole overhead of the proxy, and none of it is request handling.\n\n## The measurement\n\nTwo instruments agree.\n\n**Direct.** Fork `pynixd daemon` over an empty store, poll the socket every millisecond, connect to it. Five runs: 0.614, 0.716, 0.680, 0.738, 0.786 s. Median **0.716 s**. SIGTERM to exit: 0.113 s, five times, with no spread.\n\n**From the suite.** `startDaemon` polls every 0.1 s (`tests/functional/common/functions.sh:82`), and the scripts run under `bash -x`, so the polls are countable. Over 409 starts, control needs 409 polls and pynixd needs 2579, which puts pynixd's socket at 0.53\u20130.63 s. `killDaemon` needs 405 polls for control and 810 for pynixd.\n\nThe two differ by about 0.1 s. The direct number measures from `fork` to a connected socket over a cold store; the suite measures from its own first check, which happens after its own `fork`. Both say the same thing.\n\n## Where the 0.72 s goes\n\n`PYNIXD_LOG_LEVEL=debug` puts a timestamp on each step. From the first log line to `unix_server_listening` is **0.229 s**:\n\n| at | event |\n| --- | --- |\n| 0.000 s | `spawning_managed_daemon` |\n| 0.125 s | `daemon_socket_ready` \u2014 the upstream `nix daemon` is up and the protocol is negotiated |\n| 0.196 s | `pynixd_schema_migrated` \u2014 the store database is open and current |\n| 0.228 s | `scheduler_started`, `gc_loop_started` |\n| 0.229 s | `unix_server_listening` |\n\nSo **0.487 s happens before the first log line**: the interpreter, the imports, `load_settings()` and `setup_logging()`. `pynixd --help` costs 0.41\u20130.45 s on its own, which places almost all of it in the imports.\n\n`PYTHONPROFILEIMPORTTIME=1 pynixd --help` names 298 ms of imports, cumulative for `pynixd.__main__`:\n\n| module | cumulative |\n| --- | --- |\n| `nix_daemon_protocol` | 112.6 ms |\n| `pynixd.config` | 40.6 ms |\n| `asyncio` | 37.8 ms |\n| `pynixd.scheduler` | 26.0 ms |\n| `anyio` | 17.8 ms |\n| `pynixd.metrics` (prometheus_client) | 17.4 ms |\n| `pynixd.wire` | 15.6 ms |\n\nThe 112.6 ms of `nix_daemon_protocol` is about 60 operation modules, each 0.6\u20131.9 ms of its own. Bytecode is cached in the store, so this is the class bodies running, not compilation: the package declares about 120 pydantic models and each one costs field collection at class creation.\n\n`--help` pays for the scheduler, the metrics endpoint, the goal engine and every wire model. It needs none of them.\n\n## Levers, with what each one is worth\n\n| lever | measured |\n| --- | --- |\n| imports | 270\u2013300 ms, and about 150 ms of that is `asyncio`, `anyio`, pydantic and structlog, which a daemon cannot avoid |\n| `defer_build=True` on `WireModel` | **\u221220 ms.** Measured, not landed. See below. |\n| spawn and negotiate with the upstream `nix daemon` | 125 ms |\n| open and migrate the store database | 70 ms |\n| start the scheduler, bind the socket | 30 ms |\n| shutdown after SIGTERM | 113 ms, one 0.1 s poll of the suite per start |\n\n**The negative result, so nobody runs it twice.** `WireModel.model_config` can carry `defer_build=True`, and pydantic then builds a model's core schema on first validation rather than at class creation. The wire path never validates \u2014 `from_reader` builds the object with `__new__` and `object.__setattr__`, and `to_writer` reads it with `getattr` \u2014 so the deferral is safe on paper. It cuts the import of `pynixd.__main__` from 270 ms to 249 ms, measured three times each in the dev shell. That is 8% of the import and 3% of the start, for a semantic change to the base model of the protocol package. I did not land it.\n\nSo the field collection, and not the schema build, is the per-class cost, and that is inherent to the declarative design rather than a setting.\n\n## What is worth doing\n\nThe honest ceiling is about 0.72 s down to 0.55 s, which takes 70 s off a 587 s suite run. It is not a small piece of work:\n\n1. **Import what a subcommand needs.** `pynixd.scheduler`, `pynixd.metrics` and the store backends reach `__main__` through `pynixd.instance`, which `pynixd/__init__.py` imports at package import. Nothing can import `pynixd` cheaply today.\n2. **Reconsider the 125 ms upstream spawn.** It is over a third of the work that happens after the imports, and the daemon is not serving anyone until it finishes.\n3. **Look at the 113 ms shutdown.** `Server.close()` tears down eight things in sequence and ends with the upstream process group. The suite pays a 0.1 s poll for it, 409 times, which is 40.9 s.\n\nNone of this changes a long-lived pynixd, which pays the start cost once. It changes the suite, CI, and anything that runs pynixd per invocation.\n"}
{"issue": 450, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPVgDA", "before": "Deploying to a cluster currently means pushing a branch a GitOps engine\nreads. When the remote is unwritable (#27 -- an archived GitLab during a\ncompany migration, open-ended) there is no way to get a change onto a\ncluster at all, and on a dev cluster the git round-trip is the slowest part\nof the loop even when it works.\n\nThis is the design for applying the whole generated set directly, with the\nGitOps engine paused, converging rather than aborting on the first failure.\nEvery number below is measured on a live cluster (nixlab2, ArgoCD v3.4.6,\n919 generated objects).\n\nIt is **not** nixidae/nixidae#357 Phase 2. That one orchestrates tofu -> kubeapply -> tofu\nacross stages. This is \"apply what we already render, instead of handing it\nto git\", which is orthogonal and can compose with nixidae/nixidae#357 later.\n\n## Two of the four requirements are already built\n\n**1. Excluding the bootstrap stage needs no new option.** A unit that\ndeclares `modules` is a nested easykubenix instance, and `gitops.nix:326`\nalready says its objects \"never reach this instance's\n`kubernetes.generated`, so a plain `ekn kubeapply` (or `ekn validate`) does\nnot see them -- only `ekn kubeapply --target <name>` does.\"\n\nMeasured on nixlab2:\n\n```\nkubernetes.generated                        919 objects\nkubernetes.deploymentUnits.bootstrap        120 objects, none of them in generated\n```\n\nSo `ekn kubeapply` with no `--target` is *already* exactly \"every unit the\nGitOps engine syncs, and nothing from the bootstrap stage\". The\ndiscriminator is `deployment.units.<name>.modules != []`, not a name, so it\ngeneralises to any future hand-applied unit with no further work.\n\n**2. \"Helm order\" is already there, and already better than Helm's.**\n`ekn.resourcePriority` defaults to Helm's InstallOrder, `apply.barriers()`\ngroups by it, and `DEFAULT_BARRIER_PRIORITY = 1000` deliberately does *not*\nfollow Helm's \"unknown kind is last\" rule -- the comment records why, with a\nnumber: sorting unknowns behind the admission webhook configurations cost\nroughly six minutes of stalled apply on kube-prometheus-stack. Nothing to do\nhere.\n\nWhat is missing is only the failure policy *inside* a barrier, the engine\npause, and the fast path.\n\n## The converging apply\n\nToday `apply_and_prune` walks barriers and any `apply_one` raise aborts the\nrun mid-way. For a whole-cluster apply that is the worst outcome: half\napplied, nothing reported about the other half.\n\nProposed, and deliberately simple:\n\n- Within a barrier, apply every object and **collect** failures instead of\n  raising.\n- Drain the clean queue, then retry the failed set with backoff.\n- **The settle timer should be \"no progress for N seconds\", not \"N seconds\n  after the queue empties.\"** A barrier that is slowly succeeding -- an\n  operator coming up, a webhook becoming ready -- is making progress, and a\n  fixed deadline from the moment the clean queue emptied cuts it off. Reset\n  the timer on every successful apply.\n- Move to the next barrier when the current one is clean, or when the timer\n  expires and `--keep-going` is set.\n- End with one report listing every object that never applied and the last\n  error for each. **Exit non-zero if any remain** (see nixidae/nixidae#383 below).\n\n### The error ladder, derived from what this cluster actually contains\n\n\"Retry with fallback\" needs a classification, and guessing it produces a\nconverging apply that spends five minutes retrying a schema error. This\ntable comes from the object set above.\n\n**Retry unchanged** -- the cluster has not caught up yet:\n- `NotFound` on the *kind* (a CRD from an earlier barrier not yet served --\n  there are 188 CRDs in this set)\n- `NotFound` on the namespace (21 Namespaces, and ordering across barriers\n  is by kind, not by dependency)\n- webhook connection refused / timeout / 500 (6 ValidatingWebhookConfigurations)\n- 429, 503\n\n**Retry with `force=True`** -- and this one is load-bearing, not\nhypothetical. Every live object on a GitOps cluster is owned by the engine's\nfield manager. Measured:\n\n```\n$ kubectl get deploy -n vm victoria-metrics-operator \\\n    -o jsonpath='{range .metadata.managedFields[*]}{.manager} op={.operation}{\"\\n\"}{end}'\nargocd-controller op=Apply\nkube-controller-manager op=Update\n```\n\n`ekn` applies as `ekn`. So the *first* direct apply of an ArgoCD-managed\ncluster produces a 409 on essentially every object. Without this rung the\nmode does not work at all on its main use case.\n\n**Recreate, only behind `--allow-recreate`** -- 422 on an immutable field.\nThe common case is a completed Job: `spec.template` is immutable, and there\nare 6 Jobs in the generated set, so a second apply fails on every one of\nthem forever. Without this rung a converging apply can never reach a clean\nqueue on this cluster. Needs a deny list that recreate never touches --\nPersistentVolumeClaim, Secret, Namespace, CustomResourceDefinition, and an\nopt-out annotation.\n\n**Terminal, never retry** -- 403 Forbidden, and 422 that is not\nimmutability. Retrying an RBAC or schema error for the full settle window\nconverts a clear failure into a slow one.\n\n## Pausing the GitOps engine\n\nDo not patch Application specs. Measured on nixlab2 this morning while\ninvestigating nixidae/nixidae#445: the root Application owns every child\n(`argocd.argoproj.io/tracking-id: root:argoproj.io/Application:argocd/<name>`)\nwith `selfHeal: true`, so disabling automated sync on a child alone is\nreverted within seconds. You must patch root first, then the child, and\nrestore both -- four live spec mutations for one component, and a missed\nrestore leaves the cluster silently no longer following git.\n\n**Scale the reconciler to zero instead.** On nixlab2 that is one object:\n\n```\nStatefulSet/argo-cd-argocd-application-controller   replicas=1\n```\n\nAtomic, reversible, pauses root and children together, and there is nothing\nfor ArgoCD to fight because the thing that would fight is the thing that is\nstopped.\n\nKept generic so `ekn` never learns what an Application is:\n\n```nix\ndeployment.engine.pause = [\n  { kind = \"StatefulSet\"; namespace = \"argocd\"; name = \"argo-cd-argocd-application-controller\"; }\n];\n```\n\n`ekn` reads the current replica count, scales to 0, and restores exactly\nwhat it read. Flux would name its controllers here; kluctl names nothing.\n\nTwo properties that are not optional:\n\n- **Crash-safe resume.** Write the observed replica counts somewhere on the\n  cluster *before* scaling, so `ekn devdeploy --resume` can restore after a\n  laptop dies mid-run. A run that dies between pause and resume leaves a\n  cluster that looks healthy and has stopped following git.\n- **Say it on exit.** \"ArgoCD is paused; the cluster is ahead of git by N\n  objects; run `--resume`.\" And `ekn deploy --push` should refuse, or at\n  minimum warn hard, while that marker exists -- otherwise the branch moves\n  and nothing syncs it.\n\n## The fast path\n\nAnnotate every rendered object with a hash of itself, then read the live\nannotation and skip the apply when it matches. On a dev cluster that trades\n\"has this drifted on the cluster\" for a loop that is almost all reads.\n\n**Compute it in Nix, at the attrs -> manifest step, and the reason is\nArgoCD.** If Python computed it at apply time, the annotation would only\nexist on objects `ekn` itself last applied -- so the first fast run after\nany ArgoCD sync would find no annotation anywhere and apply all 919. Put it\nin the render and the GitOps branch carries it, ArgoCD applies it, and fast\nmode works against a cluster ArgoCD is managing. That is the whole value.\n\n`kubernetes.rawFiles` are the exception: they are parsed in Python precisely\nbecause `builtins.toJSON` reorders keys, so they hash in Python at load.\nSame annotation, one implementation per producer.\n\n**What the hash covers:** the object as rendered, minus the hash annotation\nitself. Explicitly *not* `ekn.dev/environment`, which `ekn` stamps at apply\ntime -- so the same object applied by ArgoCD and by `ekn` hashes the same.\n\n**Read it with one LIST per kind, not 919 GETs.** This works because\neasykubenix already renders the unit label into the manifests, so it is\npresent whoever applied the object -- confirmed on a live ArgoCD-applied\nDeployment:\n\n```\n\"ekn.dev/deployment-unit\":\"vm-operator\"\n```\n\nSo: LIST each kind by that label, build `(namespace, kind, name) -> hash`,\ndiff against the desired set. ~60 kinds instead of 919 round trips, and that\nis what makes the mode actually fast rather than merely cheaper.\n\n### Three things that will bite\n\n1. **Fast mode and `--prune` are mutually exclusive.** A skipped apply never\n   stamps `ekn.dev/environment`, and prune selects by exactly that label. So\n   fast mode plus prune deletes objects that are present and correct. Make\n   passing both an error, not a warning.\n2. **The first render after this lands rewrites all 919 objects**, because\n   an annotation is part of the object. One full resync, expected, worth\n   announcing rather than discovering.\n3. **Name the flag for what it assumes.** The hash proves the *desired*\n   manifest has not changed since the annotation was written. It proves\n   nothing about the live object, which a controller or a person may have\n   edited. `--assume-unchanged` says that; `--fast` does not.\n\n## Gates\n\n`checks.kubeapply` already boots a kubeadm cluster under User-Mode Linux and\nis deliberately outside `checks.all` because a control plane costs minutes.\nMost of what is proposed here does not need one: the error ladder and the\nsettle timer are decided by what the API server *returns*, so they test\nagainst a fake `Api` returning scripted errors, in `checks.all`, where the\nbugs will actually be.\n\nNegative controls worth insisting on, because each of these fails silently\nin the direction of looking healthy:\n\n- a 403 is **not** retried -- the run gives up immediately;\n- a permanently failing object ends the run **non-zero** after the settle\n  window, rather than being reported as applied;\n- fast mode does **not** skip an object whose rendered hash changed;\n- `--assume-unchanged` together with `--prune` is refused;\n- resume restores the replica count that was **observed**, not a hardcoded 1.\n\nExtend `checks.kubeapply` with one end-to-end converge containing a\ndeliberately failing object, and leave the rest fast.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n**#21 is a hard blocker, not a related issue.** `ekn` exiting 0 after an\nunhandled exception turns a half-applied cluster into a green one. A\nconverging apply reports partial success by design, so its exit code is the\nentire interface for \"did this work\". Fix nixidae/nixidae#383 first.\n\n(Separately: `_git_push` already does `raise SystemExit(1)` correctly. I\nmisread a *pipeline* exit status through `| tail` and reported ekn as\nexiting 0 on a push failure -- that was wrong, and nixidae/nixidae#383 is about unhandled\nexceptions, which is a different path and still live.)\n\n**#19 matters more here than for a git deploy.** `ekn deploy` pushes the\ncache before it pushes the branch, deliberately, so CSI-mounted store paths\nare substitutable before anything syncs. A direct apply has no such\nordering unless it is built in. On a cluster using nixkube's CSI, an\nunfetchable store path is a hard mount failure.\n\n## Naming\n\n`ekn devdeploy` composes the existing primitives and says what it is for.\n`ekn kubeapply --converge --pause-engine` keeps it as flags on the command\nthat already does the applying. No strong opinion; the flags matter more\nthan the name.\n"}
{"issue": 450, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWQoFQ", "before": "**Correction: nixidae/nixidae#383 is not a blocker.** The Prerequisites section above says\n\"fix nixidae/nixidae#383 first\". That is wrong, and I verified it rather than taking the\ncorrection on trust.\n\nAgainst the `ekn` this repo's dev shell builds:\n\n```\n$ ekn -A nonexistent.attr render\nValueError: specify --file or --flake\nEXIT=1\n```\n\nThat is the exact case nixidae/nixidae#383 opens with, and it exits 1. The serious case is\ncovered too -- a failed cache push goes through `_report_nix_error`\n(`cli.py:89`), typed `NoReturn` and raising `SystemExit(1)`, so `Deploy.run`\ncannot fall through to `_finalize_commit` after it. There is already a\nguard: `tests/test_cli.py::TestTheExitCode`, which asserts on a real\nsubprocess's exit code rather than `pytest.raises(SystemExit)`, for the\nreason its own docstring gives -- an in-process check cannot see the step\nthat was wrong, because everything up to `sys.exit` was already correct.\n\nSo the exit code is trustworthy and the converging apply can be built on it.\nEverything else in Prerequisites stands: nixidae/nixidae#371 in particular matters more for\na direct apply than for a git deploy, because `ekn deploy` pushes the cache\nbefore the branch deliberately and a direct apply has no such ordering\nunless it is built in.\n\nThe negative control in Gates -- \"a permanently failing object ends the run\nnon-zero\" -- is unchanged and is still the one that matters, since a\nconverging apply reports partial success by design."}
{"issue": 450, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWQotA", "before": "Taking the implementation half. One correction to the ladder before I build it, because it removes a rung.\n\n## The `force=True` rung is already unconditional, so the 409 it recovers from does not happen\n\n`ssa_apply` takes `force: bool = True` (`ekn/src/ekn/apply.py:153`), and that default has been there since 2026-08-10. Every real apply path uses it:\n\n- `apply_one` (`apply.py:208`) calls `ssa_apply(obj, field_manager=field_manager)` and passes no `force`, so it forces.\n- `apply_one` is the only \"put this object on the cluster\" primitive \u2014 `apply_and_prune`'s barrier loop and `ekn.sops.ensure_age_identities` both go through it.\n- The only other caller is `clusterdiff.py:172`, which is `dry_run=True` and also forces.\n\nNothing in the tree ever applies with `force=False`.\n\nThe cluster measurement is right and the inference from it is not. ArgoCD genuinely owns those fields \u2014 `argocd-controller op=Apply` on the victoria-metrics-operator Deployment is not in doubt. But a server-side apply with `force=true` does not return 409 on a field-manager conflict; it takes the field. So the first direct apply of an ArgoCD-managed cluster does not produce 409s today, and the mode's main use case already works without a fallback rung.\n\n**What to do with the rung.** Drop it as written. A 409 that does reach us now means something else \u2014 a namespace that is terminating, or a resourceVersion conflict on a non-apply verb \u2014 and \"retry with force\" is the wrong response to both, because force is already on. I would rather classify a 409 as retry-unchanged with the rest of the \"cluster has not caught up\" family than keep a rung whose recovery is a no-op.\n\n**One thing worth deciding rather than inheriting.** Unconditional force means this mode silently takes every field ArgoCD owns and reports nothing about it. For a dev-deploy that is the intent. But it is also the answer to \"has this drifted on the cluster\", which nixidae/nixidae#450 says fast mode trades away \u2014 here the forcing apply throws that information away even in slow mode. If you want the converge report to name the objects it took from another manager, the way to get it is a `force=False` attempt first and a force retry on 409 \u2014 which is the rung as designed, but for the opposite reason to the one given: not because it is needed to work, but because it is the only way to see what was taken. That is a product decision, so I am not making it. Say the word and it is a flag.\n\n## What I am building first\n\nThe converging apply with the ladder, against a fake `Api` in `checks.all`, no control plane \u2014 the sequence solid-kubernetes suggested, for the reason given: it carries the most design risk and needs the least infrastructure.\n\nI will take the three negative controls as written. On the settle timer I agree with \"no progress for N seconds\" over \"N seconds after the clean queue empties\", and I will build it so the predicate is one function either way, so Carl's answer changes one place.\n\n## Also already true, so not in scope\n\n`ekn kubeapply` with no `--target` already excludes bootstrap units, and the barrier ordering already exists \u2014 both confirmed independently of the nixlab2 numbers by reading `gitops.nix:82`/`:326` and `apply.barriers()`. Agreed with both.\n"}
{"issue": 450, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWQrkg", "before": "## Correction: re-applying a terminating namespace does not prolong it\n\nI reported, as an open observation, that a namespace I had re-applied\nmid-deletion sat in Terminating for over 60 s while a control was gone in 5 s.\nThat comparison was confounded and the conclusion it suggested is wrong.\n\nControlled repeat, three arms, identical setup each time -- a namespace\nholding one pod that ignores SIGTERM, deleted with `--wait=false`, then the\npod force-removed so only the namespace itself is left to finalize:\n\n```\nA  never re-applied                        gone after 10s  (0 re-applies)\nB  re-applied once, right after delete     gone after 10s  (1 re-apply)\nC  re-applied every 5s, as a retry would   gone after 11s  (2 re-applies)\n```\n\nNo effect. The 60 s in the original observation was the pod's own grace\nperiod still running: in that run I force-removed the pod roughly 35 s after\ndeleting the namespace, and in the control I removed it immediately. The\nre-apply was not the variable.\n\n### What this settles for the design\n\nRe-queueing an object whose apply returned a `deletionTimestamp`, and\nre-applying it on each attempt, is safe. It does not keep the object alive\nand it does not starve the retry that is waiting for the namespace to go\naway. The apply is simply inert until the object is gone -- the API server\nanswers 200 with a `Warning:` header and changes nothing that matters.\n\nSo the re-queue needs no special back-off-without-applying path for this\nreason. Having one is still reasonable for request volume under N concurrent\nworkers, but it is not a correctness requirement, and the simpler\nre-apply-and-re-check is not the harmful option I implied it might be.\n\nFinding nixidae/nixidae#143 itself is unchanged and still the one to prioritise: an apply that\nreturns an object carrying `metadata.deletionTimestamp` has not converged,\nand counting it as applied is what makes a run end non-zero while blaming the\nwrong object.\n\n### Method note\n\nThe first comparison was one run against one run, with a difference in\nprocedure I did not control for. The three arms above take about a minute\nin total. A measurement that decides a branch in someone else's code is\nworth repeating before it is sent.\n"}
{"issue": 453, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPVysg", "before": "`pynixd` reads a user configuration file and has **no default location for\nit**. Resolve one with\n[`platformdirs`](https://platformdirs.readthedocs.io/).\n\n`python3Packages.platformdirs` 4.10.0 is in the pinned nixpkgs, so this is\none line in the package inputs.\n\n## Today\n\n`_ConfigFileSource` (`pynixd/config.py:478`) loads settings from a JSON\nfile, and the only way to name that file is `PYNIXD_CONFIG`:\n\n```python\nconfig: Path | None = None          # config.py:518\n...\nenv_val = self.current_state.get(\"config\")\nif env_val is None:\n    return {}                       # config.py:496-497\n```\n\nSo a user who writes a configuration file and does not also export\n`PYNIXD_CONFIG` gets **silence**: the file is not read, nothing says it was\nnot read, and every setting falls back to its built-in default. The\nmachinery to read a config file is all there; only the default path is\nmissing.\n\nThis is the opposite of the problem in nanopynix (Lillecarl/nanopynix#305),\nwhich resolves a default but does it by hand in five places. Same fix.\n\n```python\nfrom platformdirs import user_config_path\n\n# config.py:518\nconfig: Path | None = None\n# resolved when unset:\nuser_config_path(\"pynixd\") / \"config.json\"\n```\n\nVerified against the pinned package: `user_config_path` exists in 4.10.0,\nand `XDG_CONFIG_HOME=/tmp/probe` gives `/tmp/probe/pynixd`. It also handles\nthe two cases a hand-rolled `Path.home() / \".config\"` gets wrong \u2014 a\nvariable set to the empty string, and one set to a relative path, both of\nwhich the XDG specification says must be treated as unset.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `PYNIXD_CONFIG` keeps taking precedence. `platformdirs` decides the\n  default, not the policy.\n- A missing file stays a non-error \u2014 a user who never wrote one is the\n  ordinary case, and that is already how `_ConfigFileSource` behaves\n  (`config.py:500-501`).\n- A file that exists and does not parse should stay an error. Today\n  `json.load` raises, which is right.\n- Think about whether a **system-wide** location belongs beside it.\n  `pynixd` runs as a daemon (`unix_path` defaults to\n  `/run/pynixd/pynixd.sock`, `config.py:522`), and a daemon's configuration\n  usually lives in `/etc` rather than in the invoking user's home.\n  `platformdirs.site_config_path` covers that half; a daemon started by\n  systemd has whatever `HOME` the unit gives it, which may be nothing\n  useful.\n\nThat last point is the one worth a decision before writing code: for a\nservice, a user-level default may be the wrong shape entirely, and `/etc/\npynixd/config.json` with the user-level path as a fallback may be better.\n\n## Done when\n\n`pynixd` started with no `PYNIXD_CONFIG` reads the file at the resolved\ndefault, and a run with `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` pointed at a temporary directory\nreads from there."}
{"issue": 454, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWRIAA", "before": "## Correction: those percentages are calls, and calls are not seconds\n\nMy comment above leads with \"11.0% of a real render's function calls\" and then reports the work as a result. `solid-kubernetes` has since timed the whole day end to end on a quiet machine, ten runs each arm, render byte-identical:\n\n```\nto_python(kubernetes.generated)   8.204 \u00b10.162  ->  7.946 \u00b10.193    -3.1%\nwall                             10.723 \u00b10.159  -> 10.482 \u00b10.227    -2.2%\n```\n\nThat covers everything: the branch reorder, the untyped branch, twenty dashboards carried whole, cilium's pruning moved to build time, and both marker walkers. **About 30% of the render's evaluator calls went away and bought 3% of the time.**\n\n### Why the exchange rate is that bad\n\n- A module-system call costs roughly **0.1 \u00b5s** \u2014 ~2.9 M calls for 0.26 s.\n- **41%** of evaluator time is package instantiation. 3,334 derivations per render, 20 of them chart renders. No walk in `lib/default.nix` touches any of it.\n- About **2.8 s of the 8.2 s stage is outside the evaluator altogether** \u2014 IFD realisation, marshalling 919 objects, and every nanopynix primop. Neither profiler attributes primop time: the eval profiler cannot see inside one, and `EKN_PROFILE` sees the call but not the work.\n\nSo the ratio here was about **ten to one against**, not the rough parity a percentage invites you to assume.\n\n### What I did wrong\n\nNot the measurement \u2014 the framing. I titled a results write-up with a call count and put `-43.6%` and `-11.0%` into a source comment beside the words \"measured on a real render\". Both figures are correct and both are calls. A reader meets a percentage and reads time.\n\nThe fix is in the comment above `hasMarker`: the ratio now sits next to the numbers, so the next person to open that file meets the caveat before the finding.\n\n### What still stands\n\nThe work is correct, the render is byte-identical, and `kubeValueType` is no longer the first thing a profile points at. A call count remains the right instrument for *where* work happens and for a machine too loaded to time. Converting one into an expected saving is the step that does not survive contact with a stopwatch.\n\n### Where the time actually is, and it is not here\n\n- **~2.8 s outside the evaluator** \u2014 the largest unmeasured block. YAML parsing lives in it at 75 ms/MB, which is nixidae/nixidae#460's territory.\n- **41% in package instantiation**, with `nixkube/daemonset.nix:120` the single largest demand point at 20% of evaluator time. That one is nixkube's, not easykubenix's.\n\nAnything further in `lib/` is worth roughly a tenth of what its call count suggests. The `objectType` triple in `kubeValueType.nix`, at 0.3% of calls, is about 0.03% of the time \u2014 which settles it as tidiness rather than optimisation."}
{"issue": 456, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPWFbQ", "before": "`types.oneOf` is a **left fold of `either`**, and `either`'s v2 merge forces its first branch's `headError` before it looks at the second. A value that matches at position *k* therefore pays *k* branch checks.\n\nThat makes `kubeValueType`'s branch list a performance decision, and the current order is close to the worst case for Kubernetes data.\n\n```nix\nbaseType = types.oneOf [\n  nullType        # 1\n  types.bool      # 2\n  types.int       # 3\n  types.float     # 4\n  types.str       # 5\n  types.path      # 6\n  (namedListOf valueType)   # 7\n  objectType      # 8\n];\n```\n\n**The two most common node types are checked last, and `null` is checked first by every single value.**\n\n## The distribution, counted rather than guessed\n\nEvery node of a real rendered object set (solid-kubernetes' `nixlab2`, 919 objects):\n\n| node type | share | position today |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| string | 58.8% | 5 |\n| attribute set | 32.1% | **8, last** |\n| list | 5.1% | 7 |\n| int | 2.8% | 3 |\n| bool | 1.1% | 2 |\n| null | **0.02%** (44 nodes) | **1, first** |\n| float | 0% | 4 |\n\n269,929 nodes in total. Expected branch checks per node: **5.97 today, 1.55 ordered by frequency.**\n\n## Measured\n\nAgainst the real `kubeValueType`, read from its store path with the branch list substituted, merging the real tree. Identical merged result both ways.\n\nOn the set the type **actually walks** (CRDs excluded \u2014 see below), 31,939 nodes:\n\n| branch order | time |\n| --- | --- |\n| today's | 0.490 / 0.512 s |\n| by frequency | 0.420 / 0.422 s |\n\n**16%, about 0.08 s per render.**\n\nOn a CRD-heavy value (188 CRDs, 237,963 nodes), which is what this type costs if `kubernetes.crds` is ever removed:\n\n| branch order | time |\n| --- | --- |\n| today's | 2.994 / 3.027 s |\n| by frequency | 2.318 / 2.386 s |\n\n**23%, about 0.7 s.** So the win scales with how much opaque data goes through the type.\n\n## The order to use\n\n```nix\nbaseType = types.oneOf [\n  types.str\n  (namedListOf valueType)\n  objectType\n  types.int\n  types.bool\n  nullType\n  types.float\n  types.path\n];\n```\n\n**Two orderings are load-bearing and must not be sorted away:**\n\n- `types.str` **before** `types.path`. `path.check` accepts a string beginning with `/`, so every absolute-path-looking string in a manifest \u2014 and there are many \u2014 would otherwise merge as a path.\n- `namedListOf` **before** `objectType`, as the file already documents.\n\n## A second, smaller one in the same area\n\n`lib/default.nix`'s marker predicates are **19.7% of all 13.2 M calls** in a full render.\n\n```nix\nisNamedList = value: lib.isAttrs value && (value._type or null) == namedListType;\nisNumberedList = value: lib.isAttrs value && (value._type or null) == numberedListType;\nisMarkedList = value: isNamedList value || isNumberedList value;\n```\n\n`isMarkedList` asks `isAttrs` twice and reads `_type` twice. In a full render that is **238,025 duplicate `isAttrs` calls**, visible in a frequency-0 profile as `isAttrs@40:24` and `isAttrs@41:27` at 315,488 and 315,419. Hoisting both into one `let` removes them. About 2% of all calls, and free to do.\n\n(This is nixidae/nixidae#454's territory; noting the specific duplication here because it is one line.)\n\n## Repro\n\nThe harness substitutes the branch list into the real file rather than copying it, and **asserts the substitution changed something** \u2014 my first version's anchor did not match the file's indentation, so the \"reordered\" side silently evaluated the original and reported a 28x speedup that was a failed `replaceStrings`.\n\n`scratch/typeorder/real.nix`:\n\n```nix\nlet\n  pkgs = import <nixpkgs> { };\n  inherit (pkgs) lib;\n\n  src = /nix/store/<hash>-source/easykubenix;   # your easykubenix checkout\n  eknLib = lib.extend (self: super: import \"${src}/lib/default.nix\" self super);\n  original = builtins.readFile \"${src}/lib/kubeValueType.nix\";\n\n  block = lines: lib.concatStringsSep \"\\n\" ([ \"  baseType = types.oneOf [\" ] ++ lines ++ [ \"  ];\" ]);\n\n  currentBranches = block [\n    \"    nullType\"\n    \"    types.bool\"\n    \"    types.int\"\n    \"    types.float\"\n    \"    types.str\"\n    \"    types.path\"\n    \"    # `namedListOf` comes before `objectType`. A plain JSON object fails its\"\n    \"    # check, so an object still falls through to `objectType`.\"\n    \"    (namedListOf valueType)\"\n    \"    objectType\"\n  ];\n  frequencyBranches = block [\n    \"    types.str\"\n    \"    (namedListOf valueType)\"\n    \"    objectType\"\n    \"    types.int\"\n    \"    types.bool\"\n    \"    nullType\"\n    \"    types.float\"\n    \"    types.path\"\n  ];\n\n  mkType =\n    branches:\n    let\n      replaced = builtins.replaceStrings [ currentBranches ] [ branches ] original;\n    in\n    assert branches == currentBranches || replaced != original;   # <- load-bearing\n    import (builtins.toFile \"kubeValueType.nix\" replaced) { lib = eknLib; };\n\n  value = builtins.fromJSON (builtins.readFile ./generated.json);\n\n  merged =\n    type:\n    (lib.evalModules {\n      modules = [ { options.v = lib.mkOption { inherit type; }; config.v = value; } ];\n    }).config.v;\n\n  # `builtins.length` does NOT force the elements, so an earlier version of\n  # this forced nothing and reported 0.27 s for everything. Summing forces.\n  sum = list: lib.foldl' (a: b: a + b) 0 list;\n  force =\n    v:\n    if builtins.isAttrs v then sum (map force (builtins.attrValues v))\n    else if builtins.isList v then sum (map force v)\n    else builtins.seq v 1;\nin\n{\n  current = force (merged (mkType currentBranches));\n  byFrequency = force (merged (mkType frequencyBranches));\n}\n```\n\n```bash\nekn eval --file . --attr environments.<env>.config.kubernetes.generated > generated.json\nnix eval --impure --file real.nix current\nnix eval --impure --file real.nix byFrequency\n```\n\nBoth must print the same number. If they do not, the substitution changed behaviour rather than only order.\n\nCounting the node distribution:\n\n```python\nimport json, collections\nd = json.load(open(\"generated.json\"))\nc = collections.Counter()\ndef walk(v):\n    if v is None: c[\"null\"] += 1\n    elif isinstance(v, bool): c[\"bool\"] += 1\n    elif isinstance(v, int): c[\"int\"] += 1\n    elif isinstance(v, float): c[\"float\"] += 1\n    elif isinstance(v, str): c[\"str\"] += 1\n    elif isinstance(v, list):\n        c[\"list\"] += 1\n        for x in v: walk(x)\n    elif isinstance(v, dict):\n        c[\"attrs\"] += 1\n        for x in v.values(): walk(x)\nwalk(d)\nt = sum(c.values())\nfor k, n in c.most_common():\n    print(f\"{n:9,} {100*n/t:5.1f}%  {k}\")\n```\n\n## Why this was not obvious\n\nMeasuring on the full rendered output gives **25%**, and that number is wrong for the default configuration: 188 CRDs are 237,962 of those 269,929 nodes \u2014 **88%** \u2014 and they go through `kubernetes.crds`, so `kubeValueType` never sees them. The honest figure for a render today is the 16% above. I report both because the 25% becomes real the moment CRDs move back into `kubernetes.resources`, which is the subject of the companion issue.\n"}
{"issue": 456, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWRX1A", "before": "**Correction to the distribution table, and a cross-environment check.** You are implementing from that table, so this matters now rather than later.\n\nThe table in the issue counts every node of the rendered output. But 88% of those nodes are CRDs, which go through `kubernetes.crds` and never reach this type. The distribution of the nodes it **actually walks** is different, and `list` in particular is twice what I said.\n\nMeasured on two environments of solid-kubernetes -- `nixlab2` (the live one) and `nixlab` (the broadest render fixture, a different component set):\n\n| node type | nixlab2 | nixlab |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| str | 59.3% | 60.0% |\n| attrs | 25.3% | 25.5% |\n| list | **10.5%** | **9.6%** |\n| int | 2.8% | 2.7% |\n| bool | 2.0% | 2.1% |\n| null | 0.1% | 0.1% |\n| float | 0% | 0% |\n\n31,967 and 28,707 walked nodes respectively. **The distribution is stable across environments** -- every share within a point -- so ordering by it is sound and not a fit to one cluster. That was the thing worth checking before you encode it.\n\n(The issue's original row for `list` said 5.1%. That was diluted by the CRDs, which are mostly strings and attribute sets.)\n\n## One ordering question the numbers raise\n\nWith `list` at 10.5% and `attrs` at 25.3%, the proposed order pays more than it needs to:\n\n    str(1) + namedListOf(2) + objectType(3)  =  1.56 checks per node\n    str(1) + objectType(2) + namedListOf(3)  =  1.41 checks per node\n\nabout 9% fewer. But that swap crosses the `namedListOf` before `objectType` rule, so I am **not** proposing it -- flagging it as yours to judge. The mechanism that makes it safe already exists and you just used it in nixidae/nixidae#457: `objectType`'s `addCheck` names the markers it must refuse, so a marked list cannot type-check as an object regardless of position. If that guard is what actually enforces the rule, the ordering comment may be documenting a belt that the braces already hold. If it is load-bearing for some case I have not found, leave it exactly where it is -- 9% is not worth a subtle wrong merge.\n\n## And a note for whoever reads this next to the dashboards\n\nGrafana dashboards have a very different shape from Kubernetes objects: str 40.7%, attrs 25.0%, **int 14.2%**, **bool 11.2%**, list 7.5%. If nixidae/nixidae#457's untyped route takes them out of this type, that difference stops mattering. If it does not, `int` and `bool` deserve to be higher than their 2-3% share of manifest nodes suggests.\n\n## Repro\n\n```python\nimport collections, json, sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef count(node, c):\n    if node is None: c[\"null\"] += 1\n    elif isinstance(node, bool): c[\"bool\"] += 1\n    elif isinstance(node, int): c[\"int\"] += 1\n    elif isinstance(node, float): c[\"float\"] += 1\n    elif isinstance(node, str): c[\"str\"] += 1\n    elif isinstance(node, list):\n        c[\"list\"] += 1\n        for v in node: count(v, c)\n    elif isinstance(node, dict):\n        c[\"attrs\"] += 1\n        for v in node.values(): count(v, c)\n\ndef split(node, crds, rest):\n    \"\"\"CRDs go through kubernetes.crds, so they are not walked by the type.\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(node, dict):\n        if node.get(\"kind\") == \"CustomResourceDefinition\":\n            count(node, crds); return\n        rest[\"attrs\"] += 1\n        for v in node.values(): split(v, crds, rest)\n    elif isinstance(node, list):\n        rest[\"list\"] += 1\n        for v in node: split(v, crds, rest)\n    else:\n        count(node, rest)\n\nfor path in sys.argv[1:]:\n    crds, rest = collections.Counter(), collections.Counter()\n    split(json.loads(Path(path).read_text()), crds, rest)\n    walked = sum(rest.values())\n    print(f\"\\n== {path}: {walked:,} walked, {sum(crds.values()):,} CRD nodes\")\n    for name, n in rest.most_common():\n        print(f\"  {n:9,} {100*n/walked:5.1f}%  {name}\")\n```\n\n```bash\nekn eval --file . --attr environments.<env>.config.kubernetes.generated > generated.json\npython3 node-distribution.py generated.json\n```"}
{"issue": 456, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWRYTg", "before": "Landed as `75660245`, both the reorder and the `isMarkedList` duplication.\n\nThe order is now `str`, `namedListOf`, `objectType`, `int`, `bool`, `null`,\n`float`, `path`, with the untyped branch from nixidae/nixidae#457 last. Expected branch\nchecks per node go from 5.97 to 1.55. 554 tests green, identical output.\n\n**Both load-bearing orderings are now tests rather than comments**, because\na comment does not survive somebody re-counting frequencies and re-sorting:\n\n- an absolute-path-looking string stays a string (`str` before `path`) \u2014\n  the fixture uses `/var/lib/grafana`, `/bin/sh` and a `/nix/store/...`\n  string, and asserts every value is still a `str`;\n- a lone `mkNamedList` still becomes a list (`namedListOf` before\n  `objectType`).\n\n`str` before `path` in particular reads as an arbitrary choice until the\nday `path.check` accepts a `mountPath` and merges it as a path \u2014 copying a\nfile into the store, or failing because none exists.\n\n`isMarkedList` now asks `isAttrs` once and reads `_type` once. One `let`,\n238,025 duplicate calls.\n\n## One correction worth keeping\n\nThe headline was 25% and is **16%**. The 25% was measured over the whole\nrendered output, of which 188 CRDs are 237,962 of 269,929 nodes \u2014 88% that\nnever reach this type at all. On what the type really walks it is 16%,\nabout 0.08s a render. The 23% figure for CRD-heavy data stands and is what\nthis is worth *if* nixidae/nixidae#457's follow-up folds `kubernetes.crds` in.\n\nThat ranking matters for what to do next: dashboards 0.58s, this 0.08s."}
{"issue": 456, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWRYsw", "before": "**Before/after on a real render, with `75660245` and `00dbc7e4` both in. Output is byte-identical; the timing difference is zero.**\n\nI did not need the re-lock: `UMBRELLA_DEV=easykubenix` makes the umbrella read that source as a directory instead of from its lock, so the render used the working tree with no commit and no push. (`nix/resolve.nix` documents it; worth knowing for any future \"measure this before it lands\".)\n\nWorkload: solid-kubernetes `environments.nixlab2`, 919 objects, 35 deployment units, 25 MB of YAML. Fully warm store, five runs each side, **interleaved** so drift cancels.\n\n```\noutput                    byte-identical, 24,977,886 bytes\n                          wall        to_python(kubernetes.generated)\nbefore (pinned)           10.301 s    7.909 s\nafter  (both landed)      10.319 s    7.900 s\n```\n\n**The difference is 0.009 s on the stage. It is zero**, and the run-to-run spread is 0.05-0.1 s, so it would be zero at ten times the effect.\n\n## This is my recommendation failing to show up, and the mechanism is fine\n\nWorth being plain about, since I am the one who put the 16% in front of you.\n\nThe reorder saves 16% of what the type spends, and the type spends **0.49 s** of a 7.9 s stage. So the win is ~0.08 s, about 1% of the stage, under this render's noise. Nothing is wrong with the change or with the 16% \u2014 the denominator is simply much bigger than the thing being improved.\n\nThe untyped branch contributes nothing at all yet, for a different reason: no module registers anything through it. It sits last, so today it costs about 2% of the type's work and buys nothing until a caller exists.\n\n## Where the prize actually is\n\nBoth landed changes are **prerequisites that pay nothing on their own**:\n\n- the reorder becomes visible when opaque data goes through the type \u2014 **23% of 3.0 s** on the 188 CRDs, which is what nixidae/nixidae#457's `kubernetes.crds` removal would create;\n- the untyped branch becomes visible when the dashboards use it \u2014 **0.58 s**, measured.\n\nAnyone measuring either alone and concluding \"no effect\" would be reading the meter correctly and the mechanism wrongly. That is the sentence I would put in the changelog.\n\n## What I would take from it\n\nNothing to revert and nothing to fix. The byte-identical output across 919 objects on a machine that is not yours is the assertion you asked for, and it holds. But I would not expect a render-level number from these two until a caller uses the untyped route, and I would rather say that now than have it look like a regression in someone's benchmark later."}
{"issue": 457, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPWMjg", "before": "Some Kubernetes values are large, opaque and **nobody merges them field by field**: a CRD's OpenAPI schema, a Grafana dashboard, an imported blob. Today `kubeValueType` walks every leaf of them through nixpkgs' `either`/`oneOf` v2-merge-coherence machinery, and `kubernetes.crds` exists as a separate option purely to dodge that for one of the two cases.\n\nThis proposes a branch of `kubeValueType` that carries such a value **whole**, so it can live in `kubernetes.resources` like any other object \u2014 routed, annotated and transformed by the same machinery \u2014 while costing nothing to type.\n\nCredit for the idea to Carl; the numbers and the constraint below are mine.\n\n## The shape\n\nA marker, the same trick `mkNamedList`/`mkNumberedList` already use:\n\n```nix\n{ _type = \"untyped\"; content = <anything>; }\n```\n\nand a branch whose `check` is that marker, whose `merge` takes the single definition's `content` whole, and for which **two definitions are an error rather than a merge** \u2014 nothing looks inside, so nothing can merge:\n\n```nix\n(mkOptionType {\n  name = \"untypedValue\";\n  description = \"a value carried whole, never walked\";\n  check = value: builtins.isAttrs value && (value._type or null) == \"untyped\";\n  merge =\n    loc: defs:\n    if builtins.length defs == 1 then\n      (builtins.head defs).value.content\n    else\n      throw \"The option `${lib.showOption loc}` is untyped and has ${toString (builtins.length defs)} definitions. An untyped value is taken whole, so it can be set once.\";\n})\n```\n\nA legacy `merge` is fine here \u2014 `either` handles a branch without `merge.v2` by calling `merge loc defs` and computing `headError` from `check`.\n\n## Measured\n\nAgainst the real `kubeValueType` with the branch substituted in. Identical merged output in every row.\n\n| workload | today | with the branch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 39 Grafana dashboards, 55,429 nodes | 0.740 s | **0.158 / 0.162 s** |\n| the real object set, 31,939 nodes, no untyped values in it | 0.490 / 0.512 s | 0.420 / 0.422 s |\n\nVerified separately, not assumed:\n\n- one definition unwraps to the content, with no `_type` or `content` left in the output;\n- two definitions abort, with a message naming the option path and the count.\n\n## It is not `lib.types.anything`\n\n`types.attrsOf types.anything` measures the same on this data (0.159 s) because `anything`'s merge takes a **lone** definition whole. That is why it looks identical. On two definitions it **deep-merges silently**, which is the opposite of the wanted semantic, and the difference is invisible until the day someone sets the value twice.\n\n## Where the branch goes\n\nMeasured on a 269,929-node tree containing **no untyped values at all**, so this is pure tax:\n\n| position | time |\n| --- | --- |\n| no branch (today) | 3.506 s |\n| branch first | 3.789 s (+8%) |\n| branch last | 3.572 s (+2%) |\n\n**Last.** An untyped value is found once per marker \u2014 a few hundred times in a whole render \u2014 so the nine checks it then pays do not signify, while every ordinary value is untouched by a branch it never reaches.\n\n## The constraint, which reading the code did not give\n\n`objectType` is\n\n```nix\nobjectType = types.addCheck (conditionalAttrsOf valueType) (\n  x: !(isNamedList x) && !(isNumberedList x)\n);\n```\n\nIts check is `isAttrs` plus a guard against the two list markers \u2014 so **it matches an untyped marker too**, merges it as a plain object, and leaves `_type` and `content` in the rendered manifest.\n\nI did not spot this by reading. It surfaced as a differing leaf count on the run with the branch last: 37,455 against 37,416. The fix is exactly what the file already does for `mkNamedList`, and the file's own comment predicts it:\n\n> A marked attribute set must never type-check as a plain JSON object. [\u2026] Without this guard, the attribute map would accept a marked attribute set, because its own check is only `isAttrs`.\n\n```nix\nobjectType = types.addCheck (conditionalAttrsOf valueType) (\n  x: !(isNamedList x) && !(isNumberedList x) && !((x._type or null) == \"untyped\")\n);\n```\n\nWith the guard, the branch may sit anywhere in the fold, and all orderings agree on the output.\n\n## `kubernetes.crds`, and why it should be LAST\n\n`kubernetes.crds` exists only so 188 CRDs and their 237,963 nodes never reach `kubeValueType`. If the untyped branch lands, that option has no reason to be separate, the \"is it a CRD\" special case leaves every path that currently has to know, and the tree becomes uniform.\n\n**What it costs, measured on those 188 CRDs:**\n\n| route | time |\n| --- | --- |\n| today, `kubernetes.crds` as `listOf attrs` | 0.294 / 0.312 s |\n| in `kubernetes.resources` as untyped markers | **0.300 / 0.309 s** |\n| in `kubernetes.resources` as ordinary typed objects | 2.994 / 3.027 s |\n| the same, with nixidae/nixidae#456's branch reorder | 2.318 / 2.386 s |\n\nSo the generic route is **free if CRDs go in as untyped markers** and costs **+2.7 s a render** if they go in typed. Uniformity is affordable; typing them is not.\n\nCarl's instruction, and I agree with it: **do this one last**, after the branch and the reorder are in and measured, and only on the untyped route.\n\n## Suggested order of work\n\n1. `objectType`'s guard (one line, no behaviour change on its own).\n2. The untyped branch, placed last.\n3. nixidae/nixidae#456's branch reorder.\n4. Only then, `kubernetes.crds` \u2014 measure the real render before and after.\n\n## Repro\n\n`scratch/typeorder/untyped.nix` \u2014 substitutes both the branch list and `objectType`'s guard into the real file, and **asserts each substitution changed something**. That assert is not decoration: an earlier harness of mine missed an anchor's indentation and silently measured the original while reporting a 28x speedup.\n\n<details>\n<summary>The harness</summary>\n\n```nix\nlet\n  pkgs = import <nixpkgs> { };\n  inherit (pkgs) lib;\n\n  src = /nix/store/<hash>-source/easykubenix;\n  eknLib = lib.extend (self: super: import \"${src}/lib/default.nix\" self super);\n  original = builtins.readFile \"${src}/lib/kubeValueType.nix\";\n\n  block = lines: lib.concatStringsSep \"\\n\" ([ \"  baseType = types.oneOf [\" ] ++ lines ++ [ \"  ];\" ]);\n\n  currentBranches = block [\n    \"    nullType\"\n    \"    types.bool\"\n    \"    types.int\"\n    \"    types.float\"\n    \"    types.str\"\n    \"    types.path\"\n    \"    # `namedListOf` comes before `objectType`. A plain JSON object fails its\"\n    \"    # check, so an object still falls through to `objectType`.\"\n    \"    (namedListOf valueType)\"\n    \"    objectType\"\n  ];\n\n  untypedDefinition = [\n    \"    (mkOptionType {\"\n    ''      name = \"untypedValue\";''\n    ''      description = \"a value carried whole, never walked\";''\n    ''      check = value: builtins.isAttrs value && (value._type or null) == \"untyped\";''\n    \"      merge =\"\n    \"        loc: defs:\"\n    \"        if builtins.length defs == 1 then\"\n    \"          (builtins.head defs).value.content\"\n    \"        else\"\n    ''          throw \"The option `''${lib.showOption loc}` is untyped and has ''${toString (builtins.length defs)} definitions.\";''\n    \"    })\"\n  ];\n\n  withReorderUntypedLast = block ([\n    \"    types.str\"\n    \"    (namedListOf valueType)\"\n    \"    objectType\"\n    \"    types.int\"\n    \"    types.bool\"\n    \"    nullType\"\n    \"    types.float\"\n    \"    types.path\"\n  ] ++ untypedDefinition);\n\n  currentObjectType = lib.concatStringsSep \"\\n\" [\n    \"  objectType = types.addCheck (conditionalAttrsOf valueType) (\"\n    \"    x: !(isNamedList x) && !(isNumberedList x)\"\n    \"  );\"\n  ];\n  guardedObjectType = lib.concatStringsSep \"\\n\" [\n    \"  objectType = types.addCheck (conditionalAttrsOf valueType) (\"\n    ''    x: !(isNamedList x) && !(isNumberedList x) && !((x._type or null) == \"untyped\")''\n    \"  );\"\n  ];\n\n  mkType =\n    branches:\n    let\n      replaced = builtins.replaceStrings [ currentBranches ] [ branches ] original;\n      guarded =\n        if branches == currentBranches then\n          replaced\n        else\n          builtins.replaceStrings [ currentObjectType ] [ guardedObjectType ] replaced;\n    in\n    assert branches == currentBranches || replaced != original;\n    assert branches == currentBranches || guarded != replaced;\n    import (builtins.toFile \"kubeValueType.nix\" guarded) { lib = eknLib; };\n\n  wrap = value: { _type = \"untyped\"; content = value; };\n\n  run =\n    { type, value }:\n    let\n      merged =\n        (lib.evalModules {\n          modules = [ { options.v = lib.mkOption { inherit type; }; config.v = value; } ];\n        }).config.v;\n      sum = list: lib.foldl' (a: b: a + b) 0 list;\n      force =\n        v:\n        if builtins.isAttrs v then sum (map force (builtins.attrValues v))\n        else if builtins.isList v then sum (map force v)\n        else builtins.seq v 1;\n    in\n    force merged;\n\n  dashboards = builtins.fromJSON (builtins.readFile ./dashboards.json);\nin\n{\n  dashTyped = run {\n    type = eknLib.conditionalAttrsOf (mkType currentBranches);\n    value = dashboards;\n  };\n  dashUntyped = run {\n    type = eknLib.conditionalAttrsOf (mkType withReorderUntypedLast);\n    value = lib.mapAttrs (_: wrap) dashboards;\n  };\n\n  setTwiceAborts =\n    let\n      evaluated = lib.evalModules {\n        modules = [\n          { options.v = lib.mkOption { type = mkType withReorderUntypedLast; }; }\n          { config.v = wrap { a = 1; }; }\n          { config.v = wrap { b = 2; }; }\n        ];\n      };\n    in\n    (builtins.tryEval (builtins.seq (builtins.toJSON evaluated.config.v) \"merged\")).success;\n}\n```\n\n</details>\n\n`dashTyped` and `dashUntyped` must print the same number. `setTwiceAborts` must print `false`.\n\nGetting the inputs out of a real configuration:\n\n```bash\nekn eval --file . --attr environments.<env>.config.kubernetes.generated > generated.json\n```\n\nthen split out the dashboards (they live as JSON *strings* inside labelled ConfigMaps, so a node count over the manifest does not see them):\n\n```python\nimport json, sys\nout = {}\ndef walk(node):\n    if isinstance(node, dict):\n        labels = (node.get(\"metadata\") or {}).get(\"labels\") or {}\n        if node.get(\"kind\") == \"ConfigMap\" and \"grafana_dashboard\" in labels:\n            for key, text in (node.get(\"data\") or {}).items():\n                out[key.removesuffix(\".json\")] = json.loads(text)\n        for v in node.values(): walk(v)\n    elif isinstance(node, list):\n        for v in node: walk(v)\nwalk(json.load(open(\"generated.json\")))\njson.dump(out, open(\"dashboards.json\", \"w\"))\n```\n\n## Context\n\nProfiled on solid-kubernetes' `nixlab2`: a full render is ~10 s wall, of which the Nix evaluator is 4.8 s. Of that, 59% is the module system rather than package evaluation, and `lib/types.nix` plus `lib/modules.nix` are 19.4% of self time. Dashboards alone are 55,429 nodes \u2014 **larger than every other object in the render put together** \u2014 and they are most of what `kubeValueType` actually does today.\n"}
{"issue": 457, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWRhEA", "before": "**Consumer-side result for `00dbc7e4`, measured at a load average of 38 \u2014 because a frequency-0 profile is a call count, and a call count does not care about contention.**\n\nTwenty dashboards in solid-kubernetes now register through `mkUntyped`. Two frequency-0 renders of the same environment and the same easykubenix, differing only in that:\n\n```\ntyped     11,877,520 calls   1,486,043 stacks\nuntyped    9,452,346 calls   1,482,932 stacks\n```\n\n**2,425,174 calls removed \u2014 20.4% of every function call the evaluator makes.** Renders byte-identical.\n\n## Where they went\n\nAbsolute counts, not shares \u2014 a share moves when the total moves.\n\n| file | before | after | delta |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| nixpkgs `lib/types.nix` | 2,142,130 | 1,103,632 | **-1,038,498** |\n| `lib/conditionalAttrsOf.nix` | 1,053,095 | 610,710 | -442,385 |\n| nixpkgs `lib/modules.nix` | 2,760,429 | 2,350,704 | -409,725 |\n| `lib/kubeValueType.nix` | 335,890 | 151,336 | -184,554 |\n| nixpkgs `lib/attrsets.nix` | 559,686 | 399,647 | -160,039 |\n| `lib/default.nix` | 1,937,325 | 1,866,790 | -70,535 |\n| nixpkgs `lib/options.nix` | 110,808 | 62,486 | -48,322 |\n\n**43% of the saving is `lib/types.nix`** \u2014 the `either`/`oneOf` merge-coherence machinery. Which closes the loop on the argument this issue opened with: an opaque value is expensive because every leaf goes through a recursive type, not because anything about it is inherently large.\n\n## Two checks that it changed only what it should\n\n- **Package-code calls moved by 6**, out of 1,092,065. The dashboards touch no package evaluation, and now there is a number saying so.\n- **The stacks barely move**: 3,111 fewer out of 1.49 M. An untyped value removes repeated calls along paths that still exist rather than removing paths \u2014 the opposite of what deleting code would look like. Worth knowing if you ever want a signature for \"this change did what untyping does\".\n\n## Two caveats\n\n**20% of calls is not 20% of time.** A quiet measurement earlier put four dashboards at 0.200 s, so twenty are worth roughly 0.4-0.5 s of a ~7 s stage \u2014 about 6-7%. Calls fall three times faster than seconds here because these are module-system calls, the cheap kind; a leaf-frame partition on a timed profile puts package-code calls at roughly 7x each. Please do not convert this number into seconds.\n\n**This is one environment.** The node distribution held across two, but the dashboard share is a property of how much Grafana a cluster ships.\n\n## And where it points next\n\n`lib/default.nix` lost only 70,535 of its 1,937,325 calls, so the marker predicates are almost entirely non-dashboard work \u2014 and it is now the **second busiest file in the render**, behind `lib/modules.nix` and ahead of `lib/types.nix`. That is nixidae/nixidae#454's territory, and it is a bigger share of what is left than I expected."}
{"issue": 458, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWRqWg", "before": "**The 11x is per BYTE, not per call.** Measured, because it decides whether the answer is \"make the fallback cheaper\" or \"batch the primop calls\".\n\nTwo corpora holding the same 9.8 MB of the same YAML: `many` is 200 calls of one document, `few` is 20 calls of ten documents. Equal bytes, ten times the call count. Floor is a run that reads the same bytes and parses nothing.\n\n| attribute | time | parse, floor subtracted |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `manyRead` / `fewRead` (floor) | 1.120 s / 1.106 s | -- |\n| `manyJson` | 1.206 s | 0.09 s |\n| `fewJson` | 1.207 s | 0.10 s |\n| `manyYaml` | 1.872 s | **0.76 s** |\n| `fewYaml` | 1.833 s | **0.73 s** |\n\nTen times fewer calls changes nothing. **The Nix/Python boundary is not the cost.** The parser is, at roughly **75 ms/MB against 10 ms/MB** -- about 8x per byte. (The 11x I quoted earlier came from a single 51 KB document, so it carried a little per-call overhead with it; 8x is the per-byte figure.)\n\nSo batching would not help. Converting once into JSON, in a derivation that is cached, is the shape that does -- which is the same conclusion as nixidae/nixidae#459 arrives at from the other direction.\n\n## Repro\n\n```nix\n# scratch/parsecorpus/default.nix\nlet\n  n = count: builtins.genList (i: i) count;\n  read = dir: i: extension: builtins.readFile (./. + \"/${dir}/c${toString i}.${extension}\");\n  # NOT builtins.length: it needs only the list spine and forces nothing.\n  size =\n    v:\n    if builtins.isAttrs v then builtins.foldl' (a: b: a + b) 0 (map size (builtins.attrValues v))\n    else if builtins.isList v then builtins.foldl' (a: b: a + b) 0 (map size v)\n    else 1;\n  total = list: builtins.foldl' (a: b: a + b) 0 (map size list);\nin\n{\n  manyYaml = total (map (i: builtins.fromYAML11Stream (read \"many\" i \"yaml\")) (n 200));\n  fewYaml  = total (map (i: builtins.fromYAML11Stream (read \"few\"  i \"yaml\")) (n 20));\n  manyJson = total (map (i: builtins.fromJSON (read \"many\" i \"json\")) (n 200));\n  fewJson  = total (map (i: builtins.fromJSON (read \"few\"  i \"json\")) (n 20));\n  manyRead = builtins.foldl' (a: i: a + builtins.stringLength (read \"many\" i \"yaml\")) 0 (n 200);\n  fewRead  = builtins.foldl' (a: i: a + builtins.stringLength (read \"few\"  i \"yaml\")) 0 (n 20);\n}\n```\n\nCorpus: take any ~50 KB YAML document, write 200 files of one copy and 20 files of ten copies joined by `---`, each with a distinct trailing comment so Nix cannot answer the second call from the first thunk. Matching JSON: the same documents as one array, padded with trailing newlines to keep the files distinct.\n\nRun through `ekn` so the primop is registered:\n\n```bash\nfor a in manyRead fewRead manyJson fewJson manyYaml fewYaml; do\n  printf '%-10s: ' \"$a\"\n  /usr/bin/env bash -c \"TIMEFORMAT='%3R s'; time ekn eval --file scratch/parsecorpus --attr $a\"\ndone\n```\n\nOne wrinkle worth naming rather than hiding: `fewYaml` returns 98,380 against 98,200 for the others, which is 9 extra nodes per file. The `---` separators yield null documents that the JSON side does not have. Trivial nodes, no effect on the timing, but the counts do not match exactly and I would rather say so than have someone find it."}
{"issue": 458, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWRrTg", "before": "I measured the two traps again before building on them, because nixidae/nixidae#459's design rests on them. Both tables hold, and both are **paid once per evaluation, not once per derivation**. That changes what to do about them.\n\n## The costs are one-time, not per-derivation\n\n`nativeBuildInputs = [ pkgs.nix ]`, N derivations in one evaluation:\n\n| derivations | time |\n| --- | --- |\n| bare `runCommand`, no nix | 0.245 s |\n| 1 | 0.851 s |\n| 10 | 0.943 s |\n| 100 | 1.116 s |\n\n`NIX_PATH = \"nixpkgs=${pkgs.path}\"`:\n\n| derivations | time |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1 | 6.765 s |\n| 10 | 7.107 s |\n\nSo the marginal cost of naming `pkgs.nix` in one more derivation is about 2.7 ms. The 0.6 s is the package expression being forced once. Same for `pkgs.path`: the 334 MB import happens once, then it is memoised.\n\nThis matters for nixidae/nixidae#459. A hundred transform derivations do not cost a hundred times 0.6 s. They cost 0.6 s.\n\n## `builtins.storePath` cannot be the answer here\n\nTwo findings that rule out the spelling both this issue and nixidae/nixidae#459 recommend.\n\n**1. It is forbidden under `--pure-eval`:**\n\n```\nerror: 'builtins.storePath' is not allowed in pure evaluation mode\n```\n\n`nix/sources.nix` supports a pure-eval consumer on purpose, and documents why:\n\n> It is also the only mechanism that works under `--pure-eval`, where `builtins.getEnv` answers \"\". That is what lets a flake consumer resolve a pinned umbrella rather than the moving head of a branch.\n\nA `storePath` in the transform path takes that away from every flake consumer.\n\n**2. On a path that is not in the local store it does not fail cleanly, it substitutes:**\n\n```\nerror: path '/nix/store/000...-does-not-exist' is required,\n       but there is no substituter that can build it\n```\n\nThat is an evaluation-time network dependency on a cache holding the exact path a lock file names. Fine on this machine, a new failure mode on a fresh one.\n\n## `sources.nixpkgs` is the right answer, and it is already here\n\n`nix/fetch.nix` returns a **string naming a store path**, not a path value:\n\n```\n{\"nixpkgs\":\"string\",\n \"asString\":\"/nix/store/akq5azpip8xzarlxqxis02lg2zz3hmcb-source\",\n \"ctx\":[\"/nix/store/akq5azpip8xzarlxqxis02lg2zz3hmcb-source\"]}\n```\n\nIt carries store context, so interpolating it creates the right dependency and imports nothing:\n\n| `NIX_PATH = \"nixpkgs=...\"` | 1 derivation | 10 derivations |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `${pkgs.path}` | 6.765 s | 7.107 s |\n| `${sources.nixpkgs}` | **0.359 s** | **0.380 s** |\n\nCheap, correct, and legal under `--pure-eval`. No `unsafeDiscardStringContext`, no lock file, no substituter dependency at evaluation time.\n\nThe general rule in the issue body still stands, and is now sharper: **reduce an input by choosing which store path to reference.** In this repository that choice is already made, by the umbrella, and the answer is `sources.nixpkgs`.\n\n## What is left of this issue\n\n- **Item 1** (the fallback drags the ekn CLI) is fixed by nixidae/nixidae#460, not here.\n- **Items 2 and 3** are traps to document, and the documentation now says: use `sources.nixpkgs`, do not reach for `builtins.storePath`, and do not fear `pkgs.nix` in a hundred derivations -- fear it in one.\n\nFor the Nix binary itself I am keeping `pkgs.nix` and paying 0.6 s once, lazily -- nothing forces it unless a configuration actually uses a build-time transform. Trading pure-eval support for 0.6 s once per render is not a trade I would make. If you disagree, the lock-file route is still available and I will take it.\n"}
{"issue": 460, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPWicw", "before": "Carl's direction, filed now and **not started**: every YAML that enters easykubenix becomes JSON in a derivation. The evaluator then only ever calls `builtins.fromJSON`.\n\nTwo separate arguments point the same way. One is speed, which is measured. The other is compatibility, which is the actual goal of this project.\n\n## 1. Speed: `fromJSON` wins per byte, and the gap is not closable\n\nMeasured on 200 distinct copies of one 51 KB document, against a 1.07 s process floor:\n\n| parse | 200 documents | each |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `builtins.fromYAML11Stream` | 0.79 s | 3.9 ms |\n| `builtins.fromJSON` | 0.07 s | **0.35 ms** |\n\nRestated per byte: about 75 ms/MB against 10 ms/MB.\n\nTwo things that look like fixes are not:\n\n- **Call count does not matter.** 200 calls of one document and 20 calls of ten documents measured the same. So batching the parse buys nothing.\n- **A smaller converter does not touch the parse.** nixidae/nixidae#458's 0.6 s is derivation *instantiation*, a different term. Fixing it leaves the per-byte cost exactly where it is.\n\nThe only lever that moves the per-byte term is to stop parsing YAML in the evaluator.\n\n## 2. Compatibility: stop approximating Kubernetes' parser, use it\n\nThis is the stronger argument. easykubenix exists to agree with Kubernetes. YAML scalar semantics are a place where parsers disagree quietly, and the project has already been bitten.\n\n`renderChart.nix` records the case in a comment. `yq` read an unquoted `yes` as the string `\"yes\"`. YAML 1.1 -- and therefore Kubernetes' own go-yaml v2 -- reads boolean `true`. No chart in the tree emitted such a scalar (checked: 143 rendered outputs, 0 hits), so it was latent, not live. The fix was to remove `yq` and keep one parser.\n\nBut the parser we kept is still **our** parser, chosen to imitate Kubernetes. `fromYAML11Stream` and `ekn _yamlToJson` are the same nanopynix code, so they agree with each other. Nothing makes them agree with the API server. Every scalar edge -- `on`/`off`, `y`/`n`, sexagesimals, leading-zero octal, `~`, timestamps, duplicate keys, merge keys -- is a place where a match is a choice we re-made, not a property we inherited.\n\nUsing Kubernetes' own conversion code removes the class. The relevant path is the one `kubectl` uses:\n\n- `k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/util/yaml` to split a multi-document stream, and\n- `sigs.k8s.io/yaml`'s `YAMLToJSON` to convert each document.\n\nA chart is Go templates rendered by Helm and read back by Kubernetes. Converting it with the code that reads it is the only conversion that cannot drift.\n\n## 3. Shape\n\nA small Go program, one package in this repo:\n\n```\nkube-yaml2json < stream.yaml > stream.json\n```\n\nReads a YAML document stream on stdin. Writes a JSON array of documents on stdout. No flags, or one flag for whether to drop null documents.\n\n### Where it plugs in\n\nThe live chart path is:\n\n```\nhelm.nix -> importHelm -> pkgs.chart2yaml -> importYaml -> parseYAMLStream\n```\n\nSo there is one dispatch on the live path, in `easykubenix/lib/parseYamlStream.nix`, and one derivation that already produces the YAML, `easykubenix/pkgs/chart2yaml.nix`.\n\n`easykubenix/pkgs/renderChart.nix` is a third thing: the legacy public wrapper, with **its own hand-rolled copy** of the primop-vs-fallback dispatch and its own `helm template` derivation. Nothing in this repository calls it. Do not fold the converter into it -- that is dead code here, and a consumer may still import it. Decide its fate separately.\n\n### The conversion folds into the derivation that already exists\n\nWorth stating, because it removes the cost objection before it is raised.\n\n`chart2yaml` already runs `helm template` in a derivation. Adding the converter to that derivation's `nativeBuildInputs` makes it emit JSON directly. That is **no new derivation and no new IFD**. `chart2yaml` becomes `chart2json`, and `parseYAMLStream` is then not on the chart path at all.\n\nThe same holds anywhere the YAML is already produced by a build step. Only a YAML file that arrives as a plain source path needs a conversion derivation of its own.\n\nThis matters because a derivation is not free to name. nixidae/nixidae#458 measured a bare `runCommand` at 0.25 s of instantiation on every evaluation. Per-file IFD over many small YAML files could therefore lose to the primop. Folding the conversion into an existing derivation avoids the question for charts, which is where the bytes are.\n\n### The two traps from nixidae/nixidae#458 apply\n\n- Do **not** write `nativeBuildInputs = [ pkgs.nix ]`-shaped dependencies without measuring. Reference the converter by a literal `builtins.storePath` from a lock file if its package evaluation turns out to be expensive.\n- Never interpolate `pkgs.path`. It re-imports 334 MB.\n\nMeasure the Go package's instantiation cost before choosing. `buildGoModule` may be much cheaper to evaluate than `buildPythonApplication`; that is an assumption, not a measurement.\n\n## 4. What must be settled before writing code\n\n1. **`yamlVersion` has no Kubernetes equivalent.** Today `parseYAMLStream` takes `yaml11` or `yaml12`. Kubernetes has one behaviour, not two. Either `yaml12` keeps the nanopynix route, or it goes. Needs Carl's call.\n2. **Which go-yaml version Kubernetes actually uses today**, on the path `kubectl` takes. `sigs.k8s.io/yaml` has historically wrapped go-yaml v2 (YAML 1.1), and it now also ships `goyaml.v3`. Verify against the vendored source of the Kubernetes version we target, do not trust this paragraph.\n3. **Null and empty documents.** Both call sites filter `object != null` in Nix today. Decide whether the converter drops them or Nix keeps doing it. Keeping the filter in Nix preserves behaviour with no thought.\n4. **Numbers.** `YAMLToJSON` produces JSON; check what it does with integers outside int64 and with floats, and what `builtins.fromJSON` then does with them. Kubernetes quantities are strings, so the common case is safe.\n5. **Does the primop stay?** Recommend yes, as code. Carl's direction is \"always YAML -> JSON + IFD\", so nothing in easykubenix selects `fromYAML11Stream` by default any more. It stays a nanopynix-owned primop, available to a caller that wants an in-process parse with no derivation. easykubenix stops being that caller.\n6. **Duplicate keys.** go-yaml v2 and v3 differ here. Whatever Kubernetes does is what we want, which is the point of the issue.\n\n## 5. Relationship to the other issues\n\n- **#33** holds the per-byte table, the instantiation table and the `${pkgs.path}` trap. **This issue is the fix for nixidae/nixidae#458's item 1.** \"Give the fallback a small converter\" becomes \"give every path the same Go converter, and the fallback branch disappears\". nixidae/nixidae#458's other two findings are traps to document, and they land with nixidae/nixidae#459.\n- **#34** (build-time transforms) is the same derivation shape applied to transforming rather than parsing, and it hits the same two traps. The two are independent. If both land, a chart is rendered, converted and transformed without the evaluator seeing YAML once.\n- **#32** (untyped branch) is what receives the JSON cheaply.\n"}
{"issue": 461, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPWo7A", "before": "Carl's proposal, filed now and **not started**:\n\n> Since we're already memoizing expensive computations with IFD we could create library functionality that uses both the module system and optimized hand-rolled transformations, this could enable us to do a lot more with grafana and helm imports at practically 0 cost (beyond first eval and packaging bumps which isn't important, it's most important to optimize the speed for \"incremental changes\")\n\nnixidae/nixidae#459 moved a *transform* into a derivation. This moves the **module system** there too, so a typed import is evaluated once and every render after it reads JSON.\n\n## It works\n\nVerified with `ekn.lib.nixTransform` as it landed, no change to it.\n\nA transformer that imports the real `kubeValueType`, builds an `evalModules` tree around it and forces the whole result, run over **22 real kyverno CustomResourceDefinitions, 3.09 MB parsed**, inside the `dummy://` sandbox:\n\n```nix\n{ lib, value }:\nlet\n  kubeValueType = import \"${builtins.getEnv \"EKN_LIB\"}/kubeValueType.nix\" { inherit lib; };\n  evaluated = map (object:\n    (lib.evalModules {\n      modules = [\n        { options.object = lib.mkOption { type = kubeValueType; default = null; }; }\n        { object = object; }\n      ];\n    }).config.object\n  ) value;\nin\n{ count = builtins.length evaluated;\n  names = map (o: o.metadata.name) evaluated;\n  digest = builtins.hashString \"md5\" (builtins.toJSON evaluated); }\n```\n\nIt returns all 22 names and a digest. The module system is pure value manipulation, so `dummy://` is no obstacle -- it reads files and evaluates, and that is all `evalModules` needs.\n\nSo the expensive half of a Helm import can happen in a derivation, and the render reads the result.\n\n## Why this is the answer to the `kubernetes.crds` fold\n\nI was measuring the fold (fold `kubernetes.crds` into `kubernetes.resources` as untyped markers) when Carl sent this, and the measurement says the fold is not free. Three walks over the same data, measured over those 22 CRDs:\n\n| walk | 3.09 MB | per MB |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `builtins.toJSON` (floor -- anything touching every node) | 0.12 s | 0.04 |\n| `walkWithPath kubeAttrsToLists` (kubernetes.nix:148) | 0.47-0.59 s | **0.15** |\n| `hasMarker`, no marker present (the guard a fold needs) | 0.41-0.52 s | **0.14** |\n\nAt the 11.6 MB the `importYaml` comment names for a real tree, that is roughly **1.8 s for the marker pass and 1.6 s for the guard**, against the 2.6 s the type walk costs today (0.90 s -> 3.50 s with CRDs typed).\n\n`mkUntyped` removes the type walk and **neither** of the other two. The marker pass runs whenever a configuration has generators or transformers, which a real one does. And the guard is required: after a fold, a marker inside an untyped `spec` is resolved only when the marker pass happens to be on, and leaks a literal `_type` field otherwise -- so the no-transformer case, the one that looks free, is the one that ships an invalid manifest.\n\nAll three walks are over data that **does not change between renders**. That is the whole argument for this issue.\n\n## What decides whether it delivers: the cache key\n\nThe mechanism is easy. The property Carl asks for -- near-zero *incremental* cost -- is not automatic, and depends on one thing:\n\n**The build-time unit's inputs must not include the whole configuration fixpoint.**\n\nA derivation is cached by its inputs. If the input set is \"the configuration\", then any change to any part invalidates it, and every render rebuilds. The first eval being slow is fine; a rebuild on every incremental change is the failure this would be trying to prevent.\n\nWhat has a narrow input set:\n\n- **A Helm import.** chart + values + release name + namespace + the transformer source. None of it depends on the rest of the configuration.\n- **A Grafana dashboard.** The dashboard source and its transform.\n- **A CRD set from a chart.** Same inputs as the chart.\n\nWhat does not:\n\n- A `kubernetes.transformers` entry that reads the merged fixpoint -- ArgoCD's tracking-id annotation is one, because it encodes each object's own group/kind/namespace/name *after* placement.\n- Anything reading `config` broadly.\n\nSo this is not \"evaluate the configuration at build time\". It is \"evaluate the *leaves* at build time, where a leaf is a thing with declared inputs\". The boundary needs to be explicit and checkable, the same way nixidae/nixidae#459's `libs` boundary does.\n\n## Open questions\n\n1. **How the result re-enters.** nixidae/nixidae#457's untyped option is the cheap landing spot, but then the pipeline cannot reach it -- which is the same trade the `crds` bypass makes today. Worth deciding whether the build-time unit also runs the pipeline, so what comes back is genuinely finished.\n2. **Where the seam goes.** `importHelm` is the obvious first caller: it already has a narrow input set and already produces a config fragment.\n3. **Error presentation.** nixidae/nixidae#459 already gives up evaluation-time errors for build failures. Running the module system there means a module-system error inside a build log, which is worse. `builtins.toFile` naming the file after the option path helps and is not enough.\n4. **What \"packaging bumps\" cost.** Carl is explicit that a first eval and a chart bump paying full price is acceptable. Worth writing the expected first-eval cost down so nobody later reports it as a regression.\n5. **Interaction with nixidae/nixidae#460.** If YAML becomes JSON in the chart derivation, and the module evaluation also happens in a derivation, those may be the same derivation. Worth checking before building two.\n\n## Status of the numbers\n\nThe walk table above was measured on a quiet machine. The end-to-end build-time-versus-render-time comparison was not -- the machine reached a load average of 36 partway through, from an unrelated `rustc` storm -- so it is not recorded here. It will be redone when the machine is quiet.\n\nThe structural result (the module system runs in the sandbox, over the real type, with correct output) does not depend on load.\n\n## Sequence\n\nAfter nixidae/nixidae#460. Both put work in a chart's derivation and they may be one derivation, so building this first would mean building the seam twice.\n\n**The `kubernetes.crds` fold should wait for this**, rather than landing as `mkUntyped` and paying two walks it cannot avoid.\n"}
{"issue": 467, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPXTUQ", "before": "`build_primary_package` roots a volume's paths in the **node** store at\n`CSI_GCROOTS/<volume_id>/` (`csi/server.py:176`). `NodeUnpublishVolume`\nthen calls `cleanup_stale_entries` on every unpublish\n(`csi/server.py:386`), which removes any gcroot whose name is not an\nactive volume handle (`csi/cleanup.py:50`).\n\nSo a volume's paths are unrooted the instant its pod is deleted, and stay\nunrooted until the replacement pod mounts. `gc_task` is free to collect\nthem in that window: `nix store delete` refuses a path that is still\nalive (`gc_task.py:160-178`), so a root is the only thing that would have\nstopped it.\n\nFor an ordinary workload this is a re-fetch. For pynixd it is an outage:\nits `init-store` volume names `cacheEnv` (= `nixkube-pynixd-env`,\n`environments/cache/default.nix`), the pod's image is an empty scratch\nlayout whose only config is `PATH=/nix/var/result/bin`, and the one\nsubstituter certain to hold that path is pynixd itself. Recorded on\nnixlab2 on 2026-09-17:\n\n    Failed to build store path /nix/store/...-cacheEnv\n    error: no substituter that can build it\n\nwith that path already in pynixd's PVC, pushed minutes earlier. The PVC\nis the store pynixd *serves*; `init-store` is what it *boots from*, and\nthe two never meet.\n\nThis is why it worked for months and then stopped when the garbage\ncollector started working (issue nixidae/nixidae#435). The path was surviving unrooted,\nnot retained.\n\n## Suggested shape\n\nA `persist` volume attribute, whose roots live outside `CSI_GCROOTS` and\nare exempt from handle-keyed cleanup. It states the intent -- this path\nmust outlive the handle -- rather than relying on the collector not\nrunning in the window. `store.py:_extract_store_paths` already ignores\nnon-primary attribute keys, so a flag is not mistaken for a path.\n\nThe narrower alternative is to keep a root while a volume is merely being\nre-created, which is the same size and says less.\n\n## Not this\n\nMoving pynixd to a PVC plus an init container was considered and rejected.\nIt fixes the same failure by the same mechanism -- keeping the environment\nsomewhere the node GC does not reach -- at the cost of reintroducing\nstartup logic that was deliberately replaced by the CSI mount, and it\nfixes one instance rather than the class. An init container runs the same\n`nix build` with the same substituters (`niximage.nix:84-92`), so it does\nnot help the separate case where the path is on no reachable cache.\n\nDeferred by Carl for now; filed so the analysis is not lost.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 467, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWS5AA", "before": "**This issue is mis-framed, and I filed it. Correcting rather than leaving it.**\n\nThe title says \"a path that must outlive the handle\". Carl's objection is that nothing should: when a pod exits its volume is cleaned up, and there is no practical guarantee that pod returns to the same node. Keeping a gcroot alive past pod exit would pin paths on a node forever for a workload that may never come back to it.\n\nSo the volume-scoped root is **correct for its scope**. The requirement I was reaching for \u2014 a path that exists on every node regardless of what is running \u2014 is a node-level property, and volume scope structurally cannot express it. That is now nixidae/nixidae#468, with Carl's ConfigMap/CR design and a readiness gate for the deployment race he flagged.\n\n## What is left here, and it is much smaller than the title\n\nThe guard asymmetry in my previous comment is still real, but it is a different and narrower bug: a pod that is **still running**, whose `vol_data.json` is momentarily unreadable while the kubelet restarts, keeps its volume (the `is_mount_source` guard at `cleanup.py:65`) and loses its gc root (no guard at :50). That is a path collected while mounted and in use.\n\nWorth fixing on its own merits \u2014 give the gcroot loop the same mount check, keyed on `CSI_VOLUMES / gcroot.name` \u2014 but it is hardening against a rare kubelet-restart window, not the reschedule problem this issue was opened about. `shutil.rmtree(gcroot, ignore_errors=True)` at :52 also makes the `except` below it unreachable, so the `remove_gcroot_failed` warning can never fire.\n\nRetitling to that narrower bug, or closing it in favour of nixidae/nixidae#468 plus a fresh one, both seem reasonable. I have not done either.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 471, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWTXxg", "before": "## Carl's direction, recorded and parked\n\nNot being implemented now. Startup latency is below other work.\n\n**The shape he wants, in his words.** A small database \u2014 SQLite, or plain JSON\nif concurrency were not a problem, which it is \u2014 mapping\n`pod_sandbox_id | volume_id -> \"tree hash\"`.\n\n- **Every path in the farm has to be represented in the tree hash.** A hash\n  over a subset would let a tree be reused for a container that needs a path\n  the tree does not have.\n- **A datetime beside it, bumped periodically while something still needs the\n  tree.** That is the liveness signal, and it is a lease rather than a\n  refcount.\n- The point of the two together: **garbage collect the hardlink trees on their\n  own terms, separately from the Nix store**, so a container restart finds its\n  tree already built.\n\n**What it depends on:** querying the node for the `pod_sandbox_id`, which is\neasy, and for the `volume_id`, which Carl recalls having had trouble\ncollecting on nodes.\n\n**On that second one.** The node does have a way today, and it is not an API:\n`collect_active_volume_handles` (`csi/cleanup.py:15`) globs kubelet's own\n`*/volumes/kubernetes.io~csi/*/vol_data.json` and reads `volumeHandle` out of\neach. So the question is answerable.\n\nIt is worth knowing what that signal is before a collector leans on it. It is\nkubelet's on-disk state, not an authoritative answer, and nixidae/nixidae#467 is the case\nwhere it reads short: a `vol_data.json` that is briefly unreadable during a\nkubelet restart makes a live volume look inactive. A scheme that drops trees\non this evidence inherits that, and the lease datetime above is what would\nhave to cover it.\n\nThe keying sketch in the comment above stays as the cheaper alternative: it\nneeds no database and no lease, and it buys reuse across a retry but not\nacross a pod's lifetime.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 472, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPXz3w", "before": "On the CSI path, a pod whose volume names a large store path sits in `Init:0/1` forever, and the event blames the substituter:\n\n```\nWarning  NixBuild  Failed to build store path /nix/store/...-odoo-runtime\nperforming daemon worker op: 19\ndon't know how to build these paths:\n/nix/store/...-odoo-runtime\nerror: path '...' is required, but there is no substituter that can build it\n```\n\n**That message is not true, and it is what costs the time.** Everything it points at is fine:\n\n- the path is on the substituter \u2014 `nix path-info --store ssh-ng://nix@pynixd <path>` returns it;\n- the node can reach it \u2014 the same query run *inside the `nix-node` pod* succeeds;\n- name resolution is fine \u2014 `pynixd` resolves via the pod's search domain.\n\nRunning the driver's own command by hand on the node works:\n\n```\n$ nix build --extra-substituters \"ssh-ng://nix@pynixd?trusted=1&priority=20\" \\\n    --no-link /nix/store/...-odoo-runtime\nthese 4 paths will be fetched (0.0 KiB download, 1.2 GiB unpacked):\ncopying path '/nix/store/...-odoo-18.0.20260420' from 'ssh-ng://nix@pynixd'...\n```\n\nSo the real failure is the `timeout` passed to `try_console` in `src/nix/build.py::_run_nix_build` \u2014 a 1.2 GiB fetch does not finish inside it, the driver retries, each attempt starts over, and nothing ever completes.\n\n## Two separate asks\n\n1. **Report the timeout as a timeout.** Surfacing nix's \"no substituter\" line for what is a deadline sends the reader to check reachability, signatures and the push, all of which are healthy. This is the part that matters: the diagnosis is much harder than the fix.\n2. **Size the bound for a cold node.** A first mount fetches the whole closure, so the timeout wants to scale with closure size, or be configurable.\n\n## Note\n\nSwitching the workload to NRI avoids this, because NRI holds the container through an OCI `createRuntime` hook rather than inside `NodePublishVolume` \u2014 `src/nri/server.py` says as much (\"because they lack forced low timeouts\"). That is the right answer for a large closure, but someone will reach for CSI first and meet this message.\n\n(Filed separately: nixidae/nixidae#471, a retry race on the NRI path.)\n\n## Environment\n\nnixkube 0.5.0, Talos 1.14, IPv6 cluster, closure 2.0 GiB / 230 paths, substituter is an in-cluster pynixd over ssh-ng."}
{"issue": 472, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWTe0w", "before": "I read the code. **That event is not a timeout being mislabelled.** It is nix\nreporting the truth: the substituter was not on the command line.\n\nTwo proofs.\n\n**The branch.** `nix/build.py:63` catches `CommandTimeoutError` before\n`SubprocessError`, and a timeout always raises the first: `run_console` turns\nevery deadline into `CommandTimeoutError` (`subprocessing.py:142`). Its message\nis `Build timeout for <path> after 300s`. The event quotes `Failed to build\nstore path <path>`, which is `error_msg` on the other branch, so `nix` exited\nnon-zero.\n\n**The text.** Reproduced here with no substituter and nothing else changed:\n\n```\n$ nix build --no-link --option substituters \"\" /nix/store/aaaa...-not-here\ndon't know how to build these paths:\n  /nix/store/aaaa...-not-here\nerror: path '...' is required, but there is no substituter that can build it\n```\n\nByte for byte your event. That message means the substituter list was empty.\n\n## How it gets empty\n\n`get_build_args()` (`nix/build.py:25`) adds `--extra-substituters` only when\n`check_cache_connectivity()` returns true, and caches the answer for 30s. That\ncheck is `nix store ping --store ssh-ng://nix@pynixd` under a 10s deadline\n(`cache.py:30`). A node that is fetching 1.2 GiB over ssh-ng from that same\npynixd can miss a 10s ping, and then every build on that node runs with no\nsubstituter for the next 30s.\n\n**Two log lines settle it on your side:** `cache_connectivity_failed` in the\nnode plugin log around the failure, and the DEBUG line `log_command` writes,\nwhich shows whether `--extra-substituters` was on the command.\n\n## What is worth fixing, whatever the probe did\n\n1. A failed probe **silently removes the only substituter**, and the error then\n   blames the substituter's absence. Nothing in the event says a probe ran or\n   failed. That is what sent you to check reachability, signatures and the push.\n2. The 30s TTL means one missed ping poisons every build started in that window.\n\nnixidae/nixidae#367 is why the probe exists -- an unreachable substituter costs 80s per miss --\nso the answer is not to drop the probe. Either name it in the failure, or give\nit one retry before it takes the substituter away.\n\n## On ask 2\n\nThe bound is already configurable: `nixkube.nodeBuildTimeout`\n(`kubenix/daemonset.nix:268`), default 300s, reaching `NIX_BUILD_TIMEOUT`.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 473, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWTocg", "before": "Verified on a node. `umlTest` boots the nix2container image under UML and\npasses: 1068s, the full chaos suite, and `appstarter-init` reads the image's\nown store the same way it did under `streamLayeredImage`.\n\n    appstarter.setup INFO installed /etc/passwd\n    appstarter.setup INFO installed /etc/group\n    appstarter.setup INFO installed /etc/nsswitch.conf\n    appstarter.init INFO fetching /nix/store/4a005...-nodeEnv (pynixd: disabled)\n    appstarter.init INFO store holds /nix/store/4a005...-nodeEnv\n\nSo `initializeNixDatabase` is equivalent to `includeNixDB` for what the\ninitContainer does, and the `nix:` to `docker-archive:` conversion in\n`nix/uml/images.nix` carries the store references the guest needs.\n\nThe image manifest's own closure is 735.1 MiB over 335 paths and holds both\nenvironments, so `cachix push nixkube ${image}` still publishes everything a\nnode needs to boot. That is the recovery lever from nixidae/nixidae#363, and it is unchanged.\n\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 476, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPYQgg", "before": "Carl's design, and a critique of it. Nothing implemented.\n\n## The design\n\nThe image carries complete `pynixd` and `nixkube`. Startup is\n\n    tini -> appstarter -> pynixd | nixkube\n\n`appstarter` is a small Python program that **tries** the version the spec\nnames, from the volume, and falls back to the copy in the image. Because the\nimage is complete, there is no initContainer: `appstarter` does the copy from\nthe substituters into the volume itself and then execs the real application.\nWhen substitution fails it sets `FETCH_FAILED=1` before the exec, and the\napplication then emits Kubernetes events saying it is not running the version\nit was asked for, retries the fetch, and kills itself when a retry succeeds.\n\n## What it gets right\n\n**It breaks the bootstrap deadlock, and that is the point.** `ekn.cacheTo` on\nnixlab2 is pynixd, so a pynixd that cannot start cannot be given the paths\nthat would let it start; nixidae/nixidae#363 is the same cycle seen from the node. An image\nthat can always start *something* ends the cycle, because the thing that\nserves the upgrade is the thing being upgraded.\n\n**It deletes a whole failure class.** nixidae/nixidae#472 is an initContainer sitting in\n`Init:0/1` behind a message about substituters. No initContainer, no such\nstate. The fetch also stops being a CRI call, so the kubelet's\n`runtimeRequestTimeout` no longer bounds it \u2014 which is the wall in nixidae/nixidae#471.\n\n**Degraded and running beats correct and absent** for infrastructure other\nthings depend on, and announcing the skew is what makes that safe.\n\n**The layering work makes it affordable.** Measured in the spike: both\napplications in the image cost 12 layers, and a release re-ships 0.8 MiB.\nWithout that, shipping two applications is what breaks the 126-layer limit.\n\n## The critique\n\n**1. \"Kills itself when the refetch succeeds\" is the part I would change.**\nA self-kill is a restart, and restarts are the kubelet's business:\n`CrashLoopBackOff` will eventually treat a healthy upgrade as a fault. Worse\nfor pynixd specifically \u2014 pynixd *is* the substituter, so its restart is a\ncluster-wide event that fails other nodes' in-flight fetches, not a local one.\nA restart also kills builds in flight, and nothing in the process knows\nwhether one is running.\n\nMake it a *request* rather than an act: the application reports \"a newer\nversion is available and fetched\" as a condition, and something outside\ndecides when to take it. If it must self-kill, it should drain first, require\nthe fetched version to differ from the running one, and jitter.\n\n**2. `FETCH_FAILED=1` is one bit where three facts are wanted.** Pass what was\nasked for and what is running \u2014 `WANTED_STORE_PATH`, `RUNNING_STORE_PATH` \u2014\nand the event text writes itself, the retry knows its target, and the app does\nnot have to re-derive either.\n\n**3. Events expire; skew does not.** The default event TTL is an hour, so a\ncluster that has been skewed since Tuesday looks clean. Put the running\nversion on the Node as a label or annotation, or expose it as a metric, so\n`kubectl get nodes -L` answers \"who is behind\" in one command.\n\n**4. Every capability inside `appstarter` is a capability pinned to the\nimage.** That is the tension in \"freedom from container images\": the fallback\ncan only be upgraded by shipping an image. So keep `appstarter` to selection,\nfetch and exec \u2014 and keep the event loop and the refetch inside\npynixd/nixkube, which is what the design already says. Hold that line\nstrictly; it is the thing that will erode.\n\n**5. It execs code fetched at runtime, with privilege.** nixkube's node pod is\nprivileged and mounts `/` (#22). The initContainer has the same property\ntoday, so this is not a regression \u2014 but a process that *retries* widens the\nwindow from once at boot to continuously. Signature verification before exec\nshould be a stated requirement of `appstarter`, not an assumed property of the\nstore.\n\n**6. Insist on `exec`.** If `appstarter` execs, tini's child becomes the\napplication and signals work. If it ever supervises instead, it has to forward\nsignals and reap, and it becomes a second init inside a container that already\nhas one. The design is consistent here \u2014 the refetch loop lives in the app\nprecisely because `appstarter` is gone by then.\n\n**7. Readiness has to follow the application, not the container.** The fetch\nnow happens inside the main container, so the pod is Running while it is still\ncopying. If a readiness probe passes before the real pynixd is serving,\nKubernetes routes work to a store that is not there yet.\n\n**8. The volume pointer needs to be atomic.** Whatever says \"this is the\ncurrent version\" in the volume has to be swapped, not mutated \u2014 a symlink\nrename, not a directory rewritten in place \u2014 or a restart during a copy reads\nhalf a version.\n\n## Relation to existing issues\n\nThis supersedes nixidae/nixidae#469, which boots pynixd with the nodes' `init-copy`\ninitContainer. Same goal, and this reaches it without an initContainer at all.\nnixidae/nixidae#469 should close in favour of this once the shape is agreed.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5\n"}
{"issue": 476, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWUCkg", "before": "Correction to the blocking paragraph above: the variable that leaks across the\n`exec` is `PATH`, not `PYTHONPATH`. Measured on `pkgs.nixkube` -- the bash\nwrapper exports `PATH` with 90 store `bin` directories prepended, plus\n`PYTHONNOUSERSITE` and three `SETUP_*`. Imports are resolved by a 43-entry\n`site.addsitedir` list baked into the entry-point script, which does not\nsurvive an exec. Detail on nixidae/nixidae#477.\n\nThe consequence for `appstarter` is the same and no smaller: the application\nit execs would resolve `nix`, `git` and `ssh` out of the image's store rather\nthan the volume's. The venv still fixes it, because a venv entry point is a\nplain script that exports nothing.\n\nOne thing nixidae/nixidae#477 does not fix on its own: `appstarter` has to be a member of the\nsame venv-built set. A `buildPythonApplication` `appstarter` rebuilds this\nproblem whatever the applications it starts are built with.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 476, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWUDAA", "before": "Wired, both workloads, on `develop`'s local tip (unpushed).\n\n**Node.** The `initcopy` shell script is gone. `appstarter-init` runs\n`appstarter init`, and `nix-node` runs `tini -- appstarter run nixkube`.\n\n**pynixd.** Same shape, with its PVC in place of the host store. It booted\nfrom a read-only CSI ephemeral volume served by the node driver; nixkube\ngarbage collects hard on pod exit, so `cacheEnv` can be gone by the next\nstart, and it is the nixidae/nixidae#363 cycle besides. The initContainer writes the PVC at\n`/nix-volume`; the pod mounts it at `/data` and again at `/nix` with\n`subPath = \"nix\"`.\n\n**One image for both.** It carries `APPSTARTER_FALLBACK_NODE` and\n`APPSTARTER_FALLBACK_CACHE`. The two environments share a nixpkgs pin, so the\nsecond is nearly free:\n\n| | paths | size |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| image closure | | 724.5 MiB |\n| `cacheEnv` on top of the rest | 86 | 46.5 MiB |\n\nThe fallback has to come from the image. A pod spec is rendered by the same\nevaluation that produces `APPSTARTER_WANTED`, so a fallback named there would\nbe the identical path.\n\n`nodeEnv` and `cacheEnv` both gain `appstarter`: the node's store covers `/nix`\nonce the pod runs, so the image's copy cannot exec after that.\n\nA side effect worth knowing: `pushArch` does `cachix push nixkube ${image}`,\nand both environments are now in that closure, so CI publishes them as a\nconsequence.\n\nNot verified by anything that runs: no test executes `appstarter init` or\n`appstarter run` in a container. `umlTest` boots a real node through the\ninitContainer and is running locally; pynixd's PVC path is only exercised by\n`test-kind-cache` in CI.\n\nOne loose end, for nixidae/nixidae#478 or a follow-up: `--out-link /nix-volume/nix/var/result`\nregisters an indirect gcroot naming `/nix-volume/...`, which does not exist in\nthe main container's view. `startup.py` re-registers it for the node;\npynixd's `setup.py` does not. Harmless today, because pynixd's GC has no\ncollector, and not once it has one.\n\nThis supersedes nixidae/nixidae#469.\n\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 477, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPYXlA", "before": "`buildPythonApplication` wraps every entry point with `PYTHONPATH` pointing at\nthe whole propagated closure. nixidae/nixidae#476 needs one application to `exec` another that\nlives in a different store, and an inherited `PYTHONPATH` puts the wrong\nstore's paths on the second one's `sys.path`. A pyproject.nix venv resolves\nimports from the venv itself and writes no such wrapper, so the exec is clean.\n\nThe same change fixes the class of bug the propagation model keeps producing\nhere: `python.withPackages` drops an application together with everything it\npropagates, and a dependency named in `dependencies` reaches every consumer's\nruntime closure whether or not anything imports it. Both cost real work this\nweek.\n\n## Shape\n\nnanopynix owns the machinery and exports `pythonSetWith`, `ps.mkProject` and\n`mkApp`; easykubenix consumes it for `ekn` in its `default.nix`. Both are in\nthe umbrella and `sources.nanopynix` already resolves from this repository. No\n`uv.lock` is involved: the set is seeded from nixpkgs through\n`hacks.nixpkgsPrebuilt`.\n\nNine project roots here carry a `pyproject.toml`: `nixkube`, `nri-wait`,\n`pynixd-nixkube`, `grpclib-ttrpc`, `grpclib-nri` and the four `*-proto-python`\npackages.\n\n## The three parts that need a decision, not a translation\n\n1. **The proto packages generate their own sources.** `csi-proto-python` and\n   its three siblings run `protoc` in `preBuild` and write `src/`.\n   `mkProject` reads a project root as it stands, so the codegen has to become\n   a derivation that produces a root, which the set then builds.\n2. **`nixpkgsRootsFor` resolves every declared name against `python.pkgs`**\n   and throws otherwise. `kr8s`, `shellous`, `python-jsonpath` and `pynixd`\n   come from this repository's overlay or from another repository, so they\n   need `exclude` plus overlay entries. `exclude` cannot be derived from the\n   directory names: `nixkube` declares `csi`, `nri` and `cri`, whose roots are\n   `pkgs/csi-proto-python` and friends.\n3. **`pynixd` arrives as a nixpkgs `buildPythonPackage`** from its own\n   repository, so it is lifted rather than built by the set.\n\nBlocks nixidae/nixidae#476.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 477, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWUK1w", "before": "## Correction: the leak is `PATH`, not `PYTHONPATH`\n\nMeasured on `pkgs.nixkube` before touching anything.\n\n`buildPythonApplication` resolves imports by baking a 43-entry\n`site.addsitedir` list into the entry-point script itself\n(`bin/.nixkube-wrapped`). That is a property of the script, so it does not\nsurvive an `exec` and cannot reach a second program.\n\nWhat the bash wrapper beside it does export is `PATH`, with 90 store `bin`\ndirectories prepended, plus `PYTHONNOUSERSITE` and three `SETUP_*` variables.\n`grep -oE \"^export [A-Z_]+\"` over it returns exactly those, and no\n`PYTHONPATH` appears anywhere in the package.\n\nSo the nixidae/nixidae#476 hazard is real and is the other variable: an `appstarter` built\nthis way hands the application it execs a `PATH` that resolves `nix`, `git`\nand `ssh` out of the image's store rather than the volume's.\n\nThe conclusion does not change. A venv entry point is a plain script with a\nshebang into the venv: it exports nothing, and `sys.path` is one prefix\ninstead of 43 store paths.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 477, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWULXA", "before": "## Done, in eight commits\n\nAll nine projects build from `pythonSet`; the three applications ship as\nvenvs; the nixpkgs Python builders are gone. `nix build --file ./checks.nix\nall` and treefmt green, `pyright pkgs/nixkube/src` 0 errors.\n\n**Two suites were not running as gates.** `pkgs/grpclib-ttrpc/tests` (62\ntests) and `pkgs/nixkube/tests` (151) rode inside package builds, so the\nmigration would have dropped them silently. All five are now their own\n`checks` member: 252 tests in about 13 seconds. `just test` named two of the\nfive and now names all of them.\n\n**A real dependency gap, found by the venv.** nixpkgs declares `grpclib`'s\ndependencies as `h2` and `multidict`, and `grpclib/_typing.py` imports\n`typing_extensions` with no guard. It hides upstream because\n`pythonImportsCheck` names `grpclib`, whose `__init__.py` never reaches\n`_typing`. Worked around in `nix/python-set.nix`; not reported anywhere yet.\n\n**Measured**, `pkgs.nixkube` against the nixpkgs build:\n\n|                | venv       | nixpkgs    |\n| ---            | ---        | ---        |\n| closure        | 533.8 MiB  | 534.2 MiB  |\n| paths          | 206        | 167        |\n| wrapper `PATH` | 35 entries | 90 entries |\n\nThe 39 extra paths are lifted-package shims, about 0.8 KiB each, 31 KiB\ntogether. `nodeEnv` moves the same way: 191 paths and 610 MiB to 230 and 609.\n\nThe image spike needed two fixes, or it would have failed quietly:\n`propagatedBuildInputs` is empty on a builders package, and the runtime tools\nfollow the wrapper into the application's layer. With a `tools` group the\nimage is 13 layers and a release still re-ships 1.3 MiB.\n\nnixidae/nixidae#476 is unblocked.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 493, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWVaGQ", "before": "Settled, and it is a fourth outcome my taxonomy above missed: **valid on the claim, absent from the node.** Verified on the cluster by the solid-kubernetes session and against this tree.\n\nOn the claim, from inside pynixd-0:\n\n    nix path-info --store /data /nix/store/yjb1w5...-cacheEnv\n      -> /nix/store/yjb1w5...-cacheEnv\n\nValid, `db.sqlite` present at 798720 bytes. So ekn's push landed and registered, and the PVC reasoning holds exactly.\n\nThe failing build is not `appstarter-init`'s. It is the **node-side NRI fetch**, and it runs before the init container's process starts at all. Its argv carries no `--store`:\n\n    nix build --out-link /nix/var/nix-csi/containers/<id>/build \\\n      ...-init-secrets ...-cacheEnv ...-nodeEnv ...-cacheEnv ...-appstarter-wrapped\n\n`nixkube.nix`, `fetch_packages_failed`, from `nri/server.py _spawn_build_task` -> `nix/build.py fetch_packages`. Confirmed in the tree: `fetch_packages` passes no `--store` (pkgs/nixkube/src/nix/build.py:142), so it realises into the **node's** store, whose only substituter is pynixd over ssh-ng.\n\nWhy the node is fetching pynixd's own closure at all \u2014 this is the nixidae/nixidae#363 cycle re-entering through a door the PVC design left open:\n\n- `CreateContainer` stands down when a container already has `/nix` mounted (nri/server.py:214).\n- The `pynixd` container mounts the claim at `/nix`, so it is skipped.\n- `appstarter-init` mounts the claim at **`/nix-volume`**, not `/nix` (kubenix/pynixd.nix:448). The skip does not fire.\n- `extract_container_store_paths` scans `req.container.env` (nri/annotations.py:46), and that init container's env is `APPSTARTER_WANTED` \u2014 a JSON map of `cacheEnv` store paths.\n\nSo NRI extracts pynixd's own closure from the init container that exists to fetch that closure, and tries to realise it node-side from pynixd, which is down because this is pynixd's own pod starting. nri-wait then reports `{'status': 'unknown'}`, gives up after 30s, and the container dies with StartError 128.\n\nThe initContainer was added (issue nixidae/nixidae#476) precisely to break this cycle. It cannot, while NRI still pre-fetches for it.\n\nCorrection to the earlier report, from its author: nri-wait blocking is real and visible in the pod's own message, but the fallback being unreachable was the wrong framing \u2014 the fallback is downstream of a fetch that is not appstarter's.\n\nThis narrows the four candidates. \"Serve before the cutover\" and \"a second replica\" address the node's substituter and could work. \"Seed-and-converge\" only helps if the seeding is node-side, not in appstarter. \"Push outside the cluster first\" is the only one that does not depend on pynixd running.\n\nBut the minimal fix is none of those: **exempt `appstarter-init` from NRI injection.** It brings its own store and needs no node-side realisation. Two shapes, and the choice is open \u2014 mount the claim at `/nix` in the init container so the existing skip fires, or an explicit annotation that opts a container out of injection.\n\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 493, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWVajw", "before": "Fixed in nixkube `24a8a63b`, umbrella `c8e726fe`.\n\n`nixkube/pod-exclude` and `nixkube/{container}-exclude` let a container that brings its own store opt out of injection \u2014 `@system` variants, and the precedence `-rw` already uses: container-specific wins, including an explicit `false` against a pod-wide `true`. The check runs **before** anything reads the container's environment, which is the point: an excluded container's store paths must not even be extracted, let alone realised on the node.\n\n`appstarter-init` carries it in the pynixd pod.\n\nThe obvious alternative is wrong and was disproved rather than merely passed over. Mounting the claim at `/nix` in the init container *would* make the existing `/nix` check fire \u2014 and it would shadow the image's own store, which is where `appstarter`'s fallback copy lives. That fallback is what issue nixidae/nixidae#476 added, so that route trades this bug for that one.\n\n`pynixdInitIsExemptFromInjection` asserts the rendered pod carries the annotation for `appstarter-init` and not for `pynixd`. The annotation keys on the container's name, so a rename would otherwise break the exemption silently. The main container stays unannotated deliberately: it mounts `/nix` and stands down for its own reason, and annotating it would hide a regression in that check.\n\nKnown sharp edge, pre-existing and benign: `_iter_annotations`' `(-.*|)` suffix means `nixkube/X-exclude` is also matched when scanning target `X` for store-path sources, so its value `\"true\"` reaches `extract_store_paths`. It holds no store path and no `=`, so nothing comes of it, and `-rw` has always behaved the same way.\n\n**This does not close the design gap this issue was opened for.** pynixd is still its own only substituter for its own new closure, and a cluster that loses the claim still has no way back. What it closes is the specific cycle that wedged nixlab2. Leaving the issue open for the general case, unless it should be split.\n\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 503, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPaf2g", "before": "`--kubeconfig-from-tofu UNIT:OUTPUT` is the only way to point `ekn kubeapply`\nat a cluster a `tf` unit built (`ekn/src/ekn/cli.py:1262`). With no flag, `ekn`\nuses the ambient kubeconfig. Carl asks for the source to be declared instead.\n\n## Why, beyond retyping it\n\nThe ambient default is not a neutral fallback. The session working on the\nconsuming repository reports applying a full environment to the KubeVirt host\nthat runs that environment's VMs, because the ambient context was a valid,\nreachable, credentialed cluster -- the wrong one. 31 namespaces, 184 CRDs, a\nStorageClass that took `is-default-class` beside the host's real default; it\nstopped at barrier 14 of 24 on a clusterIP conflict, which is the only reason\nno workload started. There was no error to notice. I have not verified that\nmyself and it is not this repository's incident, but it is the case the option\nanswers: a declared source *removes* the default rather than guarding it.\n\n## Shape\n\n`ekn.kubeconfigFromTofu = \"infra:kubeconfig\"`, beside `ekn.environment` in\n`easykubenix/ekn.nix`, read by `ekn.eval`. Per configuration and not per unit:\nthat consumer has one infra unit and one cluster per environment, and one\nconfiguration is one environment. The flag still wins when given.\n\n**No fallback to the ambient kubeconfig when it cannot be resolved.** A fresh\nclone, no state, or the wrong operator must fail naming the unit and the\noutput. A fallback turns \"I cannot find your cluster\" into \"I applied to\nwhatever your shell pointed at\", reachable by someone who did nothing wrong.\n\n## Evaluation does not touch state, so CI is unaffected\n\nChecked here: `TofuUnit` (`ekn/src/ekn/eval.py:80`) carries `configFile` and\n`tofu`, both store paths. `evaluate_tofu_units` realises them -- a Nix build of\nthe rendered `config.tf.json`, no backend, no cloud credentials. `tofu output`\nruns only from `KubeApply._kubeconfig`, at apply time. So the declared value is\na pointer and nothing resolves it eagerly; a CI job that only evaluates a\nconfiguration never reaches a backend.\n\nOne apply-time cost stands: `tofu.prepare` runs `tofu init -input=false` unless\nthe working directory is unchanged and `.terraform` exists, so the first\nresolve after a fresh clone is a backend round trip.\n\n## Related\n\nnixidae/nixidae#357 specifies this handoff as `terranix.stages.<name>.provides.kubeconfig`,\nagainst a stage tree that was never built -- what shipped is\n`deployment.units.<name>.class = \"tf\"`. This issue is that half of nixidae/nixidae#357,\nscoped to what exists.\n\nSecondary, only if cheap: `--confirm-context` (`cli.py:1258`) would have caught\nthe incident above. Consider refusing by default when the resolved\nkubeconfig's context does not match `ekn.environment`. Whether those names line\nup in general is a guess.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 503, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWWuDg", "before": "The hazard argument here moves to nixidae/nixidae#504, which fences the apply on the\n`kube-system` Namespace uid.\n\nnixidae/nixidae#504 is the better answer to \"applied to the wrong cluster\" because it needs no\naccess to infra state: someone who deploys to Kubernetes without rights to read\nthe tofu backend can still use it, and it is one declared line rather than a\ncredential path.\n\nThis issue stands on its own as the ergonomics case -- not retyping\n`--kubeconfig-from-tofu infra:kubeconfig` on every apply. The two compose: nixidae/nixidae#504\nsays which cluster is allowed, this one says where the credential comes from.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 505, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPatbw", "before": "The UML half of nixidae/nixidae#376, with the measurement that issue asks for.\n\n**Guest RAM is a file.** UML's \"physical\" memory is an unlinked file it mmaps `MAP_SHARED`. It is sparse, so a large `mem=` costs nothing until the guest touches it -- and it only ever grows, because nothing punches holes in it.\n\n**Measured**, one guest at `mem=1024M` reading 563 MiB of store paths over hostfs:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| guest `Cached` | 79 MB -> 658 MB |\n| host blocks allocated to that guest's RAM file | 717 MiB |\n| host page cache of the same three files, by `mincore` | 563 of 563 MiB |\n\nThe host pays about 1126 MiB to read 563 MiB once: its own page cache, and the guest's copy inside the guest's RAM file. Neither half comes back while the guest runs.\n\n**The second copy buys less here than on KVM.** A guest page-cache miss on hostfs is a host `read()` that hits the host's page cache -- a memcpy, not disk I/O. Same guest, same file, host cache hot:\n\n- guest cache miss: 1.3-1.5 GB/s\n- guest cache hit: 5.1-6.5 GB/s\n\nSo a smaller `mem=` costs a 4x slower re-read at memory speed, not a disk seek. That is a much cheaper trade than nixidae/nixidae#376's QEMU case, and it makes right-sizing `mem=` a real lever on its own.\n\n**Nothing removes the copy.** hostfs is `generic_file_read_iter` plus `read_folio`: no DAX, no `O_DIRECT`, no mapping of the host's own pages. ubd's flags are `r s d c t`, where `s` is `O_SYNC`; there is no `O_DIRECT` there either. Removing the copy means teaching hostfs to map the host's pages, which is upstream work.\n\n**Giving the memory back is possible today, in one of two shapes.**\n\n1. *mconsole's `mem` device.* `config mem=-256M` allocates guest pages and `MADV_REMOVE`s them from the RAM file -- UML's own balloon. Needs `CONFIG_MCONSOLE` (allnoconfig leaves it off), `uml_dir=` and `umid=`, and a client, which is `struct.pack` on a datagram socket. Two limits: `mem_config` allocates `GFP_ATOMIC` and stops at the first failure, so it only takes pages that are already free -- drop the guest's caches first -- and `mem=+N` cannot go above the boot `mem=`.\n2. *A `page_reporting` consumer.* `CONFIG_PAGE_REPORTING` is generic mm. A UML driver that registers a `report` callback calling `os_drop_memory` shrinks continuously with no host orchestration, and growing back is free because the file is sparse. A kernel patch carried in `pkgs/uml-kernel`.\n\nvirtio-balloon does not apply: `virtio_uml` is a vhost-user transport, and there is no balloon backend to speak to.\n\nFixed separately while measuring this: every sandboxed guest's RAM file landed on the builder's disk, because Nix sets `TMPDIR=/build` and UML takes it with only a warning.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 505, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWW9Zw", "before": "Both runners now, and both mechanisms. user-mode-nixos `48bef443`, locked by nixidae `0125c92c`.\n\n`memory.py` runs on both backends and cannot tell which it is on. One guest at `memory = \"1024M\"` reading its own 362 MiB closure and dropping its cache:\n\n| | UML | QEMU |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| at boot | 149M | 349M |\n| after the read | 553M | 782M |\n| seconds later, unasked | 148M | 404M |\n\nAnswering the original question directly:\n\n- **The double copy is real and cannot be removed today.** 563 MiB read over hostfs cost the host 1126 MiB \u2014 its own page cache plus the guest's, measured with `mincore` on one side and the guest's RAM file on the other. hostfs is `generic_file_read_iter` + `read_folio`: no DAX, no `O_DIRECT`, no mapping of the host's pages. Removing it means teaching hostfs to map them, which is upstream work nobody has started.\n- **It matters less here than on KVM.** A guest cache miss on hostfs is a host `read()` that hits the host's own cache \u2014 1.3-1.5 GB/s against 5.1-6.5 GB/s for a hit. A memcpy, not a seek. So a smaller `memory` is a cheap lever under UML in a way it is not under QEMU.\n- **Reclaiming it is now automatic.** Free page reporting on both backends, at order 5 on both: `madvise(MADV_REMOVE)` under UML (#13, a patch in `pkgs/uml-kernel`), `virtio-balloon-pci,free-page-reporting=on` under QEMU (#4).\n- **Growing and shrinking on demand works too**, through UML's management console and QEMU's monitor, behind `vm.shrink()` / `vm.grow()`.\n\nNothing left open here. nixidae/nixidae#506 and nixidae/nixidae#376 carry the two halves.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 507, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPa7dQ", "before": "Third occurrence of one failure. nixidae/nixidae#432 was `tcpSocket` with the kubelet's 1s default; nixidae/nixidae#487 replaced it with `/healthz` because a TCP accept cannot tell busy from wedged. The current probe is killed too.\n\n**Measured on nixlab2 by solid-kubernetes**, pushing a 1.6 GiB closure with `ekn kubeapply`:\n\n```\nWarning Unhealthy  Liveness probe failed: Get .../healthz: context deadline exceeded\nNormal  Killing    Container pynixd failed liveness probe, will be restarted\n```\n\nContainer exit 143, reason Error, at 12:58:03 \u2014 86 seconds after pynixd's own log records the client handshake at 12:56:37. Pod returned Running with RESTARTS 1. The probe as rendered: `periodSeconds 10`, `timeoutSeconds 10`, `failureThreshold 6`, so 60 seconds of no answer. `resources.requests` cpu 100m, memory 64Mi, no limits. Even a light query costs: one batch of 859 `QueryPathInfo` calls took 3.037s.\n\n**Why `/healthz` did not close it.** ~~pynixd stalls its own loop doing store work (hardlinking is synchronous on the loop that serves the probe).~~ **Wrong \u2014 see the correction comment below.** That is a nixkube fact applied to pynixd without reading pynixd's transfer path. `/healthz` is answered by the event loop and cannot pass while the loop is stalled, which is still the point; what stalls it here is not established.\n\n**It names the wrong subsystem.** The client sees\n\n```\nkex_exchange_identification: read: Connection reset by peer\nerror: failed to start SSH connection to 'pynixd.lab2...'\n```\n\nwhich reads as DNS, a host key or a firewall, and is a probe in another namespace. nixidae/nixidae#432 records the same misdirection with a different message.\n\n**Not blocking:** a push is incremental per path, so retrying makes progress. It gets more likely as a closure grows, which is exactly when a new component lands.\n\nFix not proposed here. Serving `/healthz` off something that does not contend with the daemon, a push-aware `failureThreshold`, and a cpu request that reflects a push are all untested guesses from outside the code. What the next person should not do is raise `timeoutSeconds` again: nixidae/nixidae#432 and nixidae/nixidae#487 both did a version of that, and the third occurrence is the result.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5\n"}
{"issue": 515, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWYNMA", "before": "**The GC reading is ruled out for this path.** I raised it as a possibility above; it is not what happened.\n\n`gc_task.py:229` selects with `time.time() - GC_KEEP_SECONDS`, and `:158` offers a path only when `registrationTime < cutoff`. `GC_KEEP_SECONDS` defaults to 3600 (`constants.py:141`) and is not set on this DaemonSet \u2014 the `nix-node` container carries no `GC_*` variable at all, confirmed inside the running container.\n\nThe path's age at the failure was about twenty minutes:\n\n```\nregistrationTime  1790103343  = 2026-09-22T18:55:43Z   (on nixlab2-worker-3, read now)\nfailure window    between 18:56:50Z and 19:15:21Z      (the ReplicaSets either side of it)\n```\n\nIt still carries that same `registrationTime`, so it was not deleted and re-fetched either. Twenty minutes against a 3600s floor: `_select_old_paths` could not have offered it.\n\nSo the remaining reading is the bad one. `nix store verify --recursive --no-trust` returned success for container `ad22c728`, and `hardlink_closure` then raised `FileNotFoundError` for a file inside that same closure, with no GC able to touch it in between. The file is present on the node now.\n\nWorth noting about the node: `nixlab2-worker-3` had been power-cycled earlier that day while nix was stuck in `D` state mid-`fchown` on an ext4 journal, during a full-Ceph incident. A damaged store on that filesystem is plausible \u2014 but that is exactly what a recursive verify is supposed to catch, so it does not rescue the check.\n\n**Correction to my own paragraph on the default mismatch.** As written it reads as though a node could pick either value. It cannot: `daemonset.nix:297` renders `VERIFY_STORE_PATHS` unconditionally from `cfg.verifyStorePaths`, so a pod from this chart always carries an explicit value and `constants.py`'s default is unreachable there. It is reachable only when the daemon runs outside the DaemonSet. The two defaults still disagree and the file still misleads a reader, but no node has silently picked the wrong one.\n\nPossibly related: nixidae/nixidae#467, if a CSI volume's gc root dying with its handle can expose the same window. I have no evidence tying this failure to that one."}
{"issue": 517, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPcDdg", "before": "Can a container's /nix be built from the host store at mount time, so\nnothing persists and the volume root disappears along with nixidae/nixidae#516's race?\nMeasured on Linux 6.18, nix 2.34.8, against a 2443-path / 11.8 GiB closure.\n\n## What does not work\n\n**A bind farm as an overlayfs lowerdir.** overlayfs does not follow\nsubmounts in a lower layer. The bind source lists `['marker']` directly and\n`[]` through the overlay \u2014 you get the empty mountpoint directories.\n\n**More than 500 lower layers.** `lowerdir+` (Linux 6.7) removes the option\n*string* limit \u2014 a joined `lowerdir=` for 2048 store paths is 49,151 bytes,\nwell past the 4 KB page \u2014 but the stack limit stands: layer 501 gives\nEINVAL. (The familiar ~127 is the old single-string limit at a shorter\naverage path length, not a stack limit.)\n\n## What does work\n\n**tmpfs at /nix, one read-only bind per store path.** 2425 paths in\n**0.034s (14 us/path)**, against `hardlink_tree` at 22 us/*entry* over that\nclosure's 375,582 entries. New store paths can be created (the store\ndirectory is tmpfs); existing ones refuse writes.\n`mount_setattr(MOUNT_ATTR_RDONLY)` (5.12) does the read-only step, and\nsucceeds in a user namespace where `MS_REMOUNT|MS_RDONLY` gives EPERM.\n\n**The farm attaches as one unit.** `open_tree(OPEN_TREE_CLONE|AT_RECURSIVE)`\nthen `move_mount` carried all 600 submounts of a test farm intact \u2014 the\npattern `nri/mount.py` already uses for RO. So the farm can be built away\nfrom the daemon's namespace and moved in.\n\n**Chunked overlays, if true copy-on-write over existing store paths is\nneeded.** N inner overlays of <=500 lowers, then an outer overlay over the\ninner roots: all entries visible, outer writable, COW over an inner lower's\nfile works and leaves the original untouched.\n\n## Open, and load-bearing\n\n- **What is RW mode for?** `parse_nix_rw` documents the mechanism, not the\n  intent, and nothing else says. If it means \"nix build inside the pod\",\n  tmpfs+binds is enough and chunked overlays are a footnote. If anything\n  edits a store path in place, tmpfs+binds breaks it.\n- **CSI may not be able to use this.** Every measurement here ran in a\n  namespace we own, which is NRI's shape. CSI hands a directory to kubelet,\n  and a non-recursive bind drops submounts \u2014 the same failure as the\n  overlay case. Unverified.\n- **tmpfs upper means RAM.** Today's RW upper is disk-backed on the volume\n  root. A kubelet-managed emptyDir may be the better writable base.\n- `fs.mount-max` is 100,000 per namespace, and open_tree doubles the count\n  transiently. Mount-table memory and `/proc/mounts` parsing cost inside a\n  container with 2443 mounts: unmeasured.\n- Propagation: these tests ran in a private namespace by construction. On a\n  systemd host `/` is shared.\n- Closure-hash dedup of hardlink trees is moot under a farm \u2014 the farm is a\n  view of the shared store. If CSI keeps hardlinks, a shared closure-keyed\n  tree has several owners and nixidae/nixidae#516's \"who tears it down\" gets harder."}
{"issue": 517, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWYf7w", "before": "RW mode's purpose, from Carl: running `nix build` inside the container \u2014\ngitpod-like scenarios. Not well exercised yet, but a real use case. Nothing\nedits an existing store path in place.\n\n**That settles the route.** A bind farm gives `nix build` everything it\nneeds: new store paths land in a writable store *directory*, /nix/var/nix/db\nis writable, and existing paths stay immutable \u2014 which is what nix assumes\nanyway. Chunked overlays are a footnote, kept only as the answer if\ncopy-on-write over existing paths is ever needed.\n\nIt also sharpens the writable-base question rather than closing it. A\ngitpod builds real things, so RAM is the wrong backing store:\n\n- **CSI**: the pod declares the volume, so a kubelet-managed disk-backed\n  directory is available and kubelet cleans it up. nixkube owns nothing.\n- **NRI**: nothing was declared \u2014 injection is annotation-driven \u2014 so a RW\n  pod needs a disk-backed directory nixkube creates. That is the volume\n  root again, and the nixidae/nixidae#516 ownership question comes back with it. It is far\n  smaller than today's: empty mountpoint directories plus whatever the pod\n  builds, not a hardlinked copy of the closure. But it is not nothing, and\n  \"RW pods still need a persistent directory\" should be stated rather than\n  discovered.\n\nTwo things to check before building, neither measured:\n\n- **`auto-optimise-store` would break.** It hardlinks a new path against an\n  existing one; with existing paths bind-mounted from the host store that\n  crosses a filesystem boundary and gives EXDEV. Off by default, so this is\n  a configuration to refuse rather than a blocker.\n- **A build's rename into the store** stays within the writable directory\n  and never crosses a bind, so it should be fine \u2014 but it is the operation\n  `nix build` ends on, and worth one test before trusting it."}
{"issue": 517, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWYhLA", "before": "Publish side is done: nixidae/nixidae#518 is fixed in `4ef4ac65`, so a farm under a volume root now reaches the pod and is read-only all the way down.\n\nTeardown is the remaining mount-layer gap. `unmount` calls `umount2` with flag 0, which fails with `EBUSY` once the target carries submounts. `MNT_DETACH` is the obvious swap and it is the wrong one on its own: a lazy unmount returns success and the `is_mount` check after it then reports the mount gone whether or not anything still holds it, which is the check that stops a live pod's volume being deleted. Whoever does the farm work needs to decide what replaces that guarantee."}
{"issue": 517, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWYiVg", "before": "Policy settled, per Carl: **NRI takes mounts always**, since the worker's namespace dies with it and containers never accumulate mounts on the node. **CSI takes mounts until within 10% of `fs.mount-max`**, then falls back to a hardlink tree and says so at error level.\n\nThe fallback half is in and in use by the NRI path (`91b5081a`): `mountbudget.affordable`, `MOUNT_BUDGET_FALLBACK_RATIO` at 0.9, and `prepare_volume` returning the layout it actually got rather than the one it was asked for. It replaced a refusal that failed the container outright \u2014 which was worse, because the pod did not start for a reason about the node.\n\nThe CSI farm itself, and the teardown problem it still has, is now nixidae/nixidae#520. Direct-to-OTEL events, so this kind of thing carries its numbers instead of being grepped back out of logs, is nixidae/nixidae#519."}
{"issue": 519, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPcSyA", "before": "Everything this daemon knows leaves it as a structlog line or a Prometheus series. Both lose most of it.\n\nA log line has to be parsed back into fields by whatever collects it, and the shape is only a convention \u2014 `logger.error(\"mount_budget_exhausted\", used=..., limit=..., needed=...)` is structured when it leaves and a string by the time anything queries it. A Prometheus series keeps the number and throws away everything that made it interesting: `nixkube_hardlink_closures_total{result=\"error\"}` cannot say which container, which closure, or which store path went missing, because [the metrics module forbids a store path as a label](https://github.com/Lillecarl/nixkube/blob/develop/pkgs/nixkube/src/metrics.py) and it is right to.\n\nAn OTEL client would let the events carry what they actually know:\n\n- a build task as a span, with the closure size, the layout it got, and the time split between `nix build`, preparing the volume and the namespace mount\n- `mount_budget_exhausted` with the numbers as attributes rather than in a message, so \"which nodes fell back to hardlinks this week, and how big were the closures\" is a query\n- the nixidae/nixidae#516 race and its cousins as events on the container's span, where the ordering against the sweep is visible instead of inferred from timestamps in two log streams\n- `farm_fell_back_to_hardlinks`, which today is one error line and a counter, with no way to ask which volume\n\nThe traps worth naming up front, because they are the same ones the metrics module already documents: a store path is high-cardinality and must be an attribute rather than anything indexed, and the exporter must never block the event loop \u2014 that loop serves the CSI server, the NRI plugin and the heartbeat `nri-wait` gives up on after 30s."}
{"issue": 520, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPcZkQ", "before": "The policy, now that the NRI half has landed (#65):\n\n- **NRI: mounts, always.** The farm is built in the mount worker, whose namespace is a copy of the daemon's and dies with it. Containers do not accumulate mounts on the node \u2014 each pays for its own closure and gives it back \u2014 so the budget is effectively unreachable here.\n- **CSI: mounts until within 10% of `fs.mount-max`, then a hardlink tree, loudly.** A CSI farm has to live in the *host* namespace from publish to unpublish, because at `NodePublishVolume` there is no container to put it in yet. That one accumulates: ~40 volumes per node at 2443 paths, scaling with closure size.\n\nThe fallback half already exists and is in use by the NRI path \u2014 `mountbudget.affordable`, `MOUNT_BUDGET_FALLBACK_RATIO` at 0.9, `prepare_volume(bind_farm=...)` returning the layout it actually got, and `nixkube_mount_budget_fallbacks_total` beside the two gauges. What is missing is the CSI farm itself.\n\n**What that needs, and the part that is not wiring.** Teardown. `unmount` answers `EBUSY` over submounts, and `MNT_DETACH` alone is unsafe: after a lazy unmount the mount leaves the host namespace, so `is_mount_source` answers False and the sweep deletes a directory a running pod is rooted at. Measured \u2014 deleting a farm's directory does break a pod using it, the same way the hardlink case does: the pod's mount is rooted at that dentry, the kernel marks it deleted, lookups give `ENOENT`. So teardown needs to unmount the farm's own mounts before the parent, or `is_mount_source` needs a different way to know a volume is live.\n\ncontainerd rbinds volume mounts into containers (`internal/cri/opts/spec_linux_opts.go:154`), so visibility is not a problem \u2014 a host-side farm would be seen by the pod, submounts and all.\n\nWorth weighing against the alternative on nixidae/nixidae#517: letting NRI deliver the mount for CSI-requested volumes removes the host-namespace cost entirely, since runc runs `createRuntime` hooks after the spec mounts and before `pivot_root` specifically so hooks can manipulate mounts. That only helps deployments running both, which is why this issue exists as well."}
{"issue": 520, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWY4VQ", "before": "Now a prerequisite of nixidae/nixidae#360 (host store), 2026-10-01. Measured in the node guest: a hardlink from the host's `/nix/store` into `/nix/var` fails with EXDEV, directly and through an rbind. `link()` fails across mounts, and a NixOS host's `/nix/store` is its own read-only bind mount. So a host-store node can build a CSI volume only as a farm, and has no hardlink fallback: near `fs.mount-max` it fails the publish with an error that says so.\n\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 523, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPct_g", "before": "Most shops ship OCI images built from a Dockerfile. A test runner they want has to build those and run them, not only images from `dockerTools`.\n\n**The constraint.** A Dockerfile build fetches from the network: base images, package managers, `curl | sh`. The Nix sandbox has no network, so the build cannot be a derivation. A guest can have an uplink, so the build can run there.\n\n**A shape to test, not decided:**\n- A `build` phase on a builder guest runs BuildKit, podman or buildah with the uplink up. It writes an OCI archive to `/artifacts`, or pushes to a registry guest on the segment.\n- Later phases load the image into the guests under test, through containerd as `k8s` does now, and test it.\n- It runs outside the sandbox (`.run`, `uml-eval`, a CI job like `test-k8s-pull`), never as a sandboxed check. `--offline` must fail such a phase with a clear message.\n\n**Open questions:**\n- The layer cache between runs. A host directory the builder mounts, like `/artifacts` but kept between runs? Without one, every run pulls its base images again.\n- Which builder: BuildKit (`buildctl`, rootless) or podman/buildah.\n- A registry as a recipe (zot or distribution) against loading archives.\n- What a test states about the result: the digest, the SBOM, that it runs.\n\nRelated: nixidae/nixidae#378 (loading images one at a time)."}
{"issue": 528, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPdQxw", "before": "Explore mergerfs as the way to present a pod's Nix store, in place of the NRI bind farm and the CSI hardlink tree.\n\n**Why look.** The two current mechanisms each have a hard limit:\n- The bind farm is one read-only bind mount per store path, about 2,400 per closure. `fs.mount-max` is 100,000 per mount namespace. So the farm must stay in a worker's namespace, and CSI cannot use it: CSI publishes in the host namespace, and unmount gets `EBUSY` over the submounts (#65).\n- The hardlink tree costs about 55 us per file and cannot be made faster. It also leaves a tree to garbage-collect. nixidae/nixidae#527 may also be dentry cache that such trees build up (not verified).\n\nA single FUSE mount per pod would avoid the mount count and the per-file link cost.\n\n**Questions to answer, none of them verified:**\n- Can mergerfs show only a closure? It merges whole directories, and has no filter on entries. The branches need to be something that holds exactly the closure's store paths.\n- Read cost. FUSE passthrough, in newer mergerfs and kernels, may bring reads close to native. Measure a build or a large closure read against the bind farm.\n- One mergerfs process per pod, or one shared daemon? What happens to the pod's store when that process dies?\n- A read-write store: a writable branch on disk (not tmpfs), which is how the bind farm does it today.\n- Garbage collection must keep its current rule: the CRI's live containers decide what exists, with no refcount or state file.\n- The mount has to reach the container. CSI runs before the container exists.\n\nA success is a measurement: the mount count, the setup time for one closure, read throughput, and slab use, each against the bind farm.\n"}
{"issue": 551, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPfvkw", "before": "**OPEN.** Nothing is wired. The only transport is grpclib over a socket,\nand nixidae/nixidae#572 is waiting on the same question.\n\nWire the user's existing grpclib shims (SSH, stdio, Python\nmultiprocessing) in front of the same manifest-driven dispatcher to\nprove transport independence. Requires 002 first: per-connection\nSession ownership and disconnect reaping.\n\nMigrated from `tasks/014-transport-shims.md`.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5\n"}
{"issue": 605, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPlgdw", "before": "**OPEN.** Measured after the jj-workspace integration landed, because\n\"GBs of junk\" needs a number before it needs a policy. The junk is\nnot the worktrees.\n\n## Measured on 2026-09-01\n\n    2.5 MB    one jj workspace checkout   (removed on ExitWorktree)\n     23 MB    .jj, the whole repository's own store\n    1.8 GB    1792 DEAD huggorm store paths\n             21763 dead store paths in all\n       82%    /nix/store, 9.6 GB free of 61 GB\n        27    stale `spike:` heads from earlier sessions\n     none     min-free / max-free in nix.conf\n\nA workspace is 2.5 MB and it is deleted when the session leaves it. A\nspike's three outputs are about 4 MB together:\n\n    3.3 MB   huggorm-bindings      the compiled .so\n    656 KB   huggorm-generated\n    124 KB   bindings-src          the emitted C++\n\nSo a worktree costs almost nothing. What costs is the BUILDS a\nworktree invites: every variation is a new derivation, none of them\nrooted, and 1792 of them are sitting dead right now.\n\nThat is the same disk that filled and produced the SIGBUS inside\nsqlite in nixidae/nixidae#599. 9.6 GB free is roughly two bad afternoons.\n\n## Recommendations, ranked\n\n### 1. Turn on nix's own automatic GC\n\n`min-free` and `max-free` are unset, so nothing reclaims anything\nuntil somebody runs `nix store gc` by hand. With them set the daemon\nfrees space DURING a build instead of the build dying:\n\n    min-free = 5G\n    max-free = 20G\n\nThis is the one that removes the nixidae/nixidae#599 failure class rather\nthan measuring it. It lives in croshome, so it is Carl's.\n\n### 2. Reclaim the 1.8 GB now - DONE, 2026-09-01\n\nCarl said to run it. `nix-collect-garbage -d`:\n\n    22557 store paths deleted, 5.5 GiB freed\n    83% -> 77%, 9.3 GB -> 14 GB free\n    note: hard linking is currently saving 4.3 GiB\n\nMore than the 1.8 GB this task predicted, because `-d` drops old\nprofile generations too and the estimate counted only huggorm's\nshare. It is global - it dropped other projects' build caches - which\nis why it was a decision rather than a chore.\n\n### 3. Iterate on the EMITTED C++, not on the compiled module\n\n    nix build --file . bindings-src        seconds,  124 KB\n    nix build --file . huggorm-bindings    minutes,  3.3 MB\n\nMost emitter changes are checked by DIFFING the emitted C++ against\nthe previous store path. Compile only when the C++ actually changed\nshape. Two findings this session came from that diff alone: the three\n`@reads` accessors emitted byte-identical bodies, and 29 lambdas\ngained a return type.\n\nThe exception is anything nanobind resolves at COMPILE or RUNTIME\nrather than in the text - a missing type_caster emits fine, compiles\nfine, and fails the signature gate (#67).\n\n### 4. Do not optimise the worktrees\n\n2.5 MB, removed on exit. Leaving one open between sessions is\ncheaper than rebuilding the spike inside it.\n\n### 5. `discard_changes: true` is safe, and its name is wrong\n\nIt discards the CHECKOUT. The commits survive as an anonymous head -\nverified: a file written in a workspace was still reachable by\n`jj log -r 'files(...)'` after\n`ExitWorktree(remove, discard_changes=true)`.\n\nThe remove hook takes a snapshot from INSIDE the workspace before\nforgetting it, which is what makes that true. So there is no reason\nto avoid the flag, and no reason to commit defensively before exiting.\n\n### 6. Name every spike commit `spike:`, and sweep them\n\n27 already exist and every one is findable:\n\n    jj log -r 'description(glob:\"spike:*\")'\n\nSweeping them is a rewrite, so it wants Carl's word:\n\n    jj abandon -r 'description(glob:\"spike:*\") ~ ::@'\n\nThey are small - the cost is that after a dozen spikes nothing says\nwhich head was which experiment. The naming convention is what keeps\nthem legible, and it is already in use.\n\nMigrated from `tasks/068-what-a-spike-actually-costs.md`.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5\n"}
{"issue": 606, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPlnEQ", "before": "**MOSTLY DONE.** `Store.build_paths` is bound, so the union finally\nhas the consumer it was built for. Two shapes beside it are not, and\neach needs something this DSL does not have.\n\n## What was missing\n\nnixidae/nixidae#596 and nixidae/nixidae#600 built the whole `DerivedPath` machinery -\na real tagged union on the wire, a declared `Variant(...)`, a\ngenerated caster - and the only thing consuming it was\n`query_missing`, which says what building WOULD do.\n\n`store.py` even named the gap in a comment: query_missing is \"the\nfirst consumer rather than `build_paths`\" because it is read-only and\nruns against a chroot store.\n\n## Done\n\n    def build_paths(self, targets: \"list[DerivedPath]\") -> None:\n        Cxx(\"self.buildPaths(targets);\")\n\nOne line, because the caster made the parameter `std::vector<\nnix::DerivedPath>` already. The class is `blocking=True`, so the GIL\nis released with no marker.\n\nTwo hermetic tests, and neither needs a builder:\n\n- a path the store already holds builds as a NO-OP, twice, and the\n  set of valid paths does not move;\n- a well-formed ABSENT path raises, with libstore's own words -\n  \"no substituter that can build it\". The test asserts the parse\n  succeeded first, so it cannot pass because the wrong line threw.\n\n## Still open\n\n### A build MODE needs a C++ enum on the surface\n\nUpstream takes `BuildMode`: `bmNormal`, `bmRepair`, `bmCheck`. This\nbinds the first and takes no parameter, which is narrower than the\nC++ and never wider (CLAUDE.md goal 1).\n\n`@words` does not fit. It is for a StrEnum whose member IS the string\nlibstore parses - `ContentAddressMethod` and `HashAlgorithm` both are,\nand `pyenum.py` transforms the declaration into that module. `bmRepair`\nis a C++ enumerator with no parser behind it, so a vocabulary would be\ninventing the strings.\n\nSo this wants a marker for a real C++ enum: the enumerators, their\ndeclared names, and how each crosses. `@tagged` already carries an\nenumerator table for a union handle - `(reach, ask, {enumerator:\nname})` - so the shape is not new, but its meaning is different and\nfolding them would make one marker mean two things (the same warning\n`OutputsSpec` gave in nixidae/nixidae#600).\n\n### buildPathsWithResults needs a BuildResult value\n\nThe other half of upstream's pair: a result per target, and no\nexception on failure. That is the shape a caller wants when building\nmany things and reporting on each.\n\nIt needs `KeyedBuildResult` declared as a wire value, which is a\nstruct with a status enum, a message, timings and a map of built\noutputs - so it waits on the enum question above.\n\n### buildPathsWithResults is bigger than it looks\n\n`BuildResult` is itself a SUM TYPE - `std::variant<Success, Failure>`\n- where `Success` carries an enum and a `SingleDrvOutputs` map, and\n`Failure` IS `BuildError`, an exception class with a second enum.\nTwo enums, a map and a variant whose arm is an exception.\n\nSo it is not \"one more value type\". It wants the enum marker above\nplus a decision about how an arm that is an EXCEPTION crosses, which\nis a question this repo has not been asked yet.\n\n### And the enum marker has one ready user, not two\n\n`BuildMode` is the only C++ enum a declaration is ready to name\ntoday; `BuildResult`'s two are behind the paragraph above. CLAUDE.md\nsays one user is a helper and two is a pattern, so building the\nmarker now would be building it for one - and nixidae/nixidae#600 already\nrecorded what that costs, when `OutputsSpec` nearly got folded into\na marker that meant something else.\n\nThe marker waits for its second user.\n\n### evalStore\n\n`buildPaths` takes an optional second store, used for derivations\nonly. Not bound, and not for a mechanical reason: what a Python\ncaller wants there is a design question rather than a parameter to\npass through.\n\nMigrated from `tasks/069-nothing-can-be-built-yet.md`.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5\n"}
{"issue": 606, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWj0qA", "before": "Folded in from nixidae/nixidae#643: the FileIngestionMethod vocabulary leftover (sole checkout match is the comment at huggorm-decl/src/huggorm_decl/decl/words.py:76 \u2014 no vocabulary, no binding user). Same shape as the build MODE enum gap: an unbound C++-enum vocabulary awaiting its user."}
{"issue": 613, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPmXHg", "before": "**OPEN.** `@property` in a declaration is refused. This says what\nhonouring it would cost, so the refusal can be lifted on purpose\nrather than by whoever needs it first.\n\n## What the word means\n\n`Method.prop` is set by Python's own `@property`, and it says an\naccessor is an ATTRIBUTE rather than a call. The DSL has carried the\nword since the reader was written, with this reason:\n\n> which one an accessor is was never a property of its class -\n> nanopynix presents `StorePath.to_string()` as a method and\n> `ValidPathInfo.path` as an attribute, and both are values.\n\nThat is still a fair reading of the two shapes. `info.nar_size` is a\nfact the object holds; `hash.to_string()` is work it does.\n\n## What refuses it, and why\n\n`nbemit` refuses a bound class whose accessor sets `prop`\n(#75). Not because the binding cannot do it - `def_prop_ro`\nis one line - but because three other outputs read an accessor as a\nCALL and would then disagree with it:\n\n1. **`_identity_semantics`** writes `h.attr(\"nar_size\")()` into\n   `__repr__`, `__hash__` and `_parts`. One call per part, and a\n   property makes every one of them call a string.\n2. **`pyi.py`** writes `def nar_size(self) -> int` in the stub. A\n   caller's typechecker would promise a method over an attribute.\n3. **`wire.py`** and the generated async and RPC wrappers read a\n   part the way `_parts` sends it.\n\nSo four emitters, one fact. That is exactly the shape goal 3 exists\nfor, and it is why the fix is to teach them together or not at all.\n\n## What it would take\n\n- `nbemit`: `def_prop_ro` with `_returns` for the type, and\n  `_identity_semantics` reading `h.attr(name)` without the `()`.\n- `pyi.py`: `@property` on the emitted stub method.\n- The wire: one predicate - \"is this part a call?\" - read from the\n  same `Method.prop`, used everywhere a part is fetched.\n- A gate: a produced value with one attribute part and one method\n  part, round-tripped. Both shapes in one class, or the predicate is\n  never exercised against its opposite.\n\nThe predicate is the whole design. A boolean asked in four places\nbeats four places each deciding, and it is what makes the answer one\nfact rather than four.\n\n## What is NOT a reason to do it\n\nPrettiness. `info.nar_size` reads better than `info.nar_size()` and\nthat is worth something, but not four emitters and a wire predicate\non its own. Do this when a declaration NEEDS an attribute - an\nupstream shape a caller would misread as work, or a compatibility\nsurface that was one - and not before.\n\n## What must not be forgotten\n\n`@property` under another decorator kills the import:\n\n    AttributeError: 'property' object has no attribute '_instant'\n\n`@instant`, `@local`, `@reads` and the rest set an attribute on what\nthey are handed, and a `property` object takes none.\n\nThat used to be SILENT: `load` swallowed it, so the file read\ntree-only and so did every file importing from it. nixidae/nixidae#619 closed\nthat half - the import error now reaches a reader with Python's own\nreason attached, so a declaration written this way fails and says\nwhich line to fix.\n\nThe question it leaves is answered, 2026-09-06. **A declaration\nwrites `@property` OUTERMOST**, and the refusal says so now.\n\n## One more thing the reader fix changed\n\n`_live` now looks through `staticmethod` and `classmethod` too, for\nthe same reason it looks through `property`: none of the three carries\n`__code__`. Before that they named no live line and were dropped\nwhole, in silence.\n\nBoth are refused in `_method` now (#82), and the measurement\nis why: with the refusal removed, `@staticmethod def of(text: Str)`\nread as `of()` with no parameters at all, because `_method` reads a\nbound method by skipping the first one. Teaching them is the same\nfour-emitter problem as `@property`, and belongs to this task if\nanybody ever wants one.\n\nOpened 2026-09-02, while closing nixidae/nixidae#612.\n\n## 2026-09-03: two of the four are already right\n\nStill **OPEN**, and its own rule still applies: nothing needs an\nattribute yet. What changed is the estimate. The section above says\nfour emitters and a wire predicate, and two of those five were\nmeasured and found to need nothing.\n\n**`pyi.py` already emits `@property`.** It does not print a stub, it\nmoves the declaration's own tree - and its decorator filter keeps\n`d.id in BUILTIN_DECORATORS`, which is where `property` lives. So the\nstub agrees with the declaration today, with no edit. The claim above\nwas written from the emitter it replaced.\n\nProven by READING, not by a property reaching it. `nbemit` refuses\nthe class first, so no `@property` accessor has ever reached\n`pyi.py` - which is the dead-path shape nixidae/nixidae#612 documents, and\nthe reason to say how this was checked rather than that it was.\n`wire.py` is the other way round: its route was traced through code\nthat runs on every wire value.\n\n**`wire.py` needs no predicate.** It reads a wire value's parts\nthrough `_parts()` and nothing else - `value_to_msg` calls\n`self._sync(obj)._parts()`, and the one route that reads a part BY\nNAME is `error_to_msg`, for an exception, which is Python's own object\nand has no `_parts`. `_parts` is a METHOD either way. Only the C++\nthat builds its body changes.\n\nSo the real list is three, and one of them is new:\n\n- `nbemit._method`, which binds `def` and would bind `def_prop_ro`;\n- `nbemit.wire_fields`, whose third element is `h.attr(name)()` - one\n  expression, read by `_parts`, `__repr__`, `__hash__` and\n  `_round_trip`. That is the predicate, and it is ONE place, not\n  four. `__eq__` needs nothing: it compares `_parts()` against\n  `_parts()`, so it reads the helper rather than the fields. The one\n  site OUTSIDE `wire_fields` is `@shown`, whose repr writes\n  `h.attr(shown)()` for a class with no fields at all;\n- `manifest._method`, which carries no `prop` key at all, so nothing\n  downstream of the manifest can know. `pygen`'s wrappers read it\n  from there.\n\nThe last one is the open design question, and it is not prettiness.\nAn async wrapper's method is `async def name(self)`, and a property\ncannot be awaited. A value class does not get one - `PathInfo` is\n`threading=\"pool\"` and `blocking=False`, so `wrapped` is False and\nthere is no async form to disagree with - but a wrapped class would\nhave to refuse `@property` or answer a coroutine from an attribute.\nWhichever it is has to be a refusal, not a comment.\n\n## `__call__` is NOT this task\n\nIt was named here as \"076's family of gap\" by nixidae/nixidae#571, and\ncarried forward as \"one DSL change buys both\". Measurement refuted\nthat, and nixidae/nixidae#625 is where it went.\n\n`@property` is attribute-versus-call: the name is kept and the\nemitters read it the wrong way. `__call__` was never KEPT - the\nclass-body loop dropped every `__`-prefixed name, in silence. One is\na reading, the other is an absence, and nothing about `__call__`\nneeds `@property`.\n\n## The decorator order is answered, 2026-09-06\n\nStill **OPEN**, and its own rule still holds: nothing needs an\nattribute, so `@property` is still refused by the emitter. What is\nclosed is the sub-question this file said it had to answer - whether\nthe markers look through a descriptor, or a declaration writes\n`@property` outermost.\n\n**Outermost.** Measured both ways rather than reasoned about:\n\n    @instant                  AttributeError, at import\n    @property                 'property' object has no attribute\n    def base16(self): ...     '_instant'\n\n    @property                 reads. prop=True instant=True\n    @instant\n    def base16(self): ...\n\nThe reader does not care, and that is why the answer is free. `_apply`\nruns the file's decorators against a THROWAWAY and skips the builtin\nones, so `@instant` reaches the probe from either position; and `prop`\nis read from the TREE, not from the live object. Only Python's own\nexecution cares, and only about which object gets the attribute.\n\n### Said in the refusal, which is what the file asked for\n\n`_descriptor_hint` in `read.py`. The import failure carried Python's\nown message - accurate, and not actionable: it names the descriptor\nand the attribute and stops, so a reader had to work out on their own\nthat two lines can simply be swapped.\n\n    hash.py: the declaration does not import, so nothing says which\n    definitions exist. AttributeError: 'property' object has no\n    attribute '_instant' and no __dict__ for setting new attributes\n    Write @property OUTERMOST, above @instant. A marker sets\n    `_instant` on what it is handed, and a property object takes no\n    attribute - so the marker has to reach the function underneath\n    it. That fixes the IMPORT. An emitter still refuses a @property\n    accessor - see nixidae/nixidae#613 - so declare it as a plain method until\n    that changes.\n\nThe MARKER is derived, not listed. Every marker in `declare.py`\nwrites `_<name>` onto what it is handed, so the shape is the fact and\na list would go stale on the next marker.\n\nThe DESCRIPTOR is named, and only one of them is - which the first\nversion of this got wrong. See below.\n\n**Honest about what the swap buys**, and that half was added on\npurpose. The order fixes the IMPORT and nothing else - the emitter\nstill refuses `@property`, and `_method` still refuses\n`@staticmethod` and `@classmethod` (#82). A hint that stopped\nat the order would send a reader to a second refusal with no warning\nthat one was coming, which is worse than the raw `AttributeError` it\nreplaces.\n\n### One gate, three assertions, three perturbations\n\n`test_a_marker_over_a_descriptor_says_which_order_to_write`.\n\n1. the wrong order gets the hint, naming the marker it read and\n   where the path ends;\n2. **the advice is TRUE** - the file written the way the refusal says\n   reads, with `prop` AND `instant` both kept. This is the assertion\n   that makes the message worth having rather than plausible;\n3. the CONTROL - an import failure with no descriptor in it gets no\n   hint.\n\nEach fails on its own perturbation, and the three are different:\n\n    no hint at all              1, on \"@property OUTERMOST\"\n    hint on every failure       3, the control\n    `_apply` stops skipping     2, \"the marker was not lost\"\n    the builtin decorators\n\nThe third is worth naming. With `_apply` applying `property` to its\nown probe, `@property` outermost puts the marker UNDER a property\nobject, `getattr` answers False, and `instant` is lost in silence -\nthis repo's named failure mode, in the arm the refusal now sends\nreaders to. The skip is what prevents it, and nothing held that until\nthis gate.\n\nBoth perturbations 1 and 3 print a message beginning \"errs.py: the\ndeclaration does not import\", so the two failures LOOK the same in a\ntruncated report. Checked rather than assumed: the full traceback\nnames a different assert line for each. A gate whose perturbations\nare indistinguishable in the output is one nobody can tell apart\nlater.\n\n### `property` is the only descriptor here, and the first draft said three\n\nThe hint matched `'(property|staticmethod|classmethod)' object has no\nattribute` and branched on which. Two of those three arms could never\nrun. Measured on 3.14.7:\n\n    property       REFUSES - no __dict__ for setting new attributes\n    staticmethod   accepts an attribute\n    classmethod    accepts an attribute\n\nSo `@instant` over a `@staticmethod` IMPORTS. The declaration then\nreaches `_method`'s own refusal, which already names the real problem\n- a static method read as one that takes self loses its first\nparameter (#82). The hint was never going to be the thing a\nreader saw.\n\nDead text claiming a coverage it did not have, which is nixidae/nixidae#612's\nshape, in a file written to close a nixidae/nixidae#612 descendant. Found by\nprobing the arm rather than by reading it, after review asked what\ndrove it.\n\n**NO GATE DRIVES THE TRIM.** Putting the two arms back fails nothing:\n\n    407 passed, 11 deselected\n\n...and it cannot, because an unreachable alternation changes no\nbehaviour. That is the same position nixidae/nixidae#630 recorded for\n`tp_clear`, and the same answer: keep the correction, and say that\nnothing tests it rather than leave it looking covered.\n\nWhat IS gated is the measurement behind it. The fourth assertion in\nthe gate asserts a marker over a `@staticmethod` reaches\n`_method`'s refusal and NOT \"does not import\" - so the day a Python\nrelease makes `staticmethod` refuse an attribute, that assertion\nfails and this section is what a reader finds. It guards a language\nbehaviour rather than this repo's code, which is worth knowing about\nit.\n\n### One thing found in passing, fixed in its own commit\n\n`DeclarationError.__init__` does `where += f\":{line}\"`, and `where` is\nwhatever went onto `_READING`. That stack is annotated `list[str]`\nand nothing enforced it, so a `pathlib.Path` gave\n`TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +=: 'PosixPath' and\n'str'` - raised INSIDE the refusal, losing the message it was about\nto give.\n\nFixed at the one WRITE to the stack rather than at every read of it,\nwhich took removing two copies of that write first: `read` and\n`resolved` each hand-rolled the same append/try/finally/pop that\n`reading` already is. See the commit for the rest.\n\nMigrated from `tasks/076-an-accessor-that-is-an-attribute.md`.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5\n"}
{"issue": 628, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPn7Dg", "before": "**OPEN.** Carl:\n\n> The emitter should indeed be able to emit includes, I'm surprised\n> there's so much C++. Generally Python is very expressive with\n> decorators and allowing \"anything anywhere\" (if statements in\n> classes, bare module statements etc....) so much stuff should be\n> expressable in our \"Python DSL\" to emit C++\n\nThis is the audit that question deserves: every construct in the\nheaders, its size, and whether a declaration could say it instead.\n\nThe answer is about a third, not most - and the reason for the\nsurprise is partly a number nobody is printing.\n\n## The number IS printed, and this file first said it was not\n\n**Corrected.** The first version of this section claimed\n`CLAUDE.md:95` was stale - that nothing counts the directory. It is\naccurate. `generate.census_cpp` counts it on every build:\n\n    front door -> .../__init__.py: 34 name(s)\n    hand-written C++ in cpp/: 593 lines in 5 file(s)\n      errors.hpp: 35 lines, claimed by no module\n      libstore.hpp: 12 lines, claimed by no module\n      logging.hpp: 208 lines, claimed by no module\n\nTwo greps missed it and neither was enough: `nix run --file . check`\ndoes not carry the bindings-src build's stdout, and searching for\n\"line count\" and \"wc -l\" does not match `_code_lines`. The right\ncheck was `nix log` on the derivation, and it took three minutes.\n\nThat is the THIRD claim of this shape in one session - `<stdexcept>`\nand `errors.hpp`'s three includes were the first two - and it is the\nworst of them, because it was committed and it accused the project's\nown instructions of being wrong.\n\nThe number it reports is 593, which is CODE lines. So the figure\nCarl is reacting to is already the honest one, and the \"the comment\nratio inflates it\" answer this file first gave is also wrong.\n\n`census_cpp` also names the ORPHANS - a file no module claims, so no\ndeclaration is emitted beside it. Three of the five are orphans, and\nthat is the more useful half of the report: `logging.hpp` alone is\n208 lines that nothing derives.\n\n## What is actually there\n\n593 code lines, in 1545 total.\n\n    errors.hpp      125 total    35 code\n    eval.hpp        767 total   280 code\n    gc.hpp          165 total    58 code\n    libstore.hpp     48 total    12 code\n    logging.hpp     440 total   208 code\n\n## Derivable: about 200 lines\n\nEach of these is SHAPE - a pattern the declaration already carries\nenough information to generate.\n\n    17  the `Reach` dance          three private members, and the\n                                   [temp.spec]/6 boilerplate around\n                                   each is identical. One decorator -\n                                   `@private_member(\"fileEvalCache\")`\n                                   - could write all of it.\n    62  `LogTap`'s five overrides  nixidae/nixidae#621. Each is a virtual\n                                   signature plus a record literal\n                                   mapping parameter to field.\n    41  the four subscribe/        one shape four times: swap a slot,\n        unsubscribe functions      close what was there, answer the\n                                   new one.\n    16  `LogField`, `LogRecord`    plain structs whose every field\n                                   the declaration already names with\n                                   `@reads`.\n    16  `thread_queue`,            a typed slot, three times.\n        `process_sink`,\n        `process_queue`\n    15  the three flag/counter     \"a thread_local bool\" and \"a\n        accessors                  process-wide atomic\" are shapes.\n     8  `init_libstore`            \"call this once, ever\" is a\n                                   decorator, not a function.\n     8  `Evaluator::wrap` and      two-line constructors.\n        `wrap_builder`\n     7  `make_core`                a shared_ptr with a deleter.\n     6  `install_log_tap`          one policy statement.\n\n## Not derivable: about 300 lines\n\nEach of these is an ALGORITHM or a lifetime rule, and writing a\ndeclaration form for one of them would be inventing a language to\nexpress one program.\n\n    106  `Bridge`                  root lifetime, staged lists and\n                                   attribute sets, materialise,\n                                   symbol interning. This is the\n                                   binding's core, not a mapping.\n     52  `LogQueue`                a bounded queue whose drop policy\n                                   depends on the ACTION - never a\n                                   start or a stop - under a mutex.\n     45  `register_primop`         a C++ callback that reacquires the\n                                   GIL and re-enters Python.\n     31  the GC thread dance       two flags, a thread-exit\n                                   destructor, and a measurement\n                                   (1124 ms to 0.17 ms) as its\n                                   justification.\n     22  `EvalCore`                member ORDER is the fact. The\n                                   order could be declared; the four\n                                   objects and their references could\n                                   not.\n     22  `as_error`, `raise_as`    builds a Python object from a C++\n                                   exception, with a fallback for\n                                   when that itself throws.\n     15  `forget_file`             two caches, collect-then-erase\n                                   because a visitor holds a lock.\n      8  `cached_files`            a visitor over a concurrent map.\n      6  `gc_collect`              \"twice\" is the whole content.\n\n## The pattern behind the question, which is the real finding\n\nCarl's point stands separately from the count. Every time the DSL\nmeets something Python can express, it REFUSES rather than learns:\n\n    @property        refused        nixidae/nixidae#613\n    @staticmethod    refused        nixidae/nixidae#619, 076\n    @classmethod     refused        nixidae/nixidae#619, 076\n    a dunder         refused        nixidae/nixidae#625 - and SILENTLY until\n                                    this week\n    async def        refused        nixidae/nixidae#625\n    a virtual        cannot be said nixidae/nixidae#621\n\nSix, and five of them are open. That is not six unrelated gaps; it is\none habit. The DSL reads a declaration with `ast.parse` AND an\nimport, so it already sees decorators, class bodies, `if` branches\nand bare statements - Carl is right that the raw material is there.\nWhat it does with anything unfamiliar is decline.\n\nRefusing was the right first move each time: a silent skip is worse,\nand nixidae/nixidae#625 found one that had been silent since the reader was\nwritten. But six refusals is the point at which the answer stops\nbeing \"refuse the next one too\".\n\n## The order to take them in\n\n1. **Includes**, which Carl agreed to and nixidae/nixidae#627 has two\n   instances of. The emitter knows every error class an emitted file\n   catches and every type a `Cxx` body spells.\n2. **`@private_member`**, the cheapest real one: 17 lines, three\n   users already, and the pattern is pure boilerplate around a name.\n3. **#84's virtuals**, which is the biggest single win at 62\n   lines and also the hardest - it needs a way to say that this\n   parameter of the virtual becomes that field of the record.\n4. The rest, as anything needs them.\n\nThe baseline needs no work: `census_cpp` already reports it, and\n`logging.hpp` being the largest orphan at 208 lines is the report\npointing straight at the biggest target.\n\nNothing here says the ~300 should shrink. A helper that is an\nalgorithm is what goal 2 explicitly ALLOWS, and pretending otherwise\nwould trade a readable 50-line queue for a declaration form nobody\nelse uses.\n\nOpened 2026-09-04, from Carl's question.\n\n## `@private_member` is later, and the reason is a dependency\n\nIt looked like the cheapest item, and it is not, because of who uses\nthe thing it would generate.\n\nEvery consumer of the `Reach` dance is a HAND-WRITTEN helper in\n`eval.hpp`:\n\n    cached_files          (state.*get(FileEvalCache{}))->cvisit_all\n    forget_file           both caches\n    register_primop       (state.*get(AddPrimOp{}))(std::move(op))\n\nGenerated code never touches it. So an emitted `Reach` block has to\nbe visible to `eval.hpp`, and `huggorm-decl` does not depend on\n`huggorm-gen` - `packages/huggorm-bindings/default.nix` names all\nthree, and the arrow runs bindings -> {gen, decl, dsl} and no other\nway.\n\nTwo ways out, and both are bigger than the 17 lines they save:\n\n1. **`eval.hpp` includes a generated header.** It compiles, because\n   nothing outside the bindings build ever compiles `eval.hpp`. But a\n   helper is meant to be readable and buildable on its own, and this\n   makes one that is not - it would stop compiling standalone, which\n   is a worse property than the boilerplate it removes.\n2. **The consumers move into the emitted file**, as `Cxx` bodies on\n   the declaration. Then everything is on the generated side and the\n   header loses ~40 lines rather than 17. Defensible, and it is a\n   real change to three helpers rather than a decorator.\n\nSo this one waits on (2) being worth doing for its own sake. Carl\nnamed `@private_member` as an EXAMPLE of the appetite - \"things like\n`@private_member` and other C++ annotations\" - rather than as the\ntask, and the appetite is better spent where the emitter already owns\nthe output.\n\n## Which makes includes the real first step\n\nnixidae/nixidae#627 has two measured instances and Carl agreed to it by name.\nIt also has no dependency problem at all: the emitter already writes\nthe include block of every emitted `.cpp`, so this is a fact it\ncomputes instead of one a header carries on the emitted file's\nbehalf.\n\n**DONE, both halves, 2026-09-05.** `BODY_HEADERS` derives the\nstandard headers a `Cxx` body spells, and `decl/errors.py` says\n`header = \"nix/...\"` beside each `cxx` so the emitter writes the\ntranslator's includes too. nixidae/nixidae#627 holds the detail, including\nthat neither perturbation broke the BUILD - nix's own headers reach\n`<stdexcept>` along some chain - so the gate is a text gate on what\nthe emitter derived.\n\nSo item 1 of the order below is finished. Item 2 is blocked, item 3\nis nixidae/nixidae#621, and nixidae/nixidae#621 got harder rather than easier - see\nits own 2026-09-06 section.\n\n## The dependency is not `@private_member`'s alone. 2026-09-05\n\n`@private_member` waits on who CONSUMES what an emitter would write.\nThat test was applied to one item and it should have been applied to\nthe list, because `logging.hpp` is the largest orphan and most of its\nderivable lines fail the same test.\n\nMeasured, by grepping every consumer outside the header:\n\n    thread_queue          logging.hpp only\n    process_sink          logging.hpp only\n    process_queue         logging.hpp only\n    huggorm::LogRecord    eval.py, as a `@binding` target\n    huggorm::LogField     eval.py, as a `@binding` target\n    huggorm::subscribe_logs      eval.py, inside a `Cxx` body\n    huggorm::unsubscribe_logs    eval.py, inside a `Cxx` body\n\nSo the block splits in two, and the earlier list did not:\n\n- **Blocked, for `@private_member`'s exact reason: 32 lines.** The\n  three slots and the two structs. Every consumer is `LogTap`, which\n  is hand-written and in the same header. A `@binding` naming\n  `huggorm::LogRecord` is not a consumer of a DEFINITION - it is a\n  type the emitter binds - so generating the struct would still leave\n  `LogTap` needing it from the generated side.\n- **Not blocked: 41 lines.** The four subscribe/unsubscribe\n  functions. Their only callers are `Cxx` bodies in `eval.py`, which\n  are already on the generated side.\n\n**Which changes what nixidae/nixidae#621 is.** It was listed as \"the biggest\nsingle win at 62 lines and also the hardest\". It is also the UNLOCK:\ngenerate `LogTap` and the 32 blocked lines stop having a hand-written\nconsumer. 62 plus 32 is most of what `logging.hpp` holds that is not\n`LogQueue`'s algorithm.\n\nThe 41 unblocked lines are a separate question and this file does not\nanswer it. Moving a helper into a `Cxx` body is a RELOCATION, not a\nderivation, and the census counts `cpp/` - so doing it for the number\nwould be spending a budget `CLAUDE.md` says is not one. It needs an\nargument of its own.\n\n**And nixidae/nixidae#626's request id adds to this file**, which is worth\nsaying in the same place: about ten lines on the largest orphan, on\nthe block nixidae/nixidae#621 would delete. Small, and pointed at the target.\n\n## Remeasured 2026-09-06, after nixidae/nixidae#626, `095` and `096`\n\nThe audit above is from 2026-09-04 and its numbers have moved a lot.\n`logging.hpp` was the largest orphan at 208 code lines. It is 356\nnow, and `cpp/` as a whole went from 593 to **793**.\n\n    errors.hpp      110 total    32 code   (was 35)\n    eval.hpp        936 total   335 code   (was 280)\n    gc.hpp          165 total    58 code   (was 58)\n    libstore.hpp     48 total    12 code   (was 12)\n    logging.hpp     978 total   356 code   (was 208)\n                              -----\n                                793        (was 593)\n\n`logging.hpp` is now 45% of every hand-written line in `cpp/`, and\nthat is the number Carl's question was about. It went UP.\n\n### Where the 356 are\n\nPer top-level definition, counted the way `census_cpp` counts:\n\n     75  LogTap                    the five overrides. nixidae/nixidae#621.\n     48  LogQueue                  the bounded queue's drop policy\n     44  VerbosityDemand           NEW\n     34  ThreadLevel               NEW as a class; was ~4\n     18  subscribe_process_logs\n     13  route\n     13  unsubscribe_process_logs\n     11  set_process_demand        NEW\n     10  subscribe_logs\n      8  unsubscribe_logs\n      8  effective_verbosity\n      6  process_queue\n      6  LogField\n     11  LogRecord\n     30  six typed slots           thread_queue, process_sink,\n                                   verbosity_demand,\n                                   default_verbosity, thread_level,\n                                   thread_request - 5 lines each\n      4  install_log_tap\n\n### The growth is mostly NOT derivable, and that is the finding\n\nOf the 148 new lines, about 100 fall on the not-derivable side of\nthis file's own test:\n\n    49  VerbosityDemand + its accessor   a counted registry with a\n                                         floor and a reconcile step.\n                                         An ALGORITHM.\n    30  ThreadLevel becoming a class     a lifetime rule: a\n                                         destructor that gives a\n                                         demand back, and a DELETED\n                                         copy assignment that exists\n                                         because the aggregate form\n                                         was wrong (#96).\n    11  set_process_demand               the same pairing for the\n                                         process, under a mutex,\n                                         because the atomic form\n                                         raced.\n     4  route                            grew with the request id.\n\nSo `logging.hpp` did not grow because the codegen fell behind. It\ngrew because nixidae/nixidae#626, `095` and `096` were concurrency and\nlifetime work, which goal 2 explicitly allows a helper to be. A\ndeclaration form for \"count holders per level and reconcile a global\"\nwould be inventing a language to express one program.\n\nThe rest, about 48 lines, is derivable and joins the list above.\n\n### Two of the earlier figures change\n\n**#84 is worth more.** `LogTap` was 62 lines and is 75. With\nthe 32 lines it unblocks - the slots and the two structs, whose only\nconsumer is `LogTap` itself - it is now over 100.\n\n**\"A typed slot, three times\" is six times.** `verbosity_demand`,\n`default_verbosity` and `thread_request` joined `thread_queue`,\n`process_sink` and `thread_level`. Six identical five-line accessors,\n30 lines, and the shape has not changed at all: a function-local\nstatic of some type, returned by reference. Two of the six leak\ndeliberately and say why; the decorator would have to carry that.\n\nThat is now the second-cheapest derivable item after includes, and\nunlike `@private_member` it has NO dependency problem: every consumer\nis in `logging.hpp`, which is also where the definitions are. The\nemitter would have to write into that file, which is the same\nblocker - so it waits on nixidae/nixidae#621 too.\n\n### What this says about the habit\n\nNothing new, and that is worth recording. nixidae/nixidae#626 through `096`\nadded no refusal and no new marker, so the list of six declines is\nstill six. The DSL was not asked anything it could not answer,\nbecause the work never reached it.\n\nMigrated from `tasks/091-how-much-of-the-cpp-could-the-dsl-say.md`.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5\n"}
{"issue": 647, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPp3nQ", "before": "A guest sees the host's whole `/nix/store` today, over hostfs or virtiofs, with a tmpfs overlay for writes. composefs could present exactly the test's closure instead: one EROFS image built from the closure, over the shared host store as a data-only lower layer (`lowerdir=<erofs>::<host store>`).\n\n**What it would buy**\n- Hermetic guests: a test sees only the paths it declared, as a real NixOS does, and cannot pass by using one it did not.\n- Metadata from the image: `stat` and `readdir` are local to the guest, and only file contents cross hostfs or virtiofs.\n- The Nix database in the image, so `nixDatabase` registration could go.\n\n**What it needs**\n- An image derivation per test. nixkube builds one per closure (`nix/composefs.nix`); a system closure of 495 paths gave a 7.4 MiB image in 0.19 s of mkcomposefs.\n- EROFS before /nix mounts: the UML kernel builds it since d242afce; a QEMU guest needs it in the initrd; the container backend needs it in the host kernel.\n- Measure first: boot time and a metadata-heavy test, against today's layout.\n\nnixkube measured two traps (nixkube#68): a file-backed EROFS mount refuses an image on overlayfs, and mount.composefs then fails with EBUSY through its loop device, where the same mounts made by hand succeed.\n\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 658, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPq_Xw", "before": "Goal: `pip install` Nix bindings on RHEL 8+, SLE 15 SP3+ and Ubuntu 20.04+, for SUSE Manager and Ansible.\n\n**Route (Carl, 2026-10-01): build in PyPA's manylinux_2_28 image, not the old nanopynix lane.** The old lane (nanopynix#111, deleted in nanopynix `qxwrtwzt`) built with nixpkgs' glibc 2.42 and rewrote symbol versions down to 2.34. This build compiles against glibc 2.28 itself. The image runs inside the Nix sandbox under bwrap, and nixpkgs pins every source. `nix build --file . manylinux.check` is the gate.\n\nMeasured on the first build:\n- 16.5 MiB wheel, 51 MiB unpacked, 27 bundled libraries; Nix's libraries need at most GLIBC_2.28 and GLIBCXX_3.4.22.\n- The hermetic suite against the wheel, in the image: 644 passed, 1 skipped, 11 deselected. The nixpkgs gate gives the same counts.\n- Deviations from nixpkgs' Nix: `-ldl` on the link (Nix's meson never names libdl, which glibc keeps separate before 2.34), and no seccomp, libcpuid, lowdown or S3 AWS auth.\n\nLeft:\n- cp314 only, because the generator needs 3.14. SLE 15 and RHEL 8 ship 3.11.\n- x86_64 only.\n- The CA bundle on SUSE and RHEL paths: no HTTPS fetch is tested yet.\n- S3 auth (the AWS CRT), CI, and the PyPI upload.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 659, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWqspg", "before": "Step 4 is done: huggorm ef9a7e4, umbrella d86b6a7 (source) and 9fc6c8d (lock).\n\n- `byNixpkgs.release` is default.nix on `nixpkgs-release` (nixos-26.05, 7c8764b7). It passes `python3 = python314`, because 26.05's default python3 is 3.13 and the generator needs 3.14.\n- 26.05 holds Nix master at 2.35pre, so a pin has no `git` lane. Each `versions.nix` entry now states its minor, and a lane refuses a component scope of another minor.\n- `every-pin` (in `check`) builds the pin's emitted C++ and generator suite with no compile. 2.34.8 and 2.35.2 emit the same C++ as on unstable, and both suites pass. No pin lane has been compiled yet. A compile rebuilds patched Nix twice, so that waits for an ask.\n- \"Test `NIX_PATH` follows `pkgs`\" has no consumer here. No huggorm test resolves `<nixpkgs>`. The clause was for nanopynix's suite.\n\nStill open: step 5 (wheels for 2.35, with git blocked by nixidae/nixidae#660), and CI (#132).\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5\n"}
{"issue": 659, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWqthA", "before": "Step 5, for 2.34 and 2.35: done, at huggorm 77c72f9.\n\nBoth lanes build wheels for cp311 to cp314, smoke passes on each, and the cp314 wheel suite passes 912, the same as the lanes. Four defects were in the way. Each fix has its own commit:\n- 2bdf5b2: `Py_IsFinalizing` is public only from 3.13, so cp311 and cp312 did not compile. Before 3.13 the same function is `_Py_IsFinalizing`.\n- 7c18294: a wheel check ran in a copy of the package source. It held the gitignored `__init__.py` that `check` writes, so the smoke server and client were two builds, and the server refused the client.\n- e25bb5c: `test_contracts` read the source tree beside `tests/`.\n- 77c72f9: the wheel suite had no plugin and no daemon Nix. A plugin now builds inside the image, and patchelf points it at the wheel's renamed Nix libraries. Without that rename it fails to load (the control).\n\nNot run: the openSUSE test. The git lane stays blocked by nixidae/nixidae#660.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5\n"}
{"issue": 659, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWqt-Q", "before": "The openSUSE test passes on the 2.34 wheelhouse: Leap 16.0's Python 3.13 runs sync, async and rpc. It needed vivarium 6b7da95: SUSE stopped serving the pinned cloud image (Build18.71 answers 404), and the pin is now Build18.75. That pin will break again when SUSE rotates builds.\n\nStep 5 is done for 2.34 and 2.35. The git lane waits on nixidae/nixidae#660.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5"}
{"issue": 660, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPrL6Q", "before": "Nix master uses `#embed` with no fallback, in `local-store.cc`, `eval.cc`, `flake.cc`, `develop.cc` and `main.cc`. GCC supports `#embed` from version 15. Nix 2.36 will contain the same code.\n\nPyPA's images ship gcc-toolset-14:\n- manylinux_2_28: AlmaLinux 8.\n- manylinux_2_34: built 2026-10-02.\n\nQuay has no x86_64 `manylinux_2_39` or `manylinux_2_40`.\n\nDecision (2026-10-02): the git lane has no `manylinux` attribute. The 2.34 and 2.35 lanes ship wheels. The alternatives were refused: patching out `#embed` against a moving source, and building our own GCC 15 in the image.\n\n**This blocks the stable lane when nixpkgs moves to 2.36.** Close this issue when a PyPA image ships GCC 15, or when that move forces a decision. Details are in nixidae/nixidae#659.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 664, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPrnOQ", "before": "## What happens\n\nA build whose log carries non-UTF-8 bytes (a gzip magic, `1f 8b 08`,\nof the kind nixpkgs emits while gzipping man pages) crashes the log\npump instead of flowing through:\n\n```\nUnicodeDecodeError: 'utf-8' codec can't decode byte 0x8b in position 1:\ninvalid start byte\n```\n\nat `nanopynix/_core/_logs.py:53`, in `_field_values`:\n\n```python\nreturn [field.integer() if field.is_int() else field.text() for field in record.fields()]\n```\n\n`field.text()` decodes strict UTF-8. The crash surfaces out of\n`build_paths_with_results`, so the whole build fails.\n\n## Ground truth\n\nNix treats log text as bytes at every hop and never decodes it: the\nwire `string` is a length-prefixed byte run\n(`worker-protocol-connection.cc:53`), the builder's output splits on\n`\\n` alone (`build-log.cc:39`), and the client prints what it got\n(`terminal.cc:39` walks invalid UTF-8 one byte at a time). pynixd just\nfixed the same bug class on its side (Lillecarl/pynixd#62) by decoding\nthese fields losslessly.\n\n## Reproducer\n\npynixd's differential corpus gained a `binary-log` case for this: a\nbare `/bin/sh` derivation that prints `\\037\\213\\010not-utf8` to stderr.\nBoth differential build tests fail on it in the Nix arm, while the\npynixd arm succeeds. The pynixd case is marked to expect this issue\nuntil the oracle answers bytes as bytes."}
{"issue": 702, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPvdqQ", "before": "Measured 2026-10-06 against GKE Autopilot (europe-north1, REGULAR channel), deploying the stock manifests.\n\nGKE Warden denies both node workloads TERMINAL at admission:\n\n- DaemonSet/nix-node: containers nix-node and appstarter-init are privileged (not allowed), plus every hostPath in write mode (csi-socket, kubelet, registration, host-root, nix-root, nri-socket, nix-store, nix-root in appstarter-init).\n- StatefulSet/pynixd: appstarter-init is privileged.\n\nSo on Autopilot there is no NRI (no node or kubelet access at all), no CSI node plugin, and not even the cache as-shipped. What applies cleanly: RBAC, the CSIDriver registration, plain workloads.\n\nThe constraint behind all of it: Autopilot allows only configMap, csi, downwardAPI, emptyDir, gcePersistentDisk, nfs, persistentVolumeClaim, projected, secret volumes, no privileged, no write hostPath. That rules out every self-installed CSI node plugin, leaving only Google's managed drivers plus plain nfs.\n\nPossible direction, no design yet: serve closures over NFS. nfs is an allowed volume type needing no privileges and no host access. A Filestore instance (or any NFS server) holding a published closure set, mounted read-only into pods, would give Autopilot clusters a nix store with zero node access. Related: nixidae/nixidae#528 (mergerfs) explores alternative serving mechanisms from the other side."}
{"issue": 704, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPvprQ", "before": "Only the 2.34 lane has a gate. 14 of the 15 lanes are never built by `check`, `test` or the `huggorm` build.\n\nEvidence (verified):\n- `default.nix:283` takes every top-level attribute from `lanes.nix_2_34`. `check` builds only the 2.34 clang variant and the 2.34 tcp-store check.\n- At `4f53ac4`, `lanes.nix_2_35.bindings-src` fails in 3.7 s with the nixidae/nixidae#698 error. `lanes.nix_2_34.bindings-src` passes. Nothing ran the 2.35 one, so nixidae/nixidae#698 sat on main.\n- nanopynix CI builds 2.35 and git, but only the huggorm the umbrella lock pins, so it sees a break after landing.\n\nChange:\n- Add an aggregate over `builtins.attrNames versions`: `bindings-src` and `huggorm-generated` for each lane. About 4 s per lane, no compile. Put it in `check`.\n- Move `test_declarations.py` (codegen tests, no compiled code needed) into a per-lane check that runs with that lane's `HUGGORM_NIX_VERSION`.\n- Compile the 2.35 and git bindings on a schedule.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5\n"}
{"issue": 704, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWwwFg", "before": "Done in 245738c30419: `every-nix-src` emits `bindings-src` for every version in `versions.nix`, and `check` depends on it. Negative control: with the nixidae/nixidae#698 fix reverted it fails on the UnkeyedRealisation refusal.\n\nStill open: the per-lane run of the codegen tests, and a scheduled compile of the 2.35 and git bindings.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5"}
{"issue": 707, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdWxIjg", "before": "New patch, and an upstream candidate: `nix-call-function-checks-interrupt.patch`, from nixidae/nixidae#744 (4e0c8dc). It adds `checkInterrupt()` at the top of `EvalState::callFunction`.\n\nIt retires when upstream checks for an interrupt during pure evaluation. Plain `nix eval` has the same defect: it ignored SIGINT for 20s on 2.35.2.\n\nThe upstream change is drafted locally in `~/Code/nix`, change 89193ef4, on master. Per the decision above, it goes to Lillecarl/nix as a PR when Carl says so. On 2026-10-10 he answered \"Not yet\".\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5\n"}
{"issue": 718, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPxFTg", "before": "An architectural review of huggorm on 2026-10-06, done by five parallel reviews (DSL, IR and C++ emitter, declarations and helpers, Python surfaces and service, build and gates). This body is longer than one decision because it ranks fourteen. Each issue says which evidence was reproduced, read, or only reported.\n\nThe lens: Nix moves fast, so what does a new Nix release cost?\n\n## A. What a new Nix release costs\n1. nixidae/nixidae#704 Only 2.34 has a gate. nixidae/nixidae#698 sat on main because nothing built 2.35. **Cheapest, do first.**\n2. nixidae/nixidae#707 17 patches, none sent upstream, three forked per version, every lane builds all of Nix.\n3. nixidae/nixidae#706 Version branches copy whole methods, against the recorded rule \"detect a shape, never a version\".\n4. nixidae/nixidae#708 Private `EvalState` members, a live `builtins` sort, and CLI code copied into bodies.\n5. nixidae/nixidae#705 The version list is written in about seven places.\n6. nixidae/nixidae#709 Emitted C++ calls Nix APIs with no version seam.\n\nOne good result: the 2.35 port changed no file in huggorm-gen. The per-release cost sits in about 980 lines of `Cxx` bodies and 1,250 code lines in `cpp/`, not in the emitter.\n\n## B. Generator correctness\n7. nixidae/nixidae#710 `hash()` raises on a value with a dict part. Reproduced. **Small, do second.**\n8. nixidae/nixidae#711 No exhaustive walk over `TypeRef`. nixidae/nixidae#698 and nixidae/nixidae#710 are two instances.\n9. nixidae/nixidae#712 The reader joins import and AST by line number; an aliased marker skips every check.\n\n## C. The service\n10. nixidae/nixidae#714 Positional field numbers plus a whole-schema digest: a rebuilt client cannot reach a warm state. **Carl decides; options in the issue.**\n11. nixidae/nixidae#715 `shutdown(wait=True)` on the event loop; escrow is never freed.\n12. nixidae/nixidae#716 Three surfaces that are one; string dispatch in the runtime; watch and warm on the client.\n\n## D. Cost of working here\n13. nixidae/nixidae#713 About half the source is prose, it ships with history in it, and C++ errors have no `#line`.\n14. nixidae/nixidae#717 nanobind compiles 17 times; the generator has no tests of its own.\n\n## Why these went unnoticed\n- No per-lane gate (#132): a lane break is invisible until a consumer builds it.\n- The prose rule has no lint, so history text accumulated (#141).\n- The census counts only `cpp/` and misses 44% of hand-written C++ (#141).\n- One-level shape tests fail silently, by construction (#139).\n- Patch headers cite nanopynix tests, which this repo cannot run (#135).\n\n## Decisions for Carl\n- nixidae/nixidae#714: stable field numbers (recommended), a new framing, or accept the break.\n- nixidae/nixidae#707: which patches to send upstream. gmtime, the statisticsJSON split, the BASE_ENV_SIZE bound and temp-roots are the cheapest.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5\n"}
{"issue": 732, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPynSQ", "before": "The last nanopynix capability huggorm does not serve (from nixidae/nixidae#634).\n\nnanopynix's `register_store_implementation` registers a Python class as a `nix::Store` implementation. On huggorm it raises: `nanopynix/src/nanopynix/_engine.py:182`, \"huggorm cannot implement a Nix store in Python yet\". nanopynix's `store_impl.StoreImpl` is the Python surface it expects.\n\nWhat it needs: a C++ Store subclass whose virtuals call into Python. The DSL cannot declare one: nixidae/nixidae#621 (a declaration cannot implement a virtual) closed NOT_PLANNED, so the subclass is a hand-written helper, as `huggorm::LogTap` is. The ownership and threading choices stay Carl's, as nixidae/nixidae#634 recorded.\n\nTarget: bring back nanopynix's deleted `test_store_backend_registration.py`.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5\n"}
{"issue": 732, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdW0U0A", "before": "Complexity evaluation, 2026-10-07. Nix 2.34.8 source, nanopynix history up to 538823388fff.\n\n**What nanopynix had** (deleted in 6aae6fd9): `PyStoreImpl : nix::Store`, about 1000 lines of C++ (`py_store_impl.cpp` 700, `.hh` 289), with per-version branches for 2.35 and 2.36. It sent 15 *query* operations to Python: validity, path info as a dict, referrers, closure, missing, ATerm derivations. Everything else went to an optional `underlying` store or failed. It could not hold content: `addToStore`, `narFromPath` and `getFSAccessor` never reached Python. So it was a metadata overlay, not a store. Known defects: `nb::object` members released without the GIL, and the path-info cache turned off.\n\n**Registration is not a blocker.** f2fce6e said upstream's registration would not accept a Python store. Nix 2.34's `Implementations::registered()` is a public map of `StoreFactory` (plain `std::function`s), and nanopynix inserted into it at run time. No template per store.\n\n**The DSL path is closed:** nixidae/nixidae#621 is NOT_PLANNED. A Python store is a hand-written helper, as `LogTap` is. Carl accepts that it is not generated, and it needs no RPC.\n\n**Two shapes:**\n\n| | A: Python binary cache | B: port `StoreImpl` |\n|---|---|---|\n| C++ base | `nix::BinaryCacheStore` | `nix::Store` |\n| Python implements | `exists(key)`, `get(key) -> bytes \\| None`, `put(key, data, mime)` | up to 15 query methods, dict protocol |\n| Content | yes: Nix writes narinfo, NARs, realisations, logs through it | no |\n| What works | add, copy to and from, substitute from, read files, `nix path-info` | queries only, unless `underlying` exists |\n| Helper size | about 200 lines (Nix's own `LocalBinaryCacheStore` is 187) | about 1000 lines |\n| Per-version code | little: three virtuals | 2.35 and 2.36 branches |\n\nNeither shape builds; a binary cache cannot build, and nanopynix's builds went to `underlying`.\n\nHazards either way: Nix may call from any thread, so every call into Python takes the GIL, and every Python reference is released under it. An upload arrives as a `RestartableSource`; A can hand Python whole `bytes` first and stream later.\n\nRecommendation: A. It is a smaller helper and a real store. B adds query hooks that nothing uses.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5"}
{"issue": 732, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdW0VSw", "before": "Carl chose the layered shape, 2026-10-07: subclass `nix::Store` and open an underlying store. A method Python does not answer runs the same method, with the same arguments, on the underlying store. The underlying store may be absent; then such a method raises Nix's own `Unsupported`, as a cache store does. The point is to layer an experiment over any store type.\n\nDesign for the first step:\n\n- **Not generated.** `cpp/py_store.hpp` is a helper, as `LogTap` is: nixidae/nixidae#621 is NOT_PLANNED and there are no trampolines (f2fce6e). A declared free function `register_store_implementation(name, schemes, factory)` binds it. It has no RPC: a factory is a Python callable.\n- **Registration** inserts a `StoreFactory` into `nix::Implementations::registered()` at run time. Then `Store(\"scheme://authority?params\")` opens one through `openStore`, like any store. A duplicate name raises.\n- **The factory** gets `(scheme, authority, params)` and answers the Python store object. That object's `underlying` attribute is a huggorm `Store` or None.\n- **The delegate signal:** a hook that returns `NotImplemented` falls through. A hook the class does not define falls through too. A raised exception is an error, never a fall-through. `None` means absent where Nix has an absent answer.\n- **Hook names are the declared `Store` method names**, typed with the bound classes (`StorePath`, `PathInfo`), not base-name strings and dicts. The hooks are the *uncached* operations; Nix's path-info cache sits above them, and `path-info-cache-size=0` in the URI turns it off.\n- **Threads:** Nix may call from any thread. Every call into Python takes the GIL, and the GIL is released before the call on the underlying store. The Python object is released under the GIL, and not at all once the interpreter is finalizing. `queryPathInfoUncached` is `noexcept`: an exception goes to `callback.rethrow()`.\n- **First hooks:** the 10 pure virtuals forward or raise `Unsupported`. Python may answer `is_valid_path`, `query_path_info`, `query_path_from_hash_part`, `query_all_valid_paths`, `query_referrers` and `add_temp_root`.\n- **Later:** content operations (`add_to_store`, `nar_from_path`) need a Source and a Sink. Then forward every other virtual.\n\nKnown gap, named now: the store mixins. huggorm reaches `GcStore`, `LogStore`, `LocalFSStore` and `IndirectRootStore` by `dynamic_cast`. So a Python store over a `LocalStore` answers \"not supported\" for `collect_garbage`, `add_perm_root`, `get_build_log` and the store directories, even though the underlying store can do them. Fixing that means forwarding those interfaces too.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5"}
{"issue": 732, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdW0WKw", "before": "What the DSL lacks to declare a Python store, investigated 2026-10-07 after Carl asked whether `@virtual` alone gates it.\n\n**It had one.** `@virtual` and `@pure` with emitted nanobind trampolines existed, and f2fce6e removed them: 245 lines removed, 26 added. The reasons given were \"nothing wants a Python store\" and \"upstream registration would not accept one\". The first no longer holds, and the second is wrong (`Implementations::registered()` is public). The commit also said real use would reshape it, and it does:\n\n1. **`NB_OVERRIDE` cannot be the dispatch.** nanobind's `ticket` holds the GIL for its whole scope (`trampoline.h`), so the base call runs under the GIL. nanopynix measured that this blocks every thread for a daemon round trip and deadlocks on re-entry. The emitter must write its own dispatch: look up the override under the GIL, release the GIL, then call the base. `NotImplemented` falls through to the base. Small.\n2. **Nix's virtuals are not declarable shapes.** `queryPathInfoUncached` is protected, `noexcept`, and answers through `Callback<shared_ptr<const T>>`. `queryReferrers` fills an out-parameter. `addToStore` and `narFromPath` take a `Source&` or `Sink&`. 2.35 and 2.36 rename and move some (`registerDrvOutputUnchecked`, `ensurePath` on `Builder`, `StoreConfig(params, FilePathType)`). Teaching the DSL every one of these is nixidae/nixidae#621's \"net increase\".\n3. **The layering is not `nix::Store`'s base behaviour.** \"Fall through to underlying\" needs a C++ class whose virtuals forward. Its base answers are `unsupported(...)`.\n\n**The shape that keeps the DSL small:** a helper class `huggorm::LayeredStore : nix::Store`, written by hand once. It forwards every virtual to the underlying store and absorbs points 2 and 3. It re-exposes each operation as a plain public virtual with bound types (`is_valid_path(StorePath) -> bool`, `query_path_info(StorePath) -> optional<ValidPathInfo>`, ...). The declaration then binds that class, and the DSL needs only:\n\n- `@virtual` on a declared method: emit a trampoline class with the dispatch from point 1. This brings f2fce6e's machinery back, reshaped.\n- Subclassing from Python: an abstract class that a Python subclass may construct (the old `pointer_and_handle` guard).\n- Hooks are in-process only: keep `@virtual` methods off the async and RPC surfaces, with a reason, as the build does for callables.\n- Registration stays a helper the declaration binds, `register_store_implementation(name, schemes, cls)`, taking the Python class.\n\nOwnership needs nothing new. nanobind's `shared_ptr` caster keeps a Python-made store alive while Nix holds its `ref<Store>`, decrefs under the GIL, and skips the decref after shutdown (`stl/shared_ptr.h`, `py_deleter`).\n\nWhat changes against 18ba7bf0: the hand-written per-hook dispatch becomes emitted, and a Python store becomes `class Mine(LayeredStore)` with typed stubs, so a type checker reads the override signatures. The forwarding and Nix's awkward shapes stay hand-written. `@virtual` would also serve the next virtual a caller wants to implement, such as a fetcher `InputScheme` or a `SourceAccessor`.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5"}
{"issue": 732, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdW0Y1w", "before": "`is_trusted_client` is a hook now. A `@virtual` may answer a vocabulary: C++ holds the enum, and the trampoline parses the override's word. The 2.34 check and both lanes pass (907 each).\n\nEvery non-mixin operation that huggorm's `Store` declares and Nix dispatches virtually is now overridable. The mixins are deferred to nixidae/nixidae#736, by Carl's decision.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5"}
{"issue": 732, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdW0Z3A", "before": "c944c154: `register_drv_output(output, check_sigs)` is on `Store` and is a hook. `Realisation` gets a constructor for it. The 2.34 check and both lanes pass (911 each).\n\nWhat a Python store still cannot override:\n- `getFSAccessor` (both overloads). It needs a bound `SourceAccessor`, which is a new type with its own design. This is the largest remaining gap: Nix reads a store's files through it.\n- Virtuals with no `Store` method yet: `repairPath`, `addSignatures`, `querySubstitutablePathInfos`, `queryPartialDerivationOutputMap`, `addMultipleToStore`. Each follows the pattern above: a `Store` method with Nix's contract, then a `@virtual`.\n- The mixins: nixidae/nixidae#736, deferred.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5"}
{"issue": 736, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPzE2A", "before": "Deferred by Carl on 2026-10-08. Follows huggorm#149.\n\nA Python store cannot be a `GcStore` or `LogStore` today. Each mixin derives `nix::Store` virtually, so the `Store` part sits at a nonzero offset. nanobind never adjusts a base pointer (its porting guide lists multiple inheritance as removed), so a Python subclass of such a class would hand `Store`'s bindings a wrong pointer.\n\nThe route if this is wanted: split `LayeredStore` into a hooks object that Python subclasses, and a C++-made store that calls those hooks and may derive any mixin. `Store(\"x://\")` would then return the C++ store, not the subclass instance.\n\nWorth doing only for `GcStore` (`collectGarbage`, `findRoots`) and `LogStore` (`getBuildLogExact`, `addBuildLog`): those are operations Nix reaches through `require<>`. `LocalFSStore` and `IndirectRootStore` are claims that the store is a local directory, which a layer should not make.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5"}
{"issue": 738, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdW1MRQ", "before": "Remote evaluation of a client's files works, EXPERIMENTALLY. It is pushed on main, in three commits after `bf2ebb4b` (`Stat` as a wire value): `calls_back` in the generator, then the callback transport.\n\n```python\nasync with remote.connect(path, experimental_callbacks=True) as client:\n    store = await client.acquire(\"Store\", \"dummy://?read-only=false\")\n    state = await client.acquire(\"EvalState\", store)\n    at = await state.mount(MyAccessor(), \"source\", path_hashed_locally)\n    value = await state.eval_file(f\"{await store.print_store_path(at)}/default.nix\")\n```\n\nThe server runs with `--experimental-callbacks`. Each read the evaluator makes is one CALLBACK frame to the client, answered by REPLY or REFUSE. Tested in `tests/test_remote_eval.py`:\n- The server evaluates `import`, `readFile (./. + \"/x\")` and `readDir` from the client's tree, and the client sees each read.\n- `mount` without a path hashes through callbacks.\n- A failing client hook fails the server call.\n- A client that leaves in the middle of a callback fails that call, and the server goes on serving.\n- Either end without the flag refuses with a message that names it.\n\nStill open: flakes need a custom input scheme, and absolute paths, `<nixpkgs>` and fetchers still read the server (see above). Multiplexing is nixidae/nixidae#740.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5\n"}
{"issue": 738, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdW1Nig", "before": "**Settled: pure mode allows a mounted tree** (test `test_pure_evaluation_reads_a_mounted_tree`). With `pure-eval = true`, the evaluator reads a tree that `EvalState.mount` mounted, and refuses an absolute path on the server. The allow list wraps only `rootFS`, as the source said.\n\nStreams for remote stores are done in nixidae/nixidae#740: `nar_from_path` and `add_to_store_nar` take a client's `Sink` or `Source`.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5\n"}
{"issue": 742, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPztaw", "before": "From nixidae/nixidae#740. Over RPC, a `@posted` hook (`Sink.write`) still costs one round trip on the server's loop. The client proxy (`callbacks.py`, `Callbacks.call`) awaits the REPLY of each write before `_Hooks.run_asked` runs the next one.\n\nNix does not wait, up to 16 writes, so Nix's work overlaps the network. But throughput is at most one piece per round trip.\n\n**Proposed:** the proxy sends a posted CALLBACK and returns. The client answers a posted callback only when it fails. At the end of the call, the server waits for one final round trip, so the client has run every write in order.\n\nThis changes the protocol. Do it when a measurement over a real link asks for it.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5\n"}
{"issue": 743, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYPz0CA", "before": "From nixidae/nixidae#740. On a `_Home` (an affine object's thread), `_Hooks.post` is `ask`: Nix waits for each posted hook.\n\nA posted hook runs while the thread works, not while it waits. So a call the hook makes back to that thread goes to the executor's queue, behind the call that waits for the hook, and neither ends. `test_a_posted_hook_may_call_the_state_that_posts_it` hangs with the `isinstance(self.executor, _Home)` branch removed.\n\n**Proposed:** a `_Home` that keeps its inbox from the first post to the flush. The worker runs pending inbox work at each post, and waits on the inbox when the window is full or at the flush.\n\nNo affine class takes a `Sink` today, so this waits for one that does.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5\n"}
{"issue": 747, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP0HpQ", "before": "A pod that gets /nix only through NRI (the `nixkube/pod` annotation, no CSI volume) can start without /nix on a node that has just joined. containerd creates a container unadjusted when no NRI plugin is registered. The CSI path has no such window, because kubelet retries the mount until the driver registers.\n\nMeasured 2026-10-10 on a kubeadm cluster, containerd 2.3.3, after a worker was replaced:\n\n| time (UTC) | event |\n| --- | --- |\n| 11:51:25 | node Ready |\n| 11:51:57 | two NRI-only pods scheduled to it |\n| ~11:52:30 | both fail: `StartError`, `exec: \"/nix/store/...-python3-3.14.7-env/bin/python\": no such file or directory` |\n| 11:52:35 | nix-node containers start, the NRI plugin with them |\n\nnixidae/nixidae#382 shows the same error with a different cause: there the plugin was connected and the paths were missing.\n\ncontainerd 2.3.3 vendors NRI v0.12.0. Its built-in default validator can require a plugin per pod, through the annotation `required-plugins.noderesource.dev` (`/pod` or `/container.<name>` suffix), value a YAML list. The validator then rejects the create while `nixkube` is missing, and kubelet retries it. It must be enabled on the node: `[plugins.\"io.containerd.nri.v1.nri\".default_validator] enable = true`. The plugin map holds both `nixkube` and `<idx>-nixkube` (`pkg/api/validate.go`, `GetPluginMap`).\n\nAsk: document this in the README beside the NRI mount path. Name the race, the validator setting, and the annotation, `\"required-plugins.noderesource.dev/pod\": '[\"nixkube\"]'`. A global `required_plugins = [\"nixkube\"]` is the other shape, but nix-node itself then needs the validator's tolerate-missing annotation.\n\nNot yet verified end to end: no pod has been observed waiting on the validator. The settings are applied in solid-kubernetes and reach the nodes with the next roll.\n"}
{"issue": 749, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP0XRQ", "before": "Split from nixidae/nixidae#716 (Carl's choice).\n\n`Watcher`, `Notifier` and `Warmer` run in the client and take an `AsyncEvalState`. Over a remote backend that gives three faults:\n- A watch dies with the client.\n- It watches the client's files, not the files the server's evaluator read.\n- Detach and reclaim cannot keep it: escrow holds the state's handle, not the watch.\n\nThe destination in AGENTS.md is a state that outlives its callers, with every file an evaluation touched under a watch. So the watch belongs to the server's state.\n\nOpen questions, each a decision for Carl:\n1. Lifetime. Does a watch end when the last lease on its state goes, or is it a lease of its own that escrow holds?\n2. Ownership. Is there one watcher per state shared by all connections, or one per connection?\n3. Delivery. How does a change reach a client: a stream like `Session/Logs`, or a poll?\n4. Warming. Does `Warmer` run on the server's loop for every registered expression, and who registers?\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5\n"}
{"issue": 753, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP0z5Q", "before": "havsorm declares `dependencies = [\"huggorm\"]`. salt-pip can install it only when huggorm, huggorm-bindings and huggorm-generated are on PyPI. huggorm builds manylinux_2_28 wheels for cp311 to cp314 (huggorm#108), which covers Salt 3006 (Python 3.11 from 3006.27), 3007 (3.13) and 3008 (3.14). Nothing is published yet.\n\nGate to add: install the wheels into a Salt onedir with salt-pip, and render a #!nix SLS with it.\n\nNote for the next slice: Salt ships `salt/modules/nix.py`, so a havsorm execution module named `nix` would shadow a core module.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5\n"}
{"issue": 753, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdW3HEw", "before": "Cross-reference: huggorm#108 tracks the manylinux wheel builds this issue waits on."}
{"issue": 756, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdW3hYA", "before": "Steps 1 and 2 done at 7d04a89: `nix build --file . bundle` runs venv-salt-minion 3006.0 (Python 3.11.15) with huggorm's cp311 wheels and havsorm pip-installed into its PYTHONPATH target. A #!nix state and a #!nix pillar SLS render, imports included. The entry point is found in the pip target. SUSE's 3006.0 gives renderers no file client, so a pillar SLS now opens the pillar file client itself (9f9d4f1). Without that the bundle fails, which is the control.\n\nThe RPM pin will break when the repository moves to a new build, as the Leap image pin did (vivarium#30).\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5"}
{"issue": 758, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP1Xag", "before": "Design: event-driven Nix, and a text form of the renderer. No code yet.\n\n**Reactor gap.** `salt/utils/reactor.py:70` renders with `tag=` and `data=`. `renderers/nix.py` drops both, so a `#!nix` reactor SLS cannot read `data.id`. Fix: pass `tag`, `data` and other JSON-able kwargs into the function's arguments. Negative control: a reactor SLS that reads `data.id` fails before and renders after.\n\n**Rolling deploy (the headline).** CI pushes a closure to a cache and runs `salt-run event.send nix/release/web '{path: /nix/store/\u2026}'`. The reactor starts `runner.state.orchestrate`, and the orchestration runs `salt.state` with `batch: 10%`: `havsorm.profile`, then `service.running` with `watch`. Salt gives targeting, batching and the job cache. Nix gives an atomic switch. A new `havsorm.rollback` (previous generation) closes the `onfail` path.\n\n**Warm evaluator.** The reactor renders one event at a time in one master process, and slow renders hold up later events. A new EvalState per render is that slow case. That makes the reactor the first place for the warm-evaluator idea in nixidae/nixidae#757: one process, no per-minion key. A havsorm *engine* (a long-running process on the master or minion) is the general home for it.\n\n**Small pieces:**\n- `mine_functions: havsorm.profile_target`: the master sees which store path each minion runs.\n- A `havsorm` beacon (free store bytes, profile targets) \u2192 reactor \u2192 `havsorm.garbage_collected`.\n- A grains module (Nix version, profile targets), so a target can be \"the minions not on release X\".\n- A `master_tops` module: a Nix function of id and grains gives the top data.\n\n**Two frontmatters.** `#!nix` stays the data renderer. A text form (`#!nixtext|yaml`, or `#!nix text|yaml` through Salt's argline) returns `io.StringIO`, and the pipe already accepts that. But \"Nix prints YAML, yaml parses it\" is a worse `#!nix`. Nix as text pays off for config files: `file.managed template=\u2026`. That goes through `salt.utils.templates.TEMPLATE_REGISTRY` (3008.2 `utils/templates.py:735`), a fixed dict with no loader hook. So an extension cannot add a template engine without patching Salt. The working route today is a module function: `contents: __slot__:salt:havsorm.text(salt://motd.nix)` evaluates on the minion at state time. `#!jinja|nix` already works, with no new renderer.\n\n**Limit.** salt-ssh renders on the master, so `#!nix` works there. But the thin cannot carry a native wheel, so `havsorm.*` states do not run on salt-ssh targets.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5\n"}
{"issue": 759, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP1drg", "before": "Seen on dynhetz, pynixd 0.1.0 (store path r4yv4viik070fykk7iif9ajhc4mxkhc0).\n\nBuild 11386 (`kyverno-rendered.yaml.drv`, an IFD in an `ekn render`) was enqueued at 03:09:25Z. The journal has no `build_completed` for it, and no other event. Its client was killed about 30 minutes later. More than an hour after that, every new request for the same derivation still hung. `nix build -v` printed \"this derivation will be built\" and then nothing. No builder process started.\n\nThe same derivation built in seconds against `/nix/var/nix/daemon-socket/upstream`. Once that output was valid, pynixd served it at once. A new trivial derivation built normally through pynixd the whole time.\n\nMetrics at the time:\n\n    pynixd_build_queue_size{status=\"pending\"} -2.0\n    pynixd_build_queue_size{status=\"building\"} 6.0\n    pynixd_store_pool_in_flight_connections{store_id=\"local\"} 2.0\n\nSo the pending gauge is negative, and \"building\" counts more builds than there are store connections.\n\nLikely mechanism: `BuildQueue.enqueue` dedupes on `_by_path` while `not existing.is_done`. An entry that never reaches a store and never fails is never done. Nothing times it out, and nothing cancels it when its subscribers go. The waiting client also gets no \"waiting on somebody else's build\" activity.\n\nTwo smaller findings:\n- Most `build_enqueued` records say `description: \"unknown-unknown\"`.\n- nixidae/nixidae#723 (introspection) would have made this a one-command diagnosis. Today the only way to see the queue is `/proc/locks` and the journal."}
{"issue": 759, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdW37Ow", "before": "Probable trigger, in nixkube: its builder queue watch crashes on the removed `QueuedBuild.platform`, so no builder pod ever starts. Lillecarl/nixkube#78."}
{"issue": 760, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP1maA", "before": "A guest from an OCI image, beside `vivarium.image` (cloud disk images, UML only).\n\nWhy: a distribution's container image is pinned by digest, which SUSE's appliance builds are not (#30). It needs no kernel or disk, so it is cheaper than a VM image. havsorm wants a fleet of non-NixOS Salt minions.\n\nShape to decide:\n- Backend: `container` (crun) is the natural one. The rootfs is the image's layers.\n- Init: many images have no systemd. Either the agent is PID 1, or the guest needs an image with systemd (for example `opensuse/leap` with systemd installed in a layer we build).\n- Store: the same question as an image guest's `/nix`: a read-only view, or writable with a database (see the havsorm issue for image guests).\n- Pin: `dockerTools.pullImage` with `imageDigest` and `hash`.\n\nRelated: nixidae/nixidae#523 builds OCI images in a guest. This one boots them.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5\n"}
{"issue": 762, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP1zoQ", "before": "Investigation: can pynixd's Kubernetes integration find store paths in cluster objects and hold GC roots for them?\n\n**Problem.** nixkube roots a closure only while a pod that mounts it runs (`pkgs/nixkube/src/nix/gc.py`, `csi/cleanup.py` in nixkube). A store path that an object refers to with no pod running is unrooted, and pynixd's GC can collect it. Today the deploy-time render keeps such paths alive. Paths created at runtime have nothing to keep them alive.\n\n**Case.** solid-kubernetes plans a developer portal. A worker evaluates and builds each project's tofu config in-cluster, and a TofuUnit holds the resulting store path in `spec.configFile`. Its plan and apply Jobs are short-lived. A destroy needs the path the unit last applied, possibly months later. If that path is collected, the unit fails with `cannot destroy: /nix/store/... is not on this node`.\n\n**Questions.**\n- Which objects to read: a label or annotation selector, specific kinds, or a scan of every manifest for `/nix/store/` strings?\n- Which roots: one indirect root per object and path, released when the object goes away or stops naming the path. Do finalizers need to be considered?\n- Lifecycle: a watch, or a periodic sync; and the behaviour after pynixd restarts.\n- How this interacts with the LRU/upstream-drop GC plans (#52, nixidae/nixidae#720).\n"}
{"issue": 765, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP2HOw", "before": "Handoff for the kinds spike, paused for pynixd work (Lillecarl/pynixd#89). The design, the decisions, the measurements and a landing list are in `design/kinds.md` on the spike.\n\n**Where it is.** Local only on dynhetz, not pushed:\n- easykubenix: bookmark `spike/kinds` (f4bf74b8), on develop b55bd7c1. Baseline for benchmarks: `wsvyqyom`.\n- solid-kubernetes: bookmark `spike/kinds` (cd517c72), in the workspace `~/Code/solid-kubernetes-kinds`. Baseline workspace: `~/Code/solid-kubernetes-kinds-base`.\n\n**API changes**\n- `kubernetes.kindAliases.<alias> = \"<GroupKind>\"`, e.g. `PGCluster = \"Cluster.postgresql.cnpg.io\"`.\n- `kubernetes.apiResources`: APIResource records keyed by GroupResource, filled from `apiMappingFile` and from every CRD in `crds` or in a list definition of `objects`.\n- Keys under `objects.<ns>` are a bare kind, a GroupKind string or an alias.\n- `kubernetes.objects` takes a list of complete objects as a definition. easykubenix files each one by its key, and tree patches merge with it.\n- `importYaml`/`importHelm` return `kubernetes.objects` as a list, with no `kubernetes` argument. `pkgs.renderChart` returns `{ objects; crds; }`.\n- `apiMappings` is a deprecated override only.\n- Evaluation errors: one object under two keys, two names with one `metadata.name`, two different imports of one identity, an ambiguous bare kind, two aliases for one GroupKind.\n\n**Measured** on solid-kubernetes' nixlab3: the same 3192 objects; +0.17 % values, +0.25 % calls, +2.7 MiB; CPU within noise.\n\n**To land** (also in `design/kinds.md`):\n1. The `apiMappings` deprecation warning.\n2. Python identity keys with the group, in the same change.\n3. ekn prune reads `apiResources`. Until then a spike build scans fewer kinds, so do not deploy from it.\n4. An error for an alias whose GroupKind nothing serves.\n5. The kubeapply gate.\n6. The solid-kubernetes list change lands in the same umbrella update; then drop most of solid's 80 `apiMappings`.\n7. Trim the design doc.\n\nLater: kubenix compatibility as one `kubenix` submodule (decision 9).\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 766, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP2Nug", "before": "Decided 2026-10-12. pynixd stalls are blocking automated agents (#89).\n\n**Evidence.** At 00:50 pynixd held 20 client sessions. For 17 of them the client end of the socket was already closed (`ss -xan`, peer 0). Those requests still hold their builds. Two builds (`ptyhost-unit-run`, `pymux-unit-run`) have hung in pytest for over 2 days, with `max-silent-time = 0` and `timeout = 0` (Nix's defaults), and they fill the local slots of nixidae/nixidae#763.\n\n**Reference.** `scratch/mock_goals.py`: `SharedWork` with holder counting, waiters that wait on an event beside an outcome and never await the worker, the last release cancels, and workers run in a registry-owned task group. The sketch has no dead detection, and its global `Semaphore(2)` slot is the shape of nixidae/nixidae#763.\n\n**Plan.** Incremental; each step lands alone and passes the guest suites.\n1. A per-request cancel scope that fires when the client socket reaches EOF, so a dead client releases its builds.\n2. Per-client slot accounting (#89).\n3. Builds on `SharedWork`/`Registry` with last-let-go cancel, behind today's interfaces. This replaces `goal_holders`, `let_go`, `release_build`, `forget_request`, `nobody_wants` and the detached `create_task` in `Scheduler.execute_build`.\n4. Build timeouts: pynixd forwards the client's `max-silent-time`/`timeout`. dynhetz sets `max-silent-time` in its own `nix.conf` (croshome), not in pynixd.\n\nNot now: an upstream silence watchdog and a periodic stall report.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 766, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdW4z_A", "before": "Step 1 landed on develop (4bb1379b, 6345eacc). Every session transport now notices a client that goes:\n\n- Unix on Linux: epoll with no events, so `POLLHUP` alone wakes it. A half-close is not a hangup, as in Nix.\n- Unix on darwin/FreeBSD: kqueue `EV_EOF`, as `monitor-fd.hh:64` does. Untested here; darwin CI is its first run.\n- SSH server and reverse channel: channel EOF or close, which `nix-daemon --stdio` behind sshd sees as `POLLHUP` on its stdin pipe.\n\nThe session runs in a cancel scope that the watcher cancels. The teardown that gives build references back is shielded: `anyio.Lock` raises at once in a cancelled scope, so without the shield the request kept its hold. That was the second half of nixidae/nixidae#759.\n\n`tests/unit/test_client_hangup.py` covers it: a cancelled request releases its build, a Unix and an SSH hangup (EOF and dropped connection) end a pending build that only that client wanted, and a half-close keeps the operation. The SSH cases time out with the hook removed. All guest suites green.\n\nNot covered: upstream connections that die or stall. That is nixidae/nixidae#768 and step 4.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 766, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdW402w", "before": "Steps 2 and 4 are done and deployed on dynhetz.\n\n- Step 2 (pynixd 6c38f397): `max-jobs` counts each request's own local builds, as `worker.cc:261` counts per connection.\n- Step 4: pynixd already forwards client options. dynhetz now sets `max-silent-time = 3600` (croshome 59f0e22d). Live check: a silent build run with `--option max-silent-time 3` through pynixd ended with `timed out after 3 seconds`.\n\nAlso landed on the way: the queue gauge is counted from the queue and cannot drift (94f3c171, nixidae/nixidae#763).\n\nLeft: step 3, the SharedWork/Registry refactor, and nixidae/nixidae#768 for upstream faults.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 767, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP2T5A", "before": "`pynixd state --section queue` prints `pending: 2, building: 4, done: 12`. Diagnosing nixidae/nixidae#763 needed root and `sys.remote_exec` to read the rest from inside the daemon (`~/tmp/pynixd-queue-dump.py` on dynhetz).\n\nPer build, show:\n- build id, drv path and name, state, age, time since start\n- store, `max_build_jobs` and the other options that decide scheduling\n- holders: the requests that wait on it, with the client pid and whether the client socket is still open\n- retries and store failures\n- why a pending build has not started: no compatible store, slot full (and whose builds fill it), or nobody wants it\n\nPer request (`GoalEngine`), show: request id, client pid, operation, age, the builds it holds and what each goal waits on.\n\nThe two \"why\" fields need the scheduler to record its last decision for each build. Today that is a debug log line only (`build_waits_for_a_local_slot`).\n\nKeep `--section`, and add `--json` for agents. A plain user can run it (no root), and it finds the socket per nixidae/nixidae#764.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 768, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP2alA", "before": "A small asyncio Unix-socket proxy in the test suite, put between pynixd and a real nix-daemon (and, reversed, between a client and pynixd). A test scripts a fault and asserts what pynixd does. It covers the \"Backend disconnection\" part of nixidae/nixidae#396, and it gives nixidae/nixidae#766 its regression tests.\n\nTriggers: after N bytes in one direction, at op number X (the proxy reads the op number after the handshake), or after T seconds. Actions:\n\n| fault | expected pynixd behaviour |\n| --- | --- |\n| refuse / no socket | clean error to the client, other stores unaffected |\n| accept, never handshake | bounded wait, then error |\n| close mid-response (EOF) | connection leaves the pool; op fails cleanly or retries on a fresh one |\n| RST mid-response (SO_LINGER 0) | same as EOF |\n| idle pooled connection dies | next op reconnects, client sees nothing |\n| stall mid-response or mid-log | today: hangs. After nixidae/nixidae#766 step 4: max-silent-time/timeout ends it |\n| drip (1 byte/s) | progresses, other sessions unaffected |\n| half-close from upstream | treated as EOF after the response |\n| corrupt or truncated frame | protocol error to the client, connection discarded, no crash |\n| client side: hangup, half-close, client that never reads | nixidae/nixidae#766 step 1; a slow reader must not block other sessions |\n\nEvery test also asserts: no leaked tasks or pool connections, and no build holder left in the queue.\n\nLives in `tests/functional/` as a fixture. Runs in the guest functional suite, because it needs a real daemon. Modes that hang today start as xfail and link nixidae/nixidae#766.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 772, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP216w", "before": "The SSH listener admits a key only when an `ssh_user_keys` or `ssh_admin_keys` file names it (#94). Every new node, builder or deploy key therefore has to be written into a file first.\n\nOpenSSH user certificates remove that step. A CA signs short-lived keys, and the listener trusts the CA:\n\n- `ssh_user_cas` / `ssh_admin_cas`: `authorized_keys`-style files with `cert-authority` lines. A certificate signed by one of them gets that role.\n- Check validity dates and the `principals` against the username, as sshd does (`AuthorizedPrincipalsFile`-like), so a certificate is not a bearer token for any name.\n- Optional: take the role from a certificate extension or principal (for example `pynixd-admin`) instead of from which CA signed it.\n\nasyncssh already validates certificates: `SSHAuthorizedKeys.validate(..., ca=True)` matches `cert-authority` entries, and `validate_ca_key` is the server hook. The keyring in `pynixd/ssh_server.py` is the place to add it.\n\nUse cases: nixkube nodes get certificates from a cluster CA instead of a shared static key; deploys get short-lived admin certificates (nixkube#73).\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Opus 5"}
{"issue": 783, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP4BZA", "before": "Goal: Python environments on released Nix, with dynamic derivations and no `builder-rpc-v0`. The machinery belongs in pydd's core, so another language ecosystem can reuse it.\n\n**Requirements**\n\n- One node per package. No mode may collapse an environment into one derivation.\n- The exposed root behaves like an ordinary derivation: `override`, `overrideAttrs`, hooks.\n- Build each route both with and without EnsurePath (Lillecarl/nix#371), to show how much simpler the EnsurePath route is.\n- Every mode is compared with the others: same environment store paths in `test-projects`.\n\n**Route (chosen): nodes made on the host.** The host makes each node that the lock allows, from `graph.nix`. A nested `$out` planner makes each node that needs a build-time answer (sdist builds, local projects). The outer planner writes only the root's ATerm to `$out`, staged through a writable `dummy://` (huggorm#163).\n\n**Steps**\n\n1. Tools, the interpreter and build tools reach a node as `{ drv, out }` pairs, as artefacts do. Then a node made on the host and the same node made by a planner are one `.drv`. Land in the `builder-rpc-v0` modes first, with the oracle and `test-projects` green.\n2. `$out` protocol in `mkPlanner`, and a writer that refuses a root which names anything that is not an input of the planner. Prove it on `examples/hello` against the 2.35 daemon.\n3. Python environments on that protocol.\n\n**Measured.** Recursive Nix also works on stock 2.35, with the unchanged CLI writer. It is not the chosen route, because `builder-rpc-v0` exists to replace it. A root that names a `.drv` which is not a planner input silently gets a different path.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5\n"}
{"issue": 790, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP4uWQ", "before": "**Question.** Can a node use nixpkgs as an ordinary derivation does: `stdenv.mkDerivation`, `autoPatchelfHook` and the other setup hooks?\n\n**Today.** A node runs no stdenv. Binary wheels call the `auto-patchelf` program directly. Source builds call `scripts/fixup-elf.py`, which also follows each library's RUNPATH (#4). The rest of stdenv's work is redone by hand: `-g0`, `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH`, `CPPFLAGS` and `LDFLAGS` instead of `NIX_CFLAGS_COMPILE`.\n\n**Idea.** A mode with an evaluator in the planner (huggorm today, or plain `nix eval` with EnsurePath, nixidae/nixidae#789) imports nixpkgs from its store path and writes nodes with `stdenv.mkDerivation`. nixpkgs is in practice always in the store. This avoids copying it twice:\n\n    pkgsSrc = if builtins ? currentSystem then builtins.storePath pkgs.path else pkgs.path;\n\n**To measure**\n\n- The evaluation cost of nixpkgs in each planner, and whether a huggorm EvalState can be reused across planners.\n- Whether stdenv's `.drv` closure must be written through the restricted socket, or whether it can be an input of the planner.\n- Whether `import` of a store path inside a `builder-rpc-v0` build calls EnsurePath without #371.\n- Released Nix cannot take part: a planner there writes one derivation. The host-made nodes of `out` can use stdenv already, because the host evaluates them (#6).\n\nIs it a good idea? It gives nodes all of nixpkgs. It also ties every node's `.drv` to stdenv's, so a nixpkgs bump rebuilds every environment.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5\n"}
{"issue": 790, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdW73Nw", "before": "**Measured: nixpkgs cannot be evaluated inside a `builder-rpc-v0` planner, with or without EnsurePath.**\n\nA `mkEvaluated` planner that imports the pinned nixpkgs and returns one `stdenv.mkDerivation` (with `autoPatchelfHook`) fails after 33 s at 321 MB RSS:\n\n    NixError: error: Operation 26 not allowed inside derivation\n\n- Op 26 is `QueryPathInfo`. The `builder-rpc-v0` allow list (`src/libstore/daemon.cc`, `performOp`) holds only the `AddToStore*` ops, `SubmitOutput`, `AddTempRoot` and `IsValidPath`. Lillecarl/nix#371 adds `EnsurePath` and nothing else.\n- The caller is `fetchToStore` (`src/libfetchers/fetch-to-store.cc:122`). It copies a path literal into the store with `addToStore`, then calls `queryPathInfo` for the hash, and does not catch the refusal. nixpkgs has path literals everywhere, such as each `./setup-hook.sh`.\n- pydd's `graph.nix` has no path literal, which is why the `evaluate` mode works.\n\n**Two upstream fixes would unblock this.** Allow `QueryPathInfo` in a restricted build, which hides paths the build cannot see, as `IsValidPath` already does. Or make `fetchToStore` take the info from the add, with no second query. Either is a Nix change, built from source like nixidae/nixidae#789.\n\nProbe: a scratch `mkEvaluated` over `import nixpkgs { }` \u2192 `stdenv.mkDerivation`.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5"}
{"issue": 796, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP5TQw", "before": "**Measured.** The `drv` writer saves 18 MiB of closure (148.9 against 166.5 MiB), because libnixstore carries most of both. That is not the gain nixidae/nixidae#782 hoped for.\n\n**Proposal.**\n- Have the renderer print one symbolic graph and call `pydd-drv graph` once, not once per node. Measure planner time both ways first.\n- Give `{{\u2026}}` an escape, because a shell script may hold `{{`.\n\n---\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5"}
{"issue": 797, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdW8s7w", "before": "Split out the three actionable items: nixidae/nixidae#826 (memoise hostRow), nixidae/nixidae#827 (hint skew report), nixidae/nixidae#828 (failure cause surfacing). This issue keeps the README-trim remnant: the stale count is already fixed (Tests section is count-free), the length complaint remains."}
{"issue": 804, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP6IAA", "before": "An idea to keep, not a plan yet.\n\nEach result could give a development shell as an attribute (`root.shell`, as `pydd.python.uv` already has for its environment). The shell would hold wrappers of the ecosystem's own tools, such as `cargo` and `uv`, that feel the same to a user. But the work would go through Nix: a `cargo build` in the shell would plan with pydd and build the nodes in the store, so a crate built once is shared with every other project and with CI.\n\nOpen questions:\n- Which commands to wrap (build, run, test), and which to pass straight through (fmt, clippy, add)?\n- How a wrapper finds the project's pydd call (a file in the project, or an attribute of the shell).\n- How this relates to the CLI mode of nixidae/nixidae#803, which runs a planner on the host.\n\nAssisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5"}
{"issue": 806, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdW967A", "before": "## Where an `h1` fetch can live\n\nOnly the store that builds can check a fixed output, so only that store can fetch by `h1`. The sandbox has no network in any mode.\n\n- **huggorm `LayeredStore`** (huggorm#149): in-process only. Nix resolves a dynamic chain inside the daemon, so the layer sees the top `build_paths` and none of the nodes. It cannot do this.\n- **pynixd**: it schedules every derivation itself. It resolves the dynamic-derivation trampolines and CA, and sends each basic derivation to a backend Nix (`system_features.py`, `goals/`). So it sees each module fetch, and can do it: fetch, check `h1`, add the tree.\n- **Stock Nix parses no new hash mode.** The planner's `nix derivation add` runs in a backend Nix. So the prototype encodes the fetch as a floating CA derivation with `builder = \"builtin:fetch-go-module\"` and the `h1` in its env, as `builtin:fetchurl` is. pynixd builds it; a backend never sees it.\n- **The Nix patch** gives the same derivation a fixed path: a content-address method `h1` beside flat, nar, git and text.\n\nWhat upstream will ask: dirhash skips the executable bit, symlinks and empty directories, so two trees can share an `h1`. The method must refuse symlinks and empty directories, and fix every file at 0444. Module zips hold none of these.\n"}
{"issue": 814, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP7LYA", "before": "More ecosystems, ranked. **We track these; we may not build all of them.** Each one is worth building only if it shows something the lanes so far (uv, Poetry, Cargo, npm) do not.\n\n| rank | ecosystem | issue | why here |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Go | nixidae/nixidae#806 | next: an investigation of how to fetch with `go.sum` hashes, which are not NAR hashes; a fetch or hash primop may solve it |\n| 2 | bun, pnpm, Yarn | nixidae/nixidae#816 | readers for the npm lane; the graph and `out` exist already |\n| 3 | Ruby (Bundler) | nixidae/nixidae#805 | checksums in the lock, native extensions per gem, nixpkgs hints (`defaultGemConfig`) |\n| 4 | Elixir, Erlang | nixidae/nixidae#815 | hashes in the lock, a compile per dependency, NIFs |\n| 5 | Haskell (cabal) | nixidae/nixidae#807 | `plan.json` is a unit graph; a large Nix user base |\n| 6 | PHP (Composer) | nixidae/nixidae#808 | simple; tests a lock with weak hashes |\n| 7 | Zig | nixidae/nixidae#817 | the same hash question as Go; build it with Go's answer |\n| 8 | OCaml (opam, dune) | nixidae/nixidae#818 | a library graph; hashes one fetch away |\n| 9 | Perl (Carton) | nixidae/nixidae#809 | XS builds per distribution; no hashes in the lock |\n| 10 | C#/.NET (NuGet) | nixidae/nixidae#810 | hashes in the lock; prebuilt packages, little per-package work |\n| 11 | Dart (pub) | nixidae/nixidae#811 | hashes in the lock; whole-program compile |\n| 12 | Java/Kotlin/Scala | nixidae/nixidae#812 | no standard lock; Gradle with verification metadata at best |\n| 13 | Swift (SwiftPM) | nixidae/nixidae#813 | no hashes; no build graph as data |\n| 14 | Deno, Julia, R | nixidae/nixidae#819 | lower value; Julia has Go's hash question too |\n\nTwo questions decide the rank of each:\n\n- **Does the lock carry hashes that Nix takes?** Then a fetch is a fixed output from the lock alone, and `out` is possible when the lock is also the whole graph. Go, Zig and Julia carry hashes of their own kind, so one answer serves all three.\n- **Is there per-package work at build time?** Native extensions, build scripts, compile units. Without it, a lane shows little that npm does not.\n"}
{"issue": 814, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdW-8zA", "before": "Status: nixidae/nixidae#816 (pnpm, bun, Yarn v1) is done and landed, with five real pnpm and Yarn projects in tests/node-live. nixidae/nixidae#806 (Go) is investigated and paused: an h1 fetch needs pynixd or a Nix patch (see nixidae/nixidae#806). Next by rank: Ruby (#24)."}
{"issue": 815, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP7SfQ", "before": "A lane for `mix.lock` (Elixir) and `rebar.lock` (Erlang), one node per dependency.\n\n- **Lock:** a Hex package has its checksums in the lock (the inner and the outer SHA-256 of the tarball): a fixed-output fetch from the lock alone.\n- **Build:** each dependency compiles on its own (`mix compile` per dependency, or rebar3), so per-package work exists. A NIF with C code is the dynamic part, as a native extension is.\n- **out:** the lock names every package, so the host can make the nodes.\n\nTracked in nixidae/nixidae#814; it may not be built.\n"}
{"issue": 817, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP7fEA", "before": "A lane for Zig's `build.zig.zon`.\n\n- **Lock:** each dependency has a `hash`, Zig's own hash of the package's file tree. It is not a NAR hash, so it raises the same question as `go.sum` (#25): a fixed-output fetch cannot take it as it is.\n- **Build:** `zig build` runs the build script as a program; the build graph is known only when it runs.\n\nWorth building only together with the answer to nixidae/nixidae#806. Tracked in nixidae/nixidae#814; it may not be built.\n"}
{"issue": 818, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP7nJA", "before": "A lane for opam lock files and dune.\n\n- **Lock:** `opam lock` writes `*.opam.locked`, with versions; the opam repository holds the checksum of each archive, so the hashes are one fetch away, as for Hackage (#26).\n- **Build:** dune knows the library graph; one node per opam package is natural, one per library possible.\n\nTracked in nixidae/nixidae#814; it may not be built.\n"}
{"issue": 819, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP7tLw", "before": "Lower-value lanes, tracked together.\n\n- **Deno** (`deno.lock`): integrity hashes for npm and JSR packages. Mostly a reader for the npm lane.\n- **Julia** (`Manifest.toml`): each package has a `git-tree-sha1`, a git tree hash, not a NAR hash. Same question as nixidae/nixidae#806. Precompilation per package is the dynamic part.\n- **R** (`renv.lock`): versions, with hashes for some sources only. Packages with C or Fortran compile per package.\n\nTracked in nixidae/nixidae#814; none of these may be built.\n"}
{"issue": 820, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP7zEQ", "before": "Large CRD schemas dominate YAML output during routine inspection.\n\nMeasured on dynhetz, x86_64-linux, with easykubenix `b55bd7c1` and SOLID nixlab3. All IFDs are warm.\nThe render has 1,625 objects, including 266 CRDs. CRDs account for 83.98% of YAML representation calls.\n\n| Output | Bytes | Median serialization CPU, five runs |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Full render | 32,057,451 | 1.289 s |\n| CRDs omitted | 3,965,071 | 0.200 s |\n\nPropose opt-in `--skip-crds` on `ekn render`, `ekn diff`, and `ekn clusterdiff`.\n- Filter before YAML emission. Keep defaults and deployment commands unchanged.\n- For diff, exclude CRD files and their kustomization references on both sides.\n- For clusterdiff, exclude CRDs before decryption, live reads, dry runs, and YAML emission. Keep custom resources.\n- One-sided filtering produces false CRD deletions and kustomization changes. Reproduced through the installed `diff_manifests`.\n- Tests must cover CRD additions, deletions, modifications, and unchanged workloads. Workload changes must remain visible.\n- Define its interaction with `render --validate`; CRD schemas still support workload validation.\n\nAn early Nix filter preserves all remaining objects, but removes only 0.036% of Nix function-counter work here.\nThese figures measure serialization, not end-to-end speedup. Related: nixidae/nixidae#460, nixidae/nixidae#777.\n"}
{"issue": 821, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP75iw", "before": "`ekn render` can change string values when Kubernetes reads its YAML output.\nTested easykubenix b55bd7c1c6e358b90f7348bb2bdcd05afe3b8e00, using its packaged Python and `ekn-yaml2json`.\n\n`yaml.dump(object, Dumper=yaml.CSafeDumper, sort_keys=False, default_flow_style=False)` emits this ConfigMap:\n```\napiVersion: v1\nkind: ConfigMap\nmetadata:\n  name: probe\ndata:\n  letter: y\n  exponent: 1e+06\n```\nBoth input values are Python strings. `ekn-yaml2json --shape=list` reads `letter` as `true` and `exponent` as `1000000`.\nExpected: both values remain strings. Kubernetes requires string values in ConfigMap data.\nPyYAML's resolver leaves these strings unquoted; the Kubernetes YAML converter resolves them differently.\nPlease make output quoting match Kubernetes scalar resolution, including mapping keys.\nRegression cases should include `y`, `Y`, `n`, `N`, exponent strings, and signed 64-bit integers.\nThis concerns YAML output. It is separate from the cached YAML-to-JSON IFD converter in nixidae/nixidae#460.\n"}
{"issue": 822, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP8Aag", "before": "`ekn.gitops.flatten_manifests` spends substantial Python CPU building YAML representation nodes, even with `CSafeDumper`.\nTested easykubenix b55bd7c1c6e358b90f7348bb2bdcd05afe3b8e00 and nanopynix a3359a2ac7fbc4cc7663d1ac8571f6ca140d9821.\nThe solid-kubernetes nixlab3 fixture contains 1,625 objects and emits 32,057,451 YAML bytes.\nI rendered fully before profiling, to cache all IFD. Measurements below isolate serialization after JSON decoding.\n\n| Emitter | Median process CPU, five runs | Output |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| Current `flatten_manifests` | 1.246 s | Baseline |\n| Direct PyYAML events | 0.851 s | Byte identical |\n\nThe prototype recursively emits `MappingStart/EndEvent`, `SequenceStart/EndEvent`, and `ScalarEvent` directly from JSON values.\nIt passes those events to `yaml.emit(..., Dumper=yaml.CSafeDumper)`, avoiding the intermediate representation node tree.\nString implicit flags come from `Resolver.resolve(ScalarNode, value, (True, False))`; numeric text matches SafeRepresenter.\nThis saves 32% serialization CPU on this fixture. It does not measure total render speed.\nThe result also round-trips through the packaged Kubernetes `ekn-yaml2json --shape=list` to the original objects.\nPlease investigate this path for JSON-compatible rendered objects, preserving document order and existing output formatting.\nTests should cover scalar types, empty collections, Unicode, multiline strings, and separate document boundaries.\nExisting scalar quoting errors remain in this byte-preserving prototype; fix them separately in nixidae/nixidae#821.\nRelated: nixidae/nixidae#820 (optional CRD omission), nixidae/nixidae#460 (input conversion), nixidae/nixidae#777 (render caching).\n"}
{"issue": 822, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdXAA3g", "before": "I also tested packaged native emitters on the same warm fixture.\nFixture: Lillecarl/solid-kubernetes revision `2a071bf8`, attribute `environments.nixlab3`.\nThese measurements include Python JSON encoding, input/output file writes, and subprocess CPU.\nAll rows start from the same decoded objects. Five repetitions give these median CPU values:\n\n| Output path | CPU | Kubernetes round-trip |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| Current PyYAML | 1.218 s | Equal |\n| Direct PyYAML events | 0.842 s | Equal, byte identical |\n| libfyaml 0.9.6 `fy-tool` | 0.679 s | Equal, formatting differs |\n| yq-go 4.53.3 | 2.087 s | Ten changed values |\n\nThe libfyaml input is a YAML document stream containing compact JSON objects.\nCommand: `fy-tool --dump --mode=block --json=no input-stream.yaml`.\nIt also preserves all 27 scalar edge cases through the packaged Kubernetes converter, including nixidae/nixidae#821's strings.\nyq changes CRD enum `Off` into `false` and rounds large integer bounds. It is unsuitable here.\nDirect events offer the smaller integration change. Libfyaml offers greater CPU savings, with output-format churn and another dependency.\n"}
{"issue": 822, "kind": "comment", "id": "IC_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABdXABnw", "before": "Implementation is ready in unpublished nixidae commit `0598ebf05dc8` on dynhetz, workspace `/home/lillecarl/Code/nixidae-yaml-output`.\nThe production path retains generic rendering for raw YAML aliases, cycles, and timestamps.\nA bounded 4,096-entry scalar-resolution cache offsets the Kubernetes quoting checks from nixidae/nixidae#821.\nFive warmed process CPU samples give 1.235 seconds original versus 0.888 seconds candidate: 28% less serialization CPU.\nBoth full SOLID fixture renders preserve every byte when compared against the same inputs.\nThe packaged nixlab3 profile records 1,625 direct-emitter calls and no representation calls.\nThe Kubernetes converter preserves all 1,625 objects. Focused tests, strict lint, types, and packaged Nix gates pass.\nThe full suite reports 1,215 passes and four independently reproduced workspace fixture failures (#63).\nNothing is pushed or merged; these commits need integration from the local workspace.\n"}
{"issue": 823, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP8HbA", "before": "The example flake fails when easykubenix lives in a Jujutsu workspace without colocated `.git` metadata.\nFixture: `docs/examples/example-flake/flake.nix` and its unchanged lock file, from nixidae revision `ac31ac3a`.\nIts easykubenix input is the relative path `path:../../../.`.\n\nThe three `tests/test_cli.py::TestFlakeEval` tests pass in the main nixidae checkout.\nThey fail in `/home/lillecarl/Code/nixidae-yaml-output/easykubenix`, an isolated jj workspace.\nThe unmodified packaged evaluator also fails when given that workspace's fixture path.\nStock Nix reproduces the same error:\n```\nnix eval --impure --json --expr '(builtins.getFlake \"/path/to/jj-workspace/easykubenix/docs/examples/example-flake\").eknConfig.x86_64-linux.myapp.kubernetes.generated'\n```\nError: `path '/flake.nix' does not exist`, while evaluating `easykubenix.result` in `call-flake.nix`.\nThe source snapshot cannot resolve the relative ancestor input in this layout.\nPlease make the test fixture work outside a colocated Git checkout, or document and test the required source layout.\nThis predates the YAML changes for nixidae/nixidae#820\u2013#62. Those pass their focused tests and packaged Nix gates.\n"}
{"issue": 825, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP8UNQ", "before": "Split out of nixidae/nixidae#29, which closed on the dinit breakage and its two guards. This remainder is the third ask there: mutable tags.\n\nThe DaemonSet deploys `ghcr.io/nixidae/nixkube/scratch:1.0.1` (kubenix/daemonset.nix:225), and scratchimage.nix:42-50 pushes both `:1.0.1` and `:latest`. A floating tag means a node can start a different closure than the one CI verified.\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n\n- Deployment manifests name an immutable digest, not a floating tag.\n- The release job publishes the digest it verified, and nothing else serves as the deploy tag."}
{"issue": 826, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP8a4A", "before": "Split out of nixidae/nixidae#797 (item 1), which bundled four unrelated items.\n\n`lib/python/planner.nix:296` calls `hostRow` per row, and `:311-327` is a plain per-entry function with no cache or memo structure. A repo-wide grep for memoise/memoize/cache near `hostRow` finds nothing.\n\nNext step: a measured build showing the per-level `hostRow` evaluation cost, which decides whether memoisation is worth it."}
{"issue": 827, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP8gkQ", "before": "Split out of nixidae/nixidae#797 (item 2), which bundled four unrelated items.\n\n`lib/python/hints.nix:7-8` and `:73` source hints from `python.pkgs`, the nixpkgs version rather than the locked version. Mismatch reporting exists only for the from-nixpkgs path (`src/pydd/python/_environment.py:401-408` prints nixpkgs-supplied vs lock-requested versions). Nothing reports skew in the hint inputs themselves, though `lib/python/planner.nix:329-334` already carries the nixpkgs version beside the pinned one."}
{"issue": 828, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYP8mxg", "before": "Split out of nixidae/nixidae#797 (item 3), which bundled four unrelated items.\n\nA planner failure surfaces as `failed to obtain derivation of ...^out` with the cause buried higher in the log. A grep for `failed to obtain|derivation of` finds hits only in docs and comments \u2014 no handler or failure-wrapping code in `src/` or `lib/core/`. Planners log `plan:` progress lines, but nothing re-surfaces the cause at the failure itself.\n\nConfirming the UX needs a failing build; the fix should carry one."}
{"issue": 20, "kind": "issue", "id": "I_kwDOUQ5I1c8AAAABYOfT_w", "before": "pynix evaluates and builds through nanopynix. The common case of `nixos-rebuild` and `home-manager` is a few steps on top of that. pynix can do those steps itself, without the Nix CLI underneath.\n\nScope, kept small:\n\n- `pynix os build|switch|boot|test|dry-activate`: evaluate `config.system.build.toplevel`, build it, add a generation to `/nix/var/nix/profiles/system`, run `switch-to-configuration <action>`.\n- `pynix home build|switch`: evaluate `activationPackage`, build it, add a generation to the home-manager profile, run `activate`.\n- The target syntax of `pynix build`: `--file` plus an attribute path first, a flake target second.\n- Evaluate and build as the user. Elevate (sudo or run0) only for the profile and the activation.\n- Show progress with pynix's build monitor.\n\nNot in scope here: `--target-host`, `--build-host`, `--rollback`, generation lists, specialisation selection. Each gets its own issue when someone needs it.\n\nOpen questions:\n\n- Two subcommands, or one `pynix rebuild` that detects the configuration kind (nixidae/nixidae#256 already detects it)?\n- What does the `home-manager` wrapper do beyond `activate` that we must copy (news, `HOME_MANAGER_BACKUP_EXT`, `-b`)?\n\nPrior art: `nixos-rebuild-ng` in nixpkgs (Python), `nh`.\n"}
